(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({"id":"M3","code":"Module 3","title":"Mobile App Development","overview":"<p>Module 3 takes you into the mobile world that most Caribbean people now carry in their pockets. You will map the mobile ecosystem (content, infrastructure, devices, users, carriers, developers and the enabling environment), distinguish Android, iOS and other platforms, smart devices and the Internet of Things, and contrast the Caribbean mobile ecosystem with the global picture in statistics, infrastructure and social impact. You will examine why local apps matter, analyse the factors that shape the mobile user experience, and apply real tools and techniques, from conceptualisation, wireframing and prototyping in Figma to building with MIT App Inventor, Thunkable, Android Studio or Xcode, and testing. You will finish by planning how to distribute and market an app, choosing a revenue model, and discussing the trends shaping mobile technology. This module follows the nine sections of the DME2 course outline.</p>\n<p>In CAPE Unit 2, <b>Paper 01</b> has 45 multiple-choice items, 15 from each module, worth 20%. <b>Paper 02</b> has three compulsory 15-mark questions, one per module, worth 20% in total and sat without internet access; for Module 3 expect to develop an app concept, explain user experience factors, sketch a wireframe, and recommend revenue and marketing strategies. The <b>School-Based Assessment (Paper 031)</b> is worth 60%: in groups of 2 to 3 you design and implement a product or service (preferably for a local social or entrepreneurial need), submitting a proposal (14 marks), progress and process evidence (12) and the product (19), including a <b>30-second audio-visual short ad</b> (5). The report is limited to 1,500 words. A mobile app prototype that solves a real Caribbean problem makes an excellent SBA product.</p>\n<p>Work through the units in order. Keep a notebook for wireframe sketches and install a free tool such as Figma, MIT App Inventor or Thunkable so you can try each technique yourself. Use the easier version of an activity if you need a first step, complete each quick check-in honestly and write the short task at the end of each unit. Finish with the Module 3 Test (pass mark 70%) and the Paper 02 practice question.</p>","objectives":["By the end you will be able to discuss the relationships and interdependencies among content, infrastructure, devices, users, carriers, developers and the enabling environment within the mobile ecosystem.","By the end you will be able to distinguish among mobile operating systems (Android, iOS, HarmonyOS), platforms, smart and wearable devices, the Internet of Things and cross-platform development tools.","By the end you will be able to contrast the Caribbean mobile ecosystem with the global ecosystem across statistics, social and economic impact, applications, content production, infrastructure and policy.","By the end you will be able to examine the benefits of developing mobile applications that address local and regional Caribbean needs.","By the end you will be able to describe the factors that impact the mobile user experience and explain design strategies to address them.","By the end you will be able to apply tools and techniques to conceptualise, wireframe, prototype, build and test a mobile application.","By the end you will be able to describe approaches to distributing and marketing mobile applications, including app store optimisation and the user journey.","By the end you will be able to discuss commercial, open-source and civic revenue models for mobile applications and evaluate which suits a given app and audience.","By the end you will be able to discuss the factors that influence development and trends in the mobile ecosystem, with attention to Caribbean implications."],"glossary":[{"term":"Mobile ecosystem","def":"The connected network of content, infrastructure, devices, users, carriers, developers and policies that makes mobile technology work."},{"term":"Infrastructure (mobile)","def":"The physical and technical backbone of mobile services, such as cell towers, spectrum, data centres and submarine cables."},{"term":"Carrier (telco)","def":"A company that provides mobile voice and data connectivity, such as Flow, Digicel or Flow."},{"term":"Enabling environment","def":"The policies, regulators, laws and developer communities that allow the mobile ecosystem to grow."},{"term":"Interdependence","def":"The way each part of the mobile ecosystem relies on the others, so a failure in one affects all."},{"term":"Android","def":"Google's open-source mobile operating system used by many device makers."},{"term":"iOS","def":"Apple's closed mobile operating system, used only on Apple devices such as the iPhone."},{"term":"HarmonyOS","def":"Huawei's operating system for phones, wearables and smart devices."},{"term":"Fragmentation","def":"The wide variety of Android devices, screen sizes and OS versions that developers must support."},{"term":"SDK","def":"A software development kit: the tools, libraries and documentation needed to build apps for a platform."},{"term":"Android Studio","def":"Google's official development environment for Android apps, using Kotlin or Java."},{"term":"Xcode","def":"Apple's development environment for iOS apps, using the Swift language."},{"term":"Cross-platform tool","def":"A framework such as Flutter or React Native that builds apps for Android and iOS from one code base."},{"term":"Wearable device","def":"A smart device worn on the body, such as a smartwatch or fitness band."},{"term":"Internet of Things (IoT)","def":"Everyday objects with sensors and connectivity that collect and exchange data."},{"term":"Smartphone penetration","def":"The percentage of a population that owns or uses a smartphone."},{"term":"Submarine cable","def":"An undersea fibre-optic cable that carries international Internet traffic between islands and continents."},{"term":"Mobile money","def":"Financial services such as payments and transfers carried out through a mobile phone."},{"term":"E-government","def":"Government services delivered online or through apps, such as paying fees or renewing documents."},{"term":"Content consumption gap","def":"The imbalance in which Caribbean users consume far more imported mobile content than they produce locally."},{"term":"Diaspora","def":"Caribbean people living abroad, who stay connected to home through mobile apps and social media."},{"term":"Local app","def":"An app designed to solve problems or serve needs specific to a community, island or region."},{"term":"User experience (UX)","def":"How a person feels and performs when using a product, including ease, speed and satisfaction."},{"term":"Touch target","def":"The tappable area of a button or link; guidelines suggest about 44 to 48 points or pixels."},{"term":"Thumb zone","def":"The area of a phone screen easily reached by the thumb during one-handed use."},{"term":"Offline mode","def":"An app feature that stores data on the device so key functions work without a connection."},{"term":"Screen reader","def":"Assistive software, such as TalkBack or VoiceOver, that reads screen content aloud."},{"term":"User profile","def":"A description of target users by age, ability, digital skills, language, culture and needs."},{"term":"Conceptualisation (app)","def":"Defining an app's purpose, target users and core features before design begins."},{"term":"Core features","def":"The few essential functions an app must have to solve its main problem."},{"term":"Wireframe","def":"A simple layout sketch of app screens showing structure and placement, without final colours or images."},{"term":"User flow","def":"The path of screens and actions a user follows to complete a task in an app."},{"term":"Clickable prototype","def":"A linked set of screen designs, often made in Figma, that simulates how an app will work."},{"term":"No-code/low-code builder","def":"A visual tool such as MIT App Inventor, Thunkable, Glide or Adalo that builds apps with little or no hand-written code."},{"term":"Debugging","def":"Finding and fixing errors in an app's code or logic."},{"term":"Unit testing","def":"Testing small, individual parts of an app's code to check that each works correctly."},{"term":"User acceptance testing (UAT)","def":"Testing by real target users to confirm that the app meets their needs before release."},{"term":"Beta release","def":"A pre-launch version given to a limited group of testers to find problems and gather feedback."},{"term":"Enterprise distribution","def":"Releasing an app privately to an organisation's own staff rather than through public app stores."},{"term":"Direct download","def":"Distributing an app installation file from a website instead of an app store."},{"term":"App Store Optimisation (ASO)","def":"Improving an app's title, keywords, description, screenshots and ratings so it ranks higher in app store searches."},{"term":"User journey","def":"The stages users pass through: discover, test, review, purchase, download and retain."},{"term":"Retention","def":"Keeping users active and returning to an app after they download it."},{"term":"Freemium","def":"A revenue model in which the core app is free and premium features are paid for."},{"term":"In-app purchase","def":"Buying virtual goods, content packs or unlockable features inside an app."},{"term":"Rewarded video ad","def":"An advert users choose to watch in exchange for an in-app reward."},{"term":"Interstitial ad","def":"A full-screen advert that appears at natural breaks in an app."},{"term":"Subscription model","def":"A revenue model in which users pay a recurring monthly or annual fee for access."},{"term":"White-label app","def":"An app built once and licensed to other businesses to rebrand as their own."},{"term":"Developer fee","def":"A fee paid to an app store to publish apps, such as the Apple Developer Program fee."},{"term":"Civic app","def":"An app built for public good, such as disaster alerts, health information or public services."},{"term":"Super app","def":"A single app that bundles many services, such as messaging, payments and shopping, for example WeChat or Grab."},{"term":"5G","def":"The fifth generation of mobile networks, offering much faster speeds and lower latency."},{"term":"Edge computing","def":"Processing data close to the user or device rather than in distant data centres, to reduce delay."},{"term":"Augmented reality (AR)","def":"Technology that overlays digital content on a live view of the real world."},{"term":"Digital identity","def":"A secure electronic means of proving who you are, used for online government and banking services."},{"term":"GDPR","def":"The European Union's General Data Protection Regulation, which sets strict rules for handling personal data."}],"units":[{"id":"M3U1","title":"The mobile ecosystem","objectives":["Identify and explain the four core components of the mobile ecosystem: content, infrastructure, devices and users.","Explain the role of carriers and telcos such as TelEm, Digicel, Flow (Liberty Latin America) and UTS (Chippie) in data delivery and connectivity.","Describe mobile consumer behaviour, expectations and usage patterns in the Caribbean.","Explain the enabling environment: policy frameworks, regulators (BTP, ECTEL, OUR, TATT) and developer communities.","Discuss the relationships and interdependence among ecosystem players, using Caribbean examples.","Evaluate connectivity challenges and local opportunities in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem."],"videos":[{"title":"What is the mobile ecosystem?","q":"mobile ecosystem explained devices networks apps users"},{"title":"How mobile networks work","q":"how do mobile networks work cell towers explained simply"},{"title":"What does a telecoms regulator do?","q":"what does a telecommunications regulator do explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What is the mobile ecosystem?","html":"<p>An <b>ecosystem</b> in nature is a community of living things that depend on each other. The <b>mobile ecosystem</b> works the same way: it is the network of <b>people, organisations, technologies and rules</b> that work together so that a mobile device can do something useful. When a student in Bridgetown opens WhatsApp to send a voice note to a cousin in Curaçao, dozens of players make that moment possible.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Mobile ecosystem:</b> the interconnected set of content, infrastructure, devices and users, supported by carriers, developers and an enabling environment of policy and regulation, that allows mobile services to be created, delivered and used.</p>\n<p>There are four core <b>components</b>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Content</b>: what people use on the device. Apps, games, videos, music, news, maps, mobile banking, e-books. A national newspaper's news app and a Carnival schedule on Instagram are both mobile content.</li>\n<li><b>Infrastructure</b>: the physical and technical backbone. Cell towers, fibre, submarine cables, data centres, cloud servers, app stores and payment gateways.</li>\n<li><b>Devices</b>: the hardware people hold or wear. Smartphones, tablets, smartwatches, smart speakers, plus the operating systems (Android, iOS) that run them.</li>\n<li><b>Users</b>: the people (and businesses) who use mobile services. Students, tourists at Grace Bay, fishermen in Gouyave, a cruise port's operations team.</li>\n</ul>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 300\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Mobile ecosystem diagram: users at the centre linked to content, infrastructure, devices and carriers, all inside the enabling environment\">\n<rect x=\"6\" y=\"6\" width=\"408\" height=\"288\" rx=\"16\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"24\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">Enabling environment: policy, regulators, developer communities</text>\n<circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"160\" r=\"40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"currentColor\">USERS</text>\n<rect x=\"150\" y=\"40\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"63\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Content (apps)</text>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"142\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Devices + OS</text>\n<rect x=\"280\" y=\"142\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<text x=\"340\" y=\"165\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Infrastructure</text>\n<rect x=\"150\" y=\"240\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">Carriers (telcos)</text>\n<line x1=\"210\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<line x1=\"140\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<line x1=\"250\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<line x1=\"210\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<line x1=\"270\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"142\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n<line x1=\"80\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"58\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n<line x1=\"270\" y1=\"258\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"178\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n<line x1=\"80\" y1=\"178\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"258\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 3\"/>\n</svg>\n<p>The solid lines show each component serving the user. The dashed lines show that the components also depend on <b>each other</b>: an app (content) is useless without a device to run it and a network (carrier and infrastructure) to deliver it. Everything sits inside the <b>enabling environment</b> of laws, regulators and developer communities.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The command word in this objective is <b>discuss</b>. A list of components earns only a few marks. You must show <b>relationships</b>: \"Content depends on infrastructure because ...\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The mobile ecosystem is everything that has to work together for your phone to do something useful. It has four core parts: <b>content</b> (the apps and videos you use), <b>infrastructure</b> (towers, cables and servers), <b>devices</b> (the phone or watch) and <b>users</b> (the people). Carriers, developers and the rules set by government support all four. When you send a WhatsApp voice note from Bridgetown to Curaçao, every part plays a role.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A common misconception is that the ecosystem is just a list of four boxes. Examiners reward the <b>arrows</b> between them. Content creates demand for infrastructure: more video traffic pushes carriers to add capacity. Devices limit content: an app built for the newest iPhone may fail on an older Android phone. Users shape everything through what they download, delete and pay for. The enabling environment then sets the rules; a regulator's spectrum decision can decide whether a 5G service is even possible on an island. In a <b>discuss</b> answer, aim for at least two relationships that run both ways, each with a reason and a consequence, rather than four separate definitions.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which component is it?","intro":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at the Island Pulse Media, is mapping the ecosystem for a new beach-safety app. Sort each item into the correct component.</p>","main":"Which ecosystem component does this belong to?","bins":["Content","Infrastructure","Devices","Users"],"items":[{"t":"A lifeguard team updating flag colours each morning.","bin":3,"fb":"The lifeguards use the app to post updates, so they are users (and content contributors)."},{"t":"A tourist from Toronto checking conditions at Shoal Bay, Anguilla.","bin":3,"fb":"A person using the service is a user."},{"t":"A short safety video about rip currents.","bin":0,"fb":"Video shown to people is content."},{"t":"A cell tower on Crocus Hill carrying 4G signals.","bin":1,"fb":"Towers are part of the physical network that delivers data: infrastructure."},{"t":"A mid-range Samsung phone running Android.","bin":2,"fb":"The handset and its operating system make up the device component."},{"t":"A live wave-height map inside the app.","bin":0,"fb":"It is what people view and use on the screen, so it is content."},{"t":"The cloud server that stores the beach data.","bin":1,"fb":"Servers and data centres are back-end infrastructure, even if users never see them."},{"t":"The Google Play and Apple App Store distribution systems.","bin":1,"fb":"App stores are part of the distribution infrastructure that gets apps onto devices."},{"t":"An Apple Watch that buzzes with a storm alert.","bin":2,"fb":"Wearables are devices in the ecosystem."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can now classify the four components: content is what people use, infrastructure delivers it, devices run it and users are the people it serves.","tutor":[{"h":"Ask one question","p":"<p>Is it something you <b>see</b> (content), something you <b>hold</b> (device), something that <b>carries</b> data (infrastructure) or a <b>person</b> (user)?</p>"},{"h":"Hidden parts","p":"<p>Servers, cables and app stores are invisible to most users, but they are still part of the system.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which component? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A news video in an app.","bin":0,"fb":"Something you watch: content."},{"t":"A cell tower.","bin":1,"fb":"It carries data: infrastructure."},{"t":"A smartphone.","bin":2,"fb":"Hardware you hold: device."},{"t":"A student using the app.","bin":3,"fb":"A person: user."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which component is it? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A Grade One map of the ecosystem also shows the <b>enabling environment</b> that surrounds the four components. Jerome's beach-safety app map now has trickier items. Sort each one, including the rules and bodies that shape the whole system. Some items sit close to two components, so look for the key detail.</p>","main":"Which component, or the enabling environment?","bins":["Content","Infrastructure","Devices","Users","Enabling environment"],"items":[{"t":"The iOS update that adds a new location-permission screen.","bin":2,"fb":"The operating system is part of the device component, because it runs the hardware."},{"t":"A payment gateway that processes donations to the lifeguard fund inside the app.","bin":1,"fb":"Payment gateways are part of the technical backbone that services run on: infrastructure."},{"t":"The data centre in Miami that hosts the app's database.","bin":1,"fb":"Back-end servers and data centres are infrastructure, wherever they are located."},{"t":"A small beach bar that pays to show its happy-hour offer in the app.","bin":3,"fb":"Businesses that use a mobile service are users too, even when they pay rather than browse."},{"t":"A push notification warning that red flags are up at Shoal Bay.","bin":0,"fb":"The message people read on screen is content, even though it arrives as a notification."},{"t":"Data protection rules on how long the app may keep users' location history.","bin":4,"fb":"Laws and policy that shape how apps work belong to the enabling environment."},{"t":"A podcast episode on rip-current safety streamed in the app.","bin":0,"fb":"Audio people listen to is content."},{"t":"The BTP licensing the radio spectrum used by the island's 4G network.","bin":4,"fb":"A regulator's licensing decision is part of the enabling environment, not the infrastructure itself."},{"t":"A smart speaker in a guest house that reads the surf forecast aloud.","bin":2,"fb":"A smart speaker is hardware people use to reach the service: a device."},{"t":"A cruise passenger from Miami roaming on a local network to check the flags.","bin":3,"fb":"However they connect, a person using the service is a user."}],"win":"Excellent. You placed the tricky items (operating systems, payment gateways, businesses as users) and separated the four core components from the enabling environment that surrounds them."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Carriers and telcos","html":"<p>A <b>carrier</b> (also called a <b>mobile network operator</b>, MNO, or <b>telco</b>) owns or runs the network that connects mobile devices. Without a carrier, a smartphone is a camera and a calculator.</p>\n<p><b>What carriers do</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Build and run the radio network</b>: towers, antennas and spectrum for 2G, 3G, 4G LTE and, increasingly, 5G.</li>\n<li><b>Deliver data and voice</b>: route calls, SMS and mobile data between your phone and the Internet.</li>\n<li><b>Connect to the world</b>: buy capacity on submarine cables and international links.</li>\n<li><b>Issue SIMs and eSIMs</b>, manage numbers and offer <b>prepaid</b> and <b>postpaid</b> plans.</li>\n<li><b>Bill customers</b> and sometimes enable <b>carrier billing</b> (paying for apps through your phone credit) and <b>mobile money</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Roaming</b>: agreements that let visitors use their home SIM abroad, which matters greatly for tourism islands.</li>\n<li><b>Zero-rating</b> and bundles: offers such as \"free WhatsApp\" or social bundles, which shape what customers use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Caribbean carriers</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Carrier</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>TelEm Group</b></td><td>Government-owned telco of Sint Maarten; mobile, fixed and Internet services; operates the SMPR-1 submarine cable to Puerto Rico.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Digicel</b></td><td>A regional operator founded in Jamaica, present in many Caribbean markets, from Haiti and Jamaica to the OECS islands and both sides of St. Martin.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Flow</b> (Liberty Latin America)</td><td>Operates across much of the English-speaking Caribbean, such as Jamaica, Barbados, Trinidad and Tobago (as a fixed-line and TV provider), the OECS islands and the Cayman Islands.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>bmobile</b> (TSTT)</td><td>Trinidad and Tobago's national telco, offering mobile, fixed and Internet services.</td></tr> <tr><td><b>UTS</b> (brand <b>Chippie</b>)</td><td>A Dutch Caribbean telco with mobile services in Curaçao and Sint Maarten.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Small islands can still have several networks. On St. Martin, for example, operators such as Orange and Dauphin Telecom also serve the French side, so a single 87 km&#178; island has several competing networks. Many residents carry <b>two SIMs</b> to get the best rates, which is one reason mobile subscriptions in small islands can exceed 100 per 100 people.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Do not confuse the <b>carrier</b> (network) with the <b>device manufacturer</b> (Samsung, Apple) or the <b>app developer</b>. Paper 01 often offers all three as options.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A carrier is the company that runs the mobile network, such as Digicel, Flow, bmobile or TelEm. It builds the towers, carries your calls and data, connects the island to the rest of the world through undersea cables, and sells you a prepaid or postpaid plan. Without a carrier, your phone cannot call, text or go online away from Wi-Fi.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Carriers do more than move data: they shape behaviour. A zero-rated WhatsApp bundle makes WhatsApp the natural ordering channel for small businesses, which in turn affects whether a vendor ever needs its own app. Carrier billing lets people without bank cards pay for apps, widening the market for developers. On an 87 km&#178; island with several competing networks, roaming and dual-SIM use are normal, so an app that assumes one stable network or one phone number per person may fail. Exam trap: Paper 01 often offers Samsung or Apple as a \"carrier\". They make devices and run app stores; they do not run the radio network.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Carrier roles","intro":"<p>Keisha is writing a social media explainer for a telco client. Match each carrier service or term to its meaning.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Roaming","r":"A cruise passenger from Miami uses a US SIM on a Barbados network"},{"l":"Prepaid plan","r":"A student tops up credit before using data, with no monthly bill"},{"l":"Carrier billing","r":"A game purchase is charged to the phone account instead of a card"},{"l":"Zero-rating","r":"Using WhatsApp does not count against the data allowance"},{"l":"Spectrum","r":"Radio frequencies licensed to an operator for its network"},{"l":"eSIM","r":"A digital SIM downloaded to the phone with no plastic card"},{"l":"Mobile network operator","r":"The company that owns and runs the towers and radio network"}],"win":"Very good. You can explain how carriers deliver connectivity and shape how people pay for and use mobile services.","tutor":[{"h":"Money words","p":"<p>Prepaid, postpaid and carrier billing are about <b>how people pay</b>.</p>"},{"h":"Network words","p":"<p>Spectrum and the operator relate to the <b>radio network</b> itself.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Carrier roles (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Roaming","r":"Using your home SIM in another country"},{"l":"Prepaid","r":"Top up credit first, no monthly bill"},{"l":"Zero-rating","r":"An app that does not use up your data"},{"l":"Carrier","r":"The company that runs the mobile network"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Carrier roles (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Keisha's telco client now wants an explainer for business customers. Each card on the right is a real-world situation. Match it to the carrier service or term it shows. Some pairs are close, so read for the key detail.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Roaming","r":"A Trinidadian visitor's home SIM connects to a Jamaican network, and the charges appear on her home bill"},{"l":"eSIM","r":"A visitor scans a QR code at the airport and downloads a local data plan with no plastic card"},{"l":"Zero-rating","r":"A carrier lets customers use a government health app without it counting against their data allowance"},{"l":"Carrier billing","r":"A teenager in Kingston pays for a game upgrade from her prepaid phone credit"},{"l":"Mobile money","r":"A market vendor receives payment into a wallet linked to her phone number"},{"l":"Postpaid plan","r":"A hotel is billed at the end of each month for the calls and data its staff used"},{"l":"Spectrum licence","r":"The regulator grants an operator the right to use specific radio frequencies for its 5G network"},{"l":"International gateway","r":"The point where a carrier's core network hands traffic to a submarine cable"}],"win":"Excellent. You matched carrier services to real situations, including close pairs such as roaming and eSIM, and carrier billing and mobile money."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Consumers: behaviour, expectations and usage patterns","html":"<p><b>Consumers</b> are the users who pay for, choose and judge mobile services. Their habits drive what developers build and what carriers sell. The course outline asks you to describe their <b>behaviour</b>, <b>expectations</b> and <b>usage patterns</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Behaviour and usage patterns in the Caribbean</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mobile first, sometimes mobile only</b>: for many households the phone is the main or only Internet device.</li>\n<li><b>Prepaid dominance</b>: many users top up in small amounts, so they watch data use closely and prefer apps that work on Wi-Fi or offline.</li>\n<li><b>Messaging at the centre</b>: WhatsApp is used for family, school groups, business orders and even government notices. Small businesses in Port of Spain take orders by WhatsApp rather than through their own app.</li>\n<li><b>Social video</b>: TikTok, Instagram Reels and YouTube take a large share of data, especially around Carnival and major events.</li>\n<li><b>Peaks</b>: usage spikes during hurricanes (checking alerts), cricket matches, Carnival and when cruise ships are in port.</li>\n<li><b>Multi-SIM and cross-border use</b>: switching SIMs between networks (for example between the Dutch and French sides of St. Martin), or between home and the diaspora.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Expectations</b>: consumers expect apps to be <b>fast</b>, <b>cheap on data</b>, <b>secure</b> (especially banking), <b>always available</b>, <b>easy to use</b> on small screens and <b>relevant</b> to local life: local prices, local places, local language and Caribbean English or Papiamentu where appropriate.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Consumer behaviour</th><th>What it means for the ecosystem</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Prefers prepaid, data-conscious</td><td>Developers build lighter apps and offline modes; carriers sell social bundles.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Expects instant response</td><td>Infrastructure must keep latency low; slow apps get deleted.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trusts familiar brands</td><td>Local banks and telcos have an advantage over unknown apps.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a \"discuss\" answer, link each behaviour to a consequence for another player. \"Because many users are on prepaid plans, developers such as Jerome must reduce app size and data use.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Consumers are the people who choose, pay for and judge mobile services. In the Caribbean many use prepaid data, so they want apps that are light and cheap to run. They use WhatsApp for almost everything, watch lots of short videos, and use their phones most during storms, Carnival and big cricket matches. They expect apps to be fast, safe and about local life.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Treat behaviour as evidence. A figure of more than 100 mobile subscriptions per 100 people does not mean everyone owns two phones; it often reflects dual-SIM use, as on St. Martin with its Dutch and French sides. Prepaid use explains why many people switch off automatic updates and avoid large downloads, which pushes developers towards small app sizes and offline modes. A common misconception is that young users care mostly about looks: surveys of why people uninstall apps usually rank slowness, crashes and data use above appearance. When you describe a usage pattern, link it to a consequence for another player, such as a carrier adding capacity on days when several cruise ships are in port.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Reading consumer behaviour","intro":"<p>Island Pulse Media is planning an app for the Oranjestad Night Market. Answer each question about mobile consumers.</p>","items":[{"text":"Most target users are on prepaid data. Which design choice fits BEST?","options":["Compressed images and an offline menu","Autoplay HD video on the home screen","A 300 MB download with 3D models"],"answer":0,"fb":"Data-conscious prepaid users expect apps that use little data. Compression and offline content respect that."},{"text":"Vendors already take orders through WhatsApp. What does this tell Jerome?","options":["WhatsApp should be ignored","Vendors do not use phones","Users expect messaging-style ordering, so the app should link to or copy that flow"],"answer":2,"fb":"Existing behaviour shows what users find natural. Good apps build on familiar patterns."},{"text":"Traffic to the app will peak on Friday nights when cruise ships are in port. This is an example of...","options":["a regulatory framework","a device specification","a usage pattern"],"answer":2,"fb":"When and how often people use a service is a usage pattern."},{"text":"A user deletes the app because it takes eight seconds to load. Which expectation was not met?","options":["Speed and performance","Low price","Local relevance"],"answer":0,"fb":"Mobile users expect near-instant loading. Slow performance is a top reason for uninstalling."},{"text":"Visitors want to pay vendors by phone. Which consumer expectation is MOST important for payments?","options":["Bright colours","Large fonts only","Security and trust"],"answer":2,"fb":"Users will not enter payment details unless they trust the app is secure."}],"win":"Excellent. You connected consumer behaviour and expectations to design decisions, which is the kind of linking that earns discussion marks.","tutor":[{"h":"Behaviour vs expectation","p":"<p>Behaviour is what users <b>do</b>. Expectation is what they <b>want</b> the app to do for them.</p>"},{"h":"Think cost","p":"<p>For prepaid users, every megabyte costs money.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Reading consumer behaviour (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Prepaid users usually want apps that...","options":["use little data","stream HD video all the time"],"answer":0,"fb":"Data costs money on prepaid plans."},{"text":"Users expect a mobile app to load...","options":["quickly","slowly"],"answer":0,"fb":"Speed is a core expectation."},{"text":"Usage that peaks on Friday nights is a...","options":["usage pattern","regulation"],"answer":0,"fb":"When people use the app is a usage pattern."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Reading consumer behaviour (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Island Pulse Media now has three months of data from the Oranjestad Night Market app. Analyse each finding and choose the conclusion or action it BEST supports.</p>","items":[{"text":"70% of sessions happen on mobile data, not Wi-Fi, and most users top up in small amounts. Which conclusion is BEST supported?","options":["Users prefer video, so autoplay should be switched on for everyone","Data cost does not matter because users are already on mobile networks","The app should only work on Wi-Fi","Users are data-conscious, so heavy media should load only on request or on Wi-Fi"],"answer":3,"fb":"Small top-ups on mobile data show users watching every megabyte. Letting them choose when heavy media loads respects that without removing features."},{"text":"Cruise visitors use the app only on ship days, mainly between 10 a.m. and 3 p.m. How should the team use this usage pattern?","options":["Schedule extra server capacity and vendor promotions around ship days","Remove visitors from the target audience","Plan for the same traffic every day of the week","Send promotions to all users at 11 p.m."],"answer":0,"fb":"A usage pattern tells you when demand peaks. Matching capacity and promotions to ship days uses the evidence."},{"text":"A survey finds that residents trust local banks far more than unknown payment apps. How should the app handle payments?","options":["Ask users to send card details by WhatsApp","Link to payment options from banks and providers users already trust","Accept cash only and remove payments from the app","Build a brand-new wallet and ask users to load money into it"],"answer":1,"fb":"Trust is a consumer expectation. Using familiar, secure payment providers lowers the barrier to paying by phone."},{"text":"Several 2-star reviews say: \"Good app, but the menu is English only and my grandmother reads Spanish.\" Which expectation is not being met?","options":["Local relevance, including language","Fast loading","Low data use","Security"],"answer":0,"fb":"Relevance to local life includes the languages people actually read. Aruba has many Spanish-speaking residents."},{"text":"Why does vendors' existing habit of taking orders on WhatsApp matter to the developer?","options":["It proves vendors will never use an app","It only matters to the carrier, not the developer","It shows an existing behaviour the app can build on, which makes switching easier for users","It means the developer must build a full copy of WhatsApp"],"answer":2,"fb":"Good apps build on familiar patterns. A 'send order to vendor's WhatsApp' button may beat a whole new ordering system."},{"text":"During a hurricane watch, the app's traffic drops while weather and messaging apps spike. What does this BEST show?","options":["Users put safety and family contact first in a crisis, so non-essential apps should keep data use minimal","The app has crashed and needs recoding","The regulator has blocked the app","Users have stopped using their phones"],"answer":0,"fb":"Crisis behaviour is a usage pattern too. Non-essential apps should avoid competing for scarce bandwidth and battery."}],"win":"Excellent. You analysed real usage evidence and turned each finding into a design or business decision, which is exactly what a 'discuss' answer needs."}},{"type":"learn","title":"The enabling environment: policy, regulators and developers","html":"<p>The <b>enabling environment</b> is everything outside the network and device that makes the ecosystem possible: laws, policies, regulators, skills, funding and communities. Key examples are <b>policy</b>, <b>regulation</b> and <b>developers</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Policy frameworks</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Telecommunications laws</b> that license operators and allocate spectrum.</li>\n<li><b>Competition policy</b>: allowing more than one carrier lowers prices and improves service.</li>\n<li><b>Data protection and privacy laws</b>, cybercrime laws and electronic transactions laws that make mobile payments legal and safe.</li>\n<li><b>Universal service</b> policies that push coverage into poorer or remote communities.</li>\n<li><b>National digital strategies</b> and e-government plans.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Regulatory bodies</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Regulator</th><th>Territory</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bureau Telecommunications and Post (BTP)</b></td><td>Sint Maarten: licensing, spectrum and number management, consumer protection.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ECTEL</b> (Eastern Caribbean Telecommunications Authority)</td><td>Advises and coordinates regulation for member states Dominica, Grenada, St. Kitts and Nevis, St. Lucia, and St. Vincent and the Grenadines, each of which has a national regulatory commission.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>OUR</b> (Office of Utilities Regulation)</td><td>Jamaica: regulates telecoms alongside electricity and water.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>TATT</b> (Telecommunications Authority of Trinidad and Tobago)</td><td>Trinidad and Tobago.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Regulators decide who can operate, how spectrum is shared (for example for 5G), how numbers can be moved between carriers, and how consumer complaints are handled.</p>\n<p><b>Developer communities</b> are the people who build the content: freelancers, agencies like Island Pulse Media, university and college students, startup hubs, hackathons and online groups. They need skills, affordable computers, reliable Internet and access to app store accounts and payment systems. Events such as regional hackathons and coding clubs help grow this community.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Name one real regulator and say <b>what it does</b>. \"TATT regulates telecoms in Trinidad and Tobago\" is stronger when you add \"for example by allocating spectrum to operators\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The enabling environment is the set of rules, organisations and people around the network that make mobile services possible. Governments write laws on telecoms, privacy and online payments. Regulators such as the BTP in Sint Maarten or TATT in Trinidad and Tobago license carriers and protect customers. Developer communities, from college students to hackathon teams, build the apps.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Policy can open or close whole markets. Competition policy that allows a second carrier usually lowers prices and pushes coverage further, which grows the audience for every app. An electronic transactions law decides whether a phone payment is legally binding; without one, mobile banking stays risky. Data protection laws change how developers design sign-up screens and store data. Note the structure in the Eastern Caribbean: ECTEL advises and coordinates, while each member state has its own national regulatory commission. In an answer, name the body and its action: \"TATT manages spectrum, so it controls when 5G can launch in Trinidad and Tobago.\"</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Who regulates?","q":"A mobile customer in Philipsburg has complained for months that her carrier keeps charging her for a data bundle she cancelled. The carrier has not resolved it. Which organisation is MOST appropriate for her to contact next?","options":[{"t":"Bureau Telecommunications and Post (BTP), Sint Maarten","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. BTP is Sint Maarten's telecoms regulator, and consumer protection in the telecoms sector is part of a regulator's role."},{"t":"TATT","fb":"TATT regulates telecoms in Trinidad and Tobago, not in Sint Maarten."},{"t":"Google Play customer support","fb":"Google Play handles app purchases, not carrier data bundles. The dispute is with the network operator."},{"t":"A local app developer community","fb":"Developer communities build content. They have no authority over carrier billing."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Which player?","p":"<p>The problem is with a <b>carrier</b>. Who has authority over carriers?</p>"},{"h":"Which territory?","p":"<p>Regulators work within a country or group of countries. Where does she live?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Who regulates? (Foundation)","q":"Which organisation regulates telecoms in Sint Maarten?","options":[{"t":"Bureau Telecommunications and Post (BTP)","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. BTP is the Sint Maarten regulator."},{"t":"Apple","fb":"Apple makes devices and runs an app store; it is not a regulator."},{"t":"OUR","fb":"OUR is Jamaica's regulator."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Who regulates? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A startup in St. George's, Grenada, plans a mobile money service that sends payments by SMS short codes. The founder asks where to go about the telecoms side of the plan, such as short codes and number ranges. Which advice is MOST accurate?","options":[{"t":"Apply to ECTEL alone, because ECTEL member states do not have national regulators.","fb":"ECTEL advises and coordinates, but each member state, including Grenada, has its own national regulatory commission."},{"t":"Approach the OUR, because it regulates telecoms together with other utilities.","fb":"The OUR does regulate telecoms with other utilities, but in Jamaica, not Grenada."},{"t":"Apply to TATT, because it regulates telecoms for the whole Eastern Caribbean.","fb":"TATT regulates telecoms in Trinidad and Tobago only. Grenada is not under its authority."},{"t":"Approach Grenada's national telecommunications regulatory commission, knowing that ECTEL coordinates regulation across its member states.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Numbering and short codes are managed nationally, and ECTEL's coordination means the rules are similar across member states, which helps if the startup expands."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Interdependence: how each player affects the others","html":"<p>The heart of Specific Objective 1 is <b>relationships</b>. No player in the ecosystem works alone. A change in one part ripples through the rest.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>If this happens ...</th><th>... these players are affected</th></tr>\n<tr><td>A carrier upgrades from 3G to 4G LTE</td><td>Developers can add video and live maps; users stream more; device makers sell more 4G phones; content creators earn more views.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A regulator approves number portability</td><td>Users can switch carriers and keep their number; carriers must compete on price and quality.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A popular app such as TikTok grows fast</td><td>Data demand rises; carriers must invest in capacity; regulators may look at content and privacy issues.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A submarine cable is cut</td><td>Carriers lose international capacity; apps hosted overseas stop working; banks and airports are disrupted; users lose trust.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A data protection law is passed</td><td>Developers must change how they collect data; users gain rights; businesses update their apps.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Value chain view</b>: think of the ecosystem as a chain. Developers create <b>content</b> → app stores <b>distribute</b> it → device makers and operating systems <b>run</b> it → carriers and infrastructure <b>deliver</b> it → users <b>consume</b> it and pay → money flows back to developers, stores and carriers. Regulators and policy surround the whole chain.</p>\n<p><b>A Sint Maarten example</b>: during Hurricane Irma in September 2017, damage to power (GEBE) and towers meant that apps, mobile banking and messaging failed together. Users could not contact family; businesses could not take payments. Recovery depended on carriers, the power company, government and regulators working together. This shows the ecosystem is only as strong as its weakest link.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Use the phrase \"<b>which in turn</b>\" to show a chain of effects: \"Better 4G coverage lets developers offer video, which in turn increases data demand, which in turn pushes carriers to invest.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Every player in the mobile ecosystem depends on the others, so a change in one part affects the rest. When a carrier upgrades to 4G, developers can add video and users stream more. When an undersea cable is cut, apps, banking and messages all slow down at once. Think of it as a chain: if one link breaks, the whole chain fails, as it did in Sint Maarten after Hurricane Irma.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Interdependence runs both ways and often in loops. Better coverage lets developers add video, which raises data demand, which in turn pushes carriers to invest, which lets developers add even richer content. Loops can also be negative: a slow network leads to bad reviews, fewer downloads and less income to improve the app. Grade One answers also notice the <b>money</b> flowing back along the value chain, from users to stores, developers and carriers. A common mistake is to treat the regulator as just another link; it surrounds the whole chain and sets the rules each link follows. Use \"which in turn\" to show at least two steps of cause and effect.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Follow the voice note","intro":"<p>Zara, creative director at Island Pulse Media, sends a WhatsApp voice note from Bridgetown to a client in Kingston, Jamaica. Put the journey through the ecosystem in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Zara records the voice note in the WhatsApp app (content) on her iPhone (device)."},{"t":"The phone compresses the audio and sends it by radio to the nearest carrier tower."},{"t":"The carrier routes the data through its core network to its international gateway."},{"t":"The data crosses a submarine cable to reach the wider Internet."},{"t":"WhatsApp's servers (cloud infrastructure) receive and forward the message."},{"t":"A Jamaican carrier delivers the data to the client's Android phone."},{"t":"The client (user) plays the voice note and replies."}],"win":"Correct. You traced content through devices, carriers, cables and servers to the user, showing how every player depends on the others.","tutor":[{"h":"Start and finish","p":"<p>It starts with the sender's device and ends with the receiver listening.</p>"},{"h":"Leaving the island","p":"<p>Before data can reach Jamaica it must leave Barbados. How?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Follow the voice note (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Zara records the voice note on her phone."},{"t":"The phone sends it to the carrier tower."},{"t":"It travels by submarine cable and servers to Jamaica."},{"t":"The client plays it on their phone."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Follow the voice note (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Trace the same voice note from Bridgetown to Kingston in more detail, including encryption, the carrier's core network and the delivery receipt. Each step depends on the one before it.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Zara records the voice note in WhatsApp on her iPhone, and the app compresses the audio into a small file."},{"t":"WhatsApp encrypts the file on the phone before it leaves the device."},{"t":"The phone transmits the data by radio over the carrier's 4G network to the nearest tower."},{"t":"The tower passes the data over backhaul links into the carrier's core network."},{"t":"The carrier's international gateway hands the traffic to a submarine cable."},{"t":"WhatsApp's cloud servers receive the encrypted message and forward it towards the client."},{"t":"A Jamaican carrier's network and towers deliver the data to the client's Android phone."},{"t":"The client's app decrypts and plays the note, and a delivery receipt travels back through the servers to Zara."}],"win":"Excellent. You traced the voice note through every layer and showed where encryption, backhaul, the international gateway and the cloud servers fit."}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"The cable cut","intro":"<p>You are advising the Island Connect Nassau app, used by visitors to find restaurants, tours and taxis. One morning, a fault on an international link slows Internet across the island. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Users complain the app is not loading. The app's data is hosted on servers in the United States. What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Rewrite the app immediately; it must be badly coded.","to":"bad1","fb":"The app worked yesterday. Blaming the content ignores the infrastructure the app depends on."},{"t":"Find out which part of the ecosystem failed by checking with the carriers and the hosting provider.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. You identify the weak link before reacting, because the cause could be the network, the server or the app itself."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The carriers confirm reduced international capacity. Local traffic still works. How can the app keep serving visitors?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Tell visitors to wait until the cable is fixed.","to":"bad2","fb":"Visitors cannot wait; restaurants and taxis lose business. Resilience is part of good design."},{"t":"Add an offline mode with cached listings and keep a copy of key data on a local or regional server.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Caching and local hosting reduce dependence on a single international link."},{"t":"Switch to sending HD promotional videos instead.","to":"bad2","fb":"Heavy content makes things worse on a congested link."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>After the outage, the government asks how to reduce the risk in future. What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Support more cable routes and backup links, local data hosting, and clear rules from the regulator on outage reporting.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You involved infrastructure, policy and the regulator: the whole enabling environment."},{"t":"Nothing. Outages are just bad luck.","to":"bad3","fb":"Policy and investment can reduce risk. Ignoring the enabling environment leaves the island exposed."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Resilient ecosystem.</b> The app keeps working through future disruptions, and you can explain why: content, infrastructure, carriers, policy and users are interdependent, so resilience must be built into every layer.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wasted effort.</b> Weeks of recoding change nothing because the fault was in the network. Try again and diagnose the ecosystem first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Lost visitors.</b> Tourists give up and use other sources; local businesses lose sales. Try again and design for weak connectivity.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Repeated failures.</b> The next outage hits just as hard. Try again and think about the enabling environment.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Find the weak link","p":"<p>Which ecosystem component actually failed?</p>"},{"h":"Design for weak networks","p":"<p>What can an app do when the Internet is slow?</p>"},{"h":"Think bigger","p":"<p>Who can make the whole island less vulnerable?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The cable cut (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: the island's international Internet is slow and the tourism app will not load.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>What is the best improvement to the app?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add an offline mode with saved listings.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Offline content keeps the app useful when the network is weak."},{"t":"Add more large videos.","to":"bad","fb":"Heavy content makes slow networks worse."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You designed for the weak link in the ecosystem.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The app still fails. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"The cable cut (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher version. It is a busy cruise day and Island Connect Nassau, the app visitors use to find restaurants, tours and taxis, has stopped loading. Several choices sound reasonable; pick the one a Grade One analyst would defend. Some choices end the scenario early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Mid-morning, with four ships in port, the app stops loading for most visitors. Taxi drivers say WhatsApp is slow too. The app and its maps are hosted in the United States. What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Roll back last night's app update, since new code is the usual cause of failures.","to":"ok1","fb":"Checking recent changes is sensible, but other apps failing too points to the network. You lose valuable time."},{"t":"Post on Instagram that the app is broken and will be completely rebuilt.","to":"bad1","fb":"This damages trust and commits you to work that may not fix anything."},{"t":"Check status reports from the carriers and the hosting provider, and compare local with international traffic, before changing anything.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. WhatsApp slowing too suggests a problem beyond your app, so you diagnose the ecosystem before acting."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The carriers confirm a fault on an international link: capacity is down by about two-thirds, but traffic within the island is normal. Repairs could take several days. How do you keep the app useful today?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Switch to a 'lite' mode: serve cached listings, text-first pages and compressed images, and pause background syncing.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. You reduce how much data must cross the damaged link while keeping the core service working."},{"t":"Upload higher-quality photos so the app looks more professional while users wait.","to":"bad2","fb":"Heavier content makes a congested link even slower."},{"t":"Advise visitors to use roaming on their home SIMs instead of local networks.","to":"ok2","fb":"Roaming traffic still crosses the same congested international links, so this barely helps."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Taxi bookings are still failing because every request travels to the US server and back, even when the visitor and the driver are both in Nassau. How do you protect bookings?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Move the booking service to a server hosted locally or regionally, so island traffic no longer depends on the damaged link.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Local hosting keeps local transactions local, which is the strongest resilience fix."},{"t":"Keep everything in the US and tell drivers to phone customers instead.","to":"ok3","fb":"Phone calls work, but bookings are lost from the app and the same failure will happen next time."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The government asks you to present lessons from the outage to the regulator and the carriers. Which recommendation is the strongest?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Each app developer should buy its own satellite connection.","to":"ok4","fb":"It would help one app, but it is costly and does nothing for banks, the airport or other users."},{"t":"Route diversity (more than one cable plus a satellite backup), local or regional hosting for essential services, and rules requiring carriers to report outages and recovery times.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You involved infrastructure, hosting and the regulator: every layer of the ecosystem."},{"t":"Ban apps that are hosted overseas.","to":"bad3","fb":"This would remove most of the apps people rely on and is not a realistic policy."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Resilient ecosystem.</b> Bookings continue, visitors keep using the app, and your recommendations reduce the risk for the whole island. You showed that resilience must be built into infrastructure, hosting, content design and policy together.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Time lost.</b> The rollback changes nothing because the fault is in the network. The app recovers only when the carriers do. Try again and diagnose the ecosystem first.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Little improvement.</b> A few users notice no difference and roaming bills rise. Try again and reduce the data the app needs instead.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Partly working.</b> Visitors still get rides, but the app loses bookings and income, and nothing changes for next time. Try again and think about where the data is hosted.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>A narrow fix.</b> Your app survives the next outage, but the rest of the island does not. Try again and think about the whole ecosystem.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wasted effort.</b> Weeks of rebuilding change nothing because the app was never the problem, and users lose confidence. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Lost visitors.</b> Pages now take even longer to load and visitors give up. Try again and design for weak connectivity.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Unworkable policy.</b> Banning overseas hosting would cut residents off from most of the services they use. Try again with a recommendation that strengthens the ecosystem.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"The Caribbean mobile ecosystem: challenges and opportunities","html":"<p>The Caribbean ecosystem shares the same components as the global one, but its <b>scale</b>, <b>geography</b> and <b>economy</b> create particular challenges and opportunities.</p>\n<p><b>Connectivity challenges</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Small markets</b>: islands with tens of thousands of people (Sint Maarten, St. Kitts and Nevis, Dominica) make it expensive for carriers to recover the cost of new networks.</li>\n<li><b>Dependence on submarine cables</b>: a few cables carry most international traffic, so a single cut can slow an entire island.</li>\n<li><b>Natural hazards</b>: hurricanes (Irma 2017, Maria 2017, Beryl 2024) damage towers and power; earthquakes and volcanic activity affect some islands.</li>\n<li><b>Cost of data and devices</b>: prices are often higher than in large markets, and imported phones carry duties.</li>\n<li><b>Rural and interior gaps</b>: parts of Guyana, Belize and Jamaica's hills have weaker coverage than capital cities.</li>\n<li><b>Content hosted abroad</b>: most apps and data sit on overseas servers, adding latency and outflow of money.</li>\n<li><b>Skills and funding</b>: fewer local developers, small venture funding, and difficulty accessing some app store payout systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Local opportunities</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>High mobile adoption</b>: most adults already own phones, so mobile is the fastest route to reach people.</li>\n<li><b>Local content gaps</b>: bus routes, ferry times between islands, local events, Caribbean stories and Papiamentu or Kréyòl content are under-served.</li>\n<li><b>Tourism</b>: millions of visitors a year arrive at Caribbean airports and cruise ports wanting guides, bookings and payments.</li>\n<li><b>Mobile money and fintech</b>: serving people with limited access to banks.</li>\n<li><b>Diaspora markets</b>: Caribbean people abroad want news, music, food and remittance apps.</li>\n<li><b>Resilience apps</b>: hurricane preparedness, offline alerts and community check-ins.</li>\n<li><b>Regional cooperation</b>: bodies such as ECTEL and CARICOM's ICT work let small states act together.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For \"evaluate\", weigh both sides and reach a judgement: \"Although small market size limits investment, high phone ownership and tourism demand create strong opportunities for local developers.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The Caribbean has the same mobile ecosystem parts as the rest of the world, but small populations, hurricanes and a few undersea cables make things harder and more expensive. At the same time, almost every adult has a phone, millions of tourists arrive each year and many local needs, like ferry times or Kréyòl content, have no good app yet. Those gaps are chances for local developers.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Challenges and opportunities are often two sides of the same fact. Small markets put off big investors, but they also mean a local developer can reach a large share of the population quickly. Hurricane risk damages towers, yet it creates demand for offline alert apps that work when networks fail. Content hosted abroad adds delay and sends money overseas; regional hosting would keep both data and income closer to home. For \"evaluate\" questions, weigh at least one challenge against one opportunity and reach a judgement, supported by a named territory or event, such as the damage Hurricane Beryl caused on Carriacou in 2024.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the challenges","intro":"<p>Read Keisha's notes from a meeting with a Grenadian startup that wants to launch a farm-produce app.</p>","q":"Select every line that describes a CONNECTIVITY or INFRASTRUCTURE CHALLENGE in the Caribbean ecosystem.","lines":["Coverage is weak in the hilly farming areas of St. Andrew.","Farmers already use WhatsApp groups to sell produce.","After Hurricane Beryl, towers on Carriacou were damaged and service was lost for days.","The app will be hosted on a server in the United States, adding delay.","Many young people in Grenada are keen to learn app design.","Mobile data is expensive compared with larger markets.","Visitors to the island want to buy local spices and nutmeg.","Most international traffic depends on a small number of submarine cables."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7],"win":"Well done. You separated connectivity challenges (coverage gaps, hazard damage, overseas hosting, data cost, cable dependence) from opportunities.","miss":"Look again. WhatsApp use, keen young developers and visitor demand are opportunities, not challenges. Check every line about coverage, cost, hosting or cables.","tutor":[{"h":"Challenge or opportunity?","p":"<p>Does the line describe something that <b>blocks</b> connectivity, or something the startup could <b>use</b>?</p>"},{"h":"Infrastructure clues","p":"<p>Look for towers, cables, servers, coverage and price of data.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the challenges (Foundation)","q":"Select the lines that describe connectivity challenges.","lines":["Coverage is weak in rural hills.","Farmers already use WhatsApp.","A hurricane damaged cell towers.","Young people want to learn app design."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Weak coverage and storm damage are connectivity challenges.","miss":"WhatsApp use and keen learners are opportunities. Try again."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the challenges (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Keisha's full notes from the Grenadian farm-produce startup now cover funding, devices and markets as well as connectivity.</p>","q":"Select every line that describes a CHALLENGE in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem (connectivity, cost, hazards, hosting, skills or funding). Leave every opportunity unselected.","lines":["Most farmers already own a smartphone, even in rural parishes.","Venture funding for app startups in the region is small and hard to obtain.","Imported phones carry duties, so mid-range devices cost more than in the United States.","Grenadians in New York and Toronto want to buy local spices online.","Some app store payout systems are difficult for developers in small territories to set up.","The app's data is stored on servers in Virginia, so every request crosses the sea.","ECTEL lets small member states coordinate their telecoms regulation.","With a population of just over 100,000, the market is small, so new network investment is slow to pay back.","Hurricane preparedness apps are in demand and under-served."],"correct":[1,2,4,5,7],"win":"Excellent. You identified funding, device cost, payout access, overseas hosting and small market size as challenges, and left phone ownership, diaspora demand, regional cooperation and resilience apps as opportunities.","miss":"Look again. High phone ownership, diaspora demand, regional cooperation through ECTEL and the demand for hurricane apps are opportunities. Check every line about money, cost, hosting or market size."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Discuss relationships in the mobile ecosystem","genre":"Structured response: discussing interdependence in the mobile ecosystem","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Roti Go app lets residents and visitors in Trinidad and Tobago order local food (doubles, roti, pholourie) from small vendors and pay by phone. It is built by a local developer, runs on Android and iOS, and is used over bmobile and Digicel networks. Trinidad and Tobago's telecoms sector is regulated by the Telecommunications Authority of Trinidad and Tobago (TATT).</p>","prompt":"<p>With reference to Roti Go:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Identify FOUR components of the mobile ecosystem and give an example of each from the app. (4 marks)</li><li>Explain the role of carriers in making the app work. (3 marks)</li><li><b>Discuss</b> how the developer, users and the enabling environment (policy and regulation) depend on one another. (5 marks)</li></ol>","min":220,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Content: ... Infrastructure: ... Devices: ... Users: ...<br><b>(b)</b> Carriers such as ... provide ... which allows ... Without them ...<br><b>(c)</b> The developer depends on users because ... Users depend on the developer because ... Both depend on the enabling environment because ... For example, TATT ... which in turn ...</p>","checklist":["I named all four components with an app example for each.","I named real carriers and explained what they do, not just who they are.","I showed at least two two-way relationships.","I referred to policy or a regulator and explained its effect.","I used linking phrases such as \"because\", \"therefore\" and \"which in turn\"."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Components identified and exemplified","marks":4,"look":["content|menu|app","infrastructure|tower|server|cable|cloud","device|phone|android|ios|iphone","user|customer|vendor|resident|visitor"],"min":3,"strong":"You identified the components with clear examples from the app.","weak":"Name all four components (content, infrastructure, devices, users) and give an example from Roti Go for each."},{"criterion":"Role of carriers","marks":3,"look":["bmobile|digicel|carrier|operator|telco","data|connect|network|signal","deliver|transmit|route|coverage"],"min":2,"strong":"Clear explanation of how carriers deliver data and connectivity.","weak":"Explain what carriers actually do: run towers and networks, deliver mobile data, and link the island to the wider Internet."},{"criterion":"Discussion of interdependence","marks":5,"look":["depend|rel(y|ies)|interdepend","because|therefore|so that|which in turn","polic|law|regulat|tatt","privacy|data protection|licen|consumer","payment|bank|secur|trust"],"min":3,"strong":"You discussed two-way relationships and the enabling environment with clear reasons.","weak":"Show how each player affects the others and why. Bring in policy and the regulator: for example, data protection rules affect how the developer stores payment data, which affects user trust."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Content</b> is the app itself: the menus, photos of johnny cakes and the ordering screens. <b>Infrastructure</b> includes the cell towers, the cloud server that stores orders and the submarine cables that link the island to the Internet. <b>Devices</b> are the Android and iOS smartphones that customers and vendors use. <b>Users</b> are the residents and visitors who order food and the vendors who accept orders.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Carriers such as bmobile and Digicel run the mobile networks that deliver data between users' phones and the app's server. They provide coverage across the island, route traffic to the wider Internet through international links and sell the data plans users need. Without carriers, orders could not be sent or confirmed, so the app would be useless away from Wi-Fi.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The developer depends on users because their downloads, orders and reviews provide the income and feedback needed to improve the app. Users depend on the developer to keep the app fast, light on data and secure, especially since many are on prepaid plans. Both depend on the enabling environment. Electronic transactions and data protection laws make phone payments legal and set rules for storing customers' personal and payment data, which in turn builds the trust users need before they pay. TATT licenses carriers and protects consumers, so it influences network quality and prices, which affect how cheaply users can access the app. Therefore, a weakness in any one player, such as poor coverage or weak privacy rules, would reduce use of Roti Go, showing that the ecosystem only works when all its parts work together.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>\"Discuss\" needs reasons and connections, not a list. For every relationship, say <b>who depends on whom</b>, <b>for what</b>, and <b>what happens if it fails</b>. One well-explained two-way relationship is worth more than five names.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Discuss relationships in the mobile ecosystem (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: the mobile ecosystem, Foundation (8 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part in one or two sentences. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> the FOUR components of the mobile ecosystem and give ONE example of each from Roti Go. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE carrier that Roti Go uses and <b>state</b> ONE thing it does for the app. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Complete</b> the sentences: \"The developer needs users because ...\" and \"Users need the developer because ...\" <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[8 marks]</b></p>","min":60,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Content: ... Infrastructure: ... Devices: ... Users: ...<br><b>(b)</b> One carrier is ... It ...<br><b>(c)</b> The developer needs users because ... Users need the developer because ...</p>","checklist":["I named content, infrastructure, devices and users, with one Roti Go example for each.","I named bmobile or Digicel and said one thing it does, such as carrying the app's data.","I gave a reason for each 'needs' sentence."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Four components with examples","marks":4,"look":["content|menu|app|photo","infrastructure|tower|server|cable|cloud","device|phone|android|ios|iphone","user|customer|vendor|resident|visitor"],"min":3,"strong":"You named all four components with an example from the app.","weak":"Write all four: content (the menus), infrastructure (towers and servers), devices (phones) and users (customers and vendors)."},{"criterion":"A carrier and its role","marks":2,"look":["bmobile|digicel|carrier","data|network|signal|connect|deliver|tower"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a carrier and said what it does.","weak":"Name bmobile or Digicel and say that it carries the app's data over its mobile network."},{"criterion":"Two-way dependence","marks":2,"look":["because","order|download|money|income|review|pay|update|fix|secure|fast|work"],"min":2,"strong":"You gave a reason for both directions of the relationship.","weak":"Finish both sentences with a reason, e.g. users bring orders and income; the developer keeps the app working."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Content: the food menus and photos in the app. Infrastructure: the cell towers and the cloud server that stores orders. Devices: the Android and iOS phones that customers use. Users: the residents and visitors who order food, and the vendors.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One carrier is Digicel. It carries the app's data over its mobile network, so orders reach the vendor even away from Wi-Fi.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The developer needs users because their orders and downloads bring in money and feedback. Users need the developer because the developer keeps the app working, fast and safe for payments.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (8 marks): (a) 1 mark per component with a fitting example; (b) 1 for a named carrier, 1 for its role; (c) 1 for each sentence with a reason. Ask students to underline the Roti Go detail they used in each answer. Move them on to the Core task once they can write (c) as a full two-way relationship without the starters.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Discuss relationships in the mobile ecosystem (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: interdependence in the mobile ecosystem, Grade One extension (20 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Roti Go lets residents and visitors in Trinidad and Tobago order local food (doubles, roti, pholourie) from small vendors and pay by phone. It is built by a local developer, runs on Android and iOS, and is used over bmobile and Digicel networks; Trinidad and Tobago's telecoms sector is regulated by the Telecommunications Authority of Trinidad and Tobago (TATT). The developer now plans to: <b>expand to St. Kitts and Nevis and St. Lucia</b>; add <b>live video</b> of vendors cooking; and accept <b>mobile money</b> payments. Last hurricane season, a storm cut mobile service in parts of the island for two days and the app's orders stopped completely. The app's servers are in the United States.</p>","prompt":"<p>With reference to Roti Go:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Explain</b> how carriers and infrastructure work together to deliver the planned live video, referring to the Caribbean context. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Analyse</b> TWO two-way relationships among the developer, users and carriers, showing a chain of effects for each. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Discuss</b> how the enabling environment (regulators, policy and data protection) would affect the expansion to St. Kitts and Nevis and St. Lucia and the move to mobile money. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> which part of the ecosystem is the weakest link for Roti Go, and <b>recommend</b> ONE action to reduce the risk. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","min":380,"max":650,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Live video needs ... Carriers such as ... provide ... while infrastructure such as ... In the Caribbean, ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, the developer and users ... which in turn ... Second, carriers and users ... which in turn ...<br><b>(c)</b> Expanding to St. Kitts and Nevis and St. Lucia means ... ECTEL ... Mobile money requires ... Data protection laws ...<br><b>(d)</b> The weakest link is ... because ... Although ..., ... I recommend ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I explained how carriers (towers, data plans, international links) and infrastructure (servers, cables) combine to carry live video, with a Caribbean detail such as prepaid data or cable dependence.","I analysed two relationships that run BOTH ways, each with a 'which in turn' chain of at least two effects.","I named the regulators involved, mentioned ECTEL's coordinating role and discussed data protection and payment rules.","I chose one weakest link, weighed it against at least one other candidate and made a clear recommendation.","Every part uses details from the stimulus: the expansion, live video, mobile money, the storm and the US servers."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Carriers and infrastructure for live video","marks":4,"look":["bmobile|digicel|carrier|operator","bandwidth|capacity|4g|5g|data","server|cable|cloud|cdn|data cent","prepaid|cost|submarine|island|latency|delay|caribbean"],"min":3,"strong":"You explained how carriers and infrastructure combine to deliver video, with a Caribbean constraint.","weak":"Explain that carriers provide the radio network, data plans and international capacity, that servers and submarine cables carry the stream, and that prepaid data costs or cable dependence limit live video in the Caribbean."},{"criterion":"Two two-way relationships","marks":6,"look":["depend|rel(y|ies)|interdepend","which in turn|therefore|as a result|leads to","user|customer|vendor","carrier|bmobile|digicel|network","developer","review|download|income|demand|invest|bundle|data"],"min":4,"strong":"Both relationships run in two directions, with clear chains of effects.","weak":"For each relationship, show who depends on whom, for what, and what follows, e.g. live video raises data demand, which in turn pushes carriers to add capacity or sell food-app bundles."},{"criterion":"Enabling environment and expansion","marks":5,"look":["ectel","regulat|commission|btp|licen","data protection|privacy|personal data","mobile money|payment|financial|bank","law|polic|rule|consent"],"min":3,"strong":"You discussed regulators, ECTEL, data protection and payment rules and linked them to the expansion.","weak":"Explain that St. Kitts and Nevis and St. Lucia each have a national regulator, coordinated by ECTEL; that each territory's data protection law applies; and that mobile money brings financial rules and the need for user trust."},{"criterion":"Evaluation and recommendation","marks":5,"look":["weak(est)? link|most vulnerable|biggest risk","because","however|although|on the other hand|whereas","recommend|should","offline|cache|local|regional|backup|sms|redundan"],"min":4,"strong":"Your judgement is weighed against an alternative and leads to a practical recommendation.","weak":"Pick one weak link (e.g. infrastructure during storms, or the US servers), compare it with another, reach a judgement and recommend an action such as offline ordering or regional hosting."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Live video is the most data-hungry content Roti Go could add. Carriers such as bmobile and Digicel provide the radio network, the 4G (and in future 5G) capacity and the data plans users need, while the infrastructure behind them, including the app's cloud servers in the United States and the submarine cables linking the island to them, carries the stream. In the Caribbean, many users are on prepaid plans and international traffic depends on a few cables, so a constant live stream would be costly for users and fragile during outages. The developer would need low-resolution streams, or short pre-recorded clips, to make the feature work.</p><p><b>(b)</b> First, the developer and users depend on each other. Users rely on the developer for a fast, low-data and secure app; if live video makes the app slow, they leave poor reviews and stop ordering, which in turn cuts the developer's income and the money available to improve the app. In the other direction, users' orders and feedback guide what the developer builds next. Second, carriers and users are interdependent. Users depend on carriers for coverage and affordable data, while carriers depend on popular apps to drive demand for data. If live video becomes popular, data use rises, which in turn pushes carriers to invest in capacity or create a food-app bundle, which then makes the app cheaper to use and attracts more users.</p><p><b>(c)</b> St. Kitts and Nevis and St. Lucia are both ECTEL member states. ECTEL coordinates and advises on telecoms regulation across its members, so the rules are broadly similar, but each territory has its own national regulatory commission, so the developer must still meet local requirements. Each territory's data protection law will decide how customers' names, locations and payment details may be collected and stored, so the app may need clearer consent screens. Mobile money adds financial rules and security standards; meeting them builds the trust users need before they pay by phone. A strong policy environment therefore makes expansion slower to start but safer and more sustainable.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The weakest link is the infrastructure, in particular its dependence on a working network and on servers far away in the United States. The storm showed that when towers or links fail, orders stop completely, even though vendors and customers are on the same island. Although the developer's own code could also fail, that risk is within the developer's control, whereas network damage is not. I recommend an offline ordering mode that saves orders on the phone and sends them by SMS or as soon as any connection returns, combined with a regional or local server for order processing. This keeps local food orders flowing during outages and reduces delay every day.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (20 marks): (a) 4 = network role, infrastructure role, Caribbean constraint, implication for the feature. (b) 3 per relationship: both directions plus a chain of at least two effects. (c) 5 = ECTEL's role, national regulators, data protection, payment rules, an overall judgement. (d) 5 = a chosen link, evidence from the stimulus, comparison with an alternative, a recommendation, and a justification. Credit any well-argued weakest link (for example the US servers or prepaid data costs) if the evidence supports it.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M3U2","title":"Mobile operating systems, platforms and devices","objectives":["Distinguish among mobile operating systems: Android (open source, Google ecosystem, fragmentation), iOS (closed ecosystem, security, design philosophy) and HarmonyOS.","Compare Android and iOS development environments, app stores and revenue models.","Describe smart devices and wearables: smartphones, tablets, smartwatches and smart speakers.","Explain the Internet of Things (IoT), sensors and embedded systems, with Caribbean examples.","Explain cross-platform development tools such as Flutter, React Native and .NET MAUI.","Justify the choice of platform for an app using target audience, budget and time-to-market."],"videos":[{"title":"Android vs iOS for developers","q":"android vs ios app development differences explained"},{"title":"What is the Internet of Things?","q":"what is the internet of things IoT explained sensors embedded systems"},{"title":"Native vs cross-platform apps","q":"native vs cross platform app development flutter react native explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Operating systems and platforms: the basics","html":"<p>A <b>mobile operating system (OS)</b> is the core software that manages a mobile device's hardware (screen, battery, camera, GPS, sensors) and provides the services that apps run on. It handles the touch interface, notifications, security, permissions, storage and connection to networks.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Platform:</b> the complete environment an app is built for and distributed through. It includes the operating system, the development tools (SDKs and IDEs), the app store and the rules the owner sets. Android and iOS are both operating systems <b>and</b> platforms.</p>\n<p>There are three operating systems you must know: <b>Android</b>, <b>iOS</b> and <b>HarmonyOS</b>. You also need to know <b>platforms</b> such as app stores and development platforms, <b>devices</b> such as smart and wearable devices, and <b>related technologies</b> such as the Internet of Things and cross-platform development tools.</p>\n<p><b>Market share</b>: worldwide, Android runs on roughly seven in ten smartphones and iOS on most of the rest, according to web-traffic trackers such as StatCounter. In the Caribbean, iOS tends to have a <b>larger share</b> than the world average because of strong links with the United States: StatCounter data for Jamaica in 2026 shows roughly 60 percent Android and 40 percent iOS. Shares vary by island and change month to month, so always check the latest StatCounter or GSMA figure and quote it with its year.</p>\n<p>Older platforms you may see in past papers include <b>BlackBerry OS</b> (once very popular in the Caribbean for BBM messaging, now discontinued), <b>Windows Phone</b> (discontinued) and <b>KaiOS</b> (for low-cost feature phones).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The command word is <b>distinguish</b>. For each OS, compare on the <b>same feature</b>: \"Android is open source, whereas iOS is closed and controlled by Apple.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An operating system (OS) is the main software on a phone: it runs the screen, battery and camera, and lets apps work. A platform is bigger: the OS plus the tools for building apps, the app store and the owner's rules. Most phones run Android, iPhones run iOS and Huawei's newer phones run HarmonyOS. In Jamaica, about six in ten smartphones run Android.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Market share is not the same as market value. Android leads in the number of devices worldwide, but iOS users tend to spend more per person on apps, so a paid or subscription app may earn more on iOS even with fewer users. In the Caribbean the split is closer than the world average, and it shifts between territories and from month to month, so a Grade One answer quotes a figure with its source and year (for example, \"StatCounter, 2026\") instead of saying \"everyone has an iPhone\". Keep the two terms apart: an OS is one layer of software, while a platform adds the SDKs, the store, the review rules and the payment systems that decide how an app reaches users and earns money.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Android, iOS and HarmonyOS compared","html":"<p><b>Android</b> (Google)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Open source</b>: the core is the Android Open Source Project (AOSP), based on the Linux kernel. Any manufacturer can use and customise it: Samsung, Motorola, Xiaomi, TCL, Google Pixel.</li>\n<li><b>Google ecosystem</b>: most Android phones ship with Google Mobile Services (Play Store, Gmail, Maps, YouTube).</li>\n<li><b>Fragmentation</b>: thousands of device models with different screen sizes, chips and OS versions. Many users run older versions, so developers must test widely.</li>\n<li>Wide price range, from budget phones to flagships, which suits price-sensitive markets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>iOS</b> (Apple)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Closed ecosystem</b> (a \"walled garden\"): runs only on Apple's iPhone; iPadOS on iPad. Apple controls hardware, software and the App Store.</li>\n<li><b>Security and privacy</b>: strict app review, sandboxing, App Tracking Transparency, fast updates to most devices.</li>\n<li><b>Design philosophy</b>: consistency and simplicity, guided by Apple's Human Interface Guidelines. Tight integration with Apple Watch, AirPods and Mac.</li>\n<li>Low fragmentation: few models, most users update quickly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>HarmonyOS</b> (Huawei)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Developed by Huawei after US trade restrictions limited its access to Google services from 2019.</li>\n<li>Designed for <b>many device types</b>: phones, tablets, watches, TVs and cars, sharing tasks between them (\"distributed\" design). Its newest version, HarmonyOS NEXT, no longer runs Android apps and uses its own app store, AppGallery.</li>\n<li>Strong in mainland China; small share in the Caribbean, but Huawei devices are present in the region.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Android</th><th>iOS</th><th>HarmonyOS</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Owner</td><td>Google (open source core)</td><td>Apple</td><td>Huawei</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Openness</td><td>Open, customisable</td><td>Closed</td><td>Partly open (OpenHarmony core)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Devices</td><td>Many brands</td><td>Apple only</td><td>Mainly Huawei</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main store</td><td>Google Play (plus others)</td><td>App Store</td><td>AppGallery</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main issue</td><td>Fragmentation</td><td>Restrictions, cost</td><td>Small app catalogue outside China</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Fragmentation\" is a favourite Paper 01 term. It means <b>many different devices and OS versions in use at once</b>, not a broken screen.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Android is made by Google and is open, so many brands such as Samsung and Motorola use it, on phones from cheap to expensive. iOS is Apple's system and runs only on iPhones; Apple controls everything, which keeps it consistent and secure. HarmonyOS is Huawei's system for phones, watches and TVs. Because so many different Android phones exist, developers must test their apps on many models. This is called fragmentation.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Openness is a trade-off, not simply good or bad. Android's openness gives choice and low prices, which suits price-sensitive Caribbean markets, but it also brings fragmentation and slower security updates on budget phones. The iOS walled garden gives consistent updates and strict review, but less freedom for developers and higher device prices. HarmonyOS shows that politics shapes platforms: trade restrictions pushed Huawei to build its own ecosystem, and because HarmonyOS NEXT no longer runs Android apps, developers must build for it separately. In a \"distinguish\" answer, compare the systems on the <b>same</b> feature in one sentence, then add the consequence for developers or users.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS?","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is briefing new interns. Sort each statement to the operating system it describes.</p>","main":"Which operating system does this describe?","bins":["Android","iOS","HarmonyOS"],"items":[{"t":"Built on the open-source AOSP and used by Samsung, Motorola and TCL.","bin":0,"fb":"Many manufacturers use Android's open-source core."},{"t":"Developers struggle to test on thousands of different models and OS versions.","bin":0,"fb":"That is fragmentation, the classic Android challenge."},{"t":"Designed to share tasks across phones, watches, TVs and cars, with apps from AppGallery.","bin":2,"fb":"Distributed, multi-device design and AppGallery belong to HarmonyOS."},{"t":"Apps are distributed mainly through Google Play.","bin":0,"fb":"Google Play is Android's main store, though others exist."},{"t":"Runs only on iPhone, with Apple controlling hardware and software.","bin":1,"fb":"This closed, single-maker ecosystem is iOS."},{"t":"Apps must follow the Human Interface Guidelines and pass a strict review.","bin":1,"fb":"The HIG and App Review express Apple's design and security philosophy."},{"t":"Developed by Huawei after it lost access to Google services.","bin":2,"fb":"HarmonyOS was Huawei's response to trade restrictions from 2019."},{"t":"Most devices receive updates at the same time, so fragmentation is low.","bin":1,"fb":"Apple pushes updates to most iPhones quickly."}],"win":"Well sorted. Android is open and fragmented, iOS is closed and tightly controlled, and HarmonyOS is Huawei's distributed multi-device system.","tutor":[{"h":"Who owns it?","p":"<p>Google, Apple or Huawei? The owner often gives the answer.</p>"},{"h":"Open or closed?","p":"<p>Many manufacturers suggests open. One manufacturer suggests closed.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which OS? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Used by Samsung and many other brands.","bin":0,"fb":"Android is open to many makers."},{"t":"Only on iPhone.","bin":1,"fb":"iOS is Apple only."},{"t":"Made by Huawei.","bin":2,"fb":"HarmonyOS is Huawei's OS."},{"t":"Main store is Google Play.","bin":0,"fb":"Google Play is Android's store."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Android, iOS or HarmonyOS? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Jerome's interns have mastered the basics, so his second briefing goes deeper. Each statement describes a less obvious feature. Sort it to the operating system it describes.</p>","items":[{"t":"A developer can share an APK file on a website and users can install it outside the official store.","bin":0,"fb":"Sideloading APK files is an Android feature, because the platform is open."},{"t":"Its open-source foundation project is called OpenHarmony.","bin":2,"fb":"OpenHarmony is the open-source core behind HarmonyOS."},{"t":"App Tracking Transparency makes apps ask permission before tracking users across other companies' apps.","bin":1,"fb":"App Tracking Transparency is an Apple privacy feature on iOS."},{"t":"Its newest version no longer runs Android apps, so developers must build a separate version.","bin":2,"fb":"HarmonyOS NEXT dropped Android app support and relies on its own AppGallery catalogue."},{"t":"Its core comes from AOSP and is based on the Linux kernel.","bin":0,"fb":"AOSP, the Android Open Source Project, is built on the Linux kernel."},{"t":"In most countries, apps can be installed only through the official store after a human review.","bin":1,"fb":"Apple's walled garden normally allows one store, with alternative marketplaces only in some regions."},{"t":"Samsung adds its own One UI interface on top of it.","bin":0,"fb":"Manufacturers customise Android's open core with their own interfaces."},{"t":"Apps are built in Xcode and sent to beta testers through TestFlight.","bin":1,"fb":"Xcode and TestFlight are Apple's tools for iOS."},{"t":"Budget phones from many brands often run older versions, so security updates arrive slowly.","bin":0,"fb":"This is a consequence of Android fragmentation."},{"t":"It was designed to share tasks across a user's phone, watch, TV and car.","bin":2,"fb":"Distributed, multi-device design is the defining idea of HarmonyOS."}],"win":"Excellent. You identified the less obvious features: sideloading and AOSP for Android, App Tracking Transparency and the single store for iOS, and OpenHarmony and the end of Android app support for HarmonyOS."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Development environments, app stores and revenue","html":"<p>The course outline asks you to compare Android and iOS on three points.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Android</th><th>iOS</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Official IDE</b></td><td>Android Studio (Windows, macOS, Linux)</td><td>Xcode (macOS only, so a Mac is required)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Languages</b></td><td>Kotlin (preferred), Java</td><td>Swift (preferred), Objective-C</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>UI toolkit</b></td><td>Jetpack Compose</td><td>SwiftUI</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Testing</b></td><td>Emulator; install APK directly on a phone</td><td>Simulator; TestFlight for beta testers</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Store</b></td><td>Google Play; others such as Samsung Galaxy Store; sideloading allowed</td><td>Apple App Store (alternative stores allowed only in some regions, such as the EU)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Developer account</b></td><td>One-off registration fee (about US$25)</td><td>Annual fee (about US$99)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Review</b></td><td>Faster, more automated</td><td>Stricter human review; can take longer</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Revenue pattern</b></td><td>More users worldwide; more ad-supported and freemium apps</td><td>Fewer users but users tend to spend more per person on apps</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Revenue models</b> (studied in depth in a later unit): free with ads, freemium with <b>in-app purchases</b>, paid download, <b>subscriptions</b>. Both stores take a <b>commission</b> on digital sales, usually 15 to 30 percent depending on the programme. Physical goods and services (for example a taxi ride in Montego Bay or a meal) can usually be paid by card outside the store's system.</p>\n<p><b>Beginner and low-code tools</b>: for your SBA prototype you may use <b>MIT App Inventor</b> (block-based, Android), <b>Thunkable</b> (blocks, Android and iOS) or a <b>Figma</b> prototype. These lower the cost of entry for Caribbean students who do not own a Mac.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A common Paper 01 trap: \"Which tool is required to publish a native iOS app?\" The answer is Xcode on a Mac, not Android Studio. Remember the fee difference too: Google Play is a one-off fee, Apple is yearly.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To build an Android app, developers use Android Studio, usually with the Kotlin language. For iPhone apps they use Xcode with Swift, and Xcode only works on a Mac. Google Play charges a one-off fee of about US$25; Apple charges about US$99 every year. Both stores take a share of digital sales. A student in Castries without a Mac can still start with a free block-based tool such as MIT App Inventor.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Platform costs shape who can compete. The Mac requirement and the yearly Apple fee are real barriers for small Caribbean developers, which is one reason many start on Android or use cross-platform tools with a cloud build service. Store commission rules also matter: digital goods such as extra game levels usually go through the store's billing, but physical goods and services, such as a ferry ticket or a meal delivered in Kingston, can usually be paid by card outside it. A common misconception is that the store with more users always earns more. iOS users tend to spend more per person, so the revenue model should guide the platform choice as much as the number of users.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the platform tool","intro":"<p>Maya, the web developer at Island Pulse Media, is moving into mobile. Match each tool or term to its description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Android Studio","r":"Official IDE for building Android apps, runs on Windows, macOS and Linux"},{"l":"Xcode","r":"Apple's IDE, needed on a Mac to build and submit iOS apps"},{"l":"Kotlin","r":"Google's preferred language for Android apps"},{"l":"Swift","r":"Apple's preferred language for iOS apps"},{"l":"TestFlight","r":"Apple service for sending beta versions to testers"},{"l":"Sideloading","r":"Installing an app from outside the official store"},{"l":"MIT App Inventor","r":"Free, block-based tool for building simple Android apps"}],"win":"Very good. You can name the tools, languages and testing options for each platform, which helps you justify tool choices in Paper 02.","tutor":[{"h":"Apple or Google?","p":"<p>Xcode, Swift and TestFlight belong together. So do Android Studio and Kotlin.</p>"},{"h":"Beginner tools","p":"<p>Which tool uses coloured blocks instead of typed code?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the platform tool (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Android Studio","r":"Official tool for Android apps"},{"l":"Xcode","r":"Official tool for iOS apps, Mac only"},{"l":"Swift","r":"Language for iOS apps"},{"l":"Kotlin","r":"Language for Android apps"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the platform tool (Challenge)","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Maya is now choosing a full toolchain for a client. Match each tool, framework or format to its description. Several are close, so check the company and the language.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Jetpack Compose","r":"Android's modern toolkit for building user interfaces in Kotlin"},{"l":"SwiftUI","r":"Apple's framework for building iOS interfaces in Swift"},{"l":"Flutter","r":"Google's cross-platform framework that uses the Dart language"},{"l":"React Native","r":"Meta's cross-platform framework that uses JavaScript and React"},{"l":".NET MAUI","r":"Microsoft's cross-platform framework that uses C#"},{"l":"TestFlight","r":"Apple's service for sending beta builds to outside testers before release"},{"l":"Android App Bundle (AAB)","r":"The publishing format Google Play requires for new apps"},{"l":"Sideloading","r":"Installing an app from outside the official store, for example from a website"}],"win":"Excellent. You can name the interface toolkits, cross-platform frameworks, testing service and publishing format for each platform, and say who makes them."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Smart devices and wearables","html":"<p>A <b>smart device</b> is an electronic device that can connect to a network (Wi-Fi, mobile data, Bluetooth), run software and interact with users or other devices. You need to know both <b>smart devices</b> and <b>wearable devices</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Device</th><th>Key features</th><th>Design implication for apps</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Smartphone</b></td><td>Touch screen, camera, GPS, sensors, always connected</td><td>Main target; design for one-handed use and small screens.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tablet</b></td><td>Larger screen, often Wi-Fi only</td><td>Use extra space for split views; popular in schools and for menus at restaurants on Bay Street.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Smartwatch</b> (wearable)</td><td>Tiny screen, heart-rate and motion sensors, notifications</td><td>Glanceable info: one number, one button. Apple watchOS, Google Wear OS.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fitness band</b> (wearable)</td><td>Step, sleep and heart sensors</td><td>Data sent to a companion phone app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Smart speaker</b></td><td>Microphones, voice assistant (Alexa, Google Assistant, Siri)</td><td>Voice interface, no screen; content must work as audio.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Smart TV</b></td><td>Large screen, remote or voice control</td><td>Streaming apps; \"ten-foot\" interface.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Other wearables include <b>smart glasses</b> and <b>AR or VR headsets</b>, which display information in front of the user's eyes. Most wearables pair with a smartphone app, so the phone stays the hub of the user's personal device network.</p>\n<p><b>Caribbean examples</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A Trinidad runner tracks training for a 10K with a smartwatch app.</li>\n<li>A hotel in Negril uses tablets for check-in and smart TVs in rooms.</li>\n<li>An elderly resident in Linden uses a smart speaker to ask for the hurricane forecast.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question asks how a device affects app design, mention <b>screen size</b>, <b>input method</b> (touch, voice, buttons) and <b>sensors</b>. These link to the user experience factors in a later unit.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A smart device can connect to a network, run software and respond to people or other devices. Smartphones, tablets, smartwatches, fitness bands, smart speakers and smart TVs are all smart devices; wearables are the ones you wear. Each needs a different app design: a watch shows one number at a glance, while a smart speaker talks, which helps an elderly resident in Linden hear the hurricane forecast.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Designing for a new device is not the same as shrinking a phone app. A smartwatch app must deliver its value in a two-second glance and often depends on a companion phone app for setup and data. A voice interface has no menus to scan, so options must be short and spoken in the order users need them; it also raises privacy questions, because the device is always listening for its wake word. Grade One answers link the device to three design levers: screen size, input method (touch, voice, buttons, gestures) and sensors (heart rate, motion, GPS). Also note the phone's role as the hub: most wearables lose much of their usefulness if the paired phone has a flat battery or no signal.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which device fits?","intro":"<p>Island Pulse Media has five client briefs. Choose the most suitable device for each.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Carnival road-march timer that shows only the next band and a countdown on the wrist.","options":["Smartwatch","Smart TV","Smart speaker"],"answer":0,"fb":"Glanceable, wrist-based information suits a smartwatch."},{"text":"A hands-free recipe guide for sorrel drink while the user's hands are busy.","options":["Smart speaker","Fitness band","Tablet stand with touch only"],"answer":0,"fb":"Voice control lets the cook follow steps without touching a screen."},{"text":"A digital menu customers browse at the table in an Oranjestad restaurant.","options":["Tablet","Smartwatch","Smart speaker"],"answer":0,"fb":"A tablet's larger screen shows photos and prices clearly."},{"text":"A step-count challenge for a school health campaign.","options":["Fitness band or smartwatch","Smart TV","Smart speaker"],"answer":0,"fb":"Wearables with motion sensors count steps automatically."},{"text":"A tour app with maps and GPS for visitors walking around Willemstad.","options":["Smart TV","Smartphone","Smart speaker"],"answer":1,"fb":"Smartphones are portable, have GPS and are what visitors already carry."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched devices to tasks using screen size, input method, sensors and portability.","tutor":[{"h":"Hands and eyes","p":"<p>If hands are busy, think voice. If only a glance is possible, think wrist.</p>"},{"h":"Sensors","p":"<p>Steps and heart rate need a device worn on the body.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which device fits? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Hands-free voice control suits a...","options":["smart speaker","tablet"],"answer":0,"fb":"Smart speakers are controlled by voice."},{"text":"Counting steps suits a...","options":["fitness band","smart TV"],"answer":0,"fb":"Worn sensors count steps."},{"text":"A walking tour with GPS suits a...","options":["smart speaker","smartphone"],"answer":1,"fb":"Phones are portable and have GPS."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which device fits? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Island Pulse Media's next six briefs are harder. Each one has a constraint. Choose the BEST device or design and think about why the others fall short.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Grace Bay hotel wants guests to control the room lights and air-conditioning without installing anything new. Which is BEST?","options":["A smartwatch app guests must download","A web app opened by scanning a QR code in the room","A fitness band given to each guest","A smart TV app that needs a separate login"],"answer":1,"fb":"A web app opened by QR code works on the guest's own phone with nothing to install, which matches the constraint."},{"text":"A Carnival band wants members on the road to know when their section is about to move, even over loud music. Which feature matters MOST?","options":["Haptic vibration on a smartwatch","A smart speaker's spoken reply","A large high-resolution screen","A smart TV's ten-foot interface"],"answer":0,"fb":"Vibration on the wrist is noticed without looking or listening, which suits a noisy road march."},{"text":"A primary school in Roseau wants a reading app for pupils who share devices in class. Which is MOST suitable?","options":["Smartwatches","Smart speakers","Fitness bands","Shared tablets"],"answer":3,"fb":"Tablets have screens large enough for reading and can be shared on school Wi-Fi."},{"text":"An elderly resident with poor eyesight wants daily medicine reminders. Which is BEST, and why?","options":["A smart TV, because it has the biggest screen","A smartwatch, because its text is small and neat","A tablet game, because it is fun","A smart speaker with spoken reminders, because it needs no reading or small buttons"],"answer":3,"fb":"Voice removes the barriers of small text and fiddly controls for this user."},{"text":"A gym wants members' heart-rate data during classes without members holding their phones. Which design is BEST?","options":["Members type their heart rate into the app after class","A wall-mounted smart TV counts the people in the room","A smart speaker asks members how they feel","A wearable collects the data and syncs it to the companion phone app"],"answer":3,"fb":"Wearable sensors collect data automatically and the phone app stores and displays it."},{"text":"A Gouyave fisherman wants weather warnings at sea, where his phone often has no signal. What is the KEY limitation the app must plan for?","options":["Voice control","Connectivity: download forecasts before leaving and keep alerts small enough for weak signal","Screen colour depth","The size of the app icon"],"answer":1,"fb":"The device is fine; the limiting factor is the network, so the app must work offline and with tiny data."}],"win":"Excellent. You evaluated each brief's constraint (nothing to install, noise, shared use, eyesight, hands-free data, weak signal) before choosing the device or design."}},{"type":"learn","title":"The Internet of Things, sensors and embedded systems","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Internet of Things (IoT):</b> a network of everyday physical objects fitted with sensors, software and connectivity so that they can collect data, exchange it over the Internet and sometimes act automatically, with little human input.</p>\n<p><b>Key parts of an IoT system</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sensors</b> measure the real world: temperature, humidity, light, motion, water level, GPS location, wind speed, soil moisture.</li>\n<li><b>Actuators</b> do something: open a valve, switch on a pump, lock a door, sound an alarm.</li>\n<li><b>Embedded system</b>: a small computer built into a device to do one dedicated job, for example a microcontroller in a smart meter or a car's airbag unit. Popular learning boards include <b>Arduino</b>, <b>Raspberry Pi</b> and <b>micro:bit</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Connectivity</b>: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, mobile networks (4G, 5G, NB-IoT) or long-range low-power networks (LoRaWAN).</li>\n<li><b>Cloud and app</b>: data is stored and analysed online and shown to people on a <b>mobile app</b> or dashboard.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The mobile phone is often the <b>control centre</b> of IoT: you check your smart door camera, smart plug or car from an app.</p>\n<p><b>Caribbean IoT applications</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Area</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy</td><td>Smart electricity meters let an electricity utility read usage remotely; solar systems report output to an app.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Agriculture</td><td>Soil-moisture sensors on a Jamaican farm trigger irrigation and send alerts to the farmer's phone.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Disaster management</td><td>Weather stations and river-level sensors in Guyana or Dominica send early warnings.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tourism</td><td>Hotel rooms in Rodney Bay with smart locks, lighting and air-conditioning controlled by guests' phones.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Marine and ports</td><td>GPS trackers on boats and containers at the Port of Spain docks; sensors monitoring sargassum or water quality.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Risks</b>: weak passwords on IoT devices, privacy of data collected, dependence on power and connectivity, and cost of maintenance after hurricanes.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Distinguish clearly: a <b>smart device</b> is one connected product; <b>IoT</b> is the whole network of connected objects; an <b>embedded system</b> is the dedicated computer inside the object.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The Internet of Things (IoT) means everyday objects that have sensors and an Internet connection, so they can collect data, share it and sometimes act on their own. A sensor measures something, such as soil moisture. An actuator does something, such as opening a water valve. On a farm in Jamaica, a soil sensor can alert the farmer's phone when the crops need water.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Keep three terms apart: a <b>smart device</b> is one connected product; an <b>embedded system</b> is the dedicated computer inside it; the <b>IoT</b> is the whole network of such objects plus the cloud and apps that use their data. Connectivity is a design decision. Wi-Fi suits a hotel room, but a sensor in a Dominican river valley may need a long-range, low-power network such as LoRaWAN or NB-IoT so that its battery lasts for months. The biggest IoT risks are often not technical: default passwords, data collected without consent, and devices nobody maintains after a hurricane. A strong answer weighs the benefits (automation, early warning) against these risks.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"How an IoT farm alert works","intro":"<p>Blue Mountain Greens farm in Jamaica uses IoT to protect its crops. Put the steps of the system in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"A soil-moisture sensor in the field measures the water level in the soil."},{"t":"An embedded microcontroller reads the sensor value every 15 minutes."},{"t":"The device sends the reading over the mobile network to a cloud server."},{"t":"Software on the server compares the reading with the safe level."},{"t":"The farmer's mobile app shows a \"Soil too dry\" alert."},{"t":"The farmer taps \"Water now\" and an actuator opens the irrigation valve."}],"win":"Correct. You traced an IoT system from sensor to embedded system to network, cloud, app and actuator.","tutor":[{"h":"Sense first","p":"<p>Nothing happens until something is measured.</p>"},{"h":"Act last","p":"<p>The actuator responds only after a decision is made.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"IoT farm alert (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"A sensor measures soil moisture."},{"t":"The reading is sent to a cloud server."},{"t":"The farmer's app shows an alert."},{"t":"The irrigation valve opens."}]},
 "up": {"title":"How an IoT farm alert works (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Blue Mountain Greens has upgraded its system. Sensors in the far fields now use a long-range, low-power radio network, and the server checks the weather forecast before alerting the farmer. Put the full sequence in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"A soil-moisture sensor measures the water content of the soil."},{"t":"The embedded microcontroller reads the sensor every 15 minutes and converts the signal into a number."},{"t":"A long-range, low-power LoRaWAN radio sends the reading to a gateway at the farmhouse."},{"t":"The gateway forwards the data over the mobile network to a cloud server."},{"t":"Software on the server compares the reading with the safe level and checks the rain forecast."},{"t":"Because no rain is expected, the server sends a \"Soil too dry\" push notification to the farmer's app."},{"t":"The farmer approves watering in the app, and the command is sent back to the field."},{"t":"An actuator opens the irrigation valve, and the next sensor reading confirms the soil is wetter."}],"win":"Excellent. You traced the full loop, from sensor, embedded system, LoRaWAN gateway and cloud logic to the app, the actuator and the feedback reading."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Native, web and cross-platform development","html":"<p>A developer must decide <b>how</b> to build an app as well as <b>which</b> platform to target. <b>Cross-platform development tools</b> are a related technology you need to know.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Approach</th><th>What it means</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Weaknesses</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Native</b></td><td>Separate apps written for each OS (Kotlin for Android, Swift for iOS)</td><td>Best performance, full access to device features, platform look and feel</td><td>Two codebases, two teams, higher cost and time</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cross-platform</b></td><td>One codebase produces apps for Android and iOS (and sometimes web and desktop)</td><td>Lower cost, faster time-to-market, one team</td><td>Slightly larger apps; some device features need extra work</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Web app / PWA</b></td><td>A website that behaves like an app; a Progressive Web App can be installed and work offline</td><td>No store approval, works on any device with a browser</td><td>Limited access to some hardware; less visible than store apps</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Cross-platform tools you should name</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Flutter</b> (Google): uses the Dart language; draws its own interface, so apps look the same on every device.</li>\n<li><b>React Native</b> (Meta): uses JavaScript or TypeScript and React; useful for web developers like Maya who already know JavaScript.</li>\n<li><b>.NET MAUI</b> (Microsoft): uses C# and .NET; builds for Android, iOS, Windows and macOS.</li>\n<li>Low-code options such as <b>Thunkable</b> and <b>FlutterFlow</b> let beginners build for both platforms with drag-and-drop.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Even with cross-platform tools, publishing an iOS build still needs an Apple developer account and access to macOS (a Mac or a cloud build service).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For \"justify\" questions, link the approach to the client's constraints: \"Flutter is justified because the startup has one developer and a small budget, and needs to reach both Android and iOS users quickly.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are three ways to build an app. <b>Native</b>: a separate app for each system (Kotlin for Android, Swift for iOS), fast but costly. <b>Cross-platform</b>: one set of code for both, using tools such as Flutter or React Native. <b>Web app or PWA</b>: a website that works like an app, with no app store. A Grenadian startup with one developer would usually choose cross-platform.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The choice is about trade-offs, and the right answer depends on the constraints. Native wins when performance and deep hardware access matter, such as a banking app using biometrics or a game with heavy graphics. Cross-platform wins on cost and speed, but features added in a new OS version may reach the framework later, and some device features need extra native code. A PWA avoids store fees and review, yet on iPhone it still has limited hardware access, so it suits timetables and menus better than NFC payments. A common misconception is that cross-platform removes the need for a Mac: publishing an iOS build still needs macOS, on a Mac or through a cloud build service.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing a build approach","q":"St. Lucia Jazz Pass startup has one developer who knows JavaScript, a small budget and only eight weeks before the festival. About half its audience uses iPhones and half uses Android. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","options":[{"t":"Build an iOS-only app in Xcode.","fb":"This excludes Android users and needs Swift, which the developer does not know."},{"t":"Build one app with React Native and publish to both stores.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. React Native uses JavaScript, which the developer knows, and one codebase reaches both audiences within the budget and time."},{"t":"Build two separate native apps in Kotlin and Swift.","fb":"Native gives top performance, but two codebases are too slow and costly for one developer in eight weeks."},{"t":"Build an Android-only app in Android Studio.","fb":"This would leave out about half the audience who use iPhones."}],"tutor":[{"h":"List the constraints","p":"<p>Skills, budget, time and audience. Which option satisfies all four?</p>"},{"h":"One codebase","p":"<p>Which approach lets one person reach both platforms?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing a build approach (Foundation)","q":"A developer with little time and money needs one app for both Android and iOS. The best approach is...","options":[{"t":"Android only","fb":"This leaves out iPhone users."},{"t":"cross-platform (for example Flutter or React Native)","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. One codebase builds for both platforms."},{"t":"two separate native apps","fb":"Two codebases cost more time and money."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Choosing a build approach (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A Jamaican credit union wants a members' app with fingerprint login, card controls and tap-to-pay using NFC. Its security reviews are strict, it can afford two specialist teams, and about 60% of members use Android while 40% use iPhones. Which approach is MOST justifiable?","options":[{"t":"Two native apps, in Kotlin for Android and Swift for iOS, because the app needs deep hardware access and the strictest security, and the budget supports two teams.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. When security and hardware features matter most and the budget allows it, native development on both platforms is the strongest justification."},{"t":"A PWA, because it avoids app store fees and works on every phone with a browser.","fb":"A PWA has limited access to NFC and biometrics, especially on iPhone, so it cannot meet the tap-to-pay and security needs."},{"t":"A drag-and-drop low-code builder, because it is the fastest way to launch.","fb":"Speed is not the main constraint here. Low-code tools may not meet strict security reviews or support NFC payments well."},{"t":"An Android-only native app, because most members use Android.","fb":"This would exclude about 40% of members, and the budget can support both platforms."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Choosing a platform: audience, budget, time-to-market","html":"<p>Choosing a platform is a <b>business decision</b>, not just a technical one. The course outline names three factors; Paper 02 often asks you to <b>justify</b> a choice using them.</p>\n<p><b>1. Target audience</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Which devices do they actually use? Check analytics, surveys or StatCounter data for the country.</li>\n<li>Tourists from the US and Canada visiting the Cayman Islands are likely to include many iPhone users; a farmers' app in rural Guyana is likely to be mostly Android on budget phones.</li>\n<li>Age, income and data plans affect app size and features.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2. Budget</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Native apps for two platforms roughly double development and maintenance costs.</li>\n<li>iOS needs a Mac and an annual developer fee; Android needs only a one-off fee and runs on any computer.</li>\n<li>Low-code and cross-platform tools reduce costs for small Caribbean businesses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>3. Time-to-market</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>How quickly must the app launch? Carnival, a cricket series or the start of tourist season cannot be moved.</li>\n<li>A PWA or cross-platform app can launch faster; App Store review may add days.</li>\n<li>A <b>minimum viable product (MVP)</b> on one platform first, then expand, is a common strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Other factors</b>: device features needed (camera, NFC payments, sensors), security requirements (banking apps), team skills, maintenance and updates, and the app store rules for payments.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Sensible choice</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Bank app with high security, large budget</td><td>Native Android and iOS</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Small restaurant in Soufrière, tiny budget</td><td>PWA or WhatsApp catalogue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Startup reaching all phones, one developer</td><td>Cross-platform (Flutter, React Native)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Student SBA prototype</td><td>Figma prototype, MIT App Inventor or Thunkable</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Justify\" means give the choice <b>and</b> the reasons linked to the scenario. Use \"because\" at least twice and refer to all three factors.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Choosing a platform is a business decision. Ask three questions. Who are the users, and which phones do they have? How much money is there? How soon must the app launch? A ferry app for tourists and locals in St. Vincent and the Grenadines needs both Android and iPhone, while a small restaurant in Soufrière with a tiny budget might start with a web app or a WhatsApp catalogue.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The factors often pull in different directions, and a Grade One answer shows how the tension is resolved. A bank needs native security but may also need to launch quickly, so it might release a native app on its largest platform first and follow with the second. A common strategy is the MVP: launch the smallest useful version on the platform most of the audience uses, measure real usage, then expand. Watch for hidden costs that examiners like: yearly maintenance and OS updates, Apple's annual fee, and testing on many Android models. Your justification should use the scenario's numbers (survey percentages, budget, weeks to launch) and the word \"because\" for each factor.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Platform decision for a ferry app","intro":"<p>You are advising Bay Hopper Ferries, which runs boats between Basseterre, St. Kitts, and Charlestown, Nevis. It wants an app for timetables, tickets and delay alerts. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The owner says, \"Everyone has an iPhone, so just build for iOS.\" What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Check the audience: survey passengers and look at web analytics and StatCounter data for the island.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Decisions about platforms should rest on evidence about the target audience."},{"t":"Agree and start an iOS-only app in Xcode.","to":"bad1","fb":"An assumption is not evidence. Local workers and many visitors may use Android."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Data shows a mix: many visitors use iPhones, while most local commuters use Android. The budget is modest and the app must be ready before the tourist season in eight weeks. Which build approach?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A cross-platform app (for example Flutter) for both stores, plus a simple web timetable.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. One codebase reaches both groups within the budget and time."},{"t":"Two native apps built by two separate teams.","to":"bad2","fb":"This doubles cost and is unlikely to be ready in eight weeks."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Passengers want delay alerts even when their phone has weak signal at sea. What feature do you prioritise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Push notifications plus an offline timetable and saved tickets with QR codes.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Offline tickets and push alerts work with the realities of connectivity at sea."},{"t":"A live HD camera stream from the boat.","to":"bad3","fb":"This needs strong, constant connectivity and lots of data, which passengers will not have at sea."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Smooth sailing.</b> The app launches on time and reaches both visitors and locals. A Grade One answer would justify this: the choice matched the target audience, the budget and the time-to-market, and features suited the connectivity available.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Half the passengers left behind.</b> Local commuters on Android cannot use the app, and the ferry company receives complaints. Try again and gather audience data first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Missed the season.</b> The budget runs out and neither app is ready. Try again and consider cross-platform tools.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Unusable at sea.</b> The stream buffers endlessly and drains data. Try again and design for weak signal.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Evidence first","p":"<p>What do you need to know about the users before choosing?</p>"},{"h":"Three factors","p":"<p>Audience, budget and time-to-market. Which option fits all three?</p>"},{"h":"Signal at sea","p":"<p>Which features still work with little or no connection?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Ferry app platform (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: the ferry's passengers use both Android and iPhone, and the budget is small.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which approach do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"One cross-platform app for both stores.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. It reaches everyone at lower cost."},{"t":"An iPhone-only app.","to":"bad","fb":"Android users would be left out."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You matched the platform to the audience and budget.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Many passengers cannot use it. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Platform decision for a ferry app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief from Bay Hopper Ferries, which runs boats between Basseterre, St. Kitts, and Charlestown, Nevis. Every choice sounds possible; pick the one you could defend in Paper 02. Some choices end the project early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The owner says: \"Everyone has an iPhone, so just build for iOS. I want it before the tourist season in eight weeks, and I want to sell tickets in the app.\" What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Start an iOS app now and add Android later if passengers complain.","to":"ok1","fb":"This follows the owner's wish quickly, but it is based on an assumption, not evidence."},{"t":"Gather evidence: booking-site analytics by device, a quick survey at both terminals and StatCounter data for the islands.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Three sources of evidence give a reliable picture of the target audience."},{"t":"Promise two native apps so that nobody is left out.","to":"bad1","fb":"Nobody is left out, but two native apps will not fit one budget and eight weeks."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Analytics show 58% of bookings come from iPhones (mostly visitors) and 42% from Android phones (mostly local commuters, who travel every day). The budget covers one developer, who knows Dart and JavaScript. Which approach do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Two separate native apps, one in Kotlin and one in Swift.","to":"bad1","fb":"One developer cannot build and test two native apps in eight weeks."},{"t":"iOS first because it is the majority, and Android after the season.","to":"ok2","fb":"The commuters are the most frequent passengers. Leaving them out loses the app's most loyal users."},{"t":"One Flutter app for both stores, plus a simple web timetable for people who will not install an app.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. One codebase reaches both groups, uses the developer's Dart skills and fits the budget."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Tickets will be sold in the app. Both stores take a commission on digital goods. A ferry crossing is a real-world transport service. How do you handle payment?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Put every ticket through in-app purchase and raise prices by 30% to cover the commission.","to":"ok3","fb":"Store billing is meant for digital goods, and higher prices would send passengers back to the ticket booth."},{"t":"Ask passengers to send their card numbers by WhatsApp.","to":"bad2","fb":"This is insecure and would destroy passengers' trust."},{"t":"Use a card payment gateway, because a ferry crossing is a real-world service that can usually be paid outside the store's billing system.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. Store billing is for digital goods; real-world services normally use their own payment provider."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Passengers want delay alerts and their tickets even with weak signal at sea, and App Store review can take several days. What do you plan for launch?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Offline tickets with QR codes, but submit to both stores on launch day.","to":"ok4","fb":"The features are right, but there is no time for review or fixes."},{"t":"Offline tickets with QR codes, push alerts with an SMS fallback, and submission to both stores two weeks before the deadline.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. The features suit the connectivity at sea and the timeline allows for store review."},{"t":"A live HD camera stream from the deck, submitted the day before launch.","to":"bad3","fb":"The stream needs strong signal, and the timeline ignores review."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Smooth sailing.</b> The app launches on time for visitors and commuters, sells tickets without store commission and works at sea. You could justify every decision with evidence about the audience, budget, time-to-market and connectivity.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Half the passengers waiting.</b> The iOS app launches, but daily commuters on Android cannot use it and complaints pile up. Try again and gather evidence first.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>The regulars left out.</b> Visitors use the app, but the daily commuters who travel most cannot. Try again and weigh how often each group travels, not only their share of bookings.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Expensive tickets.</b> Sales are slow because tickets cost more in the app than at the booth. Try again and check what store billing is for.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>Late launch.</b> The app is still in review on opening day. Try again and build review time into the schedule.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Missed the season.</b> The budget runs out and neither app is ready. Try again and consider cross-platform tools.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Security failure.</b> Card details are exposed in chat messages, and the company's reputation suffers. Try again with a secure payment method.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Unusable at sea and late.</b> The stream buffers endlessly and the app misses its launch. Try again and design for weak signal and store review.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the accurate statements","intro":"<p>An intern at Island Pulse Media wrote these notes for a Paper 01 revision session. Some are wrong.</p>","q":"Select every statement that is ACCURATE.","lines":["Android's core is open source and is used by many device manufacturers.","iOS apps can be built and submitted using Android Studio on Windows.","Fragmentation means many device models and OS versions are in use at once.","HarmonyOS was developed by Huawei.","An embedded system is a small dedicated computer built into a device.","Cross-platform tools such as Flutter produce apps for both Android and iOS from one codebase.","A smart speaker is mainly controlled by touch.","Google Play charges developers a yearly fee of about US$99."],"correct":[0,2,3,4,5],"win":"Well done. You can spot accurate facts about operating systems, embedded systems and cross-platform tools, and avoid common traps.","miss":"Look again. iOS apps need Xcode on macOS; smart speakers are voice-controlled; Google Play has a one-off fee, while Apple charges yearly.","tutor":[{"h":"Tool traps","p":"<p>Which IDE belongs to which platform?</p>"},{"h":"Fee traps","p":"<p>One store charges once; the other charges every year.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the accurate statements (Foundation)","q":"Select the accurate statements.","lines":["Android is open source.","iOS runs on Samsung phones.","Flutter builds for Android and iOS from one codebase.","Smart speakers are controlled mainly by touch."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Android is open source and Flutter is cross-platform.","miss":"iOS runs only on Apple devices, and smart speakers use voice. Try again."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the accurate statements (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A second set of revision notes from the intern. These errors are harder to spot.</p>","q":"Select every statement that is ACCURATE.","lines":["Publishing an iOS app built with Flutter still requires access to macOS, on a Mac or through a cloud build service.","Because Android is open source, Android apps cannot be sold for money.","HarmonyOS NEXT can run Android APK files without changes.","Apple allows alternative app marketplaces in some regions, such as the European Union.","An embedded system is a general-purpose computer that can run any app the user installs.","Low fragmentation on iOS means developers usually test on fewer device and OS combinations than on Android.","A PWA can be installed from the browser and can work offline without going through an app store.","Both Google Play and the App Store take a commission on digital sales, usually between 15 and 30 percent.","A smart speaker's main input is touch, so apps for it need large buttons.","Kotlin is Apple's preferred language for iOS apps."],"correct":[0,3,5,6,7],"win":"Excellent. You avoided the subtle traps and selected only the accurate statements about macOS builds, alternative marketplaces, fragmentation, PWAs and store commission.","miss":"Look again. Open-source Android apps can still be sold; HarmonyOS NEXT does not run Android apps; an embedded system does one dedicated job; smart speakers use voice; Kotlin is Google's preferred language for Android."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Distinguish platforms and justify a choice","genre":"Structured response: distinguishing mobile platforms and justifying a choice","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Seaside Fit gym in Belize City wants an app where members book classes, track workouts and receive reminders. The owner also wants to link the app to members' smartwatches. She has a budget for one developer for three months. A member survey shows 55 percent use Android phones and 45 percent use iPhones.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) <b>Distinguish</b> between Android and iOS under TWO headings: openness and app distribution. (4 marks)</p><p>(b) Explain what is meant by the Internet of Things, and how the gym's app could use a wearable device. (3 marks)</p><p>(c) Recommend and <b>justify</b> a development approach for the gym's app, referring to target audience, budget and time-to-market. (6 marks)</p>","min":220,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Openness: Android is ... whereas iOS ... App distribution: Android apps are distributed through ... whereas iOS apps ...<br><b>(b)</b> The Internet of Things is ... For example, a member's smartwatch could ...<br><b>(c)</b> I recommend ... because the target audience ... In terms of budget, ... In terms of time-to-market, ... Therefore ...</p>","checklist":["I compared Android and iOS on the same feature in each heading.","I used \"whereas\" to show contrast.","I defined IoT and gave a smartwatch example.","I named a specific approach or tool (for example Flutter or React Native).","I justified my choice using the survey figures, the budget and the time limit."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Distinguishing Android and iOS","marks":4,"look":["open.?source|open|customis|manufactur","closed|apple only|walled|control","google play|play store","app store|review|sideload"],"min":3,"strong":"You contrasted the two systems clearly on each heading.","weak":"Give both sides for each heading: Android is open source and used by many makers, whereas iOS is closed and Apple only; Android apps go through Google Play (sideloading allowed), whereas iOS apps go through the App Store after a strict review."},{"criterion":"IoT and wearables","marks":3,"look":["internet of things|iot","sensor|connect|network","watch|wearable|heart|step|band"],"min":2,"strong":"Accurate IoT definition with a relevant wearable example.","weak":"Define IoT as everyday objects with sensors connected to the Internet sharing data, then link it to a smartwatch sending heart rate or steps to the gym app."},{"criterion":"Justified recommendation","marks":6,"look":["cross.?platform|flutter|react native|maui|thunkable","audience|member|55|45|both","budget|cost|one developer","time|three months|quick|faster|launch","because|therefore|so that"],"min":3,"strong":"Your recommendation is well justified using all three factors.","weak":"Recommend a specific approach and link it to each factor: the audience uses both platforms, the budget allows one developer, and three months is short."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Openness:</b> Android's core is open source, so many manufacturers such as Samsung and Motorola can use and customise it, whereas iOS is a closed system that Apple controls and that runs only on iPhones. <b>App distribution:</b> Android apps are distributed mainly through Google Play, with a one-off developer fee and the option to sideload apps from other sources, whereas iOS apps are distributed through Apple's App Store, which charges a yearly fee and applies a stricter review before an app is published.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The Internet of Things is a network of everyday objects fitted with sensors, software and connectivity so they can collect and share data over the Internet. A member's smartwatch is an IoT device: its heart-rate and motion sensors could send workout data by Bluetooth to the Seaside Fit app, which then logs calories and reminds the member of their next class.</p><p><b>(c)</b> I recommend a cross-platform approach using Flutter. The target audience is split, with 55 percent on Android and 45 percent on iPhone, so building for only one platform would exclude almost half the members. Because the budget covers only one developer, two separate native apps in Kotlin and Swift would be too expensive, whereas Flutter lets one developer maintain a single codebase for both stores. In terms of time-to-market, three months is short, and one codebase means features such as class booking and reminders only need to be built and tested once. Flutter also supports plug-ins for health data from Wear OS and Apple Watch, so the smartwatch link is possible. Therefore Flutter gives the gym the widest reach at the lowest cost in the time available.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>In \"justify\" questions, the recommendation alone earns little. Each factor you link to the scenario, using its actual figures, earns marks. Quote the data (\"55 percent on Android\") to show you are applying, not just recalling.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Distinguish platforms and justify a choice (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: mobile platforms, Foundation (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Complete</b> these two sentences: \"Android is open source, whereas iOS ...\" and \"Android apps are sold on Google Play, whereas iOS apps ...\" <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> what the Internet of Things (IoT) means and give ONE way the gym's app could use a smartwatch. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Choose</b> native, cross-platform or web for the gym's app. Give ONE reason using the survey and ONE reason using the budget. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[9 marks]</b></p>","min":70,"max":220,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Android is open source, whereas iOS ... Android apps are sold on Google Play, whereas iOS apps ...<br><b>(b)</b> The Internet of Things means ... The gym app could use a smartwatch to ...<br><b>(c)</b> I choose ... because the survey shows ... Also, the budget ...</p>","checklist":["I finished both 'whereas' sentences with the iOS side.","I explained IoT and gave one smartwatch use.","I chose an approach and gave one survey reason and one budget reason."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Android and iOS contrast","marks":4,"look":["closed|apple|iphone only|controlled","app store","open","google play|play store"],"min":3,"strong":"Both sentences show a clear contrast.","weak":"Finish each sentence with the iOS side: iOS is closed and controlled by Apple; iOS apps are sold on Apple's App Store."},{"criterion":"IoT and smartwatch","marks":2,"look":["internet|connect|sensor|object|device","heart|step|workout|remind|track|notif"],"min":2,"strong":"You explained IoT and gave a smartwatch use.","weak":"Say IoT means everyday objects with sensors connected to the Internet, then give a use such as sending heart rate to the app."},{"criterion":"Choice with two reasons","marks":3,"look":["cross.?platform|flutter|react native|both","55|45|android and ios|both|survey|members","budget|one developer|cost|cheap"],"min":2,"strong":"You chose an approach and backed it with the survey and the budget.","weak":"Choose cross-platform, say the survey shows members use both Android and iPhone, and say the budget pays for only one developer."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Android is open source, whereas iOS is closed and controlled by Apple. Android apps are sold on Google Play, whereas iOS apps are sold on Apple's App Store.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The Internet of Things means everyday objects with sensors that are connected to the Internet and share data. The gym app could use a smartwatch to record a member's heart rate and steps during a class.</p><p><b>(c)</b> I choose cross-platform, for example Flutter, because the survey shows members use both: 55 percent Android and 45 percent iPhone. Also, the budget only pays for one developer, and one codebase is cheaper than two apps.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (9 marks): (a) 1 mark for each correct side of each contrast (4); (b) 1 for the IoT idea, 1 for a smartwatch use; (c) 1 for the approach, 1 for a survey reason, 1 for a budget reason. Praise students who quote the survey figures. Move them to the Core task once they can write a 'whereas' sentence without the starter.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Distinguish platforms and justify a choice (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: platforms, IoT and build approach, Grade One extension (20 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Seaside Fit gym in Belize City wants an app where members book classes, track workouts and receive reminders, linked to members' smartwatches. A member survey shows 55 percent use Android phones and 45 percent use iPhones. A follow-up survey shows that 30 percent of members wear a smartwatch: most use an Apple Watch or a Samsung Galaxy Watch, and a few use Huawei watches. The owner has a budget for one developer for three months, wants the first version ready for the gym's anniversary, and has asked whether \"a cheap website\" would do instead of an app.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> among Android, iOS and HarmonyOS under THREE headings: openness, app distribution and fragmentation. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> how the smartwatch link would work as an IoT system, naming the sensors, the connection and the role of the phone and cloud, and <b>identify</b> ONE privacy risk. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> native, cross-platform and PWA approaches for the gym and <b>recommend</b> one, justified by audience, budget and time-to-market. <i>(7 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommend</b>, with justification, whether the first version should support Huawei watches. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","min":400,"max":680,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Openness: Android ..., whereas iOS ..., while HarmonyOS ... App distribution: ... Fragmentation: ...<br><b>(b)</b> The watch's ... sensors measure ... and send the data by ... to the phone, which ... The cloud ... One privacy risk is ...<br><b>(c)</b> A native approach would ... but ... A PWA would ... but ... A cross-platform approach ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> I recommend that the first version ... because ... Later, ...</p>","checklist":["I compared all three systems on the SAME feature under each heading.","I named real sensors, a real connection (Bluetooth) and the roles of the phone app and the cloud, plus a privacy risk.","I weighed strengths and weaknesses of all three approaches before recommending one.","My justification uses the survey figures, the one-developer budget and the three-month deadline.","My Huawei recommendation uses the evidence (only a few members) and suggests a plan for later."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Three-way distinction","marks":6,"look":["open.?source|aosp|open","closed|walled|apple only|controlled","harmony|huawei|openharmony","google play|play store|sideload","app store|appgallery|review","fragment|many (models|devices|versions)|older version"],"min":4,"strong":"You contrasted all three systems on each heading.","weak":"Under each heading give all three: Android open, iOS closed, HarmonyOS partly open; Google Play (plus sideloading), App Store, AppGallery; high, low and limited fragmentation."},{"criterion":"IoT explanation and privacy risk","marks":4,"look":["sensor|heart|motion|accelerometer|gps","bluetooth","phone|companion|app","cloud|server|sync","privacy|health data|consent|personal|breach|share"],"min":3,"strong":"Accurate account of the IoT chain with a relevant privacy risk.","weak":"Explain: heart-rate and motion sensors on the watch, data sent by Bluetooth to the phone app, synced to the cloud; risk: sensitive health data shared without consent."},{"criterion":"Evaluation and justified recommendation","marks":7,"look":["native|kotlin|swift","pwa|web app|website","cross.?platform|flutter|react native","55|45|both","one developer|budget|cost","three months|time|deadline|anniversary","however|but|whereas|although"],"min":5,"strong":"You evaluated all three approaches and justified your choice with all three factors.","weak":"Give a strength and a weakness for native, PWA and cross-platform, then recommend one using the survey split, the one-developer budget and the three-month deadline."},{"criterion":"Huawei watch decision","marks":3,"look":["huawei|harmony","few|small|minority|percent","later|phase|version 2|future|first version|mvp"],"min":2,"strong":"Your decision is justified by the evidence and planned for later.","weak":"Use the evidence: only a few members use Huawei watches, so leave them out of the first version and plan support later."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Openness:</b> Android's core (AOSP) is open source and used by many manufacturers, whereas iOS is closed and controlled entirely by Apple, while HarmonyOS is built on the partly open OpenHarmony core but used mainly on Huawei devices. <b>App distribution:</b> Android apps are distributed mainly through Google Play, with a one-off fee and sideloading allowed, whereas iOS apps go through the App Store after a strict review and a yearly fee, and HarmonyOS apps are distributed through Huawei's AppGallery, which has a smaller catalogue outside China. <b>Fragmentation:</b> Android is highly fragmented because thousands of models run different versions, whereas iOS has low fragmentation because Apple updates most iPhones at once; HarmonyOS runs on a limited range of Huawei devices, so its fragmentation is low but its reach is small.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The smartwatch is part of an IoT system. Its heart-rate and motion sensors measure the member's pulse and movement during a class, and the watch sends the data by Bluetooth to the companion app on the member's phone. The phone app combines it with the class booking and syncs it to the gym's cloud server, which stores each member's history and sends reminders back to the phone and watch. One privacy risk is that heart-rate data is sensitive health information; if it is shared with the gym's staff or advertisers without clear consent, or the server is breached, members' personal data could be exposed.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A <b>native</b> approach (Kotlin and Swift) would give the best performance and the fullest access to watch data, but two codebases are too much for one developer in three months. A <b>PWA</b> would be the cheapest and needs no store approval, but web apps on iPhone have very limited access to Bluetooth and health data, so the smartwatch link, a key requirement, would not work well. A <b>cross-platform</b> approach such as Flutter lets one developer build one codebase for both stores, and plug-ins can read workout data from Apple Health on iPhone and Health Connect on Android. I recommend Flutter because the audience is split 55 to 45 between Android and iPhone, so both platforms are needed; because the budget covers only one developer, so a single codebase is essential; and because three months is short, so features such as booking and reminders should be built and tested once. Therefore a \"cheap website\" would not meet the brief.</p><p><b>(d)</b> I recommend that the first version should not support Huawei watches. Only a few members use them, and supporting HarmonyOS would need separate work that would put the anniversary deadline at risk. Most members' watches (Apple Watch and Galaxy Watch) are covered by the Flutter approach. Huawei support can be added in a later version if the number of Huawei users grows, and in the meantime those members can still book classes on their phones.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (20 marks): (a) 2 marks per heading, awarded only when all three systems are compared on the same feature. (b) 1 sensors, 1 connection, 1 phone and cloud roles, 1 privacy risk. (c) 3 for a strength and weakness of each approach, 3 for linking the recommendation to audience, budget and time, 1 for a clear conclusion. (d) 1 decision, 1 evidence, 1 plan for later. Accept a well-justified recommendation of native development for (c) only if the student explains how the deadline would still be met.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M3U3","title":"Caribbean context vs the international mobile ecosystem","objectives":["Contrast regional and international statistics on smartphone penetration, mobile Internet use, connectivity and app downloads, quoting sources and years.","Explain the social and economic impact of mobile technology: mobile banking and mobile money, e-government and digital health.","Describe applications of mobile technology in agriculture, education, tourism and disaster management, including Hurricane Irma recovery.","Analyse the imbalance between content consumption and content production in the Caribbean, and the role of digital content creation.","Contrast mobile and broadband infrastructure: submarine cables, 4G and 5G rollout and connectivity gaps.","Discuss policy, security and ethical considerations, and how mobile connects the Caribbean diaspora."],"videos":[{"title":"The global digital divide","q":"global digital divide explained ITU internet access"},{"title":"How submarine cables power the Internet","q":"how undersea submarine internet cables work explained"},{"title":"Mobile money explained","q":"mobile money explained how it works financial inclusion"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"The numbers: global and Caribbean statistics","html":"<p>To <b>contrast</b> the Caribbean with the world, you need evidence. Use figures from reliable <b>source types</b> and always give the <b>year</b>, because mobile statistics change every year.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Source</th><th>What it publishes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ITU</b> (International Telecommunication Union, a UN agency)</td><td>Internet use, mobile subscriptions, coverage, prices; annual <i>Facts and Figures</i>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GSMA</b> (mobile industry association)</td><td><i>Mobile Economy</i> reports: subscribers, mobile Internet users, smartphone adoption, 5G.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>World Bank</b></td><td>Development indicators, such as remittances as a share of GDP and Internet users.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>StatCounter, Sensor Tower, data.ai</b></td><td>Operating system share, app downloads and spending.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Global picture (approximate)</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>ITU's 2025 figures estimate that about <b>6 billion people</b>, roughly <b>three quarters</b> of the world's population, use the Internet, while about <b>2.2 billion</b> remain offline.</li>\n<li>ITU (2025) estimates that <b>5G</b> covers just over half of the world's population, but coverage is very uneven: most people in high-income countries, very few in low-income countries.</li>\n<li>Market-research firms such as Sensor Tower report <b>well over 200 billion</b> app downloads worldwide each year.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Caribbean picture (approximate)</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>GSMA's 2024 report on Latin America and the Caribbean estimates that about <b>two thirds</b> of the region's population used mobile Internet by the end of 2023.</li>\n<li>Many Caribbean islands record <b>more than 100 mobile subscriptions per 100 people</b> in ITU data, because people carry two SIMs (common in Jamaica, where many carry both a Digicel and a Flow SIM) or visitors buy local SIMs.</li>\n<li>Smartphone ownership is high in tourism economies such as Antigua and Barbuda, Barbados and the Bahamas, but lower in Haiti and in rural interiors of Guyana and Belize.</li>\n<li>Caribbean users download many apps but very few of the apps they use are <b>made</b> in the Caribbean.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Never invent a precise figure. Write \"According to ITU (2025), roughly three quarters of the world's population uses the Internet\" rather than a decimal. Examiners reward the <b>source</b>, the <b>year</b> and the <b>comparison</b>.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To compare the Caribbean with the world, you need numbers from trusted sources, such as the ITU (a United Nations agency), the GSMA and the World Bank, and you must always give the year. Around the world, about three quarters of people use the Internet. Some Caribbean islands show more than 100 mobile subscriptions per 100 people, because many people carry two SIM cards, as many people do in Jamaica.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Statistics need interpreting, not just quoting. A subscription rate above 100 per 100 people counts SIM cards, not people, so it can hide residents who are not connected at all. Figures for \"Latin America and the Caribbean\" are dominated by large countries such as Brazil and Mexico, so they may say little about Dominica or Montserrat; point this out when you use them. Distinguish the <b>coverage gap</b> (no network) from the <b>usage gap</b> (a network is available, but people do not use it because of cost, skills or a lack of relevant content). A Grade One answer pairs each figure with its source, its year and what it does or does not show.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Reading the statistics","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is preparing an infographic comparing Caribbean and global mobile use. Help her interpret the data correctly.</p>","items":[{"text":"Antigua and Barbuda shows more than 100 mobile subscriptions per 100 people. The BEST explanation is...","options":["many people use two SIMs and visitors buy local SIMs","every resident owns exactly one phone","the data must be wrong"],"answer":0,"fb":"Subscriptions count SIM cards, not people. Dual SIMs and tourists push the figure above 100."},{"text":"Which organisation is the UN agency that publishes global ICT statistics?","options":["ITU","StatCounter","GSMA"],"answer":0,"fb":"The ITU is the United Nations agency for information and communication technologies."},{"text":"Keisha wants remittances as a share of GDP for Jamaica. Which source fits BEST?","options":["a social media poll","World Bank development indicators","Google Play"],"answer":1,"fb":"The World Bank publishes remittance data by country and year."},{"text":"Many people live within mobile broadband coverage but do not use the Internet. GSMA calls this...","options":["the coverage gap","fragmentation","the usage gap"],"answer":2,"fb":"The usage gap is people who are covered but not connected, often because of cost, skills or lack of relevant content."},{"text":"Which statement is written in the BEST exam style?","options":["According to ITU (2025), roughly three quarters of people worldwide use the Internet.","Loads of people use phones now.","74.638% of everyone is online."],"answer":0,"fb":"It names the source and year and uses an approximate, defensible figure."}],"win":"Excellent. You can interpret mobile statistics, choose a suitable source and quote figures in an exam-ready way.","tutor":[{"h":"People vs SIMs","p":"<p>A subscription is a SIM card, not a person.</p>"},{"h":"Coverage vs use","p":"<p>Living near a tower does not mean you can afford or choose to go online.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Reading the statistics (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"The UN agency for ICT statistics is...","options":["Apple","ITU"],"answer":1,"fb":"The ITU publishes global ICT data."},{"text":"Mobile subscriptions count...","options":["people","SIM cards"],"answer":1,"fb":"One person can have two SIMs."},{"text":"A good exam statistic includes...","options":["a source and a year","an exact decimal you made up"],"answer":0,"fb":"Source and year make it credible."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Reading the statistics (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Keisha's infographic is going to a regional conference, so every figure must stand up to questions. Evaluate how each statistic should be used.</p>","items":[{"text":"A report says about two thirds of people in Latin America and the Caribbean use mobile Internet (GSMA, 2024). Why should Keisha be careful using this figure for Dominica?","options":["Regional figures are dominated by large countries such as Brazil and Mexico, so they may not reflect a small island","Dominica is not part of the region","Mobile Internet figures cannot be compared between countries","GSMA figures are never reliable"],"answer":0,"fb":"A regional average is weighted by population, so small islands barely affect it. Say this, or find a country-level figure."},{"text":"Antigua and Barbuda has more than 100 subscriptions per 100 people, yet a survey finds some older residents never go online. How can both be true?","options":["Older residents only use satellite phones","Subscriptions measure Internet speed, not use","The survey must be wrong","Subscriptions count SIM cards, so dual-SIM users and visitors raise the figure while some residents have none"],"answer":3,"fb":"One person can hold several SIMs and visitors buy local ones, so a high subscription rate can sit alongside people who are not connected."},{"text":"Coverage maps show almost all of Jamaica has 4G, but many rural households still do not use mobile Internet. This is BEST described as...","options":["the usage gap","the coverage gap","fragmentation","latency"],"answer":0,"fb":"People are covered but not connected, usually because of cost, skills or a lack of relevant content: the usage gap."},{"text":"Keisha wants to compare how much Caribbean users spend on apps with the world average. Which source type is MOST suitable?","options":["App market intelligence firms such as Sensor Tower","World Bank remittance data","The ITU's annual Internet-use figures","A WhatsApp poll of her friends"],"answer":0,"fb":"App market intelligence firms track downloads and spending by country. The other sources measure different things or are not reliable."},{"text":"Which sentence would earn the MOST credit in a contrast essay?","options":["According to ITU (2025), roughly three quarters of the world's population uses the Internet, whereas Caribbean access ranges from very high in Antigua and Barbuda to much lower in Haiti.","The Caribbean has more phones than the rest of the world.","Exactly 74.638% of everyone is online, and the Caribbean is the same.","Loads of people are online everywhere now."],"answer":0,"fb":"It names a source and year, uses an approximate figure and makes a direct contrast with 'whereas'."},{"text":"Two reputable sources give different figures for the same island's Internet use. What is the BEST response in an essay?","options":["Quote the larger figure because it looks better","Quote one with its source and year, note that estimates vary by method, and use an approximate figure","Leave statistics out entirely","Average them and give the answer to three decimal places"],"answer":1,"fb":"Acknowledging that estimates vary shows critical use of evidence, which examiners reward."}],"win":"Excellent. You evaluated what each statistic can and cannot show, chose suitable sources and wrote figures the way a Grade One candidate would."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Social and economic impact: banking, e-government, health","html":"<p>Mobile technology changes how Caribbean people manage money, deal with government and look after their health.</p>\n<p><b>Mobile banking and mobile money</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mobile banking</b>: bank apps let customers check balances, pay bills (for example electricity or water) and transfer money without visiting a branch in town.</li>\n<li><b>Mobile money and digital wallets</b>: store value on a phone and pay merchants by QR code or phone number, even without a full bank account. This supports <b>financial inclusion</b> for the unbanked.</li>\n<li><b>Central bank digital currencies</b>: the Bahamas launched the <b>Sand Dollar</b> (2020) and Jamaica launched <b>JAM-DEX</b> (2022); the Eastern Caribbean Central Bank piloted <b>DCash</b>. These are designed to be used through mobile wallets.</li>\n<li><b>Remittances</b>: money sent home from the diaspora is a major income source. World Bank data shows remittances in Jamaica were roughly a sixth of GDP in 2024, and Haiti's share is among the highest in the world. Mobile apps make sending money faster and cheaper.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>E-government</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Paying taxes, renewing licences, booking appointments and receiving official alerts by phone.</li>\n<li>Government WhatsApp channels and social media pages that share announcements, especially during emergencies.</li>\n<li>Digital ID projects that let citizens prove identity online.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Digital health</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Telemedicine</b>: video consultations, important for small islands where specialists may visit only occasionally or patients travel abroad for care.</li>\n<li>Appointment reminders by SMS, medication reminders, fitness and wellness apps, vaccination records.</li>\n<li>Health campaigns on social media (for example on dengue prevention).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Economic impact</b>: GSMA estimates that mobile technologies and services contributed around 8 percent of GDP in Latin America and the Caribbean in 2023, and supported around 2 million jobs. New jobs include app developers, content creators, digital marketers and delivery drivers.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Social impact\" is about people's lives (inclusion, health, access to services). \"Economic impact\" is about money, jobs and business. Label each point so the examiner sees you know the difference.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Phones have changed daily life in the Caribbean. People pay bills and send money with banking apps instead of queuing at an office. Mobile wallets help people without bank accounts. Governments share news and services by phone, and doctors can hold video consultations with patients on small islands. These changes also create jobs, from app developers to delivery drivers, and add to each country's economy.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Label each impact correctly. Financial inclusion and access to health care are <b>social</b> impacts; jobs, GDP and cheaper remittances are <b>economic</b> impacts. Many services have both: a remittance app saves families money (economic) and keeps relatives in touch (social). Impacts are not all positive. Moving services onto phones can exclude older or rural citizens, and a digital currency only works if merchants accept it and users trust it. Everyday use of the Bahamas Sand Dollar and Jamaica's JAM-DEX has grown slowly, which shows that technology alone does not change behaviour. Evaluate impact by asking who benefits, who is left out and what conditions are needed.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the mobile service to its impact","intro":"<p>Match each Caribbean mobile service to the impact it best shows.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Paying an electricity bill in a banking app","r":"Saves time and travel to a payment office in Castries"},{"l":"A digital wallet for people without a bank account","r":"Financial inclusion for the unbanked"},{"l":"Sending money home from New York through an app","r":"Faster, cheaper remittances for families in Jamaica"},{"l":"Video consultation with a specialist in Curaçao","r":"Telemedicine reduces the need to travel for care"},{"l":"Renewing a driving licence online","r":"E-government makes public services easier to reach"},{"l":"SMS reminders for clinic appointments","r":"Fewer missed appointments and better health outcomes"},{"l":"The Bahamas Sand Dollar","r":"A central bank digital currency used through mobile wallets"}],"win":"Very good. You linked specific mobile services to their social and economic impacts, which is what \"explain the impact\" questions need.","tutor":[{"h":"Money services","p":"<p>Banking, wallets and remittances: who benefits and how?</p>"},{"h":"Public and health services","p":"<p>What does the person no longer need to do in person?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the impact (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Digital wallet","r":"Helps people without bank accounts"},{"l":"Telemedicine","r":"See a doctor without travelling"},{"l":"E-government","r":"Use public services online"},{"l":"Remittance app","r":"Send money home quickly"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the mobile service to its impact (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each card on the right names whether the impact is mainly <b>social</b> or <b>economic</b>, then explains it. Match each Caribbean mobile service to the impact it best shows.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"JAM-DEX in a mobile wallet","r":"A central bank digital currency issued by the Bank of Jamaica"},{"l":"A remittance app with lower fees than a transfer counter","r":"Economic: more of each transfer reaches families at home"},{"l":"A digital wallet linked to a phone number","r":"Social: financial inclusion for people without a bank account"},{"l":"A video consultation for a patient on Carriacou","r":"Social: specialist care without leaving the island"},{"l":"Registering a new business online","r":"Economic: lower costs and a faster start for entrepreneurs"},{"l":"A government WhatsApp channel during a storm","r":"Social: fast, trusted public information in an emergency"},{"l":"An SMS campaign on dengue prevention","r":"Social: public health awareness that can reduce illness"},{"l":"App-based delivery work","r":"Economic: new forms of income and employment"}],"win":"Excellent. You linked each service to a specific impact and labelled it social or economic, which is exactly how to structure an 'explain the impact' answer."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Applications: agriculture, education, tourism, disasters","html":"<p>You need to know the main <b>applications of mobile technology</b>. Learn at least one Caribbean example for each sector.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Sector</th><th>Mobile applications</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Agriculture</b></td><td>Weather and pest alerts by SMS; WhatsApp groups for farmers to sell produce; market price apps; IoT soil sensors; photos sent to extension officers to diagnose crop disease. Farmers in St. Vincent or Jamaica can find buyers without travelling to market.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Education</b></td><td>Google Classroom and Microsoft Teams on phones; YouTube revision; WhatsApp class groups; CXC past papers and e-books. During COVID-19 school closures in 2020 and 2021, many Caribbean students studied mainly on phones, which exposed gaps in devices and data.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tourism</b></td><td>Booking flights and hotels; maps and tour apps; mobile payments; reviews; QR-code menus in Rodney Bay restaurants; cruise passengers using roaming or Wi-Fi at Nassau's cruise port; social media marketing of Negril's Seven Mile Beach.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Disaster management</b></td><td>Early warnings, hurricane trackers, evacuation maps, emergency contacts, check-in features and offline information.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Case study: Hurricane Irma and Sint Maarten</b></p>\n<p>Hurricane Irma, a Category 5 storm, struck St. Martin on 6 September 2017. Power and mobile networks were badly damaged, and for days many residents could not contact family. As service returned, mobile phones became central to recovery:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Families abroad used social media groups and WhatsApp to locate relatives and share news.</li>\n<li>Government and relief agencies shared updates on curfews, water and food distribution through radio, social media and messaging.</li>\n<li>Photos taken on phones documented damage for insurance claims and recovery planning.</li>\n<li>Donations and remittances from the diaspora arrived through online and mobile channels.</li>\n<li>Lessons learned: the need for backup power at towers, redundant cables, offline-capable apps and agreed emergency communication plans.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Similar lessons came from Hurricane Maria in Dominica (2017) and Hurricane Beryl in Grenada's Carriacou and in St. Vincent and the Grenadines (2024).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In essay answers, one detailed local case study (such as Irma) is worth more than five vague examples. Say what happened, what mobile did, and what the lesson was.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Mobile technology helps in many parts of Caribbean life. Farmers get weather alerts and sell crops in WhatsApp groups. Students revise with Google Classroom and YouTube on their phones. Tourists book tours, read QR-code menus and pay by phone. During hurricanes, phones bring early warnings, and afterwards they help families find each other, as they did in Sint Maarten after Hurricane Irma in 2017.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Applications depend on the conditions around them. During the COVID-19 school closures, mobile learning worked for students with a smartphone and data, but it widened gaps for those sharing one phone or living with weak coverage. Disaster apps face a paradox: they are most needed exactly when networks are down, so the strongest designs push information <b>before</b> the storm and work offline afterwards. A Grade One essay uses one detailed case study rather than many vague examples: what happened, what mobile did, what failed and what was learned, such as the need for backup power at towers and redundant cables after Irma.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which sector?","intro":"<p>Malik is filming a short documentary for the Island Pulse Media about mobile technology across the Caribbean. Sort each clip into the sector it shows.</p>","main":"Which sector does this mobile application serve?","bins":["Agriculture","Education","Tourism","Disaster management"],"items":[{"t":"A Jamaican yam farmer posts produce in a WhatsApp group to find buyers.","bin":0,"fb":"Selling produce by mobile supports agriculture."},{"t":"A restaurant in Soufrière, St. Lucia, uses a QR-code menu in English and French.","bin":2,"fb":"Serving visitors in their language supports tourism."},{"t":"Residents of the Turks and Caicos Islands check a hurricane tracker and shelter list.","bin":3,"fb":"Preparing for storms is disaster management."},{"t":"A teacher posts homework in Google Classroom for students without laptops.","bin":1,"fb":"Using mobile platforms for coursework is education."},{"t":"After a storm, a family abroad uses a social media group to locate relatives.","bin":3,"fb":"Finding people after a hurricane is part of disaster response and recovery."},{"t":"A Dominica farmer receives an SMS warning of heavy rain before harvesting.","bin":0,"fb":"Weather alerts to protect crops support agriculture."},{"t":"A cruise passenger books a catamaran tour to the Tobago Cays by app.","bin":2,"fb":"Booking visitor experiences is tourism."},{"t":"A Breadfruit Valley Secondary student watches a CXC revision video on her phone on the bus.","bin":1,"fb":"Mobile learning supports education."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can give Caribbean applications of mobile technology in agriculture, education, tourism and disaster management.","tutor":[{"h":"Who is the user?","p":"<p>Farmer, student, visitor or resident in danger?</p>"},{"h":"Before and after","p":"<p>Disaster management includes preparing before a storm and recovering after it.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which sector? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A farmer gets a rain warning.","bin":0,"fb":"Agriculture."},{"t":"A student watches a revision video.","bin":1,"fb":"Education."},{"t":"A visitor books a boat tour.","bin":2,"fb":"Tourism."},{"t":"A family checks a hurricane tracker.","bin":3,"fb":"Disaster management."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which sector? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Malik's documentary now includes clips that mention more than one sector. Sort each clip by the <b>main purpose</b> of the mobile application, not by the topic it mentions.</p>","items":[{"t":"An extension officer in St. Vincent diagnoses banana leaf disease from a farmer's WhatsApp photo.","bin":0,"fb":"The purpose is protecting a crop: agriculture."},{"t":"A river-level sensor in Guyana triggers an SMS flood warning to villages downstream.","bin":3,"fb":"Early warning of a flood is disaster management."},{"t":"A Dominica teacher shares a recorded lesson on volcanic hazards with her class on WhatsApp.","bin":1,"fb":"The topic is a hazard, but the purpose is teaching a class: education."},{"t":"A cruise line's app shows passengers the walking route from the pier to the Bay Street shops in Nassau.","bin":2,"fb":"Guiding visitors is tourism."},{"t":"A market-price app shows Jamaican farmers what yams are selling for in Kingston today.","bin":0,"fb":"Price information helps farmers sell: agriculture."},{"t":"A Barbados school posts CXC past papers on a mobile-friendly portal.","bin":1,"fb":"Revision resources are education."},{"t":"A villa rental app lets guests in Anguilla unlock their door with a digital key.","bin":2,"fb":"Serving visitors' stays is tourism."},{"t":"After Hurricane Beryl, residents of Carriacou use an app to request emergency water deliveries.","bin":3,"fb":"Relief after a hurricane is disaster response and recovery."},{"t":"A hotel training app teaches new staff in St. Lucia how to welcome guests.","bin":2,"fb":"It involves training, but its purpose is serving the tourism industry."},{"t":"Before hurricane season, an app walks families in Anguilla through building an emergency kit.","bin":3,"fb":"Preparation is the first stage of disaster management."}],"win":"Excellent. You looked past the topic to the main purpose of each application, so you placed the trickier clips (a hazard lesson, a hotel training app) correctly."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Content consumption vs content production","html":"<p>One of the clearest contrasts between the Caribbean and the wider world is the <b>imbalance between consuming and producing</b> mobile content.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Content consumption</b> is using content made by others (streaming, scrolling, gaming). <b>Content production</b> is creating and publishing content and apps. <b>Digital content creation</b> is the making of videos, music, graphics, podcasts, apps and other digital products, increasingly by individuals using phones.</p>\n<p><b>The imbalance</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Caribbean users spend much of their mobile time on platforms and content made in the United States, Europe and Asia: Netflix, YouTube, TikTok, Instagram, Spotify, global games.</li>\n<li>Very few of the apps on a typical Caribbean phone are developed locally.</li>\n<li><b>Money flows out</b>: subscriptions, in-app purchases and advertising spending go mainly to foreign companies.</li>\n<li><b>Cultural impact</b>: foreign content can crowd out local stories, accents, languages (Papiamentu, Kréyòl) and history, shaping how young people see themselves.</li>\n<li><b>Data</b>: information about Caribbean users is stored and analysed overseas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Why the imbalance exists</b>: small markets, limited funding, fewer trained developers, higher data and equipment costs, difficulty receiving payouts from some platforms, and limited local hosting.</p>\n<p><b>Signs of change</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Soca, dancehall and Afro-Caribbean artists reach global audiences on YouTube, TikTok and Spotify.</li>\n<li>Caribbean creators build audiences with food, comedy, travel and patois content.</li>\n<li>Local news apps and websites such as the Jamaica Gleaner and the Trinidad Express keep island news in local hands.</li>\n<li>University and college programmes, hackathons and CAPE Digital Media itself are training producers, not just consumers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When you \"analyse\" the imbalance, give a <b>cause</b>, an <b>effect</b> and a <b>solution</b>. For example: small markets (cause) mean few local apps, so money flows abroad (effect); training and regional funding for creators (solution) can help.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Content consumption means using things other people made, like watching TikTok or Netflix. Content production means making and sharing your own videos, music or apps. Caribbean users consume a lot of mobile content, but most of it is made in the United States, Europe or Asia. That means money and data go abroad, and local stories and languages such as Papiamentu are seen less.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Avoid treating the imbalance as simply \"bad\". Global platforms also give Caribbean creators a stage: soca and dancehall artists reach worldwide audiences on YouTube and Spotify, which would have been very hard a generation ago. The deeper issue is <b>who owns the platform</b>. A creator on TikTok produces content, but the platform owns the audience data and keeps much of the advertising income. A strong \"analyse\" answer separates the cause (small markets, limited funding, payout barriers), the effect (money and data flowing out, local culture crowded out) and a realistic solution (training, regional funding, local platforms and hosting), and notes that solutions take time.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Explaining the imbalance","q":"A report states that most of the apps used daily in Guyana are produced outside the Caribbean. Which statement BEST explains an ECONOMIC effect of this imbalance?","options":[{"t":"Young people may hear fewer local accents and stories.","fb":"This is a real effect, but it is cultural and social, not economic."},{"t":"Phones in Guyana have smaller screens.","fb":"Screen size has nothing to do with where content is produced."},{"t":"The ITU publishes statistics every year.","fb":"This is a fact about a source, not an effect of the imbalance."},{"t":"Spending on subscriptions, in-app purchases and advertising flows mainly to foreign companies rather than local businesses.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This is an economic effect: money leaves the local economy instead of creating local jobs and income."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Key word","p":"<p>The question says <b>economic</b>. Which option is about money and business?</p>"},{"h":"Rule out","p":"<p>One option is true but social or cultural. Do not be distracted.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Explaining the imbalance (Foundation)","q":"Most apps used in the Caribbean are made abroad. An ECONOMIC effect is that...","options":[{"t":"local accents are heard less","fb":"That is a cultural effect."},{"t":"money spent on apps goes mainly to foreign companies","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. That is about money, so it is economic."},{"t":"phones get heavier","fb":"That is not related."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Explaining the imbalance (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"The Government of Guyana wants to reduce the imbalance between content consumption and content production in the country. Which policy would MOST directly tackle a ROOT CAUSE of the imbalance?","options":[{"t":"A fund and training programme that helps local creators and developers build, publish and get paid for apps and content.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Limited funding, skills and payout access are root causes, and this policy addresses all three."},{"t":"Blocking access to foreign streaming apps so that residents watch more local content.","fb":"This restricts users without building any local capacity, and would be very unpopular and hard to enforce."},{"t":"A national campaign asking residents to spend less time on their phones.","fb":"It might reduce consumption, but it does nothing to increase local production."},{"t":"Raising import duties on smartphones to protect local businesses.","fb":"Higher device prices reduce access for everyone, including would-be producers."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Infrastructure: cables, 4G and 5G, and gaps","html":"<p>Mobile and broadband <b>infrastructure</b> decides how fast, reliable and affordable connectivity is. The Caribbean contrasts sharply with large continental markets.</p>\n<p><b>Submarine cables</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Almost all international Internet traffic travels through <b>fibre-optic cables on the seabed</b>, not satellites.</li>\n<li>Small islands often depend on a <b>few cables</b>. Sint Maarten is linked by systems such as TelEm's <b>SMPR-1</b> cable to Puerto Rico (repaired in 2024 after years out of service) and other regional systems; the Eastern Caribbean Fiber System links many of the OECS islands.</li>\n<li>A cable cut, from a ship's anchor, earthquake or storm, can slow a whole country. <b>Redundancy</b> (more than one cable route) is essential for resilience.</li>\n<li><b>Satellite</b> services, including newer low-earth-orbit services such as Starlink, can provide backup and reach remote areas, but at a higher cost per user.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>4G and 5G rollout</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Global picture</th><th>Caribbean picture</th></tr>\n<tr><td>4G LTE</td><td>Widespread in most countries</td><td>The main mobile network in most territories; coverage good in towns, weaker in some rural and mountainous areas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5G</td><td>ITU (2025): covers just over half the world's population, mostly in high-income countries</td><td>Launched in some markets by operators such as Digicel and Flow; rollout is gradual and depends on spectrum licensing by regulators and on cost</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fixed fibre</td><td>Expanding fast in large cities</td><td>Growing (fibre-to-the-home in several islands), but uneven</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Connectivity gaps</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Geographic</b>: Guyana's interior, Belize's rural districts and small outer islands such as Carriacou or Saba.</li>\n<li><b>Affordability</b>: data can cost a larger share of income than in richer countries.</li>\n<li><b>Resilience</b>: power cuts and hurricanes take towers offline.</li>\n<li><b>Data centres</b>: few local or regional data centres, so content is hosted far away, adding latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Contrast with a specific comparison: \"Whereas large markets often have many cable routes, a small island such as Sint Maarten depends on a few, so a single fault has a bigger impact.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Almost all Internet traffic between the islands and the rest of the world travels through cables on the seabed, not by satellite. Many small islands depend on just a few of these cables, so if one is damaged, the whole island slows down. Most Caribbean territories use 4G, and 5G is arriving slowly. Remote places such as Guyana's interior or Saba still have weaker coverage than the capital cities.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Resilience comes from <b>redundancy</b>: two cables taking different routes, backup power at towers and satellite links for emergencies. Each layer costs money, and small markets make that cost hard to recover, which is why governments and regulators often help to fund it. Low-earth-orbit satellite services can reach remote villages and act as a backup, but they cost more per user and cannot carry a whole island's traffic as cheaply as fibre. Do not assume 5G solves everything: without fibre links to the towers, enough licensed spectrum and affordable 5G phones, a 5G launch reaches few people. In a contrast answer, use \"whereas\" to compare one specific factor, such as the number of cable routes.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Rebuilding after the storm","intro":"<p>Using lessons from Hurricane Irma, put these stages of restoring mobile service after a major hurricane in a sensible order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Before the season: carriers install backup generators at key towers and agree an emergency plan with government."},{"t":"During the storm: towers and power lines are damaged and most mobile service is lost."},{"t":"Immediately after: crews assess damage and restore power to the core network and main towers."},{"t":"Temporary coverage is provided with portable cell sites and satellite links where cables or towers are down."},{"t":"Residents use restored service to contact family, and agencies send updates by SMS and social media."},{"t":"Longer term: towers are rebuilt stronger and backup cable routes and offline-capable apps are planned."}],"win":"Correct. You sequenced preparation, impact, restoration and longer-term resilience, which shows how infrastructure and disaster management connect.","tutor":[{"h":"Before, during, after","p":"<p>Disaster management has three phases. Start with preparation.</p>"},{"h":"Power first","p":"<p>Towers cannot work without electricity.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Rebuilding after the storm (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Prepare backup generators before the season."},{"t":"The storm damages towers."},{"t":"Crews restore power and towers."},{"t":"Towers are rebuilt stronger for the future."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Rebuilding after the storm (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A fuller recovery plan, based on lessons from Hurricane Irma. Put all eight stages in order, from preparation to long-term resilience.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Before the season: carriers and government agree an emergency communication plan and test it in a drill."},{"t":"When a warning is issued: backup generators are fuelled, and shelter information is pushed to phones while networks still work."},{"t":"During the storm: towers and power lines are damaged and most mobile service is lost."},{"t":"Immediately after: crews assess damage to the core network, the cable landing station and the main towers."},{"t":"Priority repairs restore the core network and the towers serving hospitals and the emergency operations centre."},{"t":"While full repairs continue, portable cell sites and satellite links give temporary coverage to areas still cut off."},{"t":"Residents contact family, and agencies send verified updates by SMS and social media to counter rumours."},{"t":"Longer term: a review of what went wrong leads to stronger towers, a second cable route and offline-capable apps."}],"win":"Excellent. You sequenced preparation, warning, impact, assessment, priority repair, temporary coverage, communication and long-term resilience."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Policy, security and ethical considerations","html":"<p>This objective also covers <b>policy, security and ethical considerations</b>. These matter more as more of Caribbean life moves onto phones.</p>\n<p><b>Policy</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Data protection laws</b>: for example, Barbados passed a Data Protection Act in 2019 and Jamaica in 2020; Sint Maarten has a personal data protection ordinance. These set rules for collecting, storing and sharing personal data.</li>\n<li><b>Cybercrime and electronic transactions laws</b> that make online payments legally valid and punish fraud and hacking.</li>\n<li><b>Spectrum and licensing</b> for 5G, set by regulators such as BTP, ECTEL member commissions, OUR and TATT.</li>\n<li><b>Universal service</b> funds that pay for coverage in poor or remote areas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Security</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Scams and phishing</b> by SMS and WhatsApp (\"smishing\"): fake bank texts, fake prize messages.</li>\n<li><b>SIM-swap fraud</b>: criminals take over a phone number to intercept bank codes.</li>\n<li>Weak passwords, unsecured public Wi-Fi in hotels and airports, malicious apps from outside official stores.</li>\n<li>Protections: two-factor authentication, app permissions, updates, strong passwords, official stores.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Ethical considerations</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Privacy</b>: apps that collect location and contacts without clear consent.</li>\n<li><b>Misinformation</b>: false hurricane rumours or health claims spreading on WhatsApp.</li>\n<li><b>AI and deepfakes</b>: realistic fake audio or video of local figures.</li>\n<li><b>Digital divide</b>: moving services online can exclude elderly, poor or rural citizens.</li>\n<li><b>Cyberbullying</b> and the wellbeing of young users; screen-time pressure.</li>\n<li><b>Data sovereignty</b>: Caribbean data stored and used by foreign companies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Separate the three words: <b>policy</b> is the rules governments make; <b>security</b> is protecting systems and data from attack; <b>ethics</b> is about what is right and fair, even where no law exists.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>As more of life moves onto phones, three things matter. Policy is the laws governments make, such as Barbados's Data Protection Act of 2019. Security is protecting phones and data from attacks, like scam texts pretending to be from your bank. Ethics is about doing what is right even where there is no law, such as not tracking people's location without asking them first.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The three areas overlap, and examiners reward answers that show it. SIM-swap fraud is a security problem, but it is also a policy issue (how carriers must check identity before issuing a replacement SIM) and an ethical one (who bears the loss). A law can exist without being enforced: a small territory may pass a data protection act but have few staff to investigate complaints. Ethical issues often run ahead of the law, as when deepfake audio of a local politician spreads on WhatsApp before an election. A Grade One answer names the specific risk, identifies who is affected and suggests a response at more than one level: user habits, app design and policy.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Launching a disaster-alert app","intro":"<p>You are advising the Antigua and Barbuda Ready app, which will send hurricane alerts and help residents check in as safe. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The developer wants to collect every user's contacts, live location at all times and photos of their ID. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Collect everything; it might be useful later.","to":"bad1","fb":"Collecting unnecessary personal data breaks privacy principles and creates a security risk."},{"t":"Collect only what is needed, ask for clear consent and follow Antigua and Barbuda's data protection law.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Data minimisation and consent respect privacy law and build trust."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Testing shows that after a storm, networks may be down for hours or days. How should the app handle this?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Store shelter lists, emergency numbers and checklists offline, and send short SMS alerts as well as push notifications.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Offline content and SMS work when data networks are weak."},{"t":"Rely only on a live online map that needs constant data.","to":"bad2","fb":"When networks fail, an online-only app becomes useless exactly when people need it."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>During a storm, a fake message spreads on WhatsApp claiming a shelter has closed. What should the app do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Post verified updates from official sources, clearly marked, and include a \"check before you share\" message.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You tackled misinformation ethically and kept users safe."},{"t":"Ignore it; rumours are not the app's problem.","to":"bad3","fb":"False information in a disaster can put lives at risk. Trusted apps must help correct it."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>A trusted app.</b> Residents rely on Antigua and Barbuda Ready because it respects privacy, works offline and counters misinformation. A Grade One answer would explain how it balances policy, security, ethics and infrastructure limits.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Privacy failure.</b> Users refuse to install the app and the data becomes a target for hackers. Try again and collect only what is necessary.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Silent when needed.</b> After the storm, the app shows only a loading icon. Try again and design for weak connectivity.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Dangerous rumours.</b> Families travel to the wrong shelter. Try again and think about your ethical responsibility.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Data minimisation","p":"<p>Collect only the data the app truly needs.</p>"},{"h":"Networks fail","p":"<p>What still works when the Internet is down?</p>"},{"h":"Truth matters","p":"<p>How can a trusted app respond to false information?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Disaster-alert app (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: the hurricane app must keep working after a storm.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Networks may be down after the storm. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A live map that needs constant data.","to":"bad","fb":"It fails when networks are down."},{"t":"Offline shelter lists and SMS alerts.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. These work when data is down."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You designed for Caribbean connectivity realities.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The app stops working when it is needed most. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Launching a disaster-alert app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief for Antigua and Barbuda Ready, the app that will send hurricane alerts and help residents check in as safe. You must balance privacy, inclusion, infrastructure limits and misinformation. Some choices end the project early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The developer wants to collect every user's contacts, live location at all times and a photo of their ID. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Collect only what the app needs, ask for clear consent for each permission, and use location only when the user checks in or asks for help.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Data minimisation and purpose-limited consent follow data protection principles and build trust."},{"t":"Collect everything; it might be useful later.","to":"bad1","fb":"Collecting unnecessary personal data breaks privacy principles and creates a security risk."},{"t":"Collect everything, but promise to delete it after hurricane season.","to":"ok1","fb":"A promise does not fix over-collection: the data is still a target while it is held, and users may refuse to install."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The government wants the service to reach everyone, including older residents who do not have smartphones. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"SMS alerts that reach any phone, plus a feature that lets family members check in on behalf of a relative.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. You widened the service beyond smartphone users and supported the most vulnerable."},{"t":"Nothing; smartphone users are the target audience.","to":"ok2","fb":"This leaves out some of the people most at risk in a storm."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Networks may be down for days after a storm. How should the app handle this?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Push shelter lists, maps and checklists to phones before the storm, store them offline, and queue check-ins to send when any signal returns.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. The app works with the infrastructure limits instead of against them."},{"t":"Rely on a live online map that needs constant data.","to":"bad2","fb":"When networks fail, an online-only app becomes useless exactly when people need it."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>During the storm, a fake voice note claiming a shelter has closed spreads on WhatsApp. It sounds like a real official. What should the app do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Block every message in the app that mentions shelters until the storm passes.","to":"ok3","fb":"This stops the rumour but also blocks genuine safety information."},{"t":"Post a clearly marked, verified update from the disaster management office, explain how to check official sources, and report the fake to the platform.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You corrected the misinformation from a trusted source and helped users protect themselves next time."},{"t":"Ignore it; rumours are not the app's problem.","to":"bad3","fb":"False information in a disaster can put lives at risk. A trusted app must help correct it."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>A trusted, inclusive app.</b> Residents rely on Antigua and Barbuda Ready because it respects privacy, reaches people without smartphones, works offline and counters misinformation. You balanced policy, security, ethics and infrastructure limits.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Low uptake.</b> Many residents refuse the permissions and never install the app. Try again and collect only what is necessary.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Some left behind.</b> The app works well for smartphone users, but older residents miss the alerts. Try again and think about the digital divide.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Over-correction.</b> The rumour stops, but so do genuine updates about shelters. Try again with a response that corrects rather than silences.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Privacy failure.</b> Users refuse to install the app and the stored data becomes a target for hackers. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Silent when needed.</b> After the storm, the app shows only a loading icon. Try again and design for weak connectivity.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Dangerous rumours.</b> Families travel to the wrong shelter. Try again and think about the app's ethical responsibility.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"The diaspora connection","html":"<p>The Caribbean <b>diaspora</b> is the large community of Caribbean people and their descendants living abroad, in cities such as New York, Miami, Toronto, London, Amsterdam and Rotterdam. For many islands, the number of people abroad is a large share of the population at home. Mobile technology keeps these communities connected.</p>\n<p><b>How mobile bridges the diaspora</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Communication</b>: free WhatsApp calls and video chats replace expensive international calls. A grandmother in Paramaribo can see her grandchildren in Amsterdam every Sunday.</li>\n<li><b>Remittances</b>: mobile money and remittance apps let workers abroad send money home quickly, supporting families and island economies.</li>\n<li><b>News and culture</b>: diaspora audiences follow home newspapers such as De Ware Tijd in Suriname, Caribbean radio streams, Carnival livestreams and Emancipation Day events online.</li>\n<li><b>Music and creators</b>: soca, dancehall and gospel artists build fan bases abroad through YouTube, TikTok and Spotify.</li>\n<li><b>Investment and tourism</b>: diaspora members book holidays home, invest in businesses and buy property online.</li>\n<li><b>Crisis support</b>: after Hurricane Irma, diaspora groups organised fundraising and shared information through social media and messaging apps.</li>\n<li><b>Identity and heritage</b>: apps and pages on Caribbean history, recipes (pom, pepperpot, johnny cakes) and language keep second-generation members connected to their roots.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Global vs Caribbean contrast</b>: many larger countries use mobile mainly for domestic services; in the Caribbean, a big part of mobile's value is <b>cross-border</b>, linking small home populations with large communities overseas.</p>\n<p><b>Opportunity</b>: the diaspora is a ready <b>export market</b> for Caribbean apps and content, from food delivery for relatives at home to online courses in Caribbean cooking or culture.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The diaspora point is a strong way to finish a \"contrast\" essay: it shows a feature that is <b>more important</b> in the Caribbean than in many global markets.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The diaspora means Caribbean people and their families who live abroad, in cities such as New York, Toronto, London and Rotterdam. Phones keep them close to home through free WhatsApp video calls, apps for sending money, island news and Carnival livestreams. A grandmother in Paramaribo can see her grandchildren in Amsterdam every Sunday. The diaspora is also a big market for Caribbean apps and content.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The diaspora makes Caribbean mobile use unusually <b>cross-border</b>. That brings opportunities, such as an export market for food, music and heritage apps and faster remittances, but also challenges: apps must handle several currencies, time zones and payment systems, and transfer fees still take a share of every remittance. There is also a feedback loop: diaspora audiences boost the views and income of Caribbean creators, which in turn funds more local production. In a contrast essay, the diaspora is a strong closing point because it matters far more in small island states, where the population abroad can rival the population at home, than in most large global markets.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Caribbean or global?","intro":"<p>Zara is checking a draft essay by an intern. She wants to highlight sentences that describe features that are MORE typical of the Caribbean context than of large global markets.</p>","q":"Select every sentence that describes a feature MORE typical of the Caribbean mobile ecosystem.","lines":["Remittances sent by mobile from relatives abroad are a large share of some economies.","Many international links depend on a small number of submarine cables.","Large domestic app industries produce most of the apps their citizens use.","Hurricanes regularly damage towers and power, testing network resilience.","Visitors and dual-SIM users push subscriptions above 100 per 100 people.","Dozens of competing cable routes connect the country to the Internet.","Most of the content people consume is produced outside the region.","Huge domestic markets make it easy to fund nationwide 5G quickly."],"correct":[0,1,3,4,6],"win":"Well done. You identified features that set the Caribbean apart: remittances, cable dependence, hurricanes, tourism and dual-SIM use, and the consumption imbalance.","miss":"Look again. Large domestic app industries, many cable routes and huge markets for 5G describe large global economies, not small Caribbean states.","tutor":[{"h":"Size matters","p":"<p>Small populations change what is affordable and what is produced locally.</p>"},{"h":"Think geography","p":"<p>Islands, hurricanes and the sea floor shape Caribbean infrastructure.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Caribbean or global? (Foundation)","q":"Select the features more typical of the Caribbean.","lines":["Hurricanes damage mobile towers.","Huge domestic app industries.","Dependence on a few submarine cables.","Dozens of competing cable routes."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Hurricanes and cable dependence are Caribbean features.","miss":"Huge app industries and many cable routes describe large global markets. Try again."},
 "up": {"title":"Caribbean or global? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Zara has a second, harder draft to check. Some sentences describe features of large global markets; others describe features MORE typical of the Caribbean.</p>","q":"Select every sentence that describes a feature MORE typical of the Caribbean mobile ecosystem.","lines":["A large share of mobile money transfers are cross-border, between relatives abroad and families at home.","Most international traffic passes through one or two cable landing points, so a single fault can isolate the country.","Domestic app stores and payment systems are dominated by national technology giants.","Mobile subscription figures are pushed up by visitors buying local SIM cards.","Local languages such as Papiamentu and Kréyòl have far less app content than English or Spanish.","Operators can spread the cost of a 5G network across tens of millions of customers.","The population living abroad is large compared with the population at home.","Small states coordinate telecoms regulation through a regional body, as ECTEL members do.","Venture capital funds hundreds of new local app startups every year.","Peaks in app use are linked to cruise ship arrivals."],"correct":[0,1,3,4,6,7,9],"win":"Excellent. You identified cross-border money, cable dependence, tourist SIMs, under-served local languages, a large diaspora, regional regulation and cruise-driven peaks as Caribbean features.","miss":"Look again. National technology giants, 5G costs spread over tens of millions of customers and large venture capital funding describe big global markets, not small Caribbean states."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Contrast the Caribbean and global mobile ecosystems","genre":"Structured essay: contrasting the Caribbean and global mobile ecosystems","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">\"Mobile phones are everywhere in the Caribbean, just like the rest of the world. There is no real difference between our mobile ecosystem and the global one.\" (Comment made at a youth tech forum in Port of Spain)</p>","prompt":"<p>With reference to the statement above, <b>contrast</b> the Caribbean mobile ecosystem with the global mobile ecosystem. In your answer, refer to:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>statistics on usage or connectivity (with a source type and year);</li><li>mobile and broadband infrastructure;</li><li>social and economic impact, including at least one Caribbean example;</li><li>content consumption versus content production.</li></ol><p>End with a brief judgement on whether you agree with the statement. (15 marks)</p>","min":280,"max":500,"frame":"<p><b>Introduction:</b> Although mobile phones are common in both ..., the Caribbean ecosystem differs in ...<br><b>Statistics:</b> According to ITU (year), globally ... whereas in the Caribbean ...<br><b>Infrastructure:</b> Large markets ... whereas small islands such as ... depend on ...<br><b>Impact:</b> In the Caribbean, mobile ... for example ...<br><b>Consumption vs production:</b> Caribbean users mainly consume ... because ... As a result ...<br><b>Judgement:</b> I partly agree / disagree because ...</p>","checklist":["I quoted at least one statistic with a source type and year, using an approximate figure.","I used \"whereas\" or \"in contrast\" to compare the two contexts directly.","I discussed submarine cables, 4G/5G or connectivity gaps.","I gave a specific Caribbean example such as Hurricane Irma, remittances or mobile money.","I explained the consumption and production imbalance with a cause and an effect.","I ended with a clear judgement on the statement."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Statistics and infrastructure","marks":5,"look":["itu|gsma|world bank|statcounter","20[12][0-9]","cable|fibre|fiber","4g|5g|lte|coverage","gap|rural|afford|cost"],"min":3,"strong":"You supported your contrast with sourced statistics and clear infrastructure points.","weak":"Quote a figure with its source and year (for example ITU 2025) and contrast infrastructure: Caribbean islands depend on a few submarine cables and 5G rollout is gradual."},{"criterion":"Social and economic impact and Caribbean examples","marks":5,"look":["bank|money|wallet|remittan|payment","government|health|telemedic","irma|hurricane|maria|beryl","sint maarten|st\\.? maarten|jamaica|barbados|bahamas|trinidad|guyana|dominica|grenada|antigua","diaspora|abroad|overseas"],"min":3,"strong":"Your impact points are specific and grounded in Caribbean examples.","weak":"Add specific impacts (mobile money, remittances, e-government, telemedicine) and a named Caribbean case such as Hurricane Irma or the Bahamas Sand Dollar."},{"criterion":"Contrast, analysis and judgement","marks":5,"look":["whereas|in contrast|however|unlike|while","consum","produc|creat|local app|local content","because|as a result|therefore|which means","agree|disagree|conclu|judg"],"min":3,"strong":"You contrasted the contexts directly, analysed causes and effects, and reached a supported judgement.","weak":"Compare both contexts in the same sentence, explain why the consumption imbalance exists and what it causes, and finish with a clear judgement."}],"model":"<p>Although smartphones are common in both the Caribbean and the wider world, the statement is misleading because the Caribbean mobile ecosystem differs in its scale, infrastructure, impact and content.</p><p><b>Statistics.</b> According to ITU (2025), roughly three quarters of the world's population uses the Internet, while about 2.2 billion people remain offline. In the Caribbean, GSMA's 2024 report estimates that about two thirds of the Latin America and Caribbean population used mobile Internet in 2023. Some islands, such as Antigua and Barbuda, show more than 100 subscriptions per 100 people because of dual SIMs and tourists, whereas access is much lower in Haiti and rural Guyana. Caribbean use is therefore high in some places but very uneven.</p><p><b>Infrastructure.</b> Large markets are connected by many submarine cable routes and are rolling out 5G quickly, whereas small islands depend on a few cables, such as TelEm's SMPR-1 link to Puerto Rico, so a single fault can slow an entire country. In the Caribbean, 4G LTE remains the main network and 5G is arriving gradually, because small populations make investment harder to recover. Hurricanes add a risk that many global markets do not face: Hurricane Irma in 2017 knocked out power and towers in Barbuda, Anguilla and Sint Maarten for days.</p><p><b>Social and economic impact.</b> Mobile has a particularly strong impact in the Caribbean because of the diaspora. Remittance apps carry money home, and World Bank data shows remittances were roughly a sixth of Jamaica's GDP in 2024. Digital currencies such as the Bahamas Sand Dollar and mobile wallets promote financial inclusion, while telemedicine helps small islands where specialists are scarce.</p><p><b>Consumption versus production.</b> Caribbean users mainly consume content made abroad, such as Netflix, TikTok and foreign games, because local markets are small and developers lack funding and training. As a result, spending flows to foreign companies and local culture is under-represented, although Caribbean musicians and creators are beginning to reach global audiences.</p><p><b>Judgement.</b> I disagree with the statement. Phones are equally visible, but the Caribbean ecosystem is more fragile, more dependent on foreign content and more shaped by the diaspora, so it needs local apps, more resilient infrastructure and strong data protection policy.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>A \"contrast\" essay must compare <b>both</b> contexts in the same paragraph, ideally in the same sentence with \"whereas\". Describing the world in one paragraph and the Caribbean in another is description, not contrast, and caps your mark. Use approximate figures with a source and year; never invent exact numbers.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Contrast the Caribbean and global mobile ecosystems (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: Caribbean and global mobile ecosystems, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Read the statement again. Answer each part in one or two sentences, using the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Write</b> ONE sentence that uses a statistic with its source and year to compare the world and the Caribbean. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE difference in infrastructure between large countries and small islands. Use \"whereas\". <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> ONE way mobile phones help Caribbean people (money, health or government), with a Caribbean example. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> the difference between content consumption and content production, and give ONE effect on the Caribbean. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Do you agree with the statement? <b>Give</b> ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":100,"max":250,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> According to ITU (2025), ... whereas in the Caribbean ...<br><b>(b)</b> Large countries have ..., whereas small islands such as ... depend on ...<br><b>(c)</b> Mobile phones help Caribbean people to ... For example, in ...<br><b>(d)</b> Consumption means ... Production means ... Because most content is made abroad, ...<br><b>(e)</b> I disagree / agree with the statement because ...</p>","checklist":["My statistic names a source and a year.","I used 'whereas' to compare infrastructure.","I named a Caribbean example (for example remittances in Jamaica or Hurricane Irma).","I explained both consumption and production and gave an effect.","I gave my opinion with a reason."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Statistic with source and year","marks":2,"look":["itu|gsma|world bank|statcounter","20[12][0-9]"],"min":2,"strong":"Your statistic has a source and a year.","weak":"Write the source and the year, for example: According to ITU (2025), about three quarters of the world uses the Internet."},{"criterion":"Infrastructure difference","marks":2,"look":["cable|5g|4g|fibre|fiber|tower|coverage","whereas|but|while"],"min":2,"strong":"You compared infrastructure using a linking word.","weak":"Compare cables or 5G: large countries have many cable routes, whereas small islands depend on a few."},{"criterion":"Impact with a Caribbean example","marks":2,"look":["bank|money|wallet|remittan|health|doctor|telemedic|government|licen|alert","jamaica|sint maarten|st\\.? maarten|bahamas|barbados|irma|sand dollar|haiti|guyana|trinidad|antigua"],"min":2,"strong":"You described an impact with a named Caribbean example.","weak":"Describe one use, such as sending remittances, and name a country, such as Jamaica."},{"criterion":"Consumption and production","marks":2,"look":["consum|watch|use","produc|make|creat","abroad|foreign|money|culture|local"],"min":2,"strong":"You explained both terms and gave an effect.","weak":"Say consumption is using content made by others, production is making it, and that money goes to foreign companies."},{"criterion":"Judgement with a reason","marks":2,"look":["agree|disagree","because"],"min":2,"strong":"You gave a clear opinion with a reason.","weak":"Say whether you agree, then give a reason using 'because'."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> According to ITU (2025), about three quarters of the world's population uses the Internet, whereas in the Caribbean use is very high in places like Antigua and Barbuda but much lower in Haiti.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Large countries have many submarine cable routes, whereas small islands such as Dominica depend on only a few, so one cable fault can slow the whole island.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Mobile phones help Caribbean people to send and receive money quickly. For example, in Jamaica, remittance apps help families receive money from relatives abroad.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Consumption means using content that other people made, such as watching TikTok. Production means making your own content or apps. Because most content is made abroad, money spent on apps goes to foreign companies.</p><p><b>(e)</b> I disagree with the statement because, although phones are everywhere, Caribbean islands have weaker infrastructure and make far fewer of their own apps.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 2 marks per part, 1 for the idea and 1 for the detail (source and year, a 'whereas' comparison, a named example, an effect, a reason). This builds the paragraph pieces of the Core essay. Once students can write each part without starters, ask them to join (a) to (e) into paragraphs.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Contrast the Caribbean and global mobile ecosystems (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended essay: Caribbean and global mobile ecosystems, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Statement A:</b> \"Mobile phones are everywhere in the Caribbean, just like the rest of the world. There is no real difference between our mobile ecosystem and the global one.\" (Comment made at a youth tech forum in Port of Spain)</p><p class=\"passage\"><b>Statement B:</b> \"Any gap that does exist is closing fast. 5G and satellite Internet will soon make island connectivity as good as anywhere.\" (Reply from another forum speaker)</p>","prompt":"<p>Write an essay that <b>evaluates</b> both statements. In your essay:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>contrast</b> the Caribbean and global ecosystems using statistics (with source types and years) and infrastructure; <i>(9 marks)</i></li><li><b>analyse</b> the social and economic impact of mobile in the Caribbean, including the diaspora; <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>discuss</b> content consumption versus production and ONE policy or security consideration; <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>reach a <b>supported judgement</b> on both statements and <b>recommend</b> TWO priorities for Caribbean governments. <i>(6 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":450,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>Introduction:</b> Statement A is ... and Statement B is ... because ...<br><b>Statistics:</b> According to ... (year), ... whereas ... However, this figure ...<br><b>Infrastructure:</b> Whereas large markets ..., small islands such as ... Statement B overlooks ...<br><b>Impact and diaspora:</b> Socially, ... Economically, ... Because the diaspora ...<br><b>Content and policy:</b> Caribbean users mainly consume ... because ... A policy or security issue is ...<br><b>Judgement and priorities:</b> On balance, ... The first priority should be ... because ... The second ...</p>","checklist":["I quoted at least two figures with sources and years, and commented on what one of them does not show.","I contrasted infrastructure directly ('whereas') and tested Statement B's claim about 5G and satellites.","I separated social from economic impact and explained the diaspora's role.","I explained the consumption and production imbalance with a cause, an effect and a solution, plus a policy or security issue.","I judged BOTH statements and recommended two justified priorities.","I stayed within 750 words and used named Caribbean examples throughout."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Statistics with sources and critical use","marks":5,"look":["itu|gsma|world bank|statcounter|sensor tower","20[12][0-9]","whereas|in contrast|unlike|while","sim|subscript|usage gap|coverage gap|regional|average|hide|mislead|does not show"],"min":3,"strong":"Sourced statistics used critically to support the contrast.","weak":"Quote figures with source and year, contrast them directly, and explain what a figure hides, such as subscriptions counting SIMs rather than people."},{"criterion":"Infrastructure and Statement B","marks":4,"look":["cable|fibre|fiber|landing","5g|4g|spectrum","satellite|low.?earth|leo","cost|afford|redundan|hurricane|backhaul|small market"],"min":3,"strong":"You contrasted infrastructure and tested the claim that 5G and satellites will close the gap.","weak":"Contrast cable routes and 5G rollout, then explain why 5G and satellites alone will not close the gap: cost per user, backhaul, spectrum, affordable phones and hurricane risk."},{"criterion":"Social and economic impact, including the diaspora","marks":5,"look":["social","econom","remittan|wallet|bank|sand dollar|jam-dex","diaspora|abroad|overseas","health|telemedic|government|inclusion"],"min":3,"strong":"You separated social and economic impacts and explained the diaspora's role.","weak":"Label impacts as social (inclusion, health) or economic (jobs, remittances), and explain how the diaspora makes Caribbean mobile use cross-border."},{"criterion":"Content production and policy or security","marks":5,"look":["consum","produc|creat|local app|local content","because|as a result|therefore","data protection|privacy|security|scam|sim.?swap|misinformation|deepfake|polic"],"min":3,"strong":"You analysed the imbalance with cause, effect and solution and discussed a policy or security issue.","weak":"Explain why the imbalance exists, what it causes and one solution, then discuss a policy or security issue such as data protection or SIM-swap fraud."},{"criterion":"Judgement and recommendations","marks":6,"look":["statement a|first statement","statement b|second statement","on balance|overall|to a (large|limited) extent|i (dis)?agree|conclu","recommend|priorit|should","because"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced judgement on both statements, with two justified priorities.","weak":"Judge each statement separately, reach an overall conclusion, and recommend two specific priorities with reasons."}],"model":"<p>Statement A is mistaken and Statement B is too optimistic. Smartphones are as visible in Port of Spain as in Toronto, but the Caribbean mobile ecosystem differs from the global one in scale, resilience, impact and ownership of content, and new technology alone will not remove those differences.</p><p><b>Statistics.</b> According to ITU (2025), roughly three quarters of the world's population uses the Internet. GSMA's 2024 report estimates that about two thirds of people in Latin America and the Caribbean used mobile Internet by the end of 2023, but that regional figure is dominated by Brazil and Mexico and says little about Dominica or Montserrat. Within the Caribbean the range is wide: Antigua and Barbuda records more than 100 subscriptions per 100 people, whereas access is far lower in Haiti and rural Guyana. Even Antigua and Barbuda's figure must be read critically, because it counts SIM cards, inflated by dual-SIM use and visitors, rather than connected people.</p><p><b>Infrastructure.</b> Whereas large markets are linked by many submarine cable routes, small islands depend on a few, such as TelEm's SMPR-1 link to Puerto Rico, so one fault can slow a whole country. Statement B overlooks cost and resilience. 5G needs licensed spectrum, fibre links to towers and affordable 5G phones, and small populations make that investment slow to recover. Low-earth-orbit satellites can reach remote areas and act as backup, but they cost more per user than fibre and cannot cheaply carry an island's full traffic. Hurricanes such as Irma in 2017 also showed that towers and power fail together, whatever the network generation.</p><p><b>Impact and the diaspora.</b> Socially, mobile wallets support financial inclusion and telemedicine brings specialists to small islands. Economically, mobile creates jobs and speeds up remittances; World Bank data shows remittances were roughly a sixth of Jamaica's GDP in 2024. Because the diaspora in cities such as New York and Rotterdam is large compared with home populations, Caribbean mobile use is unusually cross-border, which makes remittance, news and heritage apps far more important here than in most large markets.</p><p><b>Content and policy.</b> Caribbean users mainly consume content produced abroad because local markets are small and developers lack funding, training and easy access to payouts. As a result, spending and user data flow to foreign companies. Training programmes, regional funding and local hosting would help, although they will take years. Policy matters too: data protection laws, such as Barbados's 2019 Act, set rules for how personal data is used, but small territories may lack staff to enforce them, leaving users exposed to scams such as SIM-swap fraud.</p><p><b>Judgement.</b> On balance, I disagree with Statement A, because the Caribbean ecosystem is more fragile, more dependent on foreign content and more shaped by the diaspora. I only partly accept Statement B: connectivity is improving, but the gap in resilience and content production is not closing fast. The first priority should be redundant infrastructure, a second cable route and backup power at towers, because every app depends on it. The second should be funding and training for local creators, because producing content keeps money, data and culture at home.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks, maximum 750 words): (a) 9 = statistics 5 (sources, years, direct contrast, critical comment) plus infrastructure 4 (contrast and a test of Statement B). (b) 5 = social and economic impacts labelled, with the diaspora explained. (c) 5 = cause, effect and solution for the imbalance, plus a policy or security issue. (d) 6 = a judgement on each statement, an overall conclusion and two justified priorities. Cap (d) at 3 if only one statement is judged.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M3U4","title":"Benefits of developing local mobile applications","objectives":["Identify local and regional needs that mobile apps can address, including school services, transport, health, agriculture, tourism, emergency services, heritage, culture and small businesses.","Explain how universal app types (bus schedules, restaurant reviews, hotel rankings, popular destinations) become more useful when they are built with local data and local knowledge.","Explain the social and cultural linkages that local apps create through entertainment, news, history and cultural preservation.","Discuss the economic benefits of local app development: job creation, regional entrepreneurship and export potential.","Examine real Caribbean-developed apps and social impact apps for disaster preparedness, community health and charity fundraising."],"videos":[{"title":"Why build apps for local problems?","q":"building mobile apps to solve local community problems developing countries"},{"title":"Caribbean tech and app entrepreneurs","q":"Caribbean app developers tech startups entrepreneurship"},{"title":"Apps for disaster preparedness","q":"disaster preparedness mobile app hurricane alerts how it works"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Why build apps for local needs?","html":"<p>Global apps such as Google Maps, WhatsApp and TikTok are used everywhere, but they are designed for the largest markets. A <b>local mobile application</b> is designed around the needs, data, languages and habits of a particular community, island or region. You need to <b>examine the benefits</b> of developing apps that address <b>local and regional needs</b>. Here is a long list of examples.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Local need</th><th>Caribbean example of an app idea</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>School services</b></td><td>Timetables, homework alerts and bus pick-up times for students at Seagrape Bay Secondary School</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Transportation</b></td><td>Route taxi and minibus times between Kingston, Spanish Town and Portmore</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Agriculture</b></td><td>Farmers in St. Lucia or Guyana checking weather, pest alerts and market prices</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tourism</b></td><td>A cruise visitor guide to Bay Street shops, beaches and taxi fares from Nassau's cruise port</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Disaster preparedness</b>, <b>safety</b></td><td>Hurricane checklists, shelter locations and family check-in during a storm</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Health</b></td><td>Clinic hours, vaccination reminders and dengue prevention tips</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Education</b>, <b>heritage</b></td><td>A Papiamentu language app in Curaçao; an audio tour of Fort Amsterdam in Willemstad</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Entertainment</b>, <b>cultural connection</b></td><td>A Carnival schedule with J'ouvert routes and band line-ups</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Civic services</b></td><td>Reporting a pothole or a streetlight fault, paying a fee online</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Small businesses</b></td><td>Ordering johnny cakes or ribs from the lolos in Simpson Bay</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Why does \"local\" matter? Because local developers <b>know the problem first-hand</b>. A developer in Belize City knows that many routes are run by privately owned buses, that visitors often pay in US dollars, that some residents speak Spanish, Kriol or Garifuna at home, and that the country can lose power and connectivity after a hurricane. A team in California designing for a huge market is unlikely to build those details in.</p>\n<p>This unit covers three content points: benefits of apps for local needs, apps that address universal needs (such as bus schedules), and social and cultural linkages. The list of needs includes school services, safety, civic services, small businesses and heritage.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Examine\" means look closely at the benefits, give evidence or examples and consider limitations. A list of app ideas alone will not earn top marks. For each benefit, say <b>who</b> benefits, <b>how</b>, and give a <b>Caribbean example</b>.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Big global apps are built for huge markets, so they often miss local details. A local app is designed around one community's needs, places, languages and habits. A developer in Belize City knows that many buses have no fixed timetable, that visitors often pay in US dollars and that the country can lose power after a hurricane. That local knowledge makes the app more useful to the people who live in or visit the country.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>\"Examine\" asks you to look at benefits closely and test them. Local knowledge is a real advantage, but it is not automatic: a local app only beats a global one if it has better data (minibus stops, lolo opening hours) and keeps that data up to date. Grade One answers also show that \"local\" can scale. Many island needs are shared across the region, so a solution built for St. Lucia can be adapted for St. Kitts, Antigua or Grenada, turning a small market into a regional one. Finally, avoid treating local and global as enemies: many strong local apps use global building blocks, such as a map service or WhatsApp Business, and add the local layer on top.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which local need does this app meet?","intro":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at the Island Pulse Media, has a whiteboard full of app ideas from clients. Sort each idea by the main local need it addresses.</p>","main":"Which local need does this app idea mainly address?","bins":["Transport and tourism","Health and safety","Agriculture and small business","Culture, heritage and education"],"items":[{"t":"Recorded stories from elders about Emancipation Day traditions.","bin":3,"fb":"Recording oral history preserves heritage and culture for future generations."},{"t":"Push alerts when a hurricane watch is issued, with a list of shelters.","bin":1,"fb":"Warning people and guiding them to safety is a safety and disaster preparedness need."},{"t":"A Dominica farmer checks banana prices and pest alerts before harvest.","bin":2,"fb":"Market prices and pest alerts help farmers earn more: an agriculture need."},{"t":"A walking tour of Basseterre's Circus and Independence Square for cruise passengers with a map of shops.","bin":0,"fb":"Guiding visitors around attractions meets a tourism need."},{"t":"A WhatsApp-style ordering page for a nutmeg-products shop in Grenada.","bin":2,"fb":"Helping a small business take orders online meets a small business need."},{"t":"Live minibus locations between Castries and Gros Islet.","bin":0,"fb":"Helping people move around the island is a transport need."},{"t":"Reminders for a child's vaccinations at the local clinic.","bin":1,"fb":"Vaccination reminders support community health."},{"t":"A game that teaches Papiamentu words to children in Curaçao.","bin":3,"fb":"A language learning game supports education and cultural identity."}],"win":"Well sorted. You linked each app idea to the local need it serves, which is the first step in examining its benefits.","tutor":[{"h":"Ask who it helps","p":"<p>Is the user travelling, staying safe, earning a living, or learning about their culture?</p>"},{"h":"Main purpose","p":"<p>Some apps meet more than one need. Choose the one that best describes the main purpose.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which local need? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Live minibus times.","bin":0,"fb":"Transport."},{"t":"Hurricane shelter alerts.","bin":1,"fb":"Safety and disaster preparedness."},{"t":"Online orders for a lolo.","bin":2,"fb":"Small business."},{"t":"Elders telling heritage stories.","bin":3,"fb":"Culture and heritage."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which local need does this app meet? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Jerome's second whiteboard has ideas from clients across the region. Some touch more than one need, so sort each idea by the <b>main</b> need it addresses.</p>","items":[{"t":"A ferry app that shows crossing times to Anguilla and lets visitors buy tickets.","bin":0,"fb":"Moving people between islands is a transport and tourism need."},{"t":"A check-in feature that tells relatives abroad you are safe after a storm.","bin":1,"fb":"It involves family abroad, but its main purpose is safety after a disaster."},{"t":"An app that lets Bequia fishermen sell the day's catch to restaurants.","bin":2,"fb":"Helping fishers sell their produce is an agriculture and small business need."},{"t":"An app that teaches Grade 4 pupils the story of emancipation in Jamaica in 1838.","bin":3,"fb":"Teaching history to children is a heritage and education need."},{"t":"A map where residents mark stagnant water to help stop dengue mosquitoes breeding.","bin":1,"fb":"Reducing mosquito breeding protects community health."},{"t":"An online shop where St. Lucian carvers sell their work to the diaspora.","bin":2,"fb":"Reaching new buyers is a small business need, even though the buyers live abroad."},{"t":"A Kwéyòl dictionary app with audio from native speakers in St. Lucia.","bin":3,"fb":"Preserving and teaching a language is a cultural and educational need."},{"t":"A clinic queue app that tells patients when the doctor is ready to see them.","bin":1,"fb":"Improving access to care is a health need."},{"t":"A fare checker that shows official taxi fares from the cruise pier.","bin":0,"fb":"Helping visitors get around fairly is a transport and tourism need."},{"t":"An interactive map of Bonaire's historic salt pans and slave huts with audio from elders.","bin":3,"fb":"Telling the story of a historic site is a heritage need."}],"win":"Excellent. You looked past the details in each idea to the main need it serves, which is the starting point for examining its benefits."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Universal apps with a local twist","html":"<p>There are four classic examples of apps that address <b>universal issues and needs</b>: <b>bus schedules</b>, <b>restaurant reviews</b>, <b>hotel rankings</b> and <b>popular destinations</b>. Everyone, everywhere, needs to travel, eat, sleep and find interesting places. These app <b>types</b> are universal, but the most useful version for a community is often a <b>locally relevant</b> one.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Universal app type</th><th>The global version</th><th>What a local version adds</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Bus schedules</td><td>Transit apps that work well in cities with official timetables and open data</td><td>Jamaican route taxis and many Caribbean minibuses run without a fixed timetable, so a local app might use driver check-ins or rider reports. Barbados has a real local example: <b>TransitBB</b>, an iPhone app listed by the developer Leading Edge Technology Solutions, shows routes, timetables and maps for public buses.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Restaurant reviews</td><td>Global review sites list larger, well-known restaurants</td><td>Lolos, doubles vendors, food trucks and roadside stalls that may have no website, with prices in local currency and US dollars and photos of the actual dishes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hotel rankings</td><td>Global booking sites rank big resorts</td><td>Small guesthouses, villas and apartments in Bequia or on Nevis run by local owners, with honest notes about hurricane shutters and generators</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Popular destinations</td><td>Lists of the \"top 10\" sights for tourists</td><td>Hidden beaches, hiking trails up Gros Piton, sargassum updates, cultural events and safety information written by people who live there</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Why local versions add value</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Better data</b>: local developers can gather information that global companies do not have, such as minibus stops, vendor opening hours or which beaches are affected by sargassum this week.</li>\n<li><b>Localisation</b>: language (English, Spanish, Dutch, French, Papiamentu, Kreyòl), currency, place names and local expressions.</li>\n<li><b>Context</b>: features for hurricane season, power outages, cruise ship days and Carnival road closures.</li>\n<li><b>Trust</b>: users may trust a recommendation from a local creator more than an anonymous global ranking.</li>\n<li><b>Visibility for small businesses</b>: local owners who are invisible on global platforms can be found.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Local apps do not always compete with global apps. Many local apps <b>build on</b> global platforms, for example using Google Maps inside a local tour app, or linking to WhatsApp Business for orders.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 likes to test the phrase \"universal yet locally relevant\". The app <b>type</b> is universal (everyone needs bus times); the <b>content and design</b> are local (these routes, these languages, this currency).</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Some app types are universal: everyone needs bus times, places to eat, somewhere to stay and things to see. A local version keeps the same idea but adds local details. A restaurant review app for Trinidad could list the doubles vendors and food trucks, with prices in TT dollars. A bus app could use drivers' check-ins, because many minibuses have no timetable.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The exam phrase is \"universal yet locally relevant\": the <b>need</b> is universal, but the <b>data, language and context</b> are local. The hardest part of a local twist is usually the data, not the code. A global transit app relies on official timetables and open data; where these do not exist, the local developer must collect data through rider reports or driver check-ins, which raises questions about accuracy and about who keeps it updated. Trust is another hidden advantage: a recommendation from a known local creator may carry more weight than an anonymous global ranking. In an answer, name the universal app type, then explain the specific local data or feature that makes it more useful.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Universal need, local twist","intro":"<p>Match each universal app type to the local feature that would make it more useful in Trinidad and Tobago or the wider Caribbean.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Bus schedule app","r":"Rider reports of where the San Fernando maxi taxi is right now"},{"l":"Restaurant review app","r":"Photos, prices and opening hours for the doubles vendors and food trucks"},{"l":"Hotel ranking app","r":"Small family guesthouses in Tobago with notes on generators and shutters"},{"l":"Popular destinations app","r":"A weekly sargassum update for each beach"},{"l":"Event listings app","r":"Carnival road closures, J'ouvert routes and band schedules"},{"l":"Weather app","r":"Hurricane watch and warning stages explained in plain language and several languages"}],"win":"Very good. Each universal app type became more useful once local data and local context were added.","tutor":[{"h":"Same type, local content","p":"<p>Think about what a global version would miss in Trinidad and Tobago.</p>"},{"h":"Seasonal clues","p":"<p>Sargassum, Carnival and hurricane season are local, seasonal details.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Universal need, local twist (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Bus schedule app","r":"Where the minibus is right now"},{"l":"Restaurant review app","r":"Prices and photos for the doubles vendors"},{"l":"Hotel ranking app","r":"Small family guesthouses"},{"l":"Popular destinations app","r":"Weekly sargassum beach updates"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Universal need, local twist (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>A global app fails locally when it misses a local fact. Match each local problem to the feature that solves it in a Trinidad and Tobago version of a universal app.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Many maxi taxis have no published timetable","r":"Driver check-ins and rider reports show where buses are"},{"l":"Doubles vendors have no websites or online menus","r":"Vendors post photos, prices and hours from their own phones"},{"l":"Visitors and residents think in different currencies","r":"Prices shown in both TT dollars and US dollars"},{"l":"Beaches change week to week with sargassum","r":"A beach-by-beach sargassum update"},{"l":"Many households speak Spanish, Hindi or Trinidadian Creole","r":"A language choice offered on first launch"},{"l":"Power and networks can fail for days after a hurricane","r":"Key content stored on the phone for offline use"},{"l":"Carnival closes roads at short notice","r":"Live road-closure alerts on parade days"},{"l":"Family guesthouses are hard to find on global booking sites","r":"Guesthouse listings with notes on generators and shutters"}],"win":"Excellent. You linked each local fact to the feature it demands, which is how you explain why a local version beats a global one."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Social and cultural linkages","html":"<p>The <b>social and cultural linkages</b> are <b>entertainment</b>, <b>news</b>, <b>history</b>, <b>culture</b> and <b>cultural preservation</b>. A linkage is a connection. Local apps connect people to each other, to their community and to their heritage.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Entertainment</b>: local music streaming, Carnival schedules, comedy and storytelling apps give Caribbean artists a stage. Soca, dancehall, zouk and calypso artists can reach fans directly instead of relying only on global algorithms that favour bigger markets.</li>\n<li><b>News</b>: local news apps and news websites optimised for mobile, such as those of newspapers like the Jamaica Gleaner, keep residents informed about elections, power outages, road works and storm updates. Local news builds an informed community and gives the island its own voice.</li>\n<li><b>History</b>: apps can tell the stories of places such as Brimstone Hill Fortress in St. Kitts, the salt pans of Bonaire, or Emancipation in the British Caribbean (1834 and 1838) and in the Dutch Caribbean (1863). Location-based audio tours make history come alive on the spot.</li>\n<li><b>Culture and cultural preservation</b>: recipes for oil down, pepperpot and johnny cakes, recordings of elders, folk songs, and words in Creole languages can be saved before they are lost. An app becomes a <b>digital archive</b> for the next generation.</li>\n<li><b>Diaspora connection</b>: thousands of Caribbean people live in New York, Toronto, London, Amsterdam and Miami. Local apps help them follow home news, support local businesses, send money and stay part of the culture.</li>\n<li><b>Community identity</b>: when young people see their own island, accent and food in an app, it builds pride and shows that Caribbean people are <b>producers</b> of digital content, not only <b>consumers</b>. This links back to the consumption versus production imbalance you met in Unit 3.3.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Cultural preservation</b>: using digital media to record, store and share cultural heritage (language, stories, music, food, crafts, places) so it is protected and passed on.</p>\n<p>There are responsibilities too. Developers must get <b>permission</b> before recording elders or performers, credit the source, respect sacred or private traditions and avoid stereotypes. These link to the ethics and copyright topics in Module 1.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Social and cultural benefits are not the same as economic benefits. If a question asks for social benefits, talk about identity, connection, information and heritage, not jobs and money.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Local apps connect people to each other, to their community and to their heritage. A news app keeps residents informed about power cuts and storms. A Carnival app gives local artists a stage. An audio tour of Brimstone Hill brings history to life. Recording elders' stories, recipes and Creole words saves them for the next generation, and people abroad can stay close to home through these apps too.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Cultural preservation through apps raises questions a Grade One answer should acknowledge. Who owns a recorded story: the elder, the community or the developer? Developers need informed permission, must credit their sources, and should not publish sacred or private traditions without consent. There is also a risk of turning culture into a stereotype for tourists. Social benefits are strongest when the community helps create the content rather than only consuming it, which also tackles the consumption and production imbalance from Unit 3.3. Keep the categories clean: identity, information, heritage and connection are social and cultural benefits; once you mention jobs, sales or income, you have moved to economic benefits.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Social and cultural benefits quick-fire","intro":"<p>Keisha, the marketing lead at Island Pulse Media, is preparing a pitch for a heritage app. Help her choose the best answers.</p>","items":[{"text":"An app records elders in Sauteurs telling stories about traditional life. The main benefit is...","options":["export potential","cultural preservation","lower data use"],"answer":1,"fb":"Recording stories protects heritage and passes it on: cultural preservation."},{"text":"A Grenadian living in New York uses a local app to follow island news and order a gift from a St. George's shop. This shows...","options":["beta testing","a diaspora connection","a touch target"],"answer":1,"fb":"Mobile apps keep the diaspora linked to home, socially and economically."},{"text":"A local news app sends an alert about a power outage in Grenville. This is mainly a...","options":["cultural preservation linkage","news and information linkage","revenue model"],"answer":1,"fb":"Timely local news keeps the community informed, a social benefit."},{"text":"Which is a SOCIAL benefit rather than an economic benefit?","options":["The developer hires two staff","The app is sold to clients in Belize","Young people feel pride seeing their culture in an app"],"answer":2,"fb":"Pride and identity are social benefits. Jobs and sales are economic benefits."},{"text":"Before publishing recordings of a Carnival band's music, the developer must...","options":["publish them first and ask later","get permission and credit the band","compress the files to MP3 only"],"answer":1,"fb":"Cultural content still belongs to its creators. Permission and credit are ethical and legal duties."},{"text":"An audio tour plays the history of Fort George when the user arrives there. This links users to...","options":["entertainment only","agriculture","history"],"answer":2,"fb":"Location-based stories connect users to local history at the place it happened."}],"win":"Excellent. You can separate social and cultural benefits (identity, information, heritage, connection) from economic ones.","tutor":[{"h":"Social or economic?","p":"<p>Money, jobs and sales are economic. Identity, information and heritage are social and cultural.</p>"},{"h":"Preservation","p":"<p>Recording and storing culture so it is not lost is preservation.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Social and cultural benefits (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Recording elders' stories in an app is...","options":["export","cultural preservation"],"answer":1,"fb":"It protects heritage."},{"text":"A Grenadian abroad reading home news shows a...","options":["diaspora connection","battery problem"],"answer":0,"fb":"Apps link the diaspora to home."},{"text":"Pride in local culture is a...","options":["social benefit","economic benefit"],"answer":0,"fb":"Identity and pride are social."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Social and cultural benefits quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Keisha's heritage app pitch is going to a panel of elders, artists and officials. Choose the BEST answer to each of their questions.</p>","items":[{"text":"The team wants to record a spiritual ceremony in a rural community. What is the MOST appropriate first step?","options":["Record it quietly so the ceremony looks natural","Publish a short clip first to test interest","Ask community leaders for informed permission and agree what may be shared publicly","Use stock video of a similar ceremony instead"],"answer":2,"fb":"Cultural content belongs to its community. Informed permission and agreed limits come before any recording."},{"text":"Which statement BEST explains a social benefit of a local news app compared with a global news feed?","options":["It uses less battery","It informs residents about local events such as power outages and road works, helping them plan and take part in community life","It shows more international celebrity news","It earns more advertising money"],"answer":1,"fb":"Local information supports an informed, connected community. Advertising income would be an economic point."},{"text":"A Carnival app features only soca stars who already have huge global followings. Which social benefit is it MISSING?","options":["Export potential","Faster loading","Giving a stage to emerging local artists","Lower data use"],"answer":2,"fb":"A local app can help artists who are overlooked by global algorithms. Featuring only established stars misses that benefit."},{"text":"Keisha's draft says: \"The heritage app will create jobs for voice artists, which is a social benefit.\" What is wrong with it?","options":["Voice artists are not needed in heritage apps","It should say 'cultural preservation' instead","Job creation is an economic benefit, so the point is mislabelled","Nothing; jobs are a social benefit"],"answer":2,"fb":"Jobs and income are economic benefits. Mislabelling a category is a common way to lose marks."},{"text":"A cultural app shows Caribbean people only as dancers and beach vendors serving tourists. Which risk does this show?","options":["Fragmentation","Data loss","Stereotyping culture instead of representing it fairly","Sideloading"],"answer":2,"fb":"Reducing a culture to tourist images is stereotyping. Involving the community in creating content helps prevent it."},{"text":"Which design choice would MOST strengthen the app's diaspora connection?","options":["Hide the content from users outside the island","Let users abroad listen, add family stories and support local businesses from overseas","Remove the English version","Charge users abroad a higher price"],"answer":1,"fb":"Two-way participation keeps the diaspora involved, not just informed."}],"win":"Excellent. You evaluated permission, fair representation, local voices and diaspora participation, and kept social benefits separate from economic ones."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Economic benefits: jobs, entrepreneurship and export","html":"<p>The course outline lists three economic benefits of local app development. Learn them with examples.</p>\n<p><b>1. Local job creation</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Direct jobs</b>: developers, UI/UX designers, testers, content writers, photographers and marketers like the team at Island Pulse Media.</li>\n<li><b>Indirect jobs</b>: delivery riders for a food-ordering app, drivers for a rideshare app, customer support staff, and tour guides featured in a tourism app.</li>\n<li><b>Skills</b>: young people gain digital skills that keep them employable and reduce the \"brain drain\" to North America and Europe.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2. Regional entrepreneurship</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apps let small businesses <b>reach more customers</b> at low cost: a lolo in Simpson Bay can take orders before customers arrive.</li>\n<li>Start-ups can grow from one island to the region. A Caribbean app that works in Barbados can be <b>adapted</b> for St. Kitts, Antigua or Grenada, which share many similar needs.</li>\n<li>Local app developers keep <b>money circulating in the local economy</b> instead of paying commissions to overseas platforms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>3. Export potential</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>An app is a <b>digital export</b>: it can be sold or licensed abroad without shipping costs. A Caribbean team could license a hurricane preparedness app to other islands or to Pacific nations with similar storms.</li>\n<li><b>White-label</b> apps (Unit 3.8) let a developer resell the same app under different brands.</li>\n<li>Exporting digital services earns <b>foreign exchange</b> and diversifies small economies that depend heavily on tourism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Other economic benefits</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Efficiency</b>: e-government and mobile payments save time and travel. In Guyana, the <b>MMG</b> (Mobile Money Guyana) service from the telecoms company GTT has been used, for example, to let University of Guyana students pay fees from their phones.</li>\n<li><b>Financial inclusion</b>: mobile wallets can serve people without bank accounts.</li>\n<li><b>Tourism spending</b>: apps that guide visitors to local shops and restaurants spread cruise and hotel spending more widely.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Limitations to mention when you examine</b>: small populations mean small markets; development and maintenance cost money; some users have limited data; and local apps must compete with free global apps. A balanced answer acknowledges these and suggests solutions, such as regional expansion or grant funding.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Grade One answers name the <b>stakeholder</b>: \"The app benefits <b>local vendors</b> because...\", \"It benefits <b>the government</b> because...\". Each stakeholder is a fresh point.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Local apps bring money and jobs. They create direct jobs for developers, designers and marketers, and indirect jobs such as delivery riders. They help small businesses, like a lolo in Simpson Bay, reach more customers. An app can also be sold or licensed to other countries without shipping anything, which is called export potential. For example, a hurricane app built in St. Kitts could be licensed to Grenada.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Examine the conditions behind each benefit. Job creation depends on demand: a food-ordering app only creates delivery jobs if enough customers order. \"Keeping money local\" depends on where the servers, payment providers and app store commission sit, because part of every sale may still leave the region. Export potential is real but needs adaptation, support and marketing in each new territory. Grade One answers also link economic benefits to wider goals, such as diversifying economies that depend heavily on tourism and reducing the brain drain by giving young developers careers at home. Name the stakeholder for each point, add a limitation, and suggest how to overcome it, for example through regional expansion or grant funding.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Identifying an economic benefit","q":"St. Kitts start-up NevisCode builds a hurricane-preparedness app for its own island. Two years later it licenses the same app, rebranded, to disaster offices in Dominica and Grenada. Which economic benefit does this BEST illustrate?","options":[{"t":"Local job creation only","fb":"The business may hire staff, but the key event in the scenario is licensing the product to other countries."},{"t":"Cultural preservation","fb":"Cultural preservation is a social and cultural benefit about protecting heritage. The scenario is about selling the app abroad."},{"t":"Export potential","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Selling or licensing the app to other countries is a digital export that earns income from outside St. Kitts."},{"t":"Offline-first design","fb":"Offline-first is a user experience design strategy (Unit 3.5), not an economic benefit."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Find the clue","p":"<p>\"Licenses the same app ... to disaster offices in Dominica and Grenada.\"</p>"},{"h":"Inside or outside?","p":"<p>Is the income coming from inside the island or from other countries?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Identifying an economic benefit (Foundation)","q":"A Caribbean app developer sells the same app to clients in other countries. This is an example of...","options":[{"t":"cultural preservation","fb":"That is about protecting heritage."},{"t":"screen readability","fb":"That is a design factor."},{"t":"export potential","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Selling abroad is a digital export."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Identifying an economic benefit (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A Ministry report on LoloGo lists four points: (1) it employs 12 part-time delivery riders; (2) lolos keep more of each sale than they would on an overseas delivery platform; (3) its code is licensed to a food-stall association in Antigua; (4) it records the history of each traditional dish. Which judgement about the report is MOST accurate?","options":[{"t":"Points 1, 2 and 3 are economic benefits and point 4 is a cultural benefit, so the report should present them separately.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Jobs, money kept locally and licensing abroad are economic; recording dish history is cultural preservation."},{"t":"Only point 3 is an economic benefit, because export is the only economic benefit of an app.","fb":"Jobs and keeping money in the local economy are economic benefits too."},{"t":"Points 1 and 4 are social benefits, and points 2 and 3 are economic.","fb":"Employment is an economic benefit, not a social one."},{"t":"All four points are economic benefits, because they all come from the same app.","fb":"Point 4 is about heritage, so it is a cultural benefit. Coming from the same app does not make every benefit economic."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Case studies: Caribbean-developed apps","html":"<p>These are real Caribbean digital solutions you can research further. Details change quickly, so always check the latest official information before quoting in the exam or SBA.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>App or service</th><th>Where and who</th><th>Local need</th><th>Benefits to examine</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Lynk</b></td><td>Jamaica; a digital wallet from the fintech arm of NCB Financial Group, announced in late 2021</td><td>Sending and receiving money by phone, including for people without bank accounts; small merchants such as food vendors</td><td>Financial inclusion, convenience, local fintech jobs</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>WiPay</b></td><td>Trinidad and Tobago; a fintech company founded in 2016, based in Port of Spain</td><td>Online payments where many people lack credit cards; invoicing and e-commerce for small businesses</td><td>Regional entrepreneurship, expansion to other Caribbean countries (export)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>MMG</b> (Mobile Money Guyana)</td><td>Guyana; a mobile money service linked to the telecoms company GTT</td><td>Paying bills and fees from a phone, used for example for University of Guyana fees</td><td>Saves travel and queuing time, supports e-government</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>TransitBB</b></td><td>Barbados; an iPhone app by Leading Edge Technology Solutions</td><td>Bus routes, timetables and maps</td><td>A universal need served with local data</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>My Saint Martin</b></td><td>French side of the island; launched by the Collectivité in 2018, developed by the company KSI</td><td>News, public service schedules, a job directory, emergency and health information, and alerts including hurricane phases</td><td>Civic information, community participation, disaster preparedness</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>TTRideShare</b></td><td>Trinidad and Tobago; describes itself as a local rideshare app</td><td>On-demand car service</td><td>Income for local drivers, local competitor to global apps</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Related, but not an app</b>: in 2025 the Government of Sint Maarten signed an agreement with BTP, Flow and TelEm to set up a <b>Cell Broadcast</b> emergency alert system that sends warnings to all phones in an area without needing an app or the Internet. This is a useful comparison: an app can give detailed checklists and maps, but a cell broadcast reaches everyone instantly.</p>\n<p><b>A course example</b>: <b>LoloGo</b> is a food-ordering app for the lolos of Sint Maarten, used throughout this course as an SBA example. Customers browse menus, order ribs, johnny cakes or saltfish, and pick up without queuing. Benefits: more sales for small vendors, less waiting on busy cruise days, local delivery jobs, and a model that could be adapted for food stalls in Grenada or Antigua.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In an essay, one well-explained case study is worth more than five names. Use the pattern: <b>name</b>, <b>country</b>, <b>need it meets</b>, <b>benefit</b>, <b>limitation</b>. Never invent statistics about a real app.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Real Caribbean apps show the benefits in action. Lynk in Jamaica lets people send and receive money by phone, even without a bank account. WiPay in Trinidad and Tobago helps small businesses take online payments. MMG in Guyana lets people pay bills and fees from their phones. TransitBB shows bus routes in Barbados. Each one meets a local need that global apps did not fully serve.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Use case studies as evidence, not decoration. The strongest pattern is: name, country, the need it meets, the benefit and a limitation. For example, a digital wallet supports financial inclusion, but only if merchants accept it and users trust it. Compare approaches, too. Sint Maarten's Cell Broadcast agreement shows that some problems are better solved without an app: a broadcast reaches every phone in an area instantly, while an app offers depth (maps, checklists) only to people who installed it. Never invent figures about a real app; if you do not know how many users it has, describe the benefit in words. Details change quickly, so check official sources before quoting.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the economic benefits","intro":"<p>Jerome drafted this pitch for the LoloGo app. Some lines describe economic benefits; others describe social benefits or design features.</p>","q":"Select every line that describes an ECONOMIC benefit.","lines":["Lolo owners can take more orders on busy cruise ship days.","The app will create part-time delivery jobs for young people.","Menus will be available in English, Spanish and Dutch.","The app could be licensed to food stalls in Grenada and Antigua.","Customers will see photos of traditional dishes and their history.","Buttons will be at least 48 dp tall for easy tapping.","Payments stay with local businesses instead of overseas platforms.","Elders share recipes for johnny cakes in short videos."],"correct":[0,1,3,6],"win":"Correct. Sales, jobs, export licensing and keeping money in the local economy are economic benefits.","miss":"Check again: languages and button sizes are design features, while dish history and recipes are cultural benefits. Look for money, jobs and sales.","tutor":[{"h":"Follow the money","p":"<p>Does the line involve income, jobs, sales or trade?</p>"},{"h":"Design is not a benefit category","p":"<p>Button size and languages are how the app is designed, not an economic outcome.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the economic benefits (Foundation)","q":"Select the lines that describe an ECONOMIC benefit.","lines":["The app creates delivery jobs.","Elders share recipes in videos.","The app is licensed to stalls in Grenada.","Buttons are large and easy to tap."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Jobs and licensing abroad are economic.","miss":"Look for jobs and income."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the economic benefits (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Jerome's final LoloGo pitch mixes economic benefits with social benefits and design features. Some economic points are less obvious.</p>","q":"Select every line that describes an ECONOMIC benefit.","lines":["Vendors keep more of each sale than they would on an overseas delivery platform.","Audio stories help children in the diaspora learn the history of each dish.","Visitor spending spreads beyond Front Street to lolos in Simpson Bay and Cole Bay.","Young developers gain skills that let them build careers at home instead of emigrating.","Hurricane checklists are stored offline on the phone.","A white-label version could be resold to vendors' associations on other islands.","Residents feel proud to see their own food and culture in an app.","Online ordering saves customers and vendors time on busy cruise days.","Buttons use high contrast so they can be read in sunlight.","Payments through regional providers keep transaction fees in the Caribbean."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7,9],"win":"Excellent. You spotted the less obvious economic benefits too: spreading visitor spending, keeping skilled young people employed at home, efficiency and keeping fees in the region.","miss":"Look again. Dish history and pride are social and cultural benefits; offline checklists and high-contrast buttons are design features. Efficiency, skills, export and money kept in the region are all economic."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Social impact apps","html":"<p>A <b>social impact app</b> is built mainly to improve lives and communities rather than to make a profit. There are three main types below; social impact apps can also serve <b>safety</b>, <b>education</b> and <b>public services</b>.</p>\n<p><b>1. Disaster preparedness</b></p>\n<p>Hurricane Irma in September 2017 showed how fragile communication can be: power, mobile networks and roads were damaged across Sint Maarten, St. Martin, Anguilla and the BVI. A good preparedness app is designed for those conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>checklists, shelter lists and emergency numbers <b>stored on the phone</b> so they work offline;</li>\n<li>alerts before the storm and a simple \"I am safe\" check-in for families and the diaspora afterwards;</li>\n<li>maps of shelters and water distribution points;</li>\n<li>information in several languages and in plain, simple words;</li>\n<li>a low battery mode, because people may not have power for days.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2. Community health</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Clinic locations and hours, vaccination reminders, medication reminders.</li>\n<li>Mosquito and dengue alerts, healthy eating tips using local foods, and mental health support lines.</li>\n<li>Privacy is vital: health data is sensitive and must be protected (see the data protection topics in Unit 3.9).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>3. Charity fundraising</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>After a disaster, donation apps and pages let the diaspora send help quickly.</li>\n<li>Local charities, churches and school fundraisers can collect donations, sell raffle tickets or sign up volunteers.</li>\n<li>Transparency matters: show donors where the money goes to build trust.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Challenges to examine</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sustainability</b>: who pays for updates and servers after the project ends? Options include grants, sponsorship and government partnerships (Unit 3.8).</li>\n<li><b>Reach</b>: an app must be downloaded first, and not everyone has a smartphone or data. That is why a cell broadcast alert system or SMS can complement an app.</li>\n<li><b>Accuracy</b>: wrong information in an emergency can cost lives, so content must come from official sources such as the Office of Disaster Management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your Unit 2 SBA should be a product or service, preferably local, social or entrepreneurial. A social impact app concept is a strong choice because the <b>need</b> and the <b>benefit</b> are easy to justify in your proposal.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A social impact app is made mainly to help people, not to make a profit. Examples include hurricane preparedness apps with offline checklists and shelter maps, health apps with clinic hours and vaccination reminders, and charity apps that let the diaspora send donations after a disaster. Hurricane Irma in 2017 showed why such apps must work even when power and networks are down.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The hardest question for a social impact app is not \"will it help?\" but \"will it still work in three years?\" Many civic apps launch with one grant and then decay, leaving outdated shelter lists that are worse than no app at all. Plan funding (government partnership, sponsorship, donations) from the start. Reach is the second limit: an app only helps people who installed it, so it should complement SMS and cell broadcast alerts, not replace them. Third, accuracy and privacy carry higher stakes: emergency content must come from official sources, and health data needs strong protection. A Grade One answer judges a social impact app on three tests: reliable in a crisis, inclusive and sustainable.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Designing a storm-ready app","intro":"<p>The Turks and Caicos charity Rebuild Together has asked Island Pulse Media to plan a disaster preparedness app. You are advising Jerome. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The charity wants the app to load all its content live from the Internet so information is always fresh. What do you advise first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Agree: live content is always best, and everyone has Internet.","to":"bad1","fb":"After Irma, many people had no power or signal for days. A live-only app fails exactly when it is needed."},{"t":"Store the essential content (checklists, shelters, emergency numbers) on the phone so it works offline, and update it when there is a connection.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. After a hurricane, networks may be down. Essential content must work offline."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Good. Next, who should the content be written for?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Residents and visitors in English, Spanish, Dutch and French, in plain language, with official information from the Office of Disaster Management.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Multiple languages and official sources make the app inclusive and accurate."},{"t":"English only, using technical meteorological terms to sound professional.","to":"bad2","fb":"Many households speak other languages at home, and technical terms confuse people in a crisis."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The charity asks how to keep the app running for years, since it has only one grant. What do you suggest?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Plan sustainability: partner with the government and telecoms providers, seek sponsorship and add a donation feature for the diaspora.","to":"good","fb":"Strong. Sustainability planning means the app survives beyond the first grant."},{"t":"Spend the whole grant on launch and worry about updates later.","to":"bad3","fb":"An outdated shelter list is dangerous. Social impact apps need a plan for long-term maintenance."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The app works offline, speaks the community's languages, uses official information and has a funding plan. A Grade One answer would explain that a social impact app must be <b>reliable in a crisis</b>, <b>inclusive</b> and <b>sustainable</b>.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>App fails in the storm.</b> When the network goes down, the app shows only a loading icon. Try again and design for offline use.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>People left out.</b> Spanish- and Kreyòl-speaking families cannot follow the instructions. Try again and design for the whole community.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Abandoned app.</b> By the next hurricane season the shelter list is wrong and the app crashes on new phones. Try again and plan for sustainability.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"When is it used?","p":"<p>Think about the conditions during and after a hurricane.</p>"},{"h":"Who uses it?","p":"<p>The Turks and Caicos Islands are multilingual. Who might be left out?</p>"},{"h":"After launch","p":"<p>Who keeps the information correct next year?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Designing a storm-ready app (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: plan a hurricane app for the Turks and Caicos Islands.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Where should the shelter list and emergency numbers be stored?</p>","choices":[{"t":"On the phone, so they work offline.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Networks may fail after a storm."},{"t":"Only online.","to":"bad","fb":"If the network is down, users see nothing."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> The app works when it is needed most.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The app fails during the storm. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Designing a storm-ready app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief from Rebuild Together. The government is also introducing Cell Broadcast alerts, which reach every phone in an area without an app. Weigh each choice; some end the project early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The charity wants all content to load live from the Internet so it is always fresh. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Store essential content on the phone, sync updates whenever there is a connection, and show the date each item was last updated.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Offline storage keeps the app working, and the update date shows users how fresh the information is."},{"t":"Keep it live-only, but add a 'retry' button for when the network is down.","to":"ok1","fb":"A retry button does not help if the network is down for days."},{"t":"Agree: live content is always best, and everyone has Internet.","to":"bad1","fb":"After Irma, many people had no power or signal for days."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Who is the content written for, and where does it come from?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Residents and visitors in English, Haitian Creole and Spanish, in plain language, with every alert taken from the Office of Disaster Management.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. The app is inclusive and its information is accurate and official."},{"t":"Whatever volunteers post on social media, so updates arrive quickly.","to":"bad2","fb":"Unchecked posts can spread wrong information in an emergency."},{"t":"English only, with icons so that everyone can follow it.","to":"ok2","fb":"Icons help, but complex instructions such as evacuation steps still need the household's language."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The charity asks: \"If Cell Broadcast reaches every phone, is the app still worth building?\" What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Yes, as a complement: Cell Broadcast gives the instant warning, while the app adds offline checklists, shelter maps and a family check-in.","to":"n4","fb":"Strong. You identified what each channel does best and how they work together."},{"t":"No. Cancel the app, because Cell Broadcast does everything.","to":"ok3","fb":"A broadcast gives a short warning, but it cannot hold checklists, maps or check-ins."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The charity has only one grant. How do you keep the app running for years?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Spend the whole grant on launch features and plan updates later.","to":"bad3","fb":"Without funding for updates, the information will go out of date."},{"t":"Charge residents a yearly subscription.","to":"ok4","fb":"This funds the app but excludes the residents who most need it."},{"t":"A sustainability plan: a government partnership, sponsorship from telecoms providers, a diaspora donation feature, and a named team that checks shelter lists before every season.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Funding and responsibility for updates are both planned."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Storm-ready and sustainable.</b> The app works offline, speaks the community's languages, uses official information, complements Cell Broadcast and has funding and a team to keep it current. It passes all three tests: reliable in a crisis, inclusive and sustainable.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Still fails in the storm.</b> Users tap 'retry' for days with no result. Try again and store essential content on the phone.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Partly inclusive.</b> Many families follow the icons, but some miss key instructions. Try again and support the languages people speak at home.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>A gap remains.</b> Residents get the warning, but not the checklists, maps or family check-in. Try again and think about what each channel does best.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>Funded but exclusive.</b> The app survives, but many low-income households do not subscribe. Try again with funding that keeps it free.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>App fails in the storm.</b> The app shows only a loading icon when the network is down. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Dangerous confusion.</b> A false post sends families to a closed shelter. Try again with official sources.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Abandoned app.</b> By the next season the shelter list is wrong and the app crashes on new phones. Try again and plan for sustainability.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Writing an \"examine the benefits\" answer","html":"<p>Paper 02 Module 3 questions often ask you to <b>examine</b>, <b>explain</b> or <b>discuss</b> the benefits of local apps. Use a clear structure so the examiner can award each mark.</p>\n<p><b>The BASE paragraph</b> (one paragraph per benefit)</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>B</b>enefit: state it in one sentence. \"A local app creates jobs.\"</li>\n<li><b>A</b>pplication: link it to the scenario or a Caribbean example. \"LoloGo would need delivery riders in Simpson Bay and Cole Bay.\"</li>\n<li><b>S</b>takeholder: say who gains and how. \"Young people earn income and gain customer service skills.\"</li>\n<li><b>E</b>valuation: add a limitation or condition. \"However, the number of jobs depends on enough orders, so the app must be marketed well.\"</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Cover the three benefit categories</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Category</th><th>Typical points</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meeting local needs</td><td>Local data, languages, currency, context (hurricanes, cruise days); fills gaps global apps ignore</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Social and cultural</td><td>Information, community identity, heritage preservation, diaspora connection, inclusion</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Economic</td><td>Jobs, entrepreneurship, keeping money local, export and licensing, efficiency</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Command words in this unit</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Identify</b>: name it briefly. \"Transport.\"</li>\n<li><b>Explain</b>: give the reason or how it works, using \"because\" or \"so that\".</li>\n<li><b>Examine</b>: explain in detail with examples, and consider limitations.</li>\n<li><b>Discuss</b>: present benefits and limitations and reach a conclusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Common Paper 01 traps</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Confusing a <b>benefit</b> (an outcome for people) with a <b>feature</b> (what the app does). \"Offline maps\" is a feature; \"residents can find shelter when networks are down\" is the benefit.</li>\n<li>Calling job creation a social benefit. It is economic.</li>\n<li>Assuming that a local app must be built from scratch. Local apps can build on global platforms and tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For a 6-mark \"examine\" part, aim for three developed BASE paragraphs, each with a different stakeholder: the users, the local businesses and the wider community or economy.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Use the BASE pattern for each benefit. <b>B</b>: state the Benefit. <b>A</b>: Apply it to the scenario or a Caribbean example. <b>S</b>: say which Stakeholder gains and how. <b>E</b>: Evaluate with a limitation, starting \"However ...\". Write one BASE paragraph for each benefit, using a different stakeholder each time, such as users, local businesses and the wider economy.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Most lost marks come from three habits. First, describing a <b>feature</b> instead of a <b>benefit</b>: \"offline maps\" is a feature, while \"residents can find shelter when networks are down\" is the benefit. Second, mislabelling categories, such as calling job creation a social benefit. Third, stopping at the benefit with no evaluation. The E in BASE is where Grade One answers stand out: a limitation (\"this depends on enough orders\"), a condition (\"if vendors update their menus\") or a comparison (\"more accurate than global apps because ...\"), followed by a short judgement. Match length to marks: a 6-mark \"examine\" part usually needs three developed paragraphs, not six short points.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Build a BASE paragraph","intro":"<p>Put the sentences in the best order to make one developed paragraph examining a benefit of the LoloGo app.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"One key benefit of a local food-ordering app is that it increases sales for small businesses."},{"t":"For example, LoloGo lets customers order ribs and johnny cakes from Simpson Bay lolos before they arrive."},{"t":"This helps lolo owners because they can serve more customers on busy cruise ship days without long queues."},{"t":"It also keeps money in the local economy instead of paying high commissions to overseas delivery platforms."},{"t":"However, this benefit depends on vendors keeping their menus updated and on customers trusting the app."},{"t":"Overall, the app supports local entrepreneurship if it is supported with training for vendors."}],"win":"Well ordered. Benefit, example, stakeholder impact, extension, limitation, then a judgement: that is how to examine a benefit.","tutor":[{"h":"Start with the claim","p":"<p>Which sentence states the benefit in general terms?</p>"},{"h":"Signal words","p":"<p>\"For example\", \"This helps\", \"It also\", \"However\" and \"Overall\" show the order.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Build a BASE paragraph (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four sentences in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"A local app increases sales for small businesses."},{"t":"For example, LoloGo lets customers order from Simpson Bay lolos."},{"t":"This helps owners serve more customers on cruise days."},{"t":"However, vendors must keep menus updated."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Build a BASE paragraph (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>This paragraph examines a harder benefit, export potential, and weighs it against other benefits. Put all eight sentences in the best order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"A further benefit of LoloGo is its export potential, which could earn income from outside Sint Maarten."},{"t":"Because many Caribbean islands have similar roadside food vendors, the app could be licensed as a white-label product to food-stall associations in Grenada or Antigua."},{"t":"This would benefit the developer, who earns licensing fees without building a new app each time."},{"t":"It would also help to diversify Sint Maarten's economy, which depends heavily on tourism."},{"t":"However, those islands use a different currency, the EC dollar, and have their own payment providers and menus, so the app would need localising first."},{"t":"The developer would also face competition from global delivery platforms in larger markets."},{"t":"Therefore, export potential is realistic only if the app is designed from the start to be easily adapted."},{"t":"On balance, it is a valuable but longer-term benefit compared with immediate gains such as extra sales for local vendors."}],"win":"Excellent. Benefit, application, two stakeholders, two limitations, a condition and a weighed judgement: a fully developed 'examine' paragraph."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Examine the benefits of a local app","genre":"Structured response: examining the benefits of local mobile applications","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Ministry responsible for tourism and culture in Jamaica is considering funding a local mobile app called \"Island Roots\". It would combine a route taxi and minibus guide, a directory of local restaurants and cookshops, and short audio stories about the island's history and Emancipation Day traditions. Some officials argue that global apps such as Google Maps and review websites already do the job.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a response for the Ministry that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>identifies <b>two</b> local needs the app would address;</li><li>explains <b>one</b> social or cultural benefit of the app;</li><li>examines <b>two</b> economic benefits of developing the app locally;</li><li>explains why a locally developed app could be more useful than global apps, giving <b>one</b> limitation the Ministry should plan for.</li></ol>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The app addresses the need for ... and the need for ...<br><b>(b)</b> A social or cultural benefit is ... because ... For example, ...<br><b>(c)</b> First, ... This benefits ... However, ... Second, ...<br><b>(d)</b> A local app is more useful than global apps because ... One limitation is ... so the Ministry should ...</p>","checklist":["I identified two different local needs.","I explained a social or cultural benefit with a Jamaican example.","I examined two economic benefits and named who gains.","I compared the local app with global apps.","I gave a limitation and a way to address it.","I used terms such as localisation, cultural preservation, entrepreneurship and export."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Local needs and social or cultural benefits","marks":4,"look":["transport|minibus|bus","tourism|visitor|restaurant|lolo|cookshop|jerk","heritage|histor|cultur|emancipation","preserv|identity|pride|diaspora"],"min":3,"strong":"You identified local needs clearly and explained a social or cultural benefit. Next step: add a specific place or tradition.","weak":"Name the needs (transport, tourism, small business, heritage) and explain a social benefit such as cultural preservation or community identity."},{"criterion":"Economic benefits examined","marks":5,"look":["job|employ","entrepreneur|small business|vendor","export|licens|region","econom|income|revenue|money","however|but|although|depend"],"min":3,"strong":"Your economic benefits are developed with stakeholders and a limitation. Excellent examining.","weak":"Examine at least two economic benefits, such as job creation and export potential. Say who gains and add a \"however\" point."},{"criterion":"Comparison with global apps and evaluation","marks":4,"look":["global|google|international","local data|local knowledge|localis|language|currency","limitation|challenge|cost|maint|sustain","should|recommend|plan"],"min":2,"strong":"You explained what local apps add and gave a realistic limitation with a solution.","weak":"Explain what a local app adds (local data, languages, context) and suggest how to deal with a limitation such as maintenance costs or a small market."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Island Roots addresses the need for <b>transport information</b>, because many route taxi and minibus routes in Jamaica run without published timetables, and the need for <b>tourism and small business visibility</b>, because many cookshops, jerk centres and family restaurants do not appear on global review sites.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> A key cultural benefit is <b>cultural preservation</b>. Short audio stories from elders about Emancipation Day and life in communities such as Accompong record heritage that could otherwise be lost. Young people and the diaspora in New York or London can listen anywhere, which strengthens community identity and pride.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> First, the app supports <b>local entrepreneurship</b>. Cookshop and jerk centre owners in Ocho Rios become visible to cruise visitors, so they gain more customers and income. However, this depends on vendors keeping their listings updated, so the Ministry should provide short training. Second, developing the app locally creates <b>jobs and skills</b>: developers, designers, writers and voice artists in Jamaica would be employed, and the money would stay in the local economy rather than going to an overseas firm. The finished app also has <b>export potential</b>, since a similar version could be licensed to St. Kitts or Antigua.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> Global apps are designed for large markets, so they lack local data such as minibus stops, sargassum updates or Carnival road closures, and they rarely include local languages, currency or heritage. A locally developed app uses local knowledge, so it is more accurate and relevant. One limitation is <b>long-term maintenance cost</b> in a small market. The Ministry should plan funding from sponsorship by local businesses and partnerships with telecoms providers so the app stays updated.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Every benefit needs three things: the benefit, a Caribbean example and who gains. In part (d), always balance your answer with a limitation and a realistic solution: that is what \"examine\" rewards.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Examine the benefits of a local app (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: benefits of a local app, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part in one or two sentences. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> TWO local needs that Island Roots would meet. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Complete</b>: \"One social or cultural benefit is ... because ...\" <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO economic benefits of building the app locally and <b>say</b> who gains from each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE reason a local app is more useful than Google Maps, and ONE problem the Ministry should plan for. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":90,"max":250,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Island Roots would meet the need for ... and the need for ...<br><b>(b)</b> One social or cultural benefit is ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> One economic benefit is ... This helps ... Another economic benefit is ... This helps ...<br><b>(d)</b> A local app is more useful than Google Maps because ... One problem is ...</p>","checklist":["I named two different local needs.","I gave a social or cultural benefit and a reason.","I named two economic benefits and who gains from each.","I gave one reason the local app is better and one problem to plan for."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Two local needs","marks":2,"look":["transport|minibus|bus|route","tourism|visitor|restaurant|lolo|cookshop|jerk|food|heritage|histor|culture"],"min":2,"strong":"You named two local needs.","weak":"Name two needs, such as transport (minibus routes) and small business (cookshops)."},{"criterion":"Social or cultural benefit","marks":2,"look":["heritage|histor|cultur|preserv|identity|pride|diaspora|stor","because"],"min":2,"strong":"You gave a social or cultural benefit with a reason.","weak":"Say the audio stories preserve heritage, because elders' memories are recorded for young people."},{"criterion":"Economic benefits and stakeholders","marks":4,"look":["job|employ|work","sales|customer|income|business|entrepreneur|money|export|licens","lolo|cookshop|jerk|vendor|owner|developer|designer|young|resident|economy","helps|benefit|gain|earn"],"min":3,"strong":"You named two economic benefits and who gains.","weak":"Name two economic benefits (jobs, more sales, export) and say who gains, such as cookshop owners or local developers."},{"criterion":"Local advantage and a problem","marks":2,"look":["local|minibus|lolo|cookshop|jerk|language|stor|emancipation|data","cost|money|update|maint|small|problem|fund"],"min":2,"strong":"You gave a local advantage and a realistic problem.","weak":"Say the local app has information Google Maps lacks, such as minibus stops, and that updating it costs money."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Island Roots would meet the need for transport information about minibus routes and the need for visitors to find local cookshops and restaurants.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One cultural benefit is preserving heritage, because elders' stories about Emancipation Day are recorded for young people and families abroad.</p><p><b>(c)</b> One economic benefit is more sales for cookshops. This helps cookshop owners, who earn more income when visitors can find them. Another economic benefit is local jobs. This helps young developers and designers in Jamaica, who are paid to build the app.</p><p><b>(d)</b> A local app is more useful than Google Maps because it includes route taxi stops and cookshops that global apps do not show. One problem is the cost of keeping the information up to date.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): (a) 1 per need; (b) 1 benefit, 1 reason; (c) 1 per economic benefit and 1 per stakeholder; (d) 1 advantage, 1 problem. Students who finish quickly can add \"However, ...\" to each benefit in (c), which turns it into the Core task's \"examine\" style.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Examine the benefits of a local app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: benefits of local mobile applications, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Ministry responsible for tourism and culture in Jamaica is considering funding a local mobile app called \"Island Roots\". It would combine a route taxi and minibus guide, a directory of local restaurants and cookshops, and short audio stories about the island's history and Emancipation Day traditions. The Ministry has a fixed budget for the first year and two options: <b>Option 1</b>, fund a local team to build Island Roots; <b>Option 2</b>, pay a large international travel company to add Jamaican listings and audio stories to its existing global app. Some officials argue that global apps already do the job.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a report for the Ministry that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>examines</b> TWO social and cultural benefits of Island Roots, including the ethical responsibilities of recording heritage; <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>examines</b> THREE economic benefits, each for a different stakeholder, using benefit, application, stakeholder and evaluation; <i>(9 marks)</i></li><li><b>evaluates</b> Option 1 and Option 2 and <b>recommends</b> one, with a plan to keep the app sustainable; <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>explains</b> how Island Roots could be adapted for another Caribbean territory and what would need to change. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":400,"max":700,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> First, Island Roots would ... because ... However, the team must ... Second, ...<br><b>(b)</b> Benefit 1: ... For example, ... This benefits ... However, ... Benefit 2: ... Benefit 3: ...<br><b>(c)</b> Option 1 would ... whereas Option 2 would ... I recommend ... because ... To keep the app sustainable, ...<br><b>(d)</b> Island Roots could be adapted for ... by changing ...</p>","checklist":["I examined two social or cultural benefits and explained permission, credit and fair representation.","I examined three economic benefits, each with a different stakeholder and a 'however' point.","I compared both options on local data, cost, control and money kept locally, then made a recommendation.","My sustainability plan names funding sources and who keeps the content updated.","I explained what would change to adapt the app for another territory (data, currency, language, payment).","I used terms such as cultural preservation, entrepreneurship, export potential, localisation and white-label."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Social and cultural benefits with ethics","marks":6,"look":["preserv|heritage|histor|emancipation","identity|pride|community|diaspora|connect","permission|consent|credit|acknowledg","stereotyp|respect|sacred|private|fair","because|so that"],"min":4,"strong":"Two developed social or cultural benefits with clear ethical responsibilities.","weak":"Examine two benefits (for example cultural preservation and diaspora connection) and explain the duty to get permission, credit elders and avoid stereotypes."},{"criterion":"Three economic benefits examined","marks":9,"look":["job|employ|skill","entrepreneur|small business|vendor|lolo|cookshop|jerk","export|licens|white.?label|region","local economy|stays|circulat|commission|overseas","however|although|depends|but","developer|owner|government|economy|young"],"min":4,"strong":"Three economic benefits, each with a stakeholder, an application and an evaluation.","weak":"Use benefit, application, stakeholder and evaluation for three benefits, such as jobs for local developers, more sales for cookshop owners and export potential for the economy."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of options and sustainability","marks":6,"look":["option 1|local team","option 2|international|global (company|app)","whereas|however|on the other hand|but","recommend","sustain|sponsor|partner|grant|update|maint"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced evaluation of both options with a justified recommendation and a sustainability plan.","weak":"Compare both options (local data, control, money kept locally, cost, reach), recommend one and explain how it will be funded and updated."},{"criterion":"Adapting for another territory","marks":4,"look":["adapt|locali|white.?label|rebrand","grenada|antigua|st\\.? kitts|st\\.? lucia|dominica|barbados|jamaica|trinidad|curaçao|curacao|aruba|belize|guyana","currency|ec dollar|payment|language|kwéyòl|kweyol|papiamentu|route|data","partner|local (team|ministry|tourism)|research"],"min":3,"strong":"A clear adaptation plan that names what must change.","weak":"Name a territory and explain what would change: local data (routes, vendors), currency and payments, language and the heritage content, ideally with a local partner."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> First, Island Roots would support <b>cultural preservation</b>. Audio stories from elders about Emancipation Day and community life in places such as Accompong record memories that could otherwise be lost, so young people can hear their history at the sites where it happened. However, the team must gain informed permission from each storyteller, credit them by name and agree which traditions should not be shared publicly. Second, it would strengthen <b>community identity and diaspora connection</b>. Families in New York or London could listen and add their own stories, which builds pride and shows that Jamaicans produce digital content rather than only consuming it. Content should be created with the community to avoid presenting culture as a tourist stereotype.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Benefit 1: <b>local jobs and skills</b>. Building the app locally would employ developers, designers, writers and voice artists. This benefits young people, who gain careers at home instead of emigrating. However, these jobs last only if the app is funded beyond launch. Benefit 2: <b>more sales for small businesses</b>. Listing cookshops and jerk centres in Ocho Rios and Falmouth helps cruise visitors find them, so owners earn more and visitor spending spreads beyond the cruise terminal shops. However, this depends on vendors keeping menus and hours updated. Benefit 3: <b>export potential for the economy</b>. A working app could be licensed as a white-label product to tourism bodies on other islands, earning foreign exchange and diversifying an economy that depends heavily on tourism. However, it would face competition and need adapting for each market.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Option 2 would reach many visitors quickly through an established global app, whereas Option 1 would take longer to build. However, Option 2 sends the money and the data overseas, gives the Ministry little control over the content, and global companies rarely maintain minibus stops or small cookshops accurately. Option 1 keeps money, skills and data on the island and lets the community shape its own heritage content. I recommend <b>Option 1</b>, because the app's value lies in local data and culture that a global firm is unlikely to keep current. To stay sustainable, it should be funded by sponsorship from local businesses, a partnership with telecoms providers and small featured-listing fees from restaurants, with a named team responsible for checking routes and listings every quarter.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Island Roots could be adapted for Grenada by keeping the same code and design but changing the data and content: Grenada's bus routes and vendors, prices in EC dollars with local payment providers, and new heritage stories recorded with Grenadian communities. Working with a local tourism partner would help gather accurate data and win community trust.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks): (a) 3 per benefit, with the ethics mark only for specific responsibilities (permission, credit, limits on sharing, avoiding stereotypes). (b) 3 per benefit: application, named stakeholder and an evaluation. (c) 2 comparison, 2 recommendation with reasons, 2 sustainability plan. (d) 4 = territory, changed data, changed currency, payment or language, and a partner or research step. Credit a well-argued recommendation of Option 2 if the student shows how local data and money retention would be protected.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M3U5","title":"Factors that impact the mobile user experience","objectives":["Describe how screen size, resolution and the touch interface affect the mobile user experience, including touch targets and readability.","Describe how bandwidth, connectivity and data usage affect users, and explain offline-first and low-data design strategies.","Describe how portability and context of use, and device performance (processing power, storage and battery life), affect app design.","Explain accessibility and inclusive design, including screen readers (TalkBack, VoiceOver), voice control, contrast and captions.","Describe how the physical environment (lighting, noise, movement, location) and the user profile (age, ability, digital skills, language, culture, needs) shape design decisions.","Recommend and justify a design strategy for each factor."],"videos":[{"title":"Mobile UX design principles","q":"mobile UX design principles for beginners touch targets thumb zone"},{"title":"Using TalkBack and VoiceOver","q":"how blind people use smartphones screen reader TalkBack VoiceOver demo"},{"title":"Offline-first apps explained","q":"offline first mobile app design explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What is mobile user experience?","html":"<p><b>User experience (UX)</b> is how a person feels when using a product: whether it is easy, fast, pleasant and useful. <b>User interface (UI)</b> is what they see and touch: buttons, text, icons and layout. Good UI is part of good UX, but UX also includes speed, reliability, battery use and whether the app works in real conditions.</p>\n<p>Mobile UX is harder than desktop UX because phones are small, used on the move, often on weak connections, with limited battery, by every kind of person, in every kind of place. There are <b>seven factors</b>, each with a short name and a fuller description.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Factor</th><th>What it covers</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. Screen size</td><td>Screen size, resolution and touch interface</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. Bandwidth</td><td>Bandwidth, connectivity and data usage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Portability</td><td>Portability and context of use</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. Processing power</td><td>Device performance, including processing power, storage and battery life</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. Accessibility</td><td>Accessibility and inclusive design</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6. Environment</td><td>Physical environment, including lighting, noise, movement and location</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7. User profile</td><td>User profile, including age, ability, digital skills, language, culture and user needs</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>A useful memory hook: <b>\"Small Bandits Pick Pockets And Escape Unseen\"</b>: Screen, Bandwidth, Portability, Processing power, Accessibility, Environment, User profile.</p>\n<p>Think of Maria, a nurse in Bridgetown. She checks her shift schedule on an older Android phone, standing at a bus stop on Fairchild Street in bright sunshine, with traffic noise, 8% battery and only a little data left this month. Every one of the seven factors affects whether the app works for her. Designers must think about people like Maria, not only about themselves on a new phone and fast Wi-Fi.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The syllabus verb is <b>describe</b> the factors, and the course outline adds <b>explain design strategies</b>. Grade One answers do both: describe the factor, explain its effect on the user, then give a specific strategy. Factor, effect, strategy.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>User experience (UX) is how it feels to use an app: is it easy, fast and useful? User interface (UI) is what you see and touch, such as buttons and text. Seven factors shape mobile UX: screen size, bandwidth, portability, device performance, accessibility, environment and user profile. Picture a nurse checking her shift at a sunny bus stop in Bridgetown with 8% battery: every factor affects her.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A common misconception is that UX means \"nice design\". An app can look beautiful and still fail if it drains the battery, crashes on older phones or needs constant data. The seven factors also interact: a cruise visitor at Carlisle Bay is affected by the environment (glare), bandwidth (roaming costs) and user profile (unfamiliar with the island) all at once, and one strategy, such as large high-contrast buttons, can address several factors. Grade One answers follow the full chain for each factor: describe the factor, explain its effect on <b>these</b> users in <b>this</b> scenario, then give a specific strategy and say why it works. Generic advice such as \"make it user-friendly\" earns little.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Name the UX factor","intro":"<p>Maya, the web developer at Island Pulse Media, collected complaints from testers of a new app. Match each complaint to the UX factor it reveals.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Screen size","r":"\"The text is tiny and I keep tapping the wrong button.\""},{"l":"Bandwidth","r":"\"The menu photos take forever to load on mobile data in Rodney Bay.\""},{"l":"Portability","r":"\"I was checking it on the bus and had to start again after a call came in.\""},{"l":"Processing power","r":"\"My phone gets hot and the battery drops fast when I use it.\""},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"\"My screen reader just says button, button, button.\""},{"l":"Environment","r":"\"On Reduit Beach I cannot see anything on the screen in the sun.\""},{"l":"User profile","r":"\"My grandmother does not understand the icons, and she prefers Spanish.\""}],"win":"Very good. You identified all seven factors from real user complaints, which is how scenario questions test them.","tutor":[{"h":"Device or person?","p":"<p>Some complaints are about the phone (screen, processing), some about the network, some about the place, and some about the person.</p>"},{"h":"Environment vs portability","p":"<p>Environment is about the surroundings (light, noise). Portability is about being on the move and being interrupted.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Name the UX factor (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Screen size","r":"Tiny text and buttons"},{"l":"Bandwidth","r":"Photos load slowly on mobile data"},{"l":"Environment","r":"Cannot see the screen in bright sun"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"The screen reader cannot describe buttons"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Name the UX factor (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Maya's second round of testing produced subtler complaints. Each one points mainly to one factor (using the full factor names). Match each complaint to its factor.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Screen size, resolution and touch","r":"\"On my small phone the Pay and Cancel buttons sit so close together that I paid by mistake.\""},{"l":"Bandwidth, connectivity and data","r":"\"In the hills above Grenville the prices never finish loading, and the app used half my weekly data.\""},{"l":"Portability and context of use","r":"\"I was halfway through booking when my stop came, and when I reopened the app everything was gone.\""},{"l":"Device performance","r":"\"The app keeps closing itself on my three-year-old phone, and now it says my storage is full.\""},{"l":"Accessibility and inclusive design","r":"\"I use voice control because of my arthritis, but the map only opens with a two-finger twist.\""},{"l":"Physical environment","r":"\"During J'ouvert I never heard the alert, and I could not read the screen while walking in the crowd.\""},{"l":"User profile","r":"\"It is my first smartphone. I do not know what the three little lines in the corner mean.\""}],"win":"Excellent. You diagnosed each factor from indirect evidence, such as storage warnings for device performance and an unfamiliar menu icon for digital skills in the user profile."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Screen size, resolution and touch","html":"<p><b>Screen size</b> is the physical size of the display, measured diagonally in inches. Phones range from about 5 to 7 inches; tablets are larger; smartwatches are tiny. <b>Resolution</b> is the number of pixels (for example 1080 by 2400). <b>Pixel density</b> (ppi) is how many pixels fit into each inch. Two phones of the same size can have very different resolutions.</p>\n<p><b>How it affects the user</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Less space means less content per screen, so cluttered layouts are confusing.</li>\n<li>Small text is hard to read; long paragraphs need scrolling.</li>\n<li>Fingers are less precise than a mouse pointer. Small buttons placed close together cause mis-taps.</li>\n<li>Images that look sharp on one phone may look blurry on a high-density screen if the resolution is too low.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Design strategies</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Touch targets</b>: make every tappable item big enough. Apple's guidelines recommend at least <b>44 by 44 points</b>; Google's Material Design recommends at least <b>48 by 48 dp</b> (density-independent pixels), roughly 9 mm, with space between targets.</li>\n<li><b>Responsive and adaptive layouts</b>: use flexible layouts that adjust to different screen sizes and orientations rather than fixed pixel positions.</li>\n<li><b>Density-independent units</b>: design in dp (Android) or points (iOS) so items stay the same physical size on different screens; supply images at higher resolutions (for example @2x and @3x) or use vector icons (SVG).</li>\n<li><b>Readability</b>: body text of about 16 sp or larger, short lines, clear sans-serif fonts, and support for the user's own text size setting.</li>\n<li><b>Content priority</b>: show the most important action first (\"Order now\", \"Check bus times\") and hide extras in menus. Mobile-first design means starting with the smallest screen.</li>\n<li><b>Thumb zone</b>: place key buttons in the lower half of the screen, where the thumb reaches easily.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Screen size also covers the <b>touch interface</b>. Gestures such as tap, swipe, pinch to zoom and long press must be obvious or explained, and every gesture should have a visible button alternative, because some users cannot perform complex gestures.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Quote a number in Paper 02: \"Buttons should be at least 48 by 48 dp so users can tap them accurately.\" A specific, justified strategy earns more marks than \"make buttons bigger\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Phone screens are small, and fingers are less precise than a mouse. Text that is too small is hard to read, and buttons that are small or close together cause wrong taps. Designers make every button at least 48 by 48 dp on Android (44 by 44 points on iPhone), use layouts that fit any screen, and put the main buttons near the bottom, where your thumb can reach them.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Screen size, resolution and pixel density are three different things, and examiners test the difference. Two 6-inch phones can have very different resolutions, which is why designers work in density-independent units (dp or points) so a button is the same physical size everywhere, and supply images at @2x and @3x or as vectors. Touch design also includes discoverability: a swipe-to-delete gesture is quick for experienced users but invisible to others, so every gesture needs a visible alternative. The best answers justify their numbers: 48 dp is roughly 9 mm, about the width of a fingertip, which is why it reduces mis-taps, especially for someone on a bumpy minibus.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Screen and touch decisions","intro":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at Island Pulse Media, is reviewing the first screen of the LoloGo food-ordering app. Choose the best decision each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"The \"Add to order\" button is 28 by 28 dp. Jerome should...","options":["make the text inside it smaller","increase it to at least 48 by 48 dp","leave it; users can zoom in"],"answer":1,"fb":"Material Design recommends touch targets of at least 48 by 48 dp so fingers can tap accurately."},{"text":"Two buttons, \"Cancel\" and \"Pay\", are touching each other. The risk is...","options":["higher data usage","accidental taps on the wrong action","faster battery drain"],"answer":1,"fb":"Targets need spacing so users do not hit the wrong one, especially for costly actions like paying."},{"text":"The logo looks blurry on a new high-density phone. The best fix is to...","options":["use a vector (SVG) logo or higher-resolution versions","reduce the screen brightness","use a smaller font"],"answer":0,"fb":"Vector graphics, or images supplied at @2x and @3x, stay sharp on high-density screens."},{"text":"Where should the main \"Checkout\" button go for comfortable one-handed use?","options":["hidden in a menu","top left corner","bottom of the screen, in the thumb zone"],"answer":2,"fb":"The lower half of the screen is easiest for the thumb to reach."},{"text":"Users must swipe left on a dish to delete it, but some do not discover it. Jerome should...","options":["remove the delete feature","also provide a visible delete button","add more swipe gestures"],"answer":1,"fb":"Hidden gestures need a visible alternative so every user can complete the task."}],"win":"Excellent. You applied touch target sizes, spacing, resolution and the thumb zone: specific screen and touch strategies.","tutor":[{"h":"The magic numbers","p":"<p>Apple: 44 points. Google: 48 dp.</p>"},{"h":"Thumbs","p":"<p>Where does your thumb rest when you hold a phone in one hand?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Screen and touch decisions (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A good minimum touch target on Android is...","options":["20 by 20 dp","48 by 48 dp"],"answer":1,"fb":"Material Design recommends 48 dp."},{"text":"Main buttons for one-handed use go...","options":["at the bottom","at the top corner"],"answer":0,"fb":"The thumb reaches the bottom easily."},{"text":"To keep a logo sharp on every screen, use...","options":["a small JPEG","a vector (SVG)"],"answer":1,"fb":"Vectors scale without blurring."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Screen and touch decisions (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Jerome's LoloGo review now covers small budget phones and users who change their phone settings. Choose the BEST decision each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"LoloGo's \"Add\" icon is 24 dp, but its tappable area is 48 by 48 dp. Is this acceptable?","options":["No; touch targets should be about 20 dp","Yes; target size does not matter on Android","Yes; the tappable area meets the guideline even though the icon is smaller","No; the visible icon itself must be 48 dp"],"answer":2,"fb":"Guidelines apply to the touch target, not the drawn icon. A 24 dp icon inside a 48 dp target is standard practice."},{"text":"On a 5-inch budget phone, the menu crams 12 dishes with photos, prices and descriptions onto one screen. What is the BEST redesign?","options":["Shrink the text to 10 sp so everything fits","Force landscape mode","Remove the prices to save space","Show fewer items per screen with a clear Order action, and reveal details on tap"],"answer":3,"fb":"Content priority means showing what matters most first and revealing detail when the user asks for it."},{"text":"A designer placed elements at fixed pixel positions on a 6.7-inch phone. On a small phone the Checkout button is cut off. Which principle was broken?","options":["Offline-first design","Data saver mode","Responsive and adaptive layout","Lazy loading"],"answer":2,"fb":"Layouts must adapt to different screen sizes and orientations instead of using fixed positions."},{"text":"A user sets her phone's text to the largest size. Which design BEST supports her?","options":["Putting text inside images","Ignoring the system setting to protect the layout","Fixed-height boxes that cut off long text","Supporting dynamic text size, with boxes that grow to fit"],"answer":3,"fb":"Dynamic type support lets the layout respond to the user's setting without clipping."},{"text":"Deleting an order needs a long press, with no visible option. Which change BEST balances speed and discoverability?","options":["Explain the gesture in the terms and conditions","Keep the long press for regular users and add a visible delete icon with a confirmation","Remove the long press completely","Add a second hidden gesture"],"answer":1,"fb":"Shortcuts can stay, but every action needs a visible route, and a costly action needs confirmation."},{"text":"Why is 48 dp a sensible minimum target size?","options":["It is roughly 9 mm, about a fingertip's width, so it reduces mis-taps","It is the size of the app icon on the home screen","It makes the app download faster","It is required before screen readers will work"],"answer":0,"fb":"The size is based on the human finger, which is why it improves accuracy on small screens."}],"win":"Excellent. You applied target sizes correctly, prioritised content on small screens, and supported user settings and discoverable gestures."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Bandwidth, connectivity and data usage","html":"<p><b>Bandwidth</b> is how much data a connection can carry per second. <b>Connectivity</b> is whether there is a connection at all, and how stable it is. <b>Data usage</b> is how much of the user's data allowance the app consumes. You need to know all three.</p>\n<p><b>The Caribbean reality</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>4G is widespread across the region and 5G is arriving in some territories, but coverage can be weak in hilly or rural areas, inside buildings and at sea.</li>\n<li>Many users buy <b>prepaid data</b> in small amounts, so heavy apps cost them real money.</li>\n<li>After hurricanes such as Irma, networks can be down for days.</li>\n<li>Visitors on roaming plans may avoid using data at all, relying on hotel or café Wi-Fi.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>How it affects the user</b>: slow loading, spinning icons, failed payments, lost form entries, frustration and uninstalls. Users often abandon a page that takes more than a few seconds to load.</p>\n<p><b>Design strategies</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Offline-first design</b>: store essential content and data on the device so the app works with no connection, then <b>synchronise</b> when a connection returns. Example: a hurricane app keeps shelter lists offline; a school app saves homework drafts locally.</li>\n<li><b>Compress and optimise media</b>: use compressed JPEG or WebP images at the size they are displayed, not 4000-pixel originals. Use vector icons.</li>\n<li><b>Lazy loading</b>: load images only when the user scrolls to them.</li>\n<li><b>Caching</b>: keep a copy of content already downloaded so it does not need to be fetched again.</li>\n<li><b>Data saver options</b>: let users choose \"Wi-Fi only\" for video, or a \"lite\" mode with fewer images.</li>\n<li><b>Clear feedback</b>: show \"You are offline; your order will send when you reconnect\" rather than an error or a blank screen.</li>\n<li><b>Small app size</b>: a smaller download is easier on limited data and storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Offline-first</b>: a design approach in which an app is built to work without an Internet connection by default, storing data locally and synchronising with the server when a connection is available.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Do not confuse <b>bandwidth</b> (capacity) with <b>latency</b> (delay) from Unit 1. In Module 3 answers, link bandwidth to the <b>cost of data</b> for Caribbean users: it shows you understand the local context.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Bandwidth is how much data a connection can carry each second. Connectivity is whether you have a signal at all. Data usage is how much of your data allowance an app uses. Many Caribbean users buy small amounts of prepaid data and get weak signal in the hills or at sea. Good apps store important content on the phone (offline-first), shrink images and tell users clearly when they are offline.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Offline-first is more than caching. It means the app treats the phone's own storage as the main source of information and the network as an occasional bonus, so users can read, fill in forms and queue actions with no signal, and the app syncs later. That raises design questions: what happens if two people change the same record while offline, and how does the user know the information might be out of date? Strong apps show a \"last updated\" time and a clear offline message. Remember the distinction from Module 1: bandwidth is capacity and latency is delay. A video can buffer because of low bandwidth even when latency is fine, while a payment can time out because of high latency even on a fast link.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Good or poor for low connectivity?","intro":"<p>A Grenada nutmeg cooperative wants an app for farmers in the hills around Grenville, where the signal is weak. Sort each design choice.</p>","main":"Is this choice good or poor for users with weak connections and limited data?","bins":["Good for low connectivity","Poor for low connectivity"],"items":[{"t":"A \"Wi-Fi only\" setting for downloading training videos.","bin":0,"fb":"Letting users control data use protects their prepaid data."},{"t":"Forms are saved on the phone and sent when a signal returns.","bin":0,"fb":"This is offline-first: no work is lost when the connection drops."},{"t":"The app downloads all 300 images at once when it opens.","bin":1,"fb":"Lazy loading would load only what the user needs right now."},{"t":"The app shows a blank white screen when offline.","bin":1,"fb":"Users need clear feedback, not a blank screen, when there is no connection."},{"t":"The app is 400 MB because of unused graphics.","bin":1,"fb":"Large app size is costly to download and fills limited storage."},{"t":"Every screen autoplays a high-definition video introduction.","bin":1,"fb":"Autoplay HD video wastes data and loads slowly on weak signals."},{"t":"Market prices are cached so farmers can see the last update offline.","bin":0,"fb":"Caching lets users see useful data even without a signal."},{"t":"Photos are compressed and resized to the size they display at.","bin":0,"fb":"Optimised images load faster and use less data."}],"win":"Well sorted. Offline-first, caching, compression and user data controls are the key strategies for low connectivity.","tutor":[{"h":"Think prepaid","p":"<p>Would this choice cost the farmer more data, or less?</p>"},{"h":"No signal test","p":"<p>Imagine the signal drops. Does the user still see something useful?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Prices cached for offline viewing.","bin":0,"fb":"Works without signal."},{"t":"Autoplay HD video on every screen.","bin":1,"fb":"Wastes data."},{"t":"Compressed photos.","bin":0,"fb":"Faster and lighter."},{"t":"Blank screen when offline.","bin":1,"fb":"No feedback for the user."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Good or poor for low connectivity? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>The Grenada nutmeg cooperative's developer has a second list of choices. These are less obvious: think about battery, repeated downloads and what happens when the signal drops mid-task.</p>","items":[{"t":"Images are served in WebP format at the size they are displayed.","bin":0,"fb":"Modern compression at the right size cuts data use without losing quality."},{"t":"A failed upload is retried every second until it succeeds.","bin":1,"fb":"Constant retries waste battery and data. Good apps wait longer between attempts or retry when the signal returns."},{"t":"Each screen shows \"Last updated 2 hours ago\" when the phone is offline.","bin":0,"fb":"Users can judge how fresh the saved information is."},{"t":"The login expires every 15 minutes and needs a connection to renew.","bin":1,"fb":"Farmers would be locked out of saved data whenever the signal drops."},{"t":"Delivery forms are saved on the phone and queued to send when a signal returns.","bin":0,"fb":"This is offline-first: no work is lost."},{"t":"The home screen waits for the server before showing anything.","bin":1,"fb":"Users see a blank screen on a weak signal. Show saved content first, then refresh."},{"t":"Training videos can be downloaded over Wi-Fi for watching later.","bin":0,"fb":"Users control when large files use data."},{"t":"Custom fonts are downloaded from the Internet every time the app opens.","bin":1,"fb":"Fonts should be bundled with the app or cached, not fetched repeatedly."},{"t":"Only the prices that changed are downloaded, not the whole price list.","bin":0,"fb":"Downloading only the changes keeps each update tiny."},{"t":"Map tiles are fetched at full detail even when the user is zoomed out.","bin":1,"fb":"Fetching unnecessary detail wastes data; load detail only when needed."}],"win":"Excellent. You judged the subtler choices: retry behaviour, session expiry, server-first loading, repeated font downloads and map detail all matter on weak connections."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Portability, context of use and device performance","html":"<p><b>Portability and context of use</b></p>\n<p>Phones go everywhere. <b>Context of use</b> means the situation in which the app is used: where, when, why, and what else the user is doing. A person might use the same app standing in a crowded minibus, walking along Bay Street in Nassau, waiting at the airport or lying on the couch.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Short sessions</b>: mobile users often use apps in quick bursts of a minute or less.</li>\n<li><b>Interruptions</b>: calls, notifications, arriving at a stop, a child needing attention.</li>\n<li><b>One-handed use</b>: the other hand is holding a bag, a railing or a drink.</li>\n<li><b>Divided attention</b>: users glance at the screen rather than read carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Strategies</b>: save progress automatically so users can resume after an interruption; keep tasks short (two or three taps to the main goal); put key controls in the thumb zone; use large, clear buttons and short text; support quick actions such as notifications that open the right screen; never lose what the user typed.</p>\n<p><b>Device performance: processing power, storage and battery life</b></p>\n<p>Not everyone has the newest phone. Many users in the region have <b>budget or older Android phones</b> with slower processors, less memory (RAM), limited storage and ageing batteries.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Effect on the user</th><th>Design strategy</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Processing power</b> (CPU, GPU, RAM)</td><td>Lag, slow animations, freezing, crashes</td><td>Simple animations, efficient code, test on low-end devices, avoid running heavy tasks on the main screen</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Storage</b></td><td>\"Storage full\" messages; users delete large apps first</td><td>Small app size, compressed assets, let users clear cache, store large files in the cloud</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Battery life</b></td><td>Fast drain, phone heats up, users uninstall</td><td>Limit GPS and background activity, use dark mode options, reduce constant syncing, batch network requests</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>GPS, the camera, video playback, bright screens and constant network use drain the battery fastest. A tour app that tracks location every second around Willemstad will empty the battery before lunch; checking location every few minutes, or only when the app is open, is kinder.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Processing power\" is part of the wider factor, \"device performance\". Mention all three parts (processing, storage, battery) and you show the examiner you know the full content.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>People use phones everywhere: on the bus, walking along Bay Street, waiting at the airport. They use apps in short bursts, often with one hand, and get interrupted by calls. So apps should save progress automatically and keep tasks short. Many people also have older phones with slow processors, little storage and weak batteries, so apps should be light and avoid draining power with constant GPS.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Design for the typical phone in your audience, not the developer's phone. A flagship handset hides problems that appear at once on a three-year-old budget Android: slow start-up, jerky animation, and the app being closed in the background when memory runs low, which wipes unsaved work. That links performance to portability, because auto-save protects users from both interruptions and memory limits. Battery use is a trade-off between accuracy and endurance; a tour app can use precise GPS only while the user is navigating and less precise location the rest of the time. In an answer, name all three parts of device performance (processing, storage and battery) and include testing on low-end devices in your strategy.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Designing for interruptions","q":"The Bajan bus app RouteMate asks users to fill in a five-step form to buy a ticket. Testers on real buses in Bridgetown often lose everything they typed when a call comes in or they reach their stop. Which design strategy BEST addresses this portability problem?","options":[{"t":"Increase the resolution of the images on the form.","fb":"Resolution affects how sharp images look. It does not stop users losing their progress."},{"t":"Require users to stay on the screen until the form is complete.","fb":"Apps cannot stop calls or bus stops. Designers must expect interruptions, not forbid them."},{"t":"Add a long tutorial video before the form.","fb":"A long video adds time and data use. It makes interruptions more likely, not less harmful."},{"t":"Save the user's progress automatically so they can resume where they left off, and shorten the form.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Mobile users are often interrupted, so auto-saving and shorter tasks protect their work."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Real life on the bus","p":"<p>Interruptions will happen. What protects the user when they do?</p>"},{"h":"Rule out the other factors","p":"<p>Which options are really about screen quality or data rather than being on the move?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Designing for interruptions (Foundation)","q":"Users on the bus lose their form when a call comes in. The best fix is to...","options":[{"t":"add a long video","fb":"That makes things slower."},{"t":"use sharper images","fb":"That does not save their work."},{"t":"auto-save their progress","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. They can resume after the interruption."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Designing for interruptions (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"RouteMate's team has three proposals for its five-step ticket form, which Bridgetown bus passengers use with one free hand and often less than a minute. Proposal A: auto-save every field and reopen at the last step. Proposal B: cut the form to two steps by remembering the user's usual route and payment card. Proposal C: both A and B, plus a notification that reopens an interrupted purchase. Which evaluation is BEST?","options":[{"t":"Proposal A alone is enough, because saving progress solves every portability problem.","fb":"Auto-save protects work, but a five-step form is still slow for a one-handed user with less than a minute."},{"t":"Proposal B alone is best, because a shorter form can never be interrupted.","fb":"Shorter forms reduce the risk, but a call can still interrupt a two-step task and lose the user's work."},{"t":"None of them; passengers should be told to buy tickets before boarding.","fb":"Designers must work with the real context of use, not ask users to change it."},{"t":"Proposal C is strongest, because it reduces the chance of interruption, protects work when one happens and helps users resume quickly.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It combines prevention (fewer steps), protection (auto-save) and recovery (a notification), which suits the context of use."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Accessibility and inclusive design","html":"<p><b>Accessibility</b> means designing so that people with disabilities can use the app. <b>Inclusive design</b> goes further: designing from the start for the widest possible range of people, including older users, people with temporary injuries (a broken arm) and people in difficult situations (bright sun, a noisy street). The World Health Organization estimates that about 1 in 6 people worldwide live with a significant disability, so this is a large group of users, not a small extra.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Need</th><th>Assistive technology or setting</th><th>Design strategy</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Blind or low vision</td><td><b>Screen readers</b>: <b>TalkBack</b> (Android) and <b>VoiceOver</b> (iOS) read the screen aloud; screen magnification; large text settings</td><td>Give every button and image a text label (content description or accessibility label); use real text, not text inside images; support dynamic text size; logical reading order</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour blindness, low vision</td><td>High contrast and colour correction settings</td><td>Strong <b>contrast</b> (WCAG recommends at least 4.5:1 for normal text); never use colour alone (\"fields in red are wrong\"); add icons or words</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deaf or hard of hearing</td><td>Captions, visual alerts, vibration</td><td><b>Captions</b> and transcripts for all video and audio; visual and vibration alerts as well as sounds</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Limited movement or dexterity</td><td><b>Voice control</b> (Voice Access on Android, Voice Control on iOS), switch access</td><td>Large touch targets, no complex gestures, no time limits that rush the user, every action reachable without precise swipes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cognitive or learning differences</td><td>Simplified modes, reading aids</td><td>Plain language, consistent layout, clear icons with labels, one task per screen, helpful error messages</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Why it matters</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ethical and social</b>: everyone has a right to information and services, especially emergency, health and government apps.</li>\n<li><b>Legal</b>: many countries require public services to be accessible.</li>\n<li><b>Business</b>: more users can use the app, and features such as captions help everyone (people watching videos on a quiet bus).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Test with accessibility tools: turn on TalkBack or VoiceOver and try to complete a task with your eyes closed; use the free Google Accessibility Scanner on Android; check contrast with an online contrast checker. WebAIM is a good starting point.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Name the real tools. \"Label all buttons so that screen readers such as TalkBack and VoiceOver can announce them\" is a precise, mark-earning strategy.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Accessibility means making sure people with disabilities can use an app. Inclusive design goes further, designing from the start for as many people as possible, including older users and someone with a broken arm. Label every button so screen readers such as TalkBack and VoiceOver can read it aloud, use strong colour contrast, add captions to videos and never use colour alone to show an error.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Accessibility features often help everyone, an idea known as the curb-cut effect: captions help a deaf user and a commuter on a quiet bus; large targets help a user with a tremor and someone on a bumpy ferry. That makes accessibility a business case as well as an ethical and legal one, since about 1 in 6 people worldwide live with a significant disability (WHO). Testing matters more than good intentions: switch on TalkBack or VoiceOver and try to complete a task with your eyes closed, then check contrast against the WCAG ratio of 4.5:1 for normal text. A precise answer names the user need, the assistive technology and the design strategy together.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the accessibility problems","intro":"<p>Zara, the creative director at Island Pulse Media, is auditing a draft of the \"Carnival Live TT\" app before launch. Some lines describe accessibility barriers.</p>","q":"Select every line that describes an ACCESSIBILITY problem.","lines":["The parade map buttons are images with no text label.","Light yellow text is placed on a white background.","All videos of the Grand Parade have captions.","Errors are shown only by turning the field red.","The app supports the phone's large text setting.","Users must pinch and rotate with two fingers to open the schedule.","Each band's photo has a short description for screen readers.","A ticket offer disappears after 5 seconds with no way to pause it."],"correct":[0,1,3,5,7],"win":"Correct. Missing labels, poor contrast, colour-only signals, complex gestures and strict time limits are all accessibility barriers.","miss":"Look again. Captions, large text support and image descriptions are good practice. Find the lines that would block a screen reader user, a colour-blind user or someone with limited dexterity.","tutor":[{"h":"Close your eyes","p":"<p>Which lines would make the app impossible to use with a screen reader?</p>"},{"h":"Colour and time","p":"<p>Colour alone and short time limits exclude some users.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the accessibility problems (Foundation)","q":"Select the lines that are ACCESSIBILITY problems.","lines":["Buttons have no text labels.","Videos have captions.","Errors are shown only in red.","The app supports large text."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Missing labels and colour-only errors are barriers.","miss":"Captions and large text are good practice."},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the accessibility problems (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Zara's second audit of Carnival Live TT goes deeper. The obvious problems are fixed, but some subtler barriers remain.</p>","q":"Select every line that describes an ACCESSIBILITY problem.","lines":["The Buy Ticket button's screen reader label is \"button_2\".","Captions are generated automatically and nobody checks them for errors.","Body text meets a 4.5:1 contrast ratio against its background.","The parade map can be zoomed by pinching or with + and − buttons.","Required fields are marked only with a red border.","The screen reader reads the schedule in a jumbled order because of the layout.","With the large text setting on, labels overflow and are cut off.","Every video has a link to a full transcript.","Every sound alert also comes with vibration and a visual banner.","A 'session expired' message appears after 60 seconds with no option to extend it."],"correct":[0,1,4,5,6,9],"win":"Excellent. You caught the subtler barriers: meaningless labels, unchecked captions, colour-only signals, a jumbled reading order, clipped large text and an unforgiving time limit.","miss":"Look again. A 4.5:1 contrast ratio, zoom buttons, transcripts and vibration with banners are good practice. Check each line from the point of view of a screen reader user, a colour-blind user, someone using large text and someone who needs more time."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Physical environment and user profile","html":"<p><b>Physical environment</b> (lighting, noise, movement and location)</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Environment</th><th>Effect</th><th>Design strategy</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Lighting</b>: bright Caribbean sun on Grace Bay beach, or a dark room at night</td><td>Glare makes low-contrast screens unreadable; bright screens dazzle at night</td><td>High contrast, bold text, avoid thin pale fonts, support light and dark modes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Noise</b>: a J'ouvert road march, a busy cruise pier, a quiet hospital ward</td><td>Users cannot hear sounds or voice prompts; sound may be inappropriate</td><td>Captions, visual and vibration alerts, never rely on sound alone</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Movement</b>: walking, on a bumpy minibus, on a ferry to Anguilla</td><td>Shaky hands, mis-taps, hard to read</td><td>Large targets, simple screens, \"undo\" for mistakes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Location</b>: outdoors, at sea, in rural areas, across borders</td><td>Weak signal, roaming charges, different languages or currency</td><td>Offline-first, location-aware content, currency and language options</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>User profile</b> (age, ability, digital skills, language, culture and user needs)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Age</b>: children need simple, safe, colourful designs; older adults may need larger text, clearer labels and fewer steps.</li>\n<li><b>Ability</b>: see accessibility above.</li>\n<li><b>Digital skills</b> (also called tech-savviness, including literacy): first-time smartphone users need clear onboarding, familiar icons with text labels and helpful error messages; experienced users want shortcuts.</li>\n<li><b>Language</b>: Many Caribbean territories are multilingual: Aruba and Curaçao use Papiamento or Papiamentu, Dutch, English and Spanish, and Haiti uses Kreyòl and French. Offer English plus Spanish, Dutch, French, Kreyòl or Papiamentu where appropriate, and use plain language. Allow space for longer translated words.</li>\n<li><b>Culture</b>: local images, names, foods and holidays; correct currency (for example XCD in the Eastern Caribbean, JMD in Jamaica, or the Caribbean guilder, XCG, which replaced the ANG in Curaçao and Sint Maarten in 2025); respectful content; local date formats.</li>\n<li><b>User needs</b>: what problem is the user trying to solve? A cruise visitor with six hours needs speed; a resident needs reliability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Designers find out about the user profile through <b>user research</b>: surveys, interviews and observation. They often summarise it in a <b>persona</b>, an imagined but realistic profile such as \"Mr. Richardson, 68, retired electricity-company technician in Linstead, uses WhatsApp daily but nothing else\". You will use personas in Unit 3.6.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Environment is about the <b>surroundings</b>; user profile is about the <b>person</b>. \"Bright sunlight\" is environment. \"An older user with weaker eyesight\" is user profile. Keep them separate in your answers.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The physical environment is where the app is used: bright sun on Grace Bay beach, a noisy J'ouvert road march or a bumpy minibus. Designers use high contrast, vibration alerts and big buttons to cope. The user profile is about the person: their age, abilities, digital skills, language and culture. An app for Aruba should offer several languages and show prices in the right currency.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Keep the two factors separate in your answers, then show how they combine. \"Bright sunlight\" is environment; \"an older user with weaker eyesight\" is user profile; an older user reading in bright sunlight needs strategies for both. The user profile is discovered, not guessed: surveys, interviews and observation feed into personas, and a persona is only useful if it is based on real research. Culture includes details that global apps get wrong, such as local date formats, the Eastern Caribbean dollar, the Jamaican dollar or the Caribbean guilder (which replaced the Netherlands Antillean guilder in 2025). Language also affects layout: translated words are often longer than English ones, so buttons and menus need room to grow.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"The beach vendor app","intro":"<p>The Seven Mile Beach Vendors Association wants an app so visitors can order drinks and snacks from their sunbeds. Jerome asks you to make the key UX decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Zara's draft uses a stylish light grey text on a white background. The app will be used outdoors in strong sun. What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Switch to high-contrast colours and bold, larger text so the screen is readable in sunlight.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Glare makes low-contrast text unreadable outdoors."},{"t":"Keep the design; users can turn up their brightness.","to":"bad1","fb":"Even at full brightness, pale grey on white disappears in Caribbean sun, and full brightness drains the battery."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Visitors include cruise passengers on roaming plans who avoid using mobile data. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep the app very light, with a QR code at each sunbed that opens a small menu page, compressed images and the beach bar's free Wi-Fi.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. You reduced data use and download size for users with expensive roaming."},{"t":"Require a 250 MB app download with video menus.","to":"bad2","fb":"A large download on roaming data is costly. Many visitors would not bother."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Testing shows older visitors and some Spanish-speaking guests struggle with the icons. What is your final change?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Remove all text so the design looks cleaner.","to":"bad3","fb":"Icons alone confuse many users and are unreadable by screen readers."},{"t":"Add text labels to icons, larger touch targets, a language switch (English, Spanish, French, Italian) and labels for screen readers.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You responded to the user profile and accessibility needs."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> Orders flow from the sunbeds. A Grade One answer would explain each decision as <b>factor, effect, strategy</b>: environment (glare, so high contrast), bandwidth (roaming, so a light app), and user profile and accessibility (labels, languages, large targets).</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Unreadable screen.</b> Visitors squint, give up and walk to the bar instead. Try again and design for the environment.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Nobody downloads it.</b> Cruise passengers refuse to pay roaming charges. Try again and think about bandwidth and data costs.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Confused users.</b> Older and non-English-speaking visitors cannot tell what the icons mean. Try again and design for the user profile.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Where is it used?","p":"<p>Think about the sun on Seven Mile Beach.</p>"},{"h":"Who is using it?","p":"<p>Cruise visitors on roaming, older guests, many languages.</p>"},{"h":"Factor, effect, strategy","p":"<p>Each decision should solve a named factor.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The beach vendor app (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: an ordering app used on a sunny beach.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The text is pale grey on white. The app is used in bright sun. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep it; it looks stylish.","to":"bad","fb":"Pale text vanishes in sunlight."},{"t":"Use high-contrast, bold text.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. It stays readable in glare."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You designed for the environment.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Nobody can read it outdoors. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"The beach vendor app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief from the Seven Mile Beach Vendors Association. Each decision involves more than one UX factor, and several choices are partly right. Some choices end the project early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Zara's draft uses stylish light grey text on a white background. The app will be used outdoors in strong sun. What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"High-contrast colours and bold, larger text, checked with a contrast tool against the 4.5:1 guideline.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. You fixed the problem and checked it against a measurable standard."},{"t":"Keep the design; users can turn up their brightness.","to":"bad1","fb":"Pale grey on white disappears in Caribbean sun even at full brightness, which also drains the battery."},{"t":"Add a dark mode and let users switch it on in the settings.","to":"ok1","fb":"A dark mode can help some users, but the default screen is still unreadable in the sun, and few visitors will find the setting."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Most customers are cruise passengers on roaming plans who avoid mobile data and will visit the beach only once. How should they get the menu?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A 250 MB app with video menus.","to":"bad2","fb":"A large download on roaming data is costly, so few visitors would bother."},{"t":"A native app, but kept small at 40 MB.","to":"ok2","fb":"Smaller is better, but a one-time visitor on roaming data is still unlikely to download any app."},{"t":"A QR code at each sunbed that opens a light web menu (compressed images, well under 1 MB) over the beach bar's free Wi-Fi.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Nothing to install, almost no data, and it suits one-off visitors."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Guests order while walking back from the sea, with wet hands, sunscreen and often a drink in one hand. Which change helps MOST?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Large touch targets in the thumb zone, a one-tap 'order again' button and an 'undo' for mistaken orders.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. You designed for movement, one-handed use and imprecise taps."},{"t":"Swipe gestures for every action, to keep the screen clean.","to":"bad3","fb":"Precise gestures are hard with wet hands on the move, and they are invisible to new users."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Older visitors and some Spanish-speaking guests struggle with the icons, and one guest uses VoiceOver. What is your final change?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A language switch only.","to":"ok3","fb":"Translation helps Spanish speakers, but unlabelled icons still confuse older users and screen readers."},{"t":"Text labels beside the icons, a language switch (English, Spanish, French, Italian) and accessibility labels tested with VoiceOver and TalkBack.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You addressed the user profile and accessibility, and tested with real assistive technology."},{"t":"Remove all text so the design looks cleaner.","to":"bad4","fb":"Icons alone confuse many users and are unreadable by screen readers."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Orders flowing from every sunbed.</b> Your decisions follow factor, effect, strategy throughout: environment (glare, so tested high contrast), bandwidth and user profile (one-off roaming visitors, so a QR web menu), portability (movement and wet hands, so large targets and undo) and accessibility (labels, languages, screen reader testing).</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Still squinting.</b> Most visitors never find the dark mode and struggle with the default screen. Try again and fix the default design.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Few downloads.</b> The app works well, but most one-off visitors never install it. Try again with something they do not need to install.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Partly fixed.</b> Spanish-speaking guests can order, but older visitors and the VoiceOver user still cannot. Try again and cover every user.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Unreadable screen.</b> Visitors give up and walk to the bar instead. Try again and design for the environment.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Nobody downloads it.</b> Cruise passengers refuse to pay roaming charges. Try again and think about bandwidth and data costs.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Mistaken orders.</b> Wet fingers trigger the wrong swipes and guests receive drinks they did not order. Try again and design for movement.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Confused users.</b> Older and non-English-speaking visitors cannot tell what the icons mean. Try again and design for the user profile.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Factor, effect, strategy: exam summary","html":"<p>Use this summary table to revise. In Paper 02, a three-part answer (factor, effect on the user, design strategy) earns the most marks.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Effect on the user</th><th>Key design strategies</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Screen size, resolution, touch</td><td>Hard to read, mis-taps, cluttered screens</td><td>Touch targets 44 pt (iOS) or 48 dp (Android), responsive layouts, readable text, vector icons, thumb zone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bandwidth, connectivity, data</td><td>Slow loading, failures, high data cost</td><td>Offline-first, caching, compressed images, lazy loading, Wi-Fi-only options</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Portability, context of use</td><td>Interruptions, one hand, short attention</td><td>Auto-save, short tasks, one-handed layouts, clear notifications</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Device performance</td><td>Lag, crashes, storage full, battery drain</td><td>Lightweight code and assets, test on low-end phones, limit GPS and background tasks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accessibility, inclusive design</td><td>Some users cannot use the app at all</td><td>Labels for TalkBack and VoiceOver, contrast, captions, voice control support, no colour-only signals</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Physical environment</td><td>Glare, noise, movement, weak signal</td><td>High contrast, visual and vibration alerts, large targets, offline content</td></tr>\n<tr><td>User profile</td><td>Confusion, exclusion, mistrust</td><td>Personas, plain language, multiple languages, local culture and currency, onboarding</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>How factors overlap</b>: one strategy can solve several factors. Large, high-contrast buttons help small screens, sunlight glare, movement, older users and people with limited dexterity. Offline-first helps bandwidth, location, battery (fewer network calls) and disaster resilience. Mention these links in \"discuss\" answers to show depth.</p>\n<p><b>Command words</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Describe</b> a factor: say what it is and how it affects the user.</li>\n<li><b>Explain</b> a strategy: say what to do <b>and why</b> it works.</li>\n<li><b>Justify</b> a design choice: give a reason linked to the user and the scenario.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Paper 01 traps</b>: confusing environment with user profile; thinking resolution and screen size are the same; thinking accessibility is only for blind users; forgetting that storage and battery are now part of device performance.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In your SBA proposal, include a short UX table like this one for your own app. It shows the examiner that your design decisions are based on your real users, which supports marks in the Proposal and Product sections.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>For every UX question, use three steps. <b>Factor</b>: name it, such as bandwidth. <b>Effect</b>: say how it affects these users, such as slow loading for farmers with weak signal. <b>Strategy</b>: say what the designer should do and why, such as storing prices offline. One strategy can help with several factors: large, high-contrast buttons help in sunlight, on a moving bus and for older users.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Grade One answers add two moves. First, <b>tie each effect to the scenario</b>: not \"slow loading\" but \"farmers in the hills around Grenville lose market prices when the signal drops\". Second, <b>prioritise</b>: when asked which factor matters most, judge by consequence (what happens if it fails) and by who is affected, especially vulnerable users. Watch for the traps: confusing environment with user profile, treating resolution as the same as screen size, assuming accessibility is only about blindness, and forgetting that storage and battery now sit under device performance. Overlaps are a strength in \"discuss\" answers: show how offline-first helps bandwidth, battery and disaster resilience at once.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Build a factor, effect, strategy answer","intro":"<p>Put the sentences in order to make one well-developed Paper 02 answer about a UX factor for a farmers' app in rural Guyana.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"One factor that affects the user experience is bandwidth and connectivity."},{"t":"Many farmers in rural Guyana have weak mobile signals and buy small amounts of prepaid data."},{"t":"As a result, an app that loads large images or needs a constant connection would be slow, costly and often fail."},{"t":"To address this, the app should use an offline-first design that stores prices and advice on the phone."},{"t":"It should also compress images and synchronise new data only when a signal is available."},{"t":"This means farmers can still use the app in the fields without wasting their data."}],"win":"Well ordered. Factor, context, effect, strategy, extra strategy and justification: a complete answer.","tutor":[{"h":"Name it first","p":"<p>Start with the sentence that names the factor.</p>"},{"h":"Problem before solution","p":"<p>Describe the effect before you suggest the strategy.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Factor, effect, strategy (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four sentences in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"One factor is bandwidth."},{"t":"Rural farmers have weak signals and little data."},{"t":"Heavy apps would be slow and costly."},{"t":"The app should be offline-first with compressed images."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Build a factor, effect, strategy answer (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>This answer goes further: it links a second factor and ends with a justified priority. Put all eight sentences in the best order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"One factor that affects the user experience is bandwidth, connectivity and data usage."},{"t":"Many farmers in rural Guyana have weak mobile signals in the fields and buy small amounts of prepaid data."},{"t":"As a result, an app that needs a constant connection would fail when prices are most needed, and large images would use up their data."},{"t":"To address this, the app should use an offline-first design that stores prices and advice on the phone and downloads only the changes when a signal is available."},{"t":"It should also compress images and offer a Wi-Fi-only option for training videos."},{"t":"This works because farmers can read the latest saved prices in the field without spending data."},{"t":"The same strategy also helps device performance, because fewer network requests save battery on older phones."},{"t":"Therefore, offline-first design should be a priority, since it addresses two factors at once for these users."}],"win":"Excellent. Factor, context, effect, strategy, a second strategy, why it works, a link to a second factor and a justified priority: a Grade One answer."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Describe UX factors and strategies","genre":"Structured response: describing factors that impact the mobile user experience","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Bahamas Health Link app will remind residents about clinic appointments and medication, and send dengue and storm health alerts. Its users include older residents in Fox Hill and Bain Town, young parents, and Spanish- and Kreyòl-speaking families. Many use older Android phones on prepaid data, and some have visual impairments.</p>","prompt":"<p>For the Health Link app:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>describe</b> how <b>three</b> different factors could affect the user experience;</li><li>for each factor, <b>explain</b> one design strategy to address it;</li><li><b>justify</b> which factor the developers should prioritise first, with a reason linked to the users.</li></ol>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>Factor 1:</b> ... affects users because ... To address this, the app should ... This works because ...<br><b>Factor 2:</b> ...<br><b>Factor 3:</b> ...<br><b>Priority:</b> The developers should prioritise ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I described three different factors from the syllabus list.","I explained the effect of each factor on the Health Link users.","I gave a specific strategy for each factor (with a tool, number or technique).","I named real tools such as TalkBack or VoiceOver where relevant.","I justified a priority with a reason linked to the users."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Description of factors and their effects","marks":5,"look":["bandwidth|data|connect|offline|signal","access|visual|impair|blind|screen reader","profile|older|elder|age|language|spanish|kreyol|creole","perform|battery|storage|processing|older android|low-end","screen|text|touch|button"],"min":3,"strong":"You described three factors clearly and linked each to the Health Link users.","weak":"Choose three factors from the syllabus (for example bandwidth, accessibility and user profile) and describe how each affects these users."},{"criterion":"Design strategies explained","marks":5,"look":["offline|cache|compress|sync","talkback|voiceover|label|contrast|caption","translat|language|plain","48|44|large|bigger|font|text size","because|so that|this means|this helps"],"min":3,"strong":"Your strategies are specific and explained. Excellent use of real tools and measurements.","weak":"Make each strategy specific: offline-first storage, labels for TalkBack and VoiceOver, 48 dp buttons, translations into Spanish and Kreyòl. Then say why it works."},{"criterion":"Justified priority","marks":3,"look":["priorit|first|most important","because|since|as","health|medic|appointment|alert|safety"],"min":2,"strong":"Your priority is clearly justified by the users and the purpose of the app.","weak":"State which factor matters most and explain why for these specific users, for example that health alerts must reach everyone."}],"model":"<p><b>Factor 1: Bandwidth, connectivity and data usage.</b> Many Health Link users are on prepaid data, and networks can fail during storms. If reminders and alerts need a constant connection, users could miss appointments or important dengue warnings, and heavy images would use up their data. The app should use an <b>offline-first</b> design, storing appointment and medication reminders on the phone so they trigger even without a signal, and syncing small text updates when a connection returns. This works because the most important information is always available and uses very little data.</p>\n<p><b>Factor 2: Accessibility.</b> Some users have visual impairments, and older users may have weaker eyesight. Small, low-contrast text or unlabelled icons would make the app unusable for them. The app should label every button so that screen readers such as <b>TalkBack</b> (on Android) and VoiceOver can read it aloud, support the phone's large text setting, and use strong contrast of at least 4.5:1. This allows visually impaired residents to manage their health independently.</p>\n<p><b>Factor 3: User profile.</b> Users range from young parents to older residents in Fox Hill, and many families speak Spanish or Kreyòl at home. Complex menus and English-only medical terms could confuse them. The app should offer English, Spanish and Kreyòl, use plain language, and pair every icon with a text label. This helps every household understand when and where to go.</p>\n<p><b>Priority:</b> The developers should prioritise <b>accessibility and the user profile together</b>, because a health app that some residents cannot read or understand fails its main purpose. Health information is vital, so it must reach the most vulnerable users first; offline storage can then make sure it works in every condition.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Use the same three-part pattern for every factor: <b>factor, effect on these users, specific strategy and why it works</b>. Quote real tools (TalkBack, VoiceOver) and real numbers (48 dp, 4.5:1) to lift your answer to Grade One.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Describe UX factors and strategies (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: mobile UX factors, Foundation (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> THREE UX factors that could affect Health Link users. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>For TWO of your factors, <b>give</b> ONE design strategy each and <b>say</b> how it helps the users. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Which factor should the developers fix first? <b>Give</b> ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[9 marks]</b></p>","min":80,"max":220,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Three factors are ..., ... and ...<br><b>(b)</b> For ..., the app should ... This helps because ... For ..., the app should ... This helps because ...<br><b>(c)</b> The developers should fix ... first because ...</p>","checklist":["I named three different UX factors.","I gave a strategy for two factors and said how each helps.","I chose one factor to fix first and gave a reason linked to the users."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Three factors named","marks":3,"look":["bandwidth|data|connect|signal","access|visual|impair|screen reader|eyesight","profile|older|age|language|spanish|kreyol|creole|perform|battery|storage|screen|environment"],"min":2,"strong":"You named three relevant factors.","weak":"Name three factors from the list, such as bandwidth, accessibility and user profile."},{"criterion":"Two strategies with how they help","marks":4,"look":["offline|save|store|compress|cache","talkback|voiceover|label|large text|contrast|language|translat|plain","helps|because|so that|means"],"min":2,"strong":"Your two strategies are specific and you said how they help.","weak":"Give a clear strategy, such as storing reminders offline or labelling buttons for TalkBack, and say how it helps."},{"criterion":"Priority with a reason","marks":2,"look":["first|priorit","because"],"min":2,"strong":"You chose a priority and gave a reason.","weak":"Say which factor comes first and give a reason using 'because'."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Three factors are bandwidth and data usage, accessibility and user profile.</p><p><b>(b)</b> For bandwidth, the app should store reminders on the phone so they work offline. This helps because users on prepaid data still get their reminders with no signal. For accessibility, the app should label every button so TalkBack can read it aloud. This helps because users with visual impairments can hear what each button does.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The developers should fix accessibility first because a health app must work for everyone, including residents who cannot see the screen well.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (9 marks): (a) 1 per factor; (b) for each factor, 1 for the strategy and 1 for how it helps; (c) 1 choice, 1 reason. Encourage students to name one tool or number (TalkBack, 48 dp) in (b). When they can add the \"effect on users\" sentence without help, move them to the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Describe UX factors and strategies (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: mobile UX factors and strategies, Grade One extension (20 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Bahamas Health Link app will remind residents about clinic appointments and medication, and send dengue and storm health alerts. Its users include older residents in Fox Hill and Bain Town, young parents, and Spanish- and Kreyòl-speaking families. Many use older Android phones on prepaid data, and some have visual impairments. Before the rainy season, the budget allows only <b>three</b> improvements. The Ministry has also asked for a new <b>live clinic map</b> that shows waiting times in real time using GPS and constant updates.</p>","prompt":"<p>For the Health Link app:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>describe</b> THREE different UX factors, explaining how each affects a named group of users; <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>explain</b> a specific design strategy for each factor, with a tool, measurement or technique, and why it works; <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>analyse</b> how ONE of your strategies addresses more than one factor; <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>evaluate</b> the proposed live clinic map against the users' constraints and <b>justify</b> the order in which the three improvements should be made. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","min":350,"max":650,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Factor 1: ... affects ... because ... Factor 2: ... Factor 3: ...<br><b>(b)</b> For factor 1, the app should ... This works because ... For factor 2, ... For factor 3, ...<br><b>(c)</b> The strategy of ... also helps ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> The live map would ... However, ... I would make the improvements in this order: first ..., because ...; second ...; third ...</p>","checklist":["I described three factors, each linked to a named user group from the stimulus.","Each strategy names a real tool, number or technique (TalkBack, VoiceOver, 48 dp, 4.5:1, offline-first).","I explained clearly how one strategy helps more than one factor.","I weighed the live map's benefit against data, battery and older phones, and suggested a lighter alternative or condition.","I justified my priority order using consequences for the most vulnerable users."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Three factors linked to named users","marks":6,"look":["bandwidth|data|connect|signal|offline","access|visual|impair|screen reader","profile|language|spanish|kreyol|kreyòl|older resident|digital skill","perform|battery|storage|older android|older phone","fox hill|bain town|parent|families|residents"],"min":4,"strong":"Three factors, each linked to a specific group in the stimulus.","weak":"Describe three factors and name the users affected by each, such as prepaid users, visually impaired residents and Kreyòl-speaking families."},{"criterion":"Specific strategies explained","marks":6,"look":["offline|cache|sync|store","talkback|voiceover","4\\.5|contrast|48|44|dp|large text|dynamic","translat|language|plain|label","because|so that|this works|this means"],"min":4,"strong":"Specific, measurable strategies with clear explanations.","weak":"Make each strategy precise (offline-first storage, TalkBack and VoiceOver labels, 4.5:1 contrast, translations) and explain why it works."},{"criterion":"Strategy across factors","marks":3,"look":["also|both|as well|at once|more than one","battery|perform|environment|sunlight|profile|older|bandwidth|data","because"],"min":2,"strong":"You showed clearly how one strategy helps several factors.","weak":"Pick one strategy (for example offline-first or large high-contrast text) and explain the second and third factor it helps."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of the live map and justified priorities","marks":5,"look":["live (clinic )?map|gps|real.?time","data|battery|older phone|prepaid|signal","however|but|although|on the other hand","instead|alternative|refresh|on request|text|sms|condition","first|second|third|priorit"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced evaluation of the map and a clear, justified order of priorities.","weak":"Weigh the map's benefit against data, battery and older phones, suggest a lighter alternative, then rank the three improvements with reasons."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Factor 1: bandwidth, connectivity and data usage.</b> Many users are on prepaid data, and networks can fail during storms, so families relying on mobile data could miss reminders and dengue alerts if the app needs a constant connection. <b>Factor 2: accessibility.</b> Residents with visual impairments, and older residents in Fox Hill and Bain Town with weaker eyesight, cannot use small, low-contrast text or unlabelled icons. <b>Factor 3: user profile.</b> Spanish- and Kreyòl-speaking families and older residents with limited digital skills may misunderstand English-only medical terms and complex menus, leading to missed appointments.</p><p><b>(b)</b> For bandwidth, the app should be <b>offline-first</b>: reminders are stored on the phone and fire without a signal, and only small text updates are synced when a connection returns. This works because the critical information never depends on the network. For accessibility, every button should have a meaningful label for <b>TalkBack</b> and <b>VoiceOver</b>, text should support the phone's large text setting, and contrast should meet at least <b>4.5:1</b>. This works because blind and low-vision users can hear and read every control. For the user profile, the app should offer English, Spanish and Kreyòl, use plain language and put a text label beside every icon, because users understand instructions best in their home language.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Offline-first storage also improves <b>device performance</b>: fewer network requests save battery and processing power on older Android phones. It helps during storms as well, when power and signal may both be limited, so one strategy addresses bandwidth, performance and resilience at once.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The live clinic map would help parents choose the shortest queue. However, constant GPS and live updates would use prepaid data and drain batteries on older phones, and the map would be hard for visually impaired users to read. A lighter alternative is a text list of nearby clinics with an estimated waiting time that refreshes only when the user asks, with SMS updates for those without data. I would make the improvements in this order: <b>first</b>, accessibility labels and contrast, because a health app that some residents cannot use fails its purpose; <b>second</b>, offline-first reminders, because alerts must arrive during storms; <b>third</b>, translations and plain language, which reach the families who need them. The live map should wait until these core needs are met.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (20 marks): (a) 2 per factor (factor plus a named user group). (b) 2 per strategy (a specific tool or number plus why it works). (c) 1 strategy named, 1 second factor, 1 explanation. (d) 1 benefit of the map, 1 constraint, 1 alternative or condition, 2 for a justified priority order. Accept other orders of priority if they are justified by consequences for the users in the stimulus.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M3U6","title":"Tools and techniques to create a mobile application","objectives":["Conceptualise a mobile app by defining its purpose, target users, core features and minimum viable product (MVP).","Create low- and high-fidelity wireframes on paper and in Figma or Balsamiq, map user flows and build a clickable prototype.","Distinguish between native development tools (Android Studio with Kotlin or Java and the Android SDK; Xcode with Swift and the iOS SDK), cross-platform tools, and no-code or low-code builders (MIT App Inventor, Thunkable, Glide, Adalo).","Build a simple working app step by step in MIT App Inventor using the Designer, the Blocks editor and the AI Companion.","Use online development resources and communities (Stack Overflow, GitHub, official documentation) responsibly.","Plan basic testing, debugging, feedback and refinement, including unit testing, user acceptance testing and beta releases (TestFlight, Google Play testing tracks)."],"videos":[{"title":"Wireframing a mobile app","q":"how to wireframe a mobile app beginners paper and Figma"},{"title":"Figma clickable prototype tutorial","q":"Figma prototype tutorial mobile app beginners connections"},{"title":"MIT App Inventor first app","q":"MIT App Inventor beginner tutorial first app buttons labels blocks"},{"title":"What is user acceptance testing?","q":"unit testing vs user acceptance testing vs beta testing explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Conceptualisation: purpose, users, features and MVP","html":"<p>Every app begins as an idea, and most failed apps fail here: they solve no real problem, or they try to do everything at once. <b>Conceptualisation</b> is the first stage of app development. It has three parts: the app's <b>purpose</b>, its <b>target users</b> and its <b>core features</b>.</p>\n<p><b>The development process at a glance</b></p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 70\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Process: conceptualise, wireframe, prototype, build, test, refine, with a loop back from refine to wireframe\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"5\" y=\"15\" width=\"90\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"15\" width=\"90\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"215\" y=\"15\" width=\"90\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"15\" width=\"90\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"425\" y=\"15\" width=\"90\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"530\" y=\"15\" width=\"85\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<path d=\"M95 32h15M200 32h15M305 32h15M410 32h15M515 32h15\"/>\n<path d=\"M572 49v14H155V49\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"50\" y=\"37\">Conceptualise</text><text x=\"155\" y=\"37\">Wireframe</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"37\">Prototype</text><text x=\"365\" y=\"37\">Build</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"37\">Test</text><text x=\"572\" y=\"37\">Refine</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Purpose</b>: the problem the app solves, written as one clear sentence. \"LoloGo lets customers order from Sint Maarten's lolos in advance so they do not queue on busy days.\" (LoloGo is the SBA example used in this course.)</li>\n<li><b>Target users</b>: who will use it, described with research: surveys, interviews and observation. Summarise them in a <b>persona</b>, for example \"Dana, 24, hotel worker in Simpson Bay, orders lunch on a 30-minute break, uses an Android phone on prepaid data.\"</li>\n<li><b>Core features</b>: the few things the app must do to fulfil its purpose: browse the menu, place an order, get a \"ready\" notification.</li>\n<li><b>Platform and constraints</b>: Android, iOS or both? Budget, time, skills and the UX factors from Unit 3.5.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Minimum viable product (MVP)</b>: the simplest version of a product that has just enough core features to be usable by early users and to test whether the idea works, so that feedback can guide further development.</p>\n<p>A useful way to choose MVP features is the <b>MoSCoW</b> method: <b>Must have</b>, <b>Should have</b>, <b>Could have</b>, <b>Won't have (this time)</b>. The MVP contains only the Must haves. Loyalty points, live delivery tracking and recipe videos can wait for version 2.</p>\n<p>Conceptualisation is usually written up as an <b>app concept brief</b>: name, purpose, problem, target users and persona, core features, platform, competitors and success measures.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA Proposal needs exactly this thinking. A clear purpose statement, a researched persona and a short MVP feature list show the examiner that your product responds to a real local need.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Every app starts with an idea. Conceptualisation means deciding three things before you build: the <b>purpose</b> (what problem it solves, in one sentence), the <b>target users</b> (who will use it, found through research) and the <b>core features</b> (the few things it must do). An MVP, or minimum viable product, is the simplest version that works. LoloGo's MVP is just: browse a menu, place an order and get a \"ready\" alert.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>An MVP is a test, not a cheap app. Its job is to answer one question as quickly as possible: does this solve the problem for real users? That is why MoSCoW only works if \"Must have\" is judged against the purpose, not against what the client finds exciting. A common mistake is to read \"minimum\" and forget \"viable\": an MVP still has to be usable, reliable and safe, or the feedback it produces will be about bugs rather than the idea. Grade One answers also set a <b>success measure</b> at this stage, such as \"80% of test users place an order in under a minute\", so later testing has a clear target. Purpose, persona, Must haves and a measure together make a strong concept brief.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"MVP or version 2?","intro":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at the Island Pulse Media, has a long wish list for the LoloGo app. He has six weeks to launch. Sort each feature.</p>","main":"Is this feature a Must have for the MVP, or can it wait for version 2?","bins":["MVP (Must have)","Version 2 (later)"],"items":[{"t":"Place an order for pickup.","bin":0,"fb":"Ordering is the main purpose of the app, so it must be in the MVP."},{"t":"Browse each lolo's menu with prices.","bin":0,"fb":"Without a menu, customers cannot order. This is core to the purpose."},{"t":"Choose which lolo to order from.","bin":0,"fb":"Customers need to pick the vendor before they can order."},{"t":"Recipe videos from the cooks.","bin":1,"fb":"Adds cultural value but uses data and time. It can come later."},{"t":"A notification when the order is ready.","bin":0,"fb":"This delivers the key benefit: no queuing."},{"t":"Loyalty points and badges.","bin":1,"fb":"Nice to have, but the app works without it. Add it once the MVP is proven."},{"t":"Augmented reality view of each dish.","bin":1,"fb":"Impressive but costly, and not needed to order food."},{"t":"Live map tracking of delivery riders.","bin":1,"fb":"The MVP is pickup only. Delivery tracking is a later feature."}],"win":"Well sorted. An MVP keeps only the features needed to fulfil the purpose, so the team can launch, test and learn quickly.","tutor":[{"h":"The purpose test","p":"<p>Could a customer still order lunch without this feature? If yes, it can wait.</p>"},{"h":"Time and cost","p":"<p>Six weeks is short. Which features are expensive and optional?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"MVP or version 2? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Browse the menu.","bin":0,"fb":"Core to ordering."},{"t":"Place an order.","bin":0,"fb":"The main purpose."},{"t":"Loyalty badges.","bin":1,"fb":"Optional extra."},{"t":"AR view of dishes.","bin":1,"fb":"Costly and optional."}]},
 "up": {"title":"MVP or version 2? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Jerome's second list is harder. Judge every feature against LoloGo's purpose: <b>customers order ahead for pickup so they do not queue</b>. Launch is in six weeks. Some MVP features are not obvious, and some exciting ones can wait.</p>","items":[{"t":"Showing when a dish is sold out.","bin":0,"fb":"Without it, customers order food that does not exist, and the app fails its purpose."},{"t":"A vendor screen where the lolo marks an order as ready.","bin":0,"fb":"The 'ready' notification cannot be sent unless the vendor can trigger it, so this is a hidden Must have."},{"t":"Large, high-contrast text that can be read in sunlight.","bin":0,"fb":"If customers cannot read the menu outdoors, the MVP is not viable."},{"t":"Blocking orders when a lolo is closed.","bin":0,"fb":"Orders sent to a closed lolo would break customers' trust on day one."},{"t":"Card payment inside the app.","bin":1,"fb":"For pickup, customers can pay at the counter. In-app payment adds cost and security work, so it can follow once the idea is proven."},{"t":"A dark mode.","bin":1,"fb":"Pleasant, but the app works without it."},{"t":"Order history with an 'order again' button.","bin":1,"fb":"Useful for regular customers, but not needed to test whether ordering ahead works."},{"t":"Customer reviews and star ratings for each lolo.","bin":1,"fb":"Reviews need users first. They belong in a later version."},{"t":"Delivery to customers' homes.","bin":1,"fb":"The MVP is pickup only. Delivery adds riders, tracking and pricing."}],"win":"Excellent. You found the hidden Must haves (sold-out status, the vendor's ready screen, readable text, closed-lolo blocking) and postponed attractive extras such as in-app payment and reviews."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Recap: wireframes, mockups and prototypes for apps","html":"<p>You studied <b>Wireframes, Mockups and Prototypes: Designing Digital Products with Purpose</b> in Module 2, Unit 2.3 (<i>Prototypes using design principles</i>). Go back to that unit for the full notes, the \"Wireframe, mockup or prototype?\" sort and the when-to-use activity. Here is the one-minute version for app building.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>In one line</th><th>For the app</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wireframe</b></td><td>Low-fidelity skeleton: layout, structure and user flow</td><td>Paper or Balsamiq screens, labelled \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mockup</b></td><td>High-fidelity but static: colours, typography, icons, imagery</td><td>Figma, Sketch or Adobe XD screens in the app's brand style</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Prototype</b></td><td>Clickable simulation of real interactions</td><td>Linked Figma frames tested on a phone before any code</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Remember the three ideas of <b>designing with purpose</b>: start simple, iterate fast; involve users early and often; choose tools strategically. For an app, that means testing the sign-up or order flow as a prototype with real users, such as students at the Kingstown bus terminal in St. Vincent, before Jerome opens Android Studio or MIT App Inventor.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a Paper 02 app question, name the stage and its purpose in one sentence: \"A prototype, to validate the ordering flow with users before development.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are three design stages. A <b>wireframe</b> is a simple sketch of a screen's layout, using boxes and labels. A <b>mockup</b> adds the real look (colours, fonts and pictures), but nothing moves. A <b>prototype</b> links screens together so you can tap through them like a real app. Test the prototype with real users, such as students at the Kingstown bus terminal in St. Vincent, before anyone writes code.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The stages are about the cost of change. Moving a button on a paper wireframe costs seconds; moving it in a coded app can cost days, especially after testing and release. That is why designers test the riskiest part of the app, usually the main task flow, at the cheapest stage that can answer the question. Fidelity is a choice, not a ladder you must climb in order: a quick clickable prototype made from paper sketches can test a flow, while a static high-fidelity mockup is better for getting a client to approve colours and branding. In an exam answer, name the stage and its purpose in one sentence, then say what feedback it is meant to collect.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"The design journey for LoloGo","intro":"<p>Jerome, the mobile developer at Island Pulse Media, is planning the design stage of the LoloGo food-ordering app. Put his steps in the order of the design journey.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Sketch paper screens of Home, Menu and Order, labelled \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\"."},{"t":"Show the wireframes to three lolo vendors and fix the layout and flow."},{"t":"Create two mockup variations of the Home screen with real colours, fonts and food photos."},{"t":"The client chooses one visual direction."},{"t":"Link the mockups in Figma into a clickable prototype of the ordering flow."},{"t":"Test the prototype with real users and fix any confusing steps before coding."}],"win":"Correct. Wireframe, then mockup, then prototype, with user feedback at every stage: start simple, iterate fast and involve users early.","tutor":[{"h":"Structure, look, interaction","p":"<p>Each stage adds detail to the one before it.</p>"},{"h":"Feedback between stages","p":"<p>Users and the client react to each artifact before the next one is made.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Design journey (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Sketch low-fidelity wireframes."},{"t":"Design high-fidelity mockups."},{"t":"Link screens into a clickable prototype."},{"t":"Test the prototype with users before coding."}]},
 "up": {"title":"The design journey for LoloGo (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The full design journey starts before the first sketch and ends with evidence from real users. Put all eight steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Write the purpose statement and a persona based on interviews with customers and vendors."},{"t":"Draw a user flow for ordering, including what happens when a dish is sold out."},{"t":"Sketch annotated paper wireframes of each screen in the flow."},{"t":"Test the paper wireframes with three vendors and two customers, and fix the flow."},{"t":"Create two high-fidelity mockup variations of the Home screen."},{"t":"The client chooses one visual direction."},{"t":"Link the chosen mockups into a clickable Figma prototype."},{"t":"Run task-based tests on real phones, note where users hesitate, and refine before coding."}],"win":"Excellent. Research, flow, wireframes, early feedback, mockups, client choice, prototype and task-based testing: each step reduces risk before any code is written."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Wireframing on paper and in Figma or Balsamiq","html":"<p>A <b>wireframe</b> is the skeleton of your design: a simplified, low-fidelity blueprint of each app screen that focuses on <b>layout, structure and user flow</b>, showing <b>where</b> things go (headers, images, text, buttons, navigation) without final colours, photos or styling. A <b>mockup</b> then adds the visual design, still static.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Low-fidelity wireframe</th><th>High-fidelity mockup</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Tools</td><td>Paper, pencil, black marker, sticky notes; Balsamiq</td><td>Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Look</td><td>Boxes, lines, placeholder text, a box with an X for images</td><td>Real fonts, colours, icons, spacing and sample content</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Very fast, easy to throw away and redraw</td><td>Slower, more precise</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Exploring ideas, early feedback, Paper 02 sketches</td><td>Final design approval, handing over to developers</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Paper wireframing technique</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Draw phone-shaped frames (about 6 cm by 12 cm, in a 9:19.5 or similar tall ratio) using a ruler.</li>\n<li>Sketch the layout with simple shapes: rectangles for buttons, an X box for images, wavy lines for body text.</li>\n<li>Write real labels on key buttons (\"Order now\"), not \"Button 1\". <b>Keep it simple</b>: boxes for images, lines for text, basic shapes for buttons, and label the sheet \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\".</li>\n<li><b>Annotate</b>: arrows and notes explaining behaviour (\"Tapping opens the menu screen\", \"Button 48 dp, bottom of screen\").</li>\n<li>Number the screens and draw arrows between them to show the flow.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Digital wireframing in Figma</b> (free education plan available)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new design file, press <b>F</b> (Frame tool) and choose a phone preset such as Android Compact or iPhone.</li>\n<li>Use rectangles (<b>R</b>), text (<b>T</b>) and simple icons. Keep a greyscale palette for wireframes.</li>\n<li>Use <b>Auto layout</b> to stack items with even spacing, and layout grids to align content.</li>\n<li>Duplicate the frame for each screen (Home, Menu, Order, Confirmation) and name every frame clearly.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Balsamiq</b> is a wireframing tool with a deliberately hand-drawn, sketchy look. You drag ready-made controls (buttons, lists, phone frames) onto the canvas, which keeps everyone focused on layout rather than colour.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 has <b>no Internet</b>, and pencils, paper and black markers are listed as exam equipment. Practise neat, annotated paper wireframes. If you build one in Inkscape, export it as PDF and name it clearly, for example <b>CandidateNo_Q3_wireframe.pdf</b>.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A wireframe is the skeleton of a screen. It shows where things go, such as the header, pictures, text and buttons, without colours or photos. You can draw one on paper with a ruler: boxes with an X for pictures, wavy lines for text and real labels on buttons, such as \"Order now\". Figma and Balsamiq let you make the same thing on a computer, and Balsamiq keeps a hand-drawn look.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A wireframe earns marks through its <b>annotations</b>, not its neatness. Examiners look for evidence of decisions: \"Button 48 dp, in the thumb zone\", \"Image loads only on tap to save data\", \"Error message appears here\". Each annotation links the layout to a UX factor from Unit 3.5. Keep wireframes greyscale so feedback focuses on structure, not colour. In Figma, Auto layout and layout grids keep spacing consistent across frames, which makes the later mockup faster. A common mistake is to draw only the \"happy path\": strong wireframe sets also show empty states (no orders yet), error states (payment failed) and confirmation screens, because real users meet these too.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Design and prototyping terms","intro":"<p>Match each term to its best description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Low-fidelity wireframe","r":"A quick sketch of boxes and labels on paper"},{"l":"High-fidelity mockup","r":"A detailed static design with real colours, fonts and images"},{"l":"User flow","r":"The path of screens and actions a user takes to complete a task"},{"l":"Clickable prototype","r":"Linked screens that respond to taps so users can test the flow"},{"l":"Persona","r":"A but research-based profile of a typical user"},{"l":"MVP","r":"The simplest usable version with only the core features"},{"l":"Annotation","r":"A note on a wireframe explaining what an element does"}],"win":"Very good. You can distinguish sketches, mockups, flows and prototypes: the key vocabulary of the design stage.","tutor":[{"h":"Static or interactive?","p":"<p>Wireframes and mockups are static. A prototype responds to taps.</p>"},{"h":"Screens or path?","p":"<p>A wireframe is one screen; a user flow is the route between screens.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Design terms (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Wireframe","r":"A simple layout plan of a screen"},{"l":"User flow","r":"The path a user takes through the screens"},{"l":"Prototype","r":"Linked screens you can tap through"},{"l":"Persona","r":"A profile of a typical user"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Design and prototyping terms (Challenge)","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>These are the terms that lift a design answer from good to Grade One. Match each term to its description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Fidelity","r":"How closely a design resembles the finished product in detail and interactivity"},{"l":"Happy path","r":"The flow a user follows when everything goes right"},{"l":"Empty state","r":"What a screen shows before there is any data, such as 'No orders yet'"},{"l":"Annotation","r":"A note on a wireframe explaining behaviour or a design decision"},{"l":"Smart animate","r":"A Figma transition that animates matching layers between two frames"},{"l":"Flow starting point","r":"The frame where a Figma prototype begins when it is presented"},{"l":"MoSCoW","r":"A method that ranks features as Must, Should, Could and Won't have"},{"l":"Task-based usability test","r":"Users are given a goal to complete while the team observes"}],"win":"Excellent. You can use the precise vocabulary of the design stage, from fidelity and empty states to task-based testing."}},{"type":"learn","title":"User flows and clickable prototypes","html":"<p>A <b>user flow</b> maps the steps a user takes to complete one task, such as \"order johnny cakes\". It is drawn as a flowchart with standard shapes: an oval for start and end, rectangles for screens, and diamonds for decisions.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 120\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"User flow: open app, choose lolo, decision is the item available, if yes add to order then pay then order confirmed; if no return to choose lolo\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<ellipse cx=\"45\" cy=\"40\" rx=\"40\" ry=\"18\"/><rect x=\"105\" y=\"22\" width=\"90\" height=\"36\" rx=\"3\"/><path d=\"M255 18l42 22-42 22-42-22z\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"22\" width=\"80\" height=\"36\" rx=\"3\"/><rect x=\"420\" y=\"22\" width=\"60\" height=\"36\" rx=\"3\"/><ellipse cx=\"545\" cy=\"40\" rx=\"50\" ry=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M85 40h20M195 40h18M297 40h23M400 40h20M480 40h15\"/>\n<path d=\"M255 62v38H150V58\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"44\">Open app</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"44\">Choose lolo</text><text x=\"255\" y=\"44\">Available?</text><text x=\"360\" y=\"44\">Add to order</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"44\">Pay</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"44\">Confirmed</text>\n<text x=\"308\" y=\"34\">Yes</text><text x=\"205\" y=\"96\">No</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p>User flows help designers find missing screens (\"What happens if the item is sold out?\") and unnecessary steps before anything is built. Aim for the fewest taps to the user's goal.</p>\n<p>A <b>clickable prototype</b> links wireframes or mockups so that tapping a button opens the next screen. Users can try the flow on a real phone <b>before any code is written</b>, which is far cheaper than fixing a coded app.</p>\n<p><b>Building a clickable prototype in Figma</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Finish your frames (Home, Menu, Order, Confirmation) in the <b>Design</b> tab.</li>\n<li>Switch to the <b>Prototype</b> tab in the right panel.</li>\n<li>Select a button, then drag the blue connection handle (the \"noodle\") to the destination frame.</li>\n<li>Set the interaction: trigger <b>On tap</b>, action <b>Navigate to</b>, animation such as <b>Instant</b> or <b>Smart animate</b>.</li>\n<li>Add a <b>Back</b> action on back arrows so users can return.</li>\n<li>Set a <b>flow starting point</b> on the Home frame.</li>\n<li>Click <b>Present</b> (the play button) to test, or open it on your phone with the Figma mobile app and share a view link with testers.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Balsamiq, Adobe XD and even linked slides in PowerPoint or Google Slides can also make simple clickable prototypes. MIT App Inventor and Thunkable can produce <b>working</b> prototypes, which you will meet later in this unit.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Peer-test your prototypes for usability, accessibility and performance. Keep screenshots of each prototype version and the feedback you received: that is your SBA <b>Progress and Process</b> evidence.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A user flow is a map of the steps a person takes to finish one task, such as ordering johnny cakes. It uses ovals for the start and end, rectangles for screens and diamonds for decisions. A clickable prototype links the screens so that tapping a button opens the next one. In Figma you do this in the Prototype tab by dragging a connection from a button to the next frame.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>User flows are where hidden problems surface. Every diamond (decision) needs two exits, which forces questions such as \"what if the lolo is closed?\" or \"what if payment fails?\". Counting the taps to the goal gives a measurable target to improve. A clickable prototype then tests the flow with real people, and the best tests give users a task (\"order two johnny cakes for pickup\") rather than a tour, so you can watch where they hesitate. Remember the limits: a prototype fakes the data and the speed, so it cannot tell you how the app performs on a budget phone with weak signal. That needs testing on the real build.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Build a Figma clickable prototype","intro":"<p>Maya, the web developer at Island Pulse Media, is teaching an intern to turn the LoloGo wireframes into a clickable prototype. Put her steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Create phone-sized frames for Home, Menu, Order and Confirmation in the Design tab."},{"t":"Add the layout to each frame: header, images, text and labelled buttons."},{"t":"Switch to the Prototype tab."},{"t":"Drag a connection from the \"Order now\" button to the Menu frame."},{"t":"Set the interaction to On tap, Navigate to, Smart animate."},{"t":"Set the Home frame as the flow starting point."},{"t":"Click Present and tap through the flow to test it."}],"win":"Correct. Design the frames, then switch to Prototype, connect, set interactions, set a start point and test.","tutor":[{"h":"Design before prototype","p":"<p>You cannot link screens that do not exist yet.</p>"},{"h":"Test last","p":"<p>Presenting is how you check everything works.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Figma prototype (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Design the screen frames."},{"t":"Switch to the Prototype tab."},{"t":"Connect buttons to the next frames."},{"t":"Click Present to test."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Build a Figma clickable prototype (Challenge)","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Maya's intern now has to build a prototype that includes an error path and is tested by real users. Put the full process in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Create phone-sized frames for Home, Menu, Order, Sold Out and Confirmation in the Design tab."},{"t":"Lay out each frame with Auto layout so the spacing stays even."},{"t":"Switch to the Prototype tab."},{"t":"Connect each button to its destination frame (On tap, Navigate to), including the sold-out dish to the Sold Out frame, and give back arrows a Back action."},{"t":"Set the Home frame as the flow starting point."},{"t":"Click Present and tap through every path yourself to check for dead ends."},{"t":"Share a view link so testers can open the prototype in the Figma mobile app."},{"t":"Give each tester a task, note where they hesitate, then update the frames."}],"win":"Excellent. You built the frames, linked every path including the error path, checked for dead ends yourself, then tested with real users and refined."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Native development: Android Studio and Xcode","html":"<p><b>Native apps</b> are built specifically for one operating system using its official tools. They give the best performance and full access to device features (camera, GPS, sensors, notifications). An <b>SDK</b> (software development kit) is the set of tools, libraries, documentation, emulators and sample code needed to build apps for a platform. An <b>IDE</b> (integrated development environment) is the program where developers write, run and debug code.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Android</th><th>iOS</th></tr>\n<tr><td>IDE</td><td><b>Android Studio</b> (free; Windows, macOS, Linux)</td><td><b>Xcode</b> (free; macOS only, so a Mac is needed)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Languages</td><td><b>Kotlin</b> (Google's preferred language) or <b>Java</b></td><td><b>Swift</b> (modern) or Objective-C (older)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SDK</td><td><b>Android SDK</b>; UI built with Jetpack Compose or XML layouts</td><td><b>iOS SDK</b>; UI built with SwiftUI or UIKit with Interface Builder</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Testing</td><td>Android Emulator, or a real phone with USB debugging</td><td>iOS Simulator, or a real iPhone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Publishing</td><td>Google Play Console (one-time registration fee)</td><td>Apple Developer Program (annual fee) and App Store Connect</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>You may see the BlackBerry SDK in older materials. BlackBerry phones are no longer a mainstream platform, so focus on Android Studio, Xcode, SDKs, no-code tools and cross-platform tools.</p>\n<p><b>What the code looks like</b>: the same \"count johnny cakes\" button in each language.</p>\n<pre><code>// Kotlin (Android Studio, Jetpack Compose)\nvar count by remember { mutableStateOf(0) }\nButton(onClick = { count++ }) {\n    Text(\"Add johnny cake\")\n}\nText(\"Johnny cakes: $count\")\n\n// Swift (Xcode, SwiftUI)\n@State private var count = 0\nButton(\"Add johnny cake\") { count += 1 }\nText(\"Johnny cakes: \\(count)\")</code></pre>\n<p>Both follow the same idea: store a value (<b>state</b>), change it when an <b>event</b> happens (a tap), and update what the user sees. You will see exactly the same logic as visual blocks in MIT App Inventor.</p>\n<p><b>Cross-platform tools</b> let one codebase run on both Android and iOS: <b>Flutter</b> (Google, Dart language), <b>React Native</b> (Meta, JavaScript), <b>.NET MAUI</b> (Microsoft, C#) and <b>Kotlin Multiplatform</b>. They save time and money for small Caribbean teams, but may lag behind the newest native features. A <b>progressive web app (PWA)</b> is another option: a website that installs and behaves like an app.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A classic Paper 01 trap: Xcode runs only on macOS. If a question says a student has only a Windows laptop, they cannot use Xcode directly.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Native apps are built for one operating system with its own official tools. For Android, developers use Android Studio and the Kotlin language. For iPhone, they use Xcode and Swift, and Xcode only runs on a Mac. An SDK is the kit of tools, code libraries and virtual test phones (emulators) for a platform. An IDE is the program where you write, run and fix your code.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Notice that the Kotlin, Swift and App Inventor versions of the counter all follow the same pattern: store a value (state), change it when an event happens, then update the screen. Recognising this pattern is what lets developers move between tools. Native development is the strongest choice when an app needs top performance or deep hardware access, but it means two codebases, two skill sets and, for iOS, a Mac and an annual developer fee. Cross-platform tools such as Flutter, React Native and Kotlin Multiplatform reduce that cost, although new OS features may take time to reach them. A Grade One justification weighs these trade-offs against the scenario's budget, skills and deadline.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Native and cross-platform quick-fire","intro":"<p>Jerome is answering questions from students on a work-experience visit to Island Pulse Media. Choose the best answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"Which IDE is the official tool for building native Android apps?","options":["Balsamiq","Xcode","Android Studio"],"answer":2,"fb":"Android Studio is Google's official IDE for Android, with the Android SDK and emulator built in."},{"text":"Google's preferred language for new Android apps is...","options":["HTML","Swift","Kotlin"],"answer":2,"fb":"Kotlin is preferred; Java is still supported. Swift is for Apple platforms."},{"text":"A student has only a Windows laptop. Which tool can she NOT run directly?","options":["MIT App Inventor","Xcode","Android Studio"],"answer":1,"fb":"Xcode runs only on macOS. Android Studio runs on Windows; App Inventor runs in a browser."},{"text":"A set of tools, libraries, documentation and emulators for building apps for a platform is called...","options":["an SDK","a persona","a wireframe"],"answer":0,"fb":"SDK stands for software development kit."},{"text":"A small team wants ONE codebase for both Android and iOS. They should consider...","options":["the BlackBerry SDK","a cross-platform tool such as Flutter or React Native","two separate native teams"],"answer":1,"fb":"Cross-platform tools share most code across both platforms, saving time and cost."},{"text":"Which language is used with SwiftUI in Xcode?","options":["Kotlin","Dart","Swift"],"answer":2,"fb":"SwiftUI is Apple's interface framework, written in Swift. Dart is used by Flutter."}],"win":"Excellent. You can distinguish the Android and iOS toolchains and know when cross-platform tools make sense.","tutor":[{"h":"Pair them up","p":"<p>Android: Android Studio, Kotlin or Java. iOS: Xcode, Swift.</p>"},{"h":"Mac only","p":"<p>Which IDE is tied to Apple's own computers?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Native tools (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Android apps are built in...","options":["Android Studio","Xcode"],"answer":0,"fb":"Android Studio is for Android."},{"text":"iOS apps are written mainly in...","options":["Kotlin","Swift"],"answer":1,"fb":"Swift is Apple's language."},{"text":"SDK stands for...","options":["screen design key","software development kit"],"answer":1,"fb":"A kit of tools for building apps."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Native and cross-platform quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>The work-experience students have harder questions for Jerome. Choose the BEST answer each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"Jerome writes the same counter in Kotlin, Swift and App Inventor blocks. What do all three versions share?","options":["They all compile to the same app file","They all use Jetpack Compose","They store a value, change it when an event happens and update the display","They all need a Mac to build"],"answer":2,"fb":"State, event and update is the common pattern behind all three tools."},{"text":"A two-person team in Antigua knows JavaScript well and needs Android and iOS apps in ten weeks. Which approach is MOST suitable?","options":["Balsamiq","React Native","Two separate native apps","Xcode only"],"answer":1,"fb":"React Native uses JavaScript and builds for both platforms from one codebase within the time available."},{"text":"Which statement about cross-platform tools is ACCURATE?","options":["They always run faster than native apps","They remove the need for macOS when publishing to the App Store","They cannot use the phone's camera","New OS features may reach them later than they reach the native SDKs"],"answer":3,"fb":"Frameworks must add support for new OS features, so they can lag behind. iOS publishing still needs macOS."},{"text":"An Android app crashes on only one phone model. Which Android Studio tool helps MOST to find the cause?","options":["Balsamiq","Interface Builder","TestFlight","Logcat, to read the error messages and stack trace"],"answer":3,"fb":"Logcat shows the system and app messages recorded at the moment of the crash."},{"text":"Why might a bank in Trinidad still choose native development despite the higher cost?","options":["It needs top performance, the platform's full security features and complete hardware access","Cross-platform apps cannot be published in stores","Native tools remove all development costs","Native apps do not need testing"],"answer":0,"fb":"When security and hardware access matter most and the budget allows it, native development is easiest to justify."},{"text":"A student wants to build for iOS but has only a Windows laptop. What is a realistic route?","options":["Rename an Android APK file to .ipa","Use a cross-platform tool with a cloud build service that provides macOS","Install Xcode on Windows","Use Android Studio's iOS mode"],"answer":1,"fb":"Cloud build services run the macOS build step remotely. Xcode itself does not run on Windows."}],"win":"Excellent. You can see the common logic behind the tools, choose an approach from a team's skills, and explain the real limits of cross-platform development."}},{"type":"learn","title":"No-code and low-code app builders","html":"<p><b>No-code</b> tools let people build apps with visual drag-and-drop editors and no typed code. <b>Low-code</b> tools are mostly visual but allow some code or logic for extra control. These online app builders are known as <b>no-code and low-code app builders</b>, and low-code development is also an emerging trend.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>How it works</th><th>Best for</th><th>Limits</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>MIT App Inventor</b></td><td>Free, browser-based. A <b>Designer</b> for the layout and a <b>Blocks</b> editor where logic is built from jigsaw-like blocks. Tested live with the <b>AI Companion</b> app.</td><td>Learning, school projects, Android prototypes, the SBA</td><td>Simpler interfaces; strongest for Android</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Thunkable</b></td><td>Drag-and-drop designer with blocks, similar in style to App Inventor</td><td>Prototypes for both Android and iOS</td><td>Free plan limits; advanced publishing needs a paid plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Glide</b></td><td>Builds an app from a <b>spreadsheet or data table</b> (for example Google Sheets)</td><td>Directories, menus, staff lists, inventory: data-driven apps</td><td>Less control over custom design and logic</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adalo</b></td><td>Drag-and-drop screens with a built-in database and actions</td><td>Marketplace or booking apps with user accounts</td><td>Paid plans for publishing; performance limits at scale</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Advantages</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fast to build and test: an MVP in days rather than months.</li>\n<li>Low cost and low skill barrier, so small businesses, charities and students can create apps.</li>\n<li>Good for prototypes that real users can try before investing in native development.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Disadvantages</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Less control over performance, design and advanced features.</li>\n<li><b>Vendor lock-in</b>: the app depends on the platform's pricing and survival.</li>\n<li>Can become slow or limited as the number of users grows.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Choosing a tool</b>: consider the purpose, target platform (Android, iOS or both), budget, time, the team's skills, the features needed (camera, GPS, database, offline) and how much the app must grow. A Belize tour guide listing 20 sites from a spreadsheet would suit Glide. A student SBA prototype with buttons, sounds and simple logic suits MIT App Inventor. A national banking app needs native or cross-platform development with strong security.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question says \"recommend a tool\", always <b>justify</b> it with two reasons from the scenario (budget, skills, platform, features). \"MIT App Inventor, because it is free and the students have no coding experience.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>No-code tools let you build an app by dragging and dropping, with no typed code. Low-code tools are mostly visual but let you add some code. MIT App Inventor and Thunkable use snap-together blocks; Glide turns a spreadsheet into an app; Adalo builds apps with user accounts and a database. They are fast and cheap, so a tour guide in Belize could build a simple list of sites in days.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The key risk with no-code is <b>vendor lock-in</b>. The app lives on the platform, so if prices rise, features change or the company closes, the app may have to be rebuilt elsewhere. Check whether you can export your data, and plan for growth: a tool that works well for 50 users may slow down at 5,000. No-code also does not remove the need for design and testing skills; a badly planned Glide app is still a bad app. A strong recommendation names the tool and justifies it with at least two clues from the scenario (budget, skills, platform, features, deadline), then states a limitation and when the team might move to native or cross-platform development.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing the right builder","q":"Grenada Spice Farmers Cooperative keeps a Google Sheet listing 60 farms, their spices, contact numbers and opening days. The manager has no coding skills and a very small budget, and wants a simple app that buyers can search within a week. Which tool is MOST suitable?","options":[{"t":"Xcode with Swift, because native apps perform best.","fb":"Native development needs coding skills, a Mac and much more time. It is excessive for a searchable list."},{"t":"Balsamiq, because it is quick and easy.","fb":"Balsamiq makes wireframes and simple mock prototypes, not a working app connected to live data."},{"t":"Glide, because it builds an app directly from a spreadsheet with no coding.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The data already exists in a sheet, the manager cannot code and time is short: Glide fits all three."},{"t":"Android Studio with Kotlin, because Kotlin is Google's preferred language.","fb":"Kotlin is powerful but requires programming skills the manager does not have, and would take far longer than a week."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Three clues","p":"<p>Existing spreadsheet, no coding skills, one week.</p>"},{"h":"Design tool or build tool?","p":"<p>One option only makes wireframes.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing the right builder (Foundation)","q":"Which tool builds an app straight from a spreadsheet with no coding?","options":[{"t":"Xcode","fb":"Xcode needs Swift coding."},{"t":"Glide","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Glide turns spreadsheet data into an app."},{"t":"Balsamiq","fb":"Balsamiq is for wireframes."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Choosing the right builder (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A year ago the Grenada Spice Farmers Cooperative built a Glide directory from its Google Sheet, and it works well. Now 3,000 buyers, including many in the diaspora, want to place orders, pay online and receive delivery updates. The cooperative has won a grant to hire developers. Which is the BEST next step?","options":[{"t":"Plan a move to a cross-platform or native app built by developers, export the data from the sheet, and keep the Glide app running until the new app is tested.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The needs have outgrown the tool, the grant pays for developers, and keeping the old app running avoids a gap in service."},{"t":"Keep adding features in Glide indefinitely, because it worked well for the directory.","fb":"Glide suited a searchable list, but payments, orders and updates for thousands of users push against no-code limits and increase lock-in."},{"t":"Switch to Balsamiq so that the app looks more professional.","fb":"Balsamiq makes wireframes, not working apps with payments."},{"t":"Close the app and go back to taking orders on WhatsApp.","fb":"This throws away a working service and does not meet the new needs."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"MIT App Inventor: a guided tour","html":"<p><b>MIT App Inventor</b> (ai2.appinventor.mit.edu) is a free, browser-based tool from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. You sign in with a Google account and every project is saved online. It has two main views, switched with buttons at the top right.</p>\n<p><b>1. The Designer</b> (what the app looks like)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Palette</b> (left): components grouped by category. <b>User Interface</b>: Button, Label, TextBox, Image, CheckBox, ListView. <b>Layout</b>: HorizontalArrangement, VerticalArrangement, TableArrangement. <b>Media</b>: Sound, Player, TextToSpeech, Camera. <b>Storage</b>: TinyDB (saves data on the phone).</li>\n<li><b>Viewer</b> (centre): a phone-shaped preview. Drag components onto it.</li>\n<li><b>Components</b> panel: a tree of everything in the screen. Use <b>Rename</b> to give meaningful names such as <b>btnAdd</b> and <b>lblTotal</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Properties</b> panel (right): change text, font size, colours, width and height (Automatic, Fill parent or pixels), alignment and images.</li>\n<li><b>Media</b> panel: upload images and sounds (keep them small: compressed PNG or JPEG, short MP3).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Non-visible components</b> such as TextToSpeech, Notifier, TinyDB and Clock appear below the phone in the Viewer, because they work behind the scenes.</p>\n<p><b>2. The Blocks editor</b> (how the app behaves)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Built-in blocks</b>: Control (if, else, loops), Logic, Math, Text, Lists, Colors, Variables and Procedures.</li>\n<li><b>Component blocks</b>: click a component to see its blocks. Gold <b>event</b> blocks (\"when btnAdd.Click do\"), green <b>setter</b> and <b>getter</b> blocks (\"set lblTotal.Text to\"), and purple <b>method calls</b> (\"call TextToSpeech1.Speak\").</li>\n<li>Blocks snap together like puzzle pieces, so syntax errors are impossible. Warning (yellow) and error (red) icons show problems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>3. Testing your app (Connect menu)</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>AI Companion</b>: install the free MIT AI2 Companion app on a phone, choose Connect, then AI Companion, and scan the QR code or type the six-letter code. The phone and computer must be on the same Wi-Fi network. Changes appear live.</li>\n<li><b>Emulator</b>: a virtual phone on the computer if no device is available (needs the aiStarter software).</li>\n<li><b>USB</b>: connect an Android phone by cable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>4. Saving and sharing (Build and Projects menus)</b>: <b>Build</b> makes an Android app file (.apk to install directly, or .aab for Google Play). <b>Projects, Export selected project</b> saves an <b>.aia</b> source file you can submit or back up.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Rename every component before you write blocks. \"btnAdd\" and \"lblTotal\" make your blocks readable for you, your group and the moderator; \"Button1\" and \"Label3\" do not.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>MIT App Inventor is a free app builder that runs in your web browser. It has two views. The <b>Designer</b> is where you drag buttons, labels and pictures onto a phone screen. The <b>Blocks editor</b> is where you snap together blocks that tell the app what to do, such as \"when this button is clicked, add 1\". You test it live on your phone with the free AI Companion app.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>App Inventor is <b>event-driven</b>: nothing happens until an event block fires, such as a click, a timer tick or the screen opening. Understanding this explains most beginner bugs, such as a label that never changes because no event updates it. Block colours carry meaning: gold for events, green for setting and getting properties, purple for calling methods. Non-visible components such as TinyDB and TextToSpeech work behind the scenes, and TinyDB is what lets an app remember data after it closes. Good habits transfer to professional tools: rename components meaningfully, use procedures to avoid repeating blocks, and export the .aia file regularly as a backup.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"App Inventor parts and jobs","intro":"<p>Match each MIT App Inventor part to what it does.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Designer","r":"The view where you lay out the screen and its components"},{"l":"Blocks editor","r":"The view where you build the app's logic from snap-together blocks"},{"l":"Palette","r":"The list of components you can drag onto the screen"},{"l":"Properties panel","r":"Where you change a component's text, size, colour and font"},{"l":"AI Companion","r":"A phone app that shows your project live by scanning a QR code"},{"l":"HorizontalArrangement","r":"A layout component that places items side by side"},{"l":"TinyDB","r":"A non-visible component that saves data on the phone"},{"l":".aia file","r":"The exported project source file used for backup or submission"}],"win":"Very good. You know where each job is done in App Inventor, so you can follow and explain a build step by step.","tutor":[{"h":"Look or logic?","p":"<p>The Designer controls the look; Blocks control the logic.</p>"},{"h":"File types","p":"<p>.apk installs the app; .aia stores the project source.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"App Inventor parts (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Designer","r":"Lay out the screen"},{"l":"Blocks editor","r":"Build the logic"},{"l":"AI Companion","r":"Test live on a phone"},{"l":"Properties panel","r":"Change size, colour and text"}]},
 "up": {"title":"App Inventor parts and jobs (Challenge)","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Go inside the Blocks editor. Match each block type, component or file to its job.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Gold event block","r":"Runs its blocks when something happens, such as a button click"},{"l":"Green setter block","r":"Changes a component's property, such as a label's text"},{"l":"Purple method call","r":"Asks a component to perform an action, such as speaking a message"},{"l":"Procedure","r":"A named group of blocks that can be called from several places"},{"l":"Global variable","r":"A value, such as the count, that every event block can read and change"},{"l":"Clock","r":"A non-visible component that fires a timer event at set intervals"},{"l":".apk file","r":"An Android app file that can be installed directly on a phone"},{"l":"Do It","r":"Runs a single block on the connected phone to test it"}],"win":"Excellent. You can explain how blocks, components and files work together, which helps you read, debug and describe an App Inventor build."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Step-by-step build: the Johnny Cake Counter","html":"<p>You will build a working ordering counter for a Simpson Bay lolo, a tiny first version of LoloGo. Customers tap + or - to choose how many johnny cakes they want, see the total, and can hear the total read aloud (an accessibility feature). We will use a price of US$2 each.</p>\n<p><b>Part A: Designer</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Go to ai2.appinventor.mit.edu, sign in, choose <b>Projects, Start new project</b> and name it <b>JohnnyCakeCounter</b> (no spaces).</li>\n<li>Select <b>Screen1</b>. In Properties set <b>Title</b> to \"Johnny Cake Counter\", <b>AlignHorizontal</b> to Center and choose a high-contrast <b>BackgroundColor</b>.</li>\n<li>Drag a <b>Label</b> to the top. Rename it <b>lblHeading</b>; Text \"How many johnny cakes?\", FontSize 22, FontBold ticked.</li>\n<li>Drag a <b>HorizontalArrangement</b> below it. Inside it place a <b>Button</b> (rename <b>btnMinus</b>, Text \"-\"), a <b>Label</b> (rename <b>lblCount</b>, Text \"0\", FontSize 30) and another <b>Button</b> (rename <b>btnAdd</b>, Text \"+\").</li>\n<li>Set both buttons' Width and Height to <b>60 pixels</b> and FontSize to 26, so they are large touch targets.</li>\n<li>Add a <b>Label</b> below the arrangement: rename <b>lblTotal</b>, Text \"Total: US$0\", FontSize 20.</li>\n<li>Add two Buttons: <b>btnSpeak</b> (Text \"Read my total\") and <b>btnReset</b> (Text \"Start again\"), Width Fill parent.</li>\n<li>From <b>Media</b>, drag a <b>TextToSpeech</b> component. It appears under the phone as non-visible.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Part B: Blocks</b></p>\n<pre><code>initialize global count to 0\n\nto updateDisplay\n    set lblCount.Text to get global count\n    set lblTotal.Text to join \"Total: US$\" , (get global count × 2)\n\nwhen btnAdd.Click\n    set global count to (get global count + 1)\n    call updateDisplay\n\nwhen btnMinus.Click\n    if (get global count &gt; 0)\n        then set global count to (get global count - 1)\n    call updateDisplay\n\nwhen btnReset.Click\n    set global count to 0\n    call updateDisplay\n\nwhen btnSpeak.Click\n    call TextToSpeech1.Speak  message: lblTotal.Text</code></pre>\n<ol start=\"9\">\n<li>Open <b>Blocks</b>. From <b>Variables</b> drag \"initialize global name to\", rename it <b>count</b> and attach a Math <b>0</b>.</li>\n<li>From <b>Procedures</b> drag \"to procedure do\", rename it <b>updateDisplay</b>, and build the two set blocks using Text <b>join</b> and Math <b>×</b>.</li>\n<li>Build each event block by clicking the component (btnAdd, btnMinus and so on) and dragging its gold <b>when ... .Click</b> block.</li>\n<li>Choose <b>Connect, AI Companion</b>, scan the code with the MIT AI2 Companion app, and test every button.</li>\n<li>Export the project (.aia) and build an .apk. Take screenshots of the Designer, the Blocks and the running app for your evidence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The procedure <b>updateDisplay</b> avoids repeating the same two blocks four times. The <b>if</b> block stops the count going below zero, a simple form of input validation.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Extend it for practice: add a TinyDB so the order is remembered when the app closes, or a second item (saltfish, US$3 each) with its own counter and a combined total.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The Johnny Cake Counter is a small app for a lolo in Simpson Bay. Customers tap + or − to choose how many johnny cakes they want, at US$2 each, and see the total. A button reads the total aloud, which helps users with weak eyesight. In the Designer you add the buttons and labels; in the Blocks editor you make each button change the count and update the screen.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Three design ideas make this tiny app well built. First, the <b>procedure</b> updateDisplay keeps the screen logic in one place, so a change (for example, a new currency label) is made once, not four times. Second, the <b>if</b> block is input validation: it prevents an impossible state, such as minus two johnny cakes. Third, separating <b>state</b> (the global count) from <b>display</b> (the labels) means the app can later save the count in TinyDB or send it to an order system without changing the buttons. To extend it, test the boundary cases: what happens at 0, at 1 and at a very large number? A professional would also store the price in its own variable rather than typing 2 into the calculation.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Debug the blocks","intro":"<p>A Form 6 student built the Johnny Cake Counter, but testers reported problems. Here are some of her blocks and settings.</p>","q":"Select every line that contains a BUG or a design error.","lines":["initialize global count to 0","when btnAdd.Click: set global count to (get global count - 1)","when btnMinus.Click: set global count to (get global count - 1), with no if block","set lblTotal.Text to join \"Total: US$\" , (get global count × 2)","btnAdd Width and Height set to 20 pixels","when btnReset.Click: set global count to 0 but updateDisplay is never called","when btnSpeak.Click: call TextToSpeech1.Speak message lblTotal.Text","Components named Button1, Button2, Label1 and Label2"],"correct":[1,2,4,5,7],"win":"Correct. You found a logic error (subtracting on Add), missing validation, a tiny touch target, a missing display update and poor naming.","miss":"Check again: the variable start value, the total join and the speak block are correct. Look for wrong maths, a missing if, tiny buttons, a missing call and unclear names.","tutor":[{"h":"Trace it","p":"<p>Imagine tapping each button. What happens to the count and the screen?</p>"},{"h":"Not only code","p":"<p>Touch target size and component names are design errors too.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Debug the blocks (Foundation)","q":"Select the lines that contain a bug.","lines":["initialize global count to 0","when btnAdd.Click: set count to count - 1","when btnSpeak.Click: Speak lblTotal.Text","btnAdd size set to 20 pixels"],"correct":[1,3],"win":"Yes. Add should add 1, and 20 pixels is too small to tap.","miss":"Trace what the Add button does, and check the button size."},
 "up": {"title":"Debug the blocks (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A second student extended the Johnny Cake Counter with a price variable and TinyDB so the order is remembered. Testers say the total is wrong, the count can go negative, the saved order never comes back and the spoken message is odd. Trace each line carefully.</p>","q":"Select every line that contains a BUG.","lines":["initialize global count to 0","initialize global price to 2","to updateDisplay: set lblTotal.Text to join \"Total: US$\" , (get global count + get global price)","when btnAdd.Click: set global count to (get global count + 1), then call updateDisplay","when btnMinus.Click: if (get global count ≥ 0) then set global count to (get global count − 1)","when btnReset.Click: set global count to 0, then call updateDisplay","when Screen1.Initialize: set global count to TinyDB1.GetValue (tag \"count\", valueIfTagNotThere 0), then call updateDisplay","No block anywhere calls TinyDB1.StoreValue with the tag \"count\".","lblCount FontSize 30; btnAdd Width and Height 60 pixels","when btnSpeak.Click: call TextToSpeech1.Speak message: lblCount.Text"],"correct":[2,4,7,9],"win":"Excellent. You found the four subtle bugs: adding the price instead of multiplying, a ≥ 0 check that still allows −1, a value that is read but never stored, and speaking the count instead of the total.","miss":"Trace with real numbers. With count 3 and price 2, what does the total show? With count 0, does the minus check stop the count? Is the saved value ever written? Which label does the speak button read?"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Testing, debugging, feedback and refinement","html":"<p>Every app needs <b>basic app testing, debugging, feedback and refinement</b>. Testing checks that the app works and that users can use it; <b>debugging</b> is finding and fixing the cause of errors.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type of testing</th><th>Who does it</th><th>What it checks</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Unit testing</b></td><td>Developers</td><td>One small part of the code (a function or procedure) gives the right result</td><td>Does updateDisplay show US$6 when count is 3? Android Studio uses JUnit; Xcode uses XCTest.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Integration and system testing</b></td><td>Developers, testers</td><td>Parts work together; the whole app works on different devices</td><td>Ordering, then payment, then notification on an old and a new phone</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Usability testing</b></td><td>Real users observed by the team</td><td>Can users complete tasks easily?</td><td>Watch five customers order without help; note where they hesitate</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>User acceptance testing (UAT)</b></td><td>The client or end users</td><td>Does the app meet the agreed requirements before release?</td><td>The lolo owners confirm LoloGo meets their brief and sign off</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Beta testing</b></td><td>A wider group of real users before public launch</td><td>Bugs and feedback in real conditions</td><td>Apple <b>TestFlight</b>; Google Play Console <b>internal</b>, <b>closed</b> and <b>open testing</b> tracks</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Types of error</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Syntax error</b>: code breaks the language rules, so it will not run (rare in App Inventor because blocks only fit correctly).</li>\n<li><b>Logic error</b>: the app runs but gives the wrong result, such as adding instead of subtracting.</li>\n<li><b>Runtime error</b>: the app crashes while running, such as dividing by zero or a missing file.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Debugging techniques</b>: reproduce the bug step by step; read the error message; test with normal, boundary and invalid data (0, 1, 99, a blank box); use Android Studio's Logcat and breakpoints, or App Inventor's \"Do It\" option on a block while connected to the Companion; change one thing at a time; keep a <b>bug log</b> (bug, steps, fix, date).</p>\n<p><b>Feedback and refinement</b>: collect feedback with short surveys (Google Forms), interviews and observation; group the issues; prioritise by severity; fix, then <b>test again</b> (regression testing checks that fixes did not break something else). This loop repeats: build, test, learn, refine. This is called <b>iterative</b> development.</p>\n<p>Real-device testing matters in the Caribbean: test on a budget Android phone, on mobile data, in bright sunlight and with TalkBack switched on, linking to the factors in Unit 3.5.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Distinguish clearly: <b>unit testing</b> checks small parts of the code and is done by developers; <b>UAT</b> checks the whole app against the requirements and is done by the client or users. Examiners love this pair.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Testing checks that an app works and that people can use it. Debugging means finding and fixing the cause of a problem. Developers test small parts of the code (unit testing). Real users try tasks while the team watches (usability testing). The client checks the app against what was agreed (user acceptance testing). A wider group tries it before launch (beta testing). Then the team fixes problems and tests again.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each type of test answers a different question: unit testing asks \"does this piece work?\", UAT asks \"is this what we agreed?\", usability testing asks \"can people use it?\" and beta testing asks \"does it survive the real world?\". Passing one does not prove the others. Good debugging is scientific: reproduce the bug, form a guess about the cause, change one thing and test again, using normal, boundary and invalid data. After every fix, regression testing checks that nothing else broke. Know the store rules too: Google Play currently requires many new personal developer accounts to run a closed test with at least 12 testers for 14 days before an app can be published to everyone, so beta testing must be in the schedule.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Getting LoloGo ready to launch","intro":"<p>The LoloGo prototype is built. Jerome must decide how to test it before Carnival, the busiest season for the lolos. Advise him.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Jerome has tested LoloGo only on his own new phone over office Wi-Fi. Everything works. What should he do next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Publish it to Google Play immediately; it works on his phone.","to":"bad1","fb":"One new phone on fast Wi-Fi is not representative of real users."},{"t":"Test on several phones, including an older budget Android, on mobile data and in sunlight, and log every bug.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Real users have different devices and conditions. Testing only on the developer's phone hides problems."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The device tests are fixed. Now, how should he check that LoloGo meets the lolo owners' needs?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Run user acceptance testing: the owners use the app against their agreed requirements and sign off, with usability sessions with a few customers.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. UAT confirms the app does what the client agreed, and usability testing shows how real customers cope."},{"t":"Ask a fellow developer to read the code instead.","to":"bad2","fb":"A code review is useful, but it cannot tell you whether the client's needs are met or whether customers find it easy."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The owners approve. There are three weeks to Carnival. What is the best final step?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Run a beta through Google Play internal or closed testing (and TestFlight for iPhone users) with about 30 regular customers, collect feedback, fix the top issues, then launch.","to":"good","fb":"Strong. A beta finds real-world problems before the public sees them, and the feedback guides the final refinement."},{"t":"Add five new features, such as loyalty points and AR dishes, without further testing.","to":"bad3","fb":"New, untested features just before the busiest period add risk. Stabilise the MVP first."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> LoloGo launches smoothly for Carnival. A Grade One answer would explain the sequence: <b>device and system testing</b>, then <b>UAT</b> with the client, then a <b>beta</b> release with real users, with feedback and refinement after each stage.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Bad reviews.</b> The app is slow on older phones and the buttons are unreadable in sunlight. One-star ratings arrive on day one. Try again and test in real conditions.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong app.</b> The code is clean, but the owners needed a \"sold out\" option that was never built. Try again and test against the client's requirements.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Crash at Carnival.</b> The new features break the order screen during J'ouvert morning. Try again and stabilise the MVP with a beta first.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Real conditions","p":"<p>Users are not all on new phones and fast Wi-Fi.</p>"},{"h":"Who signs off?","p":"<p>Which type of testing involves the client confirming the requirements?</p>"},{"h":"Before the crowd","p":"<p>How can a small group of real users find problems before everyone else?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Getting LoloGo ready (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: LoloGo has only been tested on the developer's new phone.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>What should Jerome do before launch?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Launch now; it works on his phone.","to":"bad","fb":"One phone is not enough."},{"t":"Run a beta test with real customers on different phones.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Real users find real problems."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Testing with real users makes the launch smooth.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Bugs appear for many users. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Getting LoloGo ready to launch (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher version. Carnival is three weeks away, and Jerome has just opened a new personal Google Play developer account. Several choices are partly right; pick the one you could defend. Some choices end the scenario early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Jerome has tested LoloGo only on his own new phone over office Wi-Fi. Everything works. What should he do next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Write a test plan: unit tests for the order total, then system tests on an older budget Android and an iPhone, on mobile data and in sunlight, with every bug logged.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. A structured plan covers the code, the devices and the real conditions."},{"t":"Publish it now; it works on his phone.","to":"bad1","fb":"One new phone on fast Wi-Fi is not representative of real users."},{"t":"Ask three friends to try it on their phones and say whether they like it.","to":"ok1","fb":"Some real devices are better than none, but 'do you like it?' is not a test, and nothing is logged."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Testing finds 14 bugs, from a crash on older phones to a slightly misaligned logo. How should Jerome deal with them?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Fix them in the order they were found.","to":"ok2","fb":"Every bug gets attention, but a serious crash may wait behind cosmetic issues."},{"t":"Fix only the cosmetic ones, because they are the quickest.","to":"bad2","fb":"The app still crashes on older phones, which many customers use."},{"t":"Rank them by severity: fix crashes and wrong totals first, then usability problems, and run regression tests after each fix.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Severity-first fixing protects the core task, and regression testing protects earlier fixes."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>How should Jerome confirm that LoloGo meets the lolo owners' needs?</p>","choices":[{"t":"UAT: the owners work through the agreed requirements (menus, sold-out status, ready alerts) and sign off, plus usability sessions in which five customers complete set tasks.","to":"n4","fb":"Strong. UAT checks the agreement, and usability sessions check real customers can use it."},{"t":"Show the owners a slideshow of screenshots and ask for approval.","to":"bad3","fb":"Screenshots cannot show whether the app actually works for their business."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Three weeks remain. What is the best final step?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Run a closed test on Google Play with at least 12 regular customers for 14 days, plus TestFlight for iPhone users, fix the top issues, then launch.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. This meets Google Play's requirement for new personal accounts and gives real-world feedback before Carnival."},{"t":"Skip the closed test and publish to everyone tomorrow.","to":"bad4","fb":"A new personal account cannot publish to everyone without the closed test, and there would be no real-world feedback."},{"t":"Run the closed test, but add loyalty points and two other new features halfway through.","to":"ok3","fb":"The test runs, but untested new features add risk just before the busiest week."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Smooth Carnival launch.</b> You followed a defensible sequence: unit and system tests, severity-based fixes with regression testing, UAT and usability sessions, then a closed beta that met the store's rules, with refinement after each stage.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Problems missed.</b> Friends say it looks nice, but the crash on older phones is only found after launch. Try again with a structured test plan.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Running out of time.</b> The crash is fixed only days before Carnival, leaving no time for the beta. Try again and prioritise by severity.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Shaky launch.</b> The new loyalty feature slows the order screen on J'ouvert morning. Try again and keep the MVP stable during the beta.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Bad reviews.</b> The app is slow on older phones and unreadable in sunlight. One-star ratings arrive on day one. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Crashes for many customers.</b> The logo is perfect, but the app crashes for users with older phones. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong app.</b> The owners approved pictures, then discovered on launch day that the sold-out option does not work. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Blocked launch.</b> The app cannot go public in time, and there is no feedback from real users. Try again and plan the closed test.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Online resources, communities and exam practice","html":"<p>No developer knows everything. You need to know the main <b>online mobile development resources</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Resource</th><th>What it offers</th><th>Use it well</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Official documentation</b>: developer.android.com, developer.apple.com, the MIT App Inventor site and its tutorials, Figma Help Center</td><td>Accurate guides, references, design guidelines (Material Design, Apple Human Interface Guidelines), sample code</td><td>The most reliable source; check it first and check the version date</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Stack Overflow</b></td><td>A question-and-answer community where developers ask and answer coding questions</td><td>Search before asking; read the accepted and highly voted answers; check the date; understand code before using it</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GitHub</b></td><td>Hosts code repositories, open-source projects, version control with Git, issue tracking</td><td>Back up and share your project, track versions, study open-source examples; respect each project's licence</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Communities and forums</b></td><td>The MIT App Inventor Community forum, Reddit groups, YouTube tutorials, Google Developer Groups and local hackathons</td><td>Learn from peers; Caribbean coders can connect at regional events and competitions</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>AI coding assistants</b></td><td>Explain errors and suggest code</td><td>Treat answers as drafts: test them, understand them and acknowledge help where required</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Academic integrity</b>: the course outline requires that all code, images, audio and fonts from outside sources are properly attributed and licensed. Copying a full project from GitHub and submitting it as your own is plagiarism.</p>\n<p><b>Paper 02 practical tips for this unit</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is <b>no Internet</b> in Paper 02, so you cannot use Figma online or MIT App Inventor. Expect paper wireframes, user flow diagrams, or vector wireframes in Inkscape exported as PDF.</li>\n<li>Draw phone frames to a consistent size, label every element, annotate behaviour and touch target sizes, and number the screens with arrows for the flow.</li>\n<li>Name files as instructed, for example <b>CandidateNo_Q3_flow.pdf</b>, and check that the PDF opens before you finish.</li>\n<li>For structured questions, use the correct vocabulary: purpose, target users, persona, MVP, wireframe, prototype, SDK, IDE, UAT, beta.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>SBA link</b>: the SBA asks for a product or service implemented with digital tools, plus a proposal and evidence of incremental progress. An app concept with a brief, wireframes, a Figma prototype, an App Inventor build, test logs and refinements, together with the required <b>30-second</b> audio-visual ad, makes a strong portfolio.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Keep every version. A folder of dated screenshots (v1 wireframe, v2 prototype, v3 build, bug log) is the clearest possible evidence of \"Progress and Process\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>No developer knows everything, so they use online help. Official documentation, such as developer.android.com and developer.apple.com, is the most reliable. Stack Overflow is a question-and-answer site for coding problems. GitHub stores and shares code and keeps track of versions. Forums, YouTube tutorials and local hackathons connect you with other developers. Always give credit for any code, images or sounds you use from others.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Judge a resource the way you judge any source: who wrote it, when, and for which version? A highly voted Stack Overflow answer from many years ago may use code that no longer works with current Android versions. Open-source code on GitHub is free to study, but each project has a licence that says how it may be reused, and some licences require you to share your own changes. AI coding assistants can explain an error quickly, yet they may suggest code that looks right but is wrong, so treat every suggestion as a draft to test, understand and acknowledge. Finally, practise offline skills: Paper 02 has no Internet, so your paper wireframes and user flows must come from your own understanding.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which stage or test is this?","intro":"<p>The Dominica Trails app team kept a diary of their work. Sort each entry by the stage of development it belongs to.</p>","main":"Which stage does this diary entry describe?","bins":["Conceptualisation","Wireframing and prototyping","Building","Testing and refinement"],"items":[{"t":"We agreed the MVP: trail list, offline maps, emergency contacts.","bin":0,"fb":"Defining core features and the MVP is conceptualisation."},{"t":"We wrote Kotlin code in Android Studio for the trail list.","bin":2,"fb":"Writing code in an IDE is building."},{"t":"We linked the Figma frames so testers could tap through.","bin":1,"fb":"Linking frames creates a clickable prototype."},{"t":"We wrote a persona: Andre, 35, hiker from Roseau, uses an older Android phone.","bin":0,"fb":"Personas describe target users, part of conceptualisation."},{"t":"The tourism office checked the app against the brief and signed off.","bin":3,"fb":"The client confirming requirements is user acceptance testing."},{"t":"We dragged a ListView and a Map component into the App Inventor Designer.","bin":2,"fb":"Adding components in App Inventor is building the app."},{"t":"We sketched five screens on paper with annotations.","bin":1,"fb":"Paper sketches are low-fidelity wireframes."},{"t":"Thirty hikers tried a beta and we fixed the top three issues.","bin":3,"fb":"Beta testing followed by fixes is testing and refinement."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can place each technique in the right stage: conceptualise, wireframe and prototype, build, then test and refine.","tutor":[{"h":"Plan, draw, make, check","p":"<p>Conceptualisation plans; wireframes draw; building makes; testing checks.</p>"},{"h":"Prototype vs build","p":"<p>A Figma prototype is not the real app. App Inventor or Android Studio is building.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which stage? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Writing a persona.","bin":0,"fb":"Conceptualisation."},{"t":"Sketching screens on paper.","bin":1,"fb":"Wireframing."},{"t":"Adding components in App Inventor.","bin":2,"fb":"Building."},{"t":"A beta test with hikers.","bin":3,"fb":"Testing."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which stage or test is this? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The Dominica Trails team's diary now includes less obvious entries. Sort each one by the stage of development it belongs to.</p>","items":[{"t":"We set a success measure: 8 out of 10 hikers should find a trail in under 30 seconds.","bin":0,"fb":"Setting success measures is part of defining the concept."},{"t":"We used MoSCoW to move live weather to version 2.","bin":0,"fb":"Choosing MVP features is conceptualisation."},{"t":"We interviewed park rangers about the questions hikers ask most often.","bin":0,"fb":"User research shapes the purpose and the persona."},{"t":"We drew a user flow showing what happens when there is no signal on the trail.","bin":1,"fb":"Mapping the flow, including the error path, is part of the design stage."},{"t":"We annotated the wireframe: 'SOS button 48 dp, always visible'.","bin":1,"fb":"Annotating wireframes is design work."},{"t":"We stored the trail list in TinyDB so it works offline.","bin":2,"fb":"Adding storage to the app is building."},{"t":"We wrote a procedure in the Blocks editor to calculate the distance walked.","bin":2,"fb":"Writing the app's logic is building."},{"t":"We checked that the distance procedure gives the expected result for a sample trail.","bin":3,"fb":"Checking one small part of the code gives the right result is unit testing."},{"t":"We entered 0, 1 and 999 into the distance box to see what would break.","bin":3,"fb":"Normal, boundary and extreme data are a testing technique."},{"t":"After fixing a map bug, we re-ran all our earlier tests to check nothing else broke.","bin":3,"fb":"That is regression testing, part of testing and refinement."}],"win":"Excellent. You placed the less obvious entries correctly: success measures and research in conceptualisation, error paths in design, storage in building, and unit, boundary and regression testing in the final stage."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Plan and justify a mobile app build","genre":"Structured response: applying tools and techniques to create a mobile application","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Coralstone College's student council wants a simple mobile app called \"Coralstone Connect\" so students can check the bell schedule, see event notices, and report a broken item in a classroom. The council has no budget, four weeks, and two students who have used MIT App Inventor but cannot write Kotlin or Swift. Most students use Android phones, but some use iPhones.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a development plan for Coralstone Connect that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>states the app's <b>purpose</b>, <b>target users</b> and <b>three core features</b> for the MVP;</li><li>describes how the team should <b>wireframe</b> and <b>prototype</b> the app, naming the tools;</li><li><b>recommends and justifies</b> a development tool, comparing it with <b>one</b> alternative;</li><li>outlines a <b>testing plan</b> that includes at least two types of testing and how feedback will be used.</li></ol>","min":250,"max":480,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The purpose of Coralstone Connect is to ... The target users are ... The MVP will include ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, the team should sketch low-fidelity wireframes ... Then, in Figma, ... to create a clickable prototype ...<br><b>(c)</b> I recommend ... because ... Compared with ..., ...<br><b>(d)</b> The team should first ... Then ... Feedback will be collected by ... and used to ...</p>","checklist":["I wrote a clear purpose and described the target users.","I listed three core MVP features.","I described paper wireframes and a Figma (or Balsamiq) prototype.","I recommended a tool and justified it with the scenario (budget, skills, time, platform).","I compared the tool with one alternative.","I planned at least two types of testing and explained how feedback leads to refinement."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Conceptualisation and design stage","marks":5,"look":["purpose","student|target user|persona","mvp|core feature|schedule|bell","wirefram|sketch|paper","figma|balsamiq|prototyp","mock.?up|low.?fidelity|high.?fidelity|clickable"],"min":3,"strong":"Your concept and design plan are clear, with named tools and a focused MVP.","weak":"State the purpose, users and three core features, then describe paper wireframes followed by a clickable prototype in Figma or Balsamiq."},{"criterion":"Tool recommendation and justification","marks":5,"look":["app inventor|thunkable|glide|adalo","free|budget|cost","skill|experience|no cod|block","android studio|xcode|kotlin|swift|native|flutter","because|whereas|compared|however"],"min":3,"strong":"Your recommendation is justified with the scenario and balanced against an alternative.","weak":"Recommend a tool such as MIT App Inventor or Thunkable and justify it using budget, skills, time and platform. Compare it with native tools like Android Studio or Xcode."},{"criterion":"Testing and refinement plan","marks":5,"look":["unit test|test each|component","user acceptance|uat|council|sign","beta|testflight|internal test|closed test|companion","feedback|survey|form|interview","fix|refin|improv|iterat|bug"],"min":3,"strong":"Your testing plan uses the right types of testing in a logical order, with feedback driving refinement.","weak":"Include at least two types of testing (for example testing each feature, UAT with the council, and a beta with students), then explain how feedback is collected and used to fix and refine."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The <b>purpose</b> of Coralstone Connect is to give Coralstone College students one quick place to check school information and report problems, instead of relying on scattered WhatsApp messages. The <b>target users</b> are students aged about 12 to 19, most on Android phones, often checking the app quickly between classes; a persona could be \"Tia, 16, Form 5, uses a budget Android phone on prepaid data\". The <b>MVP</b> will include: (1) the daily bell schedule, (2) a list of event notices, and (3) a simple form to report a broken item with its room number.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The team should first sketch <b>low-fidelity wireframes</b> on paper for the Home, Schedule, Notices and Report screens, labelling buttons and annotating touch targets of at least 48 dp. They should draw a <b>user flow</b> for reporting a broken item. Next, they should recreate the screens in <b>Figma</b>, link them in Prototype mode with On tap, Navigate to interactions, and ask a few classmates to tap through the <b>clickable prototype</b> on their phones before building.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I recommend <b>MIT App Inventor</b> because it is free, which suits the zero budget, and the two developers already know its Designer and Blocks editor, so they can build within four weeks. Its AI Companion allows live testing on students' phones. Compared with <b>Android Studio with Kotlin</b>, which gives more control and better performance, App Inventor needs no typed code, whereas Kotlin would take the team months to learn. However, App Inventor is strongest on Android, so the team could later consider Thunkable or a progressive web app to serve iPhone users.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, the developers should <b>test each feature on its own</b>, similar to unit testing, for example checking that the report form will not submit with a blank room number. Second, the student council should carry out <b>user acceptance testing</b>, checking the app against the agreed MVP and signing off. Third, a <b>beta</b> version should be shared with one class (by installing the .apk) for a week. Feedback will be collected with a short Google Form and by observing students using the app. The team will log every bug, fix the most serious issues first, retest, and then release the refined version to the whole school.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>\"Apply\" and \"justify\" questions reward answers tied to the scenario. Every tool choice should quote a clue from the stimulus (no budget, four weeks, App Inventor skills, mostly Android). Use the development sequence: conceptualise, wireframe, prototype, build, test, refine.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Plan and justify a mobile app build (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: planning an app build, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Complete</b>: \"The purpose of Coralstone Connect is to ...\" and <b>name</b> the target users. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>List</b> THREE features for the MVP. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE tool for making a clickable prototype and <b>say</b> why the team should prototype before building. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> the tool the team should use to build the app and <b>give</b> ONE reason from the scenario. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE type of testing the team should do. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The purpose of Coralstone Connect is to ... The target users are ...<br><b>(b)</b> The MVP features are: 1. ... 2. ... 3. ...<br><b>(c)</b> The team could use ... to make a clickable prototype. They should prototype first because ...<br><b>(d)</b> The team should use ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> One type of testing is ...</p>","checklist":["I wrote the purpose and named the students as the target users.","I listed three MVP features from the scenario.","I named a prototyping tool and gave a reason to prototype first.","I named a build tool and gave a reason from the scenario.","I named a type of testing."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Purpose and target users","marks":2,"look":["purpose|help|let|so that|check|report","student"],"min":2,"strong":"You stated the purpose and named the users.","weak":"Say what the app is for, then name the users: students at Coralstone College."},{"criterion":"Three MVP features","marks":3,"look":["schedule|bell|timetable","event|notice","report|broken|repair"],"min":2,"strong":"You listed the core features from the scenario.","weak":"List the three features in the scenario: the bell schedule, event notices and reporting a broken item."},{"criterion":"Prototype tool and reason","marks":2,"look":["figma|balsamiq|app inventor|thunkable","test|check|fix|feedback|cheap|before|problem|mistake"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a tool and explained why to prototype first.","weak":"Name Figma, then say that testing a prototype finds problems before building, when they are cheaper to fix."},{"criterion":"Build tool and reason","marks":2,"look":["app inventor|thunkable","free|budget|know|skill|used|experience|four weeks|time"],"min":2,"strong":"You chose a suitable tool and justified it with the scenario.","weak":"Choose MIT App Inventor and give a reason, such as it is free or the students already know it."},{"criterion":"Type of testing","marks":1,"look":["unit|user acceptance|uat|beta|usability|testing each"],"min":1,"strong":"You named a type of testing.","weak":"Name one type, such as beta testing or user acceptance testing."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The purpose of Coralstone Connect is to let students check school information and report problems in one place. The target users are Coralstone College students.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The MVP features are: 1. the daily bell schedule, 2. event notices, 3. a form to report a broken item in a classroom.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The team could use Figma to make a clickable prototype. They should prototype first because testing it with students finds problems before building, when they are cheaper to fix.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The team should use MIT App Inventor because it is free and the two students already know how to use it.</p><p><b>(e)</b> One type of testing is beta testing with one class.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): (a) 1 purpose, 1 users; (b) 1 per feature; (c) 1 tool, 1 reason; (d) 1 tool, 1 scenario reason; (e) 1 testing type. Ask students to underline the scenario clue they used in (d). When they can add a comparison with a second tool, move them to the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Plan and justify a mobile app build (Challenge)","bloom":"Create","genre":"Extended response: planning, designing, building and testing an app, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Coralstone College's student council wants a mobile app called \"Coralstone Connect\" so students can check the bell schedule, see event notices and report a broken item in a classroom. The council now has a <b>US$300</b> grant and <b>six weeks</b>. Two students have used MIT App Inventor, and one is learning JavaScript; none can write Kotlin or Swift. About 70 percent of students use Android phones and 30 percent use iPhones. The council wants students to be able to attach a photo of the broken item, but the IT teacher is worried that photos might show other students' faces. The vice principal also asks how the council will know whether the app is a success.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a development plan for Coralstone Connect that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>presents an <b>app concept brief</b>: purpose, a researched persona, an MVP using MoSCoW (at least two Must haves, one Should have and one Won't have this time) and ONE measurable success measure; <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>describes</b> the design stage: a user flow for reporting a broken item with at least one decision and an error path, TWO wireframe annotations, and what the prototype test will measure; <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>evaluates</b> THREE build options (MIT App Inventor, Thunkable and a cross-platform framework such as React Native) and <b>recommends</b> one; <i>(7 marks)</i></li><li><b>plans</b> testing and refinement: THREE types of testing in a logical order, how the photo privacy risk will be handled, and how feedback will be prioritised. <i>(6 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":450,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Purpose: ... Persona: ... (based on ...). Must have: ... Should have: ... Won't have this time: ... Success measure: ...<br><b>(b)</b> User flow: Start, ... decision: ... If ..., an error screen ... Annotations: \"...\" and \"...\" The prototype test will measure ...<br><b>(c)</b> MIT App Inventor ... but ... Thunkable ... whereas ... React Native ... however ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, ... Second, ... Third, ... To protect privacy, ... Feedback will be prioritised by ...</p>","checklist":["My concept brief has a one-sentence purpose, a persona based on research, MoSCoW with all required categories and a measurable success measure.","My user flow has a decision with two exits and an error path, and my two annotations name real design decisions (sizes, positions, behaviour).","I evaluated all three build options against the budget, skills, deadline and the 70/30 Android-iPhone split before recommending one.","My testing plan has three types in a logical order, a specific privacy safeguard for photos, and severity-based prioritisation with retesting.","Every part uses details from the stimulus."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Concept brief","marks":6,"look":["purpose","persona|survey|interview|research","must","should","won't|will not","%|percent|under \\d|within \\d|measur"],"min":4,"strong":"A complete brief: purpose, researched persona, full MoSCoW and a measurable success target.","weak":"Write a purpose sentence, a persona based on a survey or interviews, MoSCoW with Must, Should and Won't items, and a success measure with a number."},{"criterion":"Design stage","marks":6,"look":["user flow|flow","decision|diamond|if ","error|fail|no signal|invalid|missing","annotat|48 ?dp|thumb|label","figma|balsamiq|prototyp","task|time|tap|complete|hesitat|measure"],"min":4,"strong":"A clear flow with a decision and an error path, purposeful annotations and a measurable prototype test.","weak":"Describe the flow with at least one decision and an error screen, give two annotations (for example a 48 dp button), and say what the prototype test will measure, such as the time to submit a report."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of build options","marks":7,"look":["app inventor","thunkable","react native|flutter|cross.?platform","android|70","iphone|ios|30","budget|300|free|paid|cost","skill|know|javascript|experience|six weeks|time"],"min":5,"strong":"All three options evaluated against the scenario's constraints, with a justified recommendation.","weak":"Give a strength and a limitation for each option using the budget, skills, deadline and the 70/30 split, then recommend one with reasons."},{"criterion":"Testing, privacy and refinement","marks":6,"look":["unit|test each|feature test","uat|user acceptance|council","beta|usability|testflight|closed test","privacy|face|consent|blur|crop|moderat|only staff","severity|priorit|most serious|critical","retest|regression|fix|refin"],"min":4,"strong":"A logical testing sequence, a specific privacy safeguard and a clear prioritisation and retest loop.","weak":"Plan three tests in order (for example feature tests, UAT with the council, a beta), add a privacy safeguard for photos, and explain how bugs are ranked and retested."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Purpose:</b> Coralstone Connect gives students one quick, reliable place to check the bell schedule and events and to report broken classroom items, replacing scattered WhatsApp messages. <b>Persona</b> (based on a short survey of 40 students): Tia, 16, Form 5, uses a budget Android phone on prepaid data and checks school information in the two minutes between classes. <b>Must have:</b> the daily bell schedule; event notices; a report form with room number and description. <b>Should have:</b> attaching a photo of the broken item. <b>Could have:</b> reminders before events. <b>Won't have this time:</b> student accounts and chat. <b>Success measure:</b> within one month, 60 percent of students open the app weekly, and test users can submit a report in under one minute.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The <b>user flow</b> for reporting runs: Start, Home, tap Report, choose room, describe the problem, decision \"Add a photo?\" (yes opens the camera; no skips), then the decision \"Room and description filled in?\". If not, an error message highlights the missing field; if yes, a confirmation screen shows \"Report sent\", then End. Two <b>annotations</b> on the Report wireframe: \"Send button 48 dp, full width at the bottom in the thumb zone\" and \"Error text appears under the empty field, in words as well as red\". The Figma <b>prototype test</b> will give six students the task \"report a broken fan in Room 12\" and measure the time taken, the number of wrong taps and where they hesitate.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>MIT App Inventor</b> is free and the two developers already know it, so building could start in week one; however, it is strongest on Android, so the 30 percent of iPhone users might be left out. <b>Thunkable</b> uses a similar block style and builds for both Android and iOS from one project, but publishing to the stores needs a paid plan, which would use part of the US$300 grant. <b>React Native</b> would give the most control and suits the student learning JavaScript, whereas the others could not contribute and six weeks is too short to learn it. I recommend <b>Thunkable</b>, because it reaches both platforms, the team's App Inventor skills transfer quickly, and the grant can cover a few months of a paid plan. If the grant falls short, an App Inventor Android version plus a simple web page for iPhone users is the fallback.</p><p><b>(d)</b> First, the developers will <b>test each feature</b> separately, including boundary cases such as a blank room number. Second, the council will carry out <b>user acceptance testing</b> against the agreed MVP and sign off. Third, one class will use a <b>beta</b> version for a week. To handle <b>privacy</b>, the photo screen will tell students to photograph only the item, photos will be visible only to the staff who handle repairs, and staff will delete any photo showing a person. Feedback from a short Google Form and observation will be logged and <b>ranked by severity</b>: crashes and failed reports first, confusing screens next, appearance last. Each fix will be retested before the refined version is released to the whole school.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks): (a) 6 = purpose, persona with a research source, three MoSCoW categories, a measurable success measure. (b) 6 = flow with a decision, an error path, two meaningful annotations, a measurable prototype test. (c) 7 = a strength and a limitation for each option (6) and a justified recommendation (1). (d) 6 = three tests in a logical order, a specific privacy safeguard, prioritisation and retesting. Accept a recommendation of App Inventor if the student explains how iPhone users will be served.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M3U7","title":"Marketing and distributing mobile applications","objectives":["Describe app distribution models: app stores (Google Play, Apple App Store), direct download and sideloading, enterprise distribution and web-based access.","Explain the stages of the user journey: discover, test, review, purchase, download, update and retain.","Apply App Store Optimisation (ASO): title, description, keywords, icon, screenshots, ratings and reviews.","Describe marketing approaches: social media, websites, email, influencers, search, paid advertising, public relations, press releases, review sites and community outreach.","Plan beta testing and pre-launch community building for a Caribbean app."],"videos":[{"title":"How app store distribution works","q":"how to publish an app on google play and apple app store explained"},{"title":"App Store Optimisation basics","q":"app store optimization ASO explained for beginners"},{"title":"Marketing a mobile app launch","q":"how to market a mobile app launch strategy beta testing"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"How apps reach users: distribution models","html":"<p>Building an app is only half the job. <b>Distribution</b> is how the finished app gets onto users' devices. You need to describe several <b>distribution models</b>, including <b>web-based access</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Model</th><th>How it works</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limitations</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Official app stores</b></td><td>The developer uploads the app to <b>Google Play</b> (Android) or the <b>Apple App Store</b> (iPhone and iPad). The store reviews it, hosts it and handles payments.</td><td>Huge reach, user trust, automatic updates, built-in payment and ratings.</td><td>Developer fees, store commission on sales, review rules and possible rejection.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Direct download (sideloading)</b></td><td>The user downloads an installation file, such as an Android <b>APK</b>, from a website and installs it outside the store.</td><td>No store approval or commission; quick for testing.</td><td>Security risks, no automatic updates, users must change settings; iPhones mostly block it.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Enterprise distribution</b></td><td>An organisation distributes a private app only to its own staff, often through a <b>mobile device management (MDM)</b> system or a private store listing.</td><td>Control and privacy; the public never sees the app.</td><td>Only for internal use; needs IT management.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Web-based access</b></td><td>The \"app\" runs in the browser. A <b>progressive web app (PWA)</b> can be added to the home screen and can work offline.</td><td>One version for every device, no store fees, instant updates, nothing large to download.</td><td>Less access to some phone features; harder to discover than a store listing.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Picture the Island Pulse Media agency. <b>Jerome</b>, their mobile developer, is building three products. A bus-times app for the public goes into <b>both stores</b>. A stock-checking app for staff at an Oranjestad jewellery chain in Aruba uses <b>enterprise distribution</b>. A Carnival schedule that changes daily is built as a <b>PWA</b>, so visitors arriving at Queen Beatrix International Airport can open it from a QR code without a big download.</p>\n<p>Many developers combine models: a store app for regular users and a web version for people with low storage or older phones, which is common across the Caribbean where mid-range Android phones dominate.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Describe approaches to distributing\" means name the model, say how it works and give one advantage or limitation. A list of store names alone earns very little.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Distribution is how a finished app gets onto people's phones. There are four main ways. <b>App stores</b>, such as Google Play and the App Store, review and host the app. <b>Direct download</b> (sideloading) means installing a file from a website. <b>Enterprise distribution</b> sends a private app only to an organisation's staff. <b>Web-based access</b> means the app runs in the browser, like a Carnival schedule opened from a QR code at Aruba's airport.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choosing a model is a judgement about <b>control, trust and reach</b>. Stores give the widest reach and the most trust, but they take a commission and can reject or delay an app. A PWA gives the developer control and instant updates, but users must find it without a store's search, and some phone features are limited, especially on iPhones. Enterprise distribution trades reach for privacy and control. Many real products combine models: a store app for regular users and a PWA for visitors with full storage or older phones. A Grade One answer recommends a model for the specific audience and explains what is gained and what is given up, rather than listing store names.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which distribution model?","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is advising four clients across the region. Sort each situation into the distribution model it describes.</p>","main":"Which distribution model is being used?","bins":["App store","Direct download","Enterprise","Web-based / PWA"],"items":[{"t":"A Barbados hotel emails its housekeeping staff a private app that only company phones can install.","bin":2,"fb":"A private app for internal staff, managed by the organisation, is enterprise distribution."},{"t":"A beta version of a game is shared as an installation file in a WhatsApp group.","bin":1,"fb":"Sharing the installation file directly, outside a store, is direct download."},{"t":"A developer posts an APK file on a website and tells users to allow \"install unknown apps\".","bin":1,"fb":"Installing an APK from outside the store is direct download, also called sideloading."},{"t":"A festival in Trinidad tells visitors to scan a QR code and tap \"Add to Home Screen\".","bin":3,"fb":"Adding a site to the home screen from the browser is how a progressive web app is installed."},{"t":"A Grenada spice tour app is approved by Apple and appears in App Store search results.","bin":0,"fb":"Apple reviewed and listed it, so it is distributed through an official app store."},{"t":"A Dominica news service works in any phone browser and saves articles for offline reading.","bin":3,"fb":"Browser-based access with offline saving describes a web app or PWA."},{"t":"Electricity-company field technicians use a meter-reading app pushed to their tablets by the IT department.","bin":2,"fb":"An app pushed by IT to staff devices through device management is enterprise distribution."},{"t":"A Barbados food delivery app updates itself automatically every week through Google Play.","bin":0,"fb":"Automatic updates handled by Google Play are a feature of store distribution."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can now tell store, direct download, enterprise and web-based distribution apart by who controls the app and how it is installed.","tutor":[{"h":"Who is the audience?","p":"<p>Public users usually mean a store or the web. Only staff of one organisation means enterprise.</p>"},{"h":"How is it installed?","p":"<p>From a store listing, from a downloaded file, from IT, or from the browser? That tells you the model.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which distribution model? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"An app listed on Google Play.","bin":0,"fb":"Listed in an official store."},{"t":"An APK file downloaded from a website.","bin":1,"fb":"Installed outside the store: direct download."},{"t":"A private app only for company staff.","bin":2,"fb":"Internal only: enterprise."},{"t":"A site added to the home screen from the browser.","bin":3,"fb":"Installed from the browser: PWA."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which distribution model? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Jerome's next set of client situations is less obvious. Look for who controls the app and how it reaches the phone, then sort each one.</p>","items":[{"t":"A Curaçao bank installs a compliance app on staff phones through its mobile device management system.","bin":2,"fb":"An app pushed to staff devices by the organisation's IT system is enterprise distribution."},{"t":"A developer shares an APK in a WhatsApp group so Carnival band members can try a test build.","bin":1,"fb":"Passing the installation file around outside a store is direct download, even when the purpose is testing."},{"t":"A Jamaican newspaper's website offers 'Install app', and it then opens full-screen from the home screen.","bin":3,"fb":"Installing from the browser to the home screen is how a PWA works."},{"t":"A Belize tour app passes Apple's review before appearing in App Store search.","bin":0,"fb":"Review, hosting and search are features of official store distribution."},{"t":"A Trinidad game studio uploads an Android App Bundle through the Play Console.","bin":0,"fb":"AAB files uploaded through the Play Console are distributed through Google Play."},{"t":"Invited testers install a pre-release build from a closed testing track on Google Play.","bin":0,"fb":"It is a test, but the app still reaches phones through the official store's testing system."},{"t":"A school timetable runs in the browser and still opens with no signal because it stores its pages.","bin":3,"fb":"Browser-based access with offline caching describes a PWA."},{"t":"A hotel chain's housekeeping app appears only in a private store listing for its employees.","bin":2,"fb":"A private listing for one organisation's staff is a form of enterprise distribution."},{"t":"A user downloads a 'free premium' copy of a paid game from an unknown website.","bin":1,"fb":"Installing from an unofficial website is direct download, and copies like this often carry malware."}],"win":"Excellent. You looked past the details to who controls distribution: store test tracks are still store distribution, private listings are enterprise, and any file passed around outside a store is direct download."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Publishing to the stores, and the risks of sideloading","html":"<p><b>Getting into the stores</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Register a developer account.</b> Google Play charges a <b>one-time registration fee of roughly US$25</b>. The Apple Developer Program costs <b>roughly US$99 per year</b>. Fees change, so always check the official sites before budgeting. Non-profits and schools can sometimes apply for fee waivers.</li>\n<li><b>Prepare the build.</b> Android apps are uploaded as an app bundle (AAB) through the <b>Google Play Console</b>; iOS apps are uploaded from <b>Xcode</b> to <b>App Store Connect</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Complete the listing.</b> Title, description, icon, screenshots, category, age rating, privacy policy link and a declaration of what data the app collects.</li>\n<li><b>Pass review.</b> Apple reviews every app and update by hand and is known for strict rules. Google uses automated and manual checks. Apps can be rejected for crashes, misleading content, missing privacy information or copying other apps.</li>\n<li><b>Release and update.</b> Updates go through the same review, then reach users automatically.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Both stores take a <b>commission</b> on paid apps and in-app sales, typically <b>up to 30%</b>, with a lower rate (around 15%) for small developers and some subscriptions. You will look at this again in the revenue models unit.</p>\n<p><b>Direct download and sideloading</b></p>\n<p><b>Sideloading</b> means installing an app from outside the official store. On Android this is possible after the user allows \"install unknown apps\". The risks are serious:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Malware</b>: fake versions of popular apps can steal passwords, banking details or WhatsApp codes.</li>\n<li><b>No review</b>: nobody has checked the app's code or privacy behaviour.</li>\n<li><b>No automatic updates</b>, so security fixes are missed.</li>\n<li><b>Trust</b>: users are rightly suspicious, so legitimate developers lose downloads.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Sideloading is reasonable for <b>testing</b> or <b>enterprise</b> use where the source is trusted. For a public Caribbean app, such as a St. Lucia market-price app for farmers, the store or a PWA is safer and more credible.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Never state an exact fee as fact in an exam. Write \"a one-time fee of about US$25 for Google Play and an annual fee of about US$99 for Apple\", which shows knowledge without risking a wrong figure.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To publish an app, a developer registers an account (about US$25 once for Google Play and about US$99 a year for Apple), uploads the app, writes the store listing and waits for review. The store can reject apps that crash or hide what data they collect. Sideloading means installing an app from outside the store. It is risky, because nobody has checked the file for malware, and updates do not arrive automatically.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Store review protects users but is not a guarantee: harmful apps sometimes get through, and genuine apps are sometimes rejected for missing privacy details. That is why the listing's data safety or privacy section matters; it is part of the review and part of how users decide whether to trust you. Sideloading is not always wrong. It is reasonable for testing, or for enterprise apps from a trusted IT department, but for a public app it teaches users a dangerous habit: fake versions of popular apps are a common way of stealing WhatsApp codes and banking logins. Exam technique: give approximate fees (\"about US$25 once\", \"about US$99 a year\"), never exact figures stated as permanent.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"The sideloading decision","q":"Grand Anse Taxi Co-op wants its new booking app on members' Android phones quickly. A volunteer suggests posting the APK on a Facebook page so passengers can install it straight away. Which is the STRONGEST reason to distribute through Google Play instead?","options":[{"t":"Facebook does not allow links to be posted.","fb":"Links can be posted on Facebook. This does not explain why the store is the better choice."},{"t":"Passengers would have to allow \"install unknown apps\", the file would not be reviewed for malware, and security updates would not arrive automatically.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This names three specific risks of sideloading: weakened device security, no review and no automatic updates. These all damage user trust in a booking app that handles personal details."},{"t":"Google Play apps are always free, so passengers save money.","fb":"Store apps can be free or paid. Price is set by the developer, not by the distribution model."},{"t":"APK files cannot be installed on Android phones.","fb":"Incorrect. APK is the Android installation format, and it can be sideloaded once the user changes a setting. The issue is risk, not possibility."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Think security","p":"<p>What protections does a store give that a file posted online does not?</p>"},{"h":"Think updates","p":"<p>How would a bug fix reach users who installed a file manually?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The sideloading decision (Foundation)","q":"What is the main RISK of installing an app from an APK file found online?","options":[{"t":"It cannot have a logo.","fb":"Logos are not affected by how an app is installed."},{"t":"It may contain malware because nobody has reviewed it.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Sideloaded files skip store review."},{"t":"It will always cost more.","fb":"Price is not the issue; security is."}]},
 "up": {"title":"The sideloading decision (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"The Grand Anse Taxi Co-op app stores passengers' names, phone numbers and pickup locations. A volunteer argues: \"Sideloading is fine. We will post the APK on our verified Facebook page, so people know it really comes from us.\" Which response BEST evaluates this argument?","options":[{"t":"The argument is wrong only because Android phones cannot install APK files.","fb":"Android can install APK files once the user allows it. The problem is risk, not possibility."},{"t":"The verified page shows who posted the link, but not that the file is safe or unchanged; users must still allow unknown apps, the file skips store review and updates will not arrive automatically, so Google Play or a PWA is safer for a public app handling personal data.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. You accepted the valid part of the argument and explained the risks that remain for an app holding personal data."},{"t":"The argument is correct: a verified page removes all the risks of sideloading.","fb":"Verification shows who posted the link, not that the file is safe or that it will be kept updated."},{"t":"The argument is correct, but the co-op should also post the APK on TikTok to reach more people.","fb":"Spreading the file more widely spreads the risks too."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"The user journey: discover to retain","html":"<p>Distribution is a process that lets users <b>discover, test, review, purchase and download</b> apps, then <b>update</b> them and keep using them (<b>retain</b>). Together these form the <b>user journey</b>.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 700 90\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"User journey: discover, test, review, purchase, download, update, retain\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"13\">\n<rect x=\"5\" y=\"25\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"45\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Discover</text>\n<rect x=\"105\" y=\"25\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Test</text>\n<rect x=\"205\" y=\"25\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Review</text>\n<rect x=\"305\" y=\"25\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Purchase</text>\n<rect x=\"405\" y=\"25\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"445\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Download</text>\n<rect x=\"505\" y=\"25\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Update</text>\n<rect x=\"605\" y=\"25\" width=\"85\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"647\" y=\"50\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">Retain</text>\n<path d=\"M85 45h20M185 45h20M285 45h20M385 45h20M485 45h20M585 45h20\"/>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Discover</b>: the user first hears about the app through store search, a friend, an Instagram Reel, a newspaper article or a poster at the Bridgetown cruise terminal.</li>\n<li><b>Test</b>: the user tries before committing: a free version, a free trial, a demo video, or a beta.</li>\n<li><b>Review</b>: the user reads <b>ratings and reviews</b>, compares screenshots and checks the permissions and data safety section. After using the app, they may leave their own review, which influences the next user's discovery.</li>\n<li><b>Purchase</b>: the user pays for the app, a subscription or an in-app item, or simply taps \"Get\" if it is free. Local payment options matter: many Caribbean users lack credit cards, so gift cards and carrier billing can help.</li>\n<li><b>Download</b>: the file installs. Large downloads put users off on prepaid data, so keep the app light.</li>\n<li><b>Update</b>: new features and security fixes arrive. Good release notes remind users why the app is improving.</li>\n<li><b>Retain</b>: the user keeps coming back. Many apps are deleted within days of being installed, so retention (through useful notifications, fresh content and a smooth experience) is often harder than getting the download.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every stage is a chance to lose the user. <b>Keisha</b>, Island Pulse Media's marketing lead, plans a tactic for each stage rather than spending everything on discovery.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a Paper 02 marketing question, organise your answer by journey stage. It shows a clear structure and stops you from writing only about advertising.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The user journey is the path a person takes with an app: <b>discover</b> it, <b>test</b> it, read <b>reviews</b>, <b>purchase</b> it (or tap \"Get\" if it is free), <b>download</b> it, receive <b>updates</b> and keep using it (<b>retain</b>). Every stage is a chance to lose the user. For example, a large download can put off someone on prepaid data, and too many notifications can make them delete the app.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The journey is a loop, not a line. A retained user who leaves a good review becomes the next user's reason to discover and trust the app, so retention feeds discovery. That is why many developers watch retention as closely as downloads: an app with many installs but few returning users has a product problem that more advertising cannot fix. Caribbean details matter at specific stages: payment options such as carrier billing at purchase, app size at download, and relevant local content at retain. In a Paper 02 answer, organise your marketing by stage and give one tactic per stage. This shows structure and stops the answer from being only about adverts.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Put the user journey in order","intro":"<p>Aaliyah, a student in Linden, finds and keeps using a new Caribbean recipe app called Pepperpot Kitchen. Arrange her journey in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Discover: she sees a TikTok of black cake made with the app."},{"t":"Test: she watches the demo video and opens the free sample recipes."},{"t":"Review: she reads the 4.6-star rating and several reviews from Guyanese users."},{"t":"Purchase: she taps \"Get\" on the free app and later buys a Christmas recipe pack."},{"t":"Download: the light 25 MB app installs quickly on her data plan."},{"t":"Update: a new version adds a shopping list and fixes a bug."},{"t":"Retain: weekly \"Sunday lunch\" notifications keep her opening the app."}],"win":"Correct order. Discover, test, review, purchase, download, update, retain: each stage needs its own marketing or design tactic.","tutor":[{"h":"Start with awareness","p":"<p>Nobody can test an app they have never heard of.</p>"},{"h":"After installation","p":"<p>Which comes first once the app is on the phone: the improvement, or the long-term habit?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Put the user journey in order (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Order the four main stages.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Discover the app"},{"t":"Read reviews and ratings"},{"t":"Download the app"},{"t":"Keep using it (retain)"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Put the user journey in order (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>This time the stage names are hidden. Each block is a moment in Aaliyah's experience with Pepperpot Kitchen. Work out which stage of the journey each block shows, then put them in order. The last block shows how the journey loops back.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"A TikTok of black cake made with the app appears on Aaliyah's feed."},{"t":"She watches the app's demo video and tries two free sample recipes on its website."},{"t":"She checks the 4.6-star rating and reads reviews from other Guyanese users."},{"t":"She taps \"Get\" on the free app."},{"t":"The light 25 MB app installs quickly on her prepaid data."},{"t":"A new version adds a shopping list, and the release notes explain what changed."},{"t":"Weekly \"Sunday lunch\" notifications keep her opening the app."},{"t":"Months later she leaves a five-star review, which a classmate reads before downloading."}],"win":"Excellent. Discover, test, review, purchase, download, update, retain, and then the loop: a retained user's review helps the next user discover and trust the app."}},{"type":"learn","title":"App Store Optimisation (ASO)","html":"<p><b>App Store Optimisation (ASO)</b> is improving an app's store listing so that it ranks higher in store search and persuades more viewers to install it. It is the app-store version of <b>SEO</b>, which you met in Module 2.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Element</th><th>Good practice</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Title</b></td><td>Short, memorable, includes the main keyword. Both stores limit titles to about 30 characters. \"SLU Bus Times\" beats \"Jerome's App v2\".</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Subtitle / short description</b></td><td>Apple has a 30-character subtitle; Google Play has an 80-character short description. State the main benefit: \"Live bus times across St. Lucia\".</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Description</b></td><td>The first lines matter most, as many users never tap \"more\". Lead with benefits, then list features, using natural keywords. Google Play indexes the full description for search.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Keywords</b></td><td>Words users actually type: \"bus\", \"Castries\", \"St. Lucia\", \"schedule\". Apple has a hidden keyword field of about 100 characters. Never copy competitors' brand names.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Icon</b></td><td>Simple, bold, readable at tiny size, with no tiny text. Test it on a dark and a light home screen.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Screenshots and preview video</b></td><td>Show real screens with short benefit captions. The first two screenshots appear in search results, so make them count. A short preview video shows the app in action.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ratings and reviews</b></td><td>Higher ratings improve ranking and trust. Ask for a rating at a happy moment (after a successful order), not on first launch. Reply politely to every negative review and fix the problem.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Other ranking signals include download numbers, retention, crash rate and how recently the app was updated. <b>Localisation</b> also matters: a listing in English, French, Spanish and Papiamentu can reach St. Lucia, nearby Martinique, Curaçao and Latin American visitors.</p>\n<p><b>Keisha</b> tests two versions of the icon using the store's listing experiments and keeps the one with more installs. This is called <b>A/B testing</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> ASO and SEO are not the same: ASO improves visibility inside app stores, SEO improves visibility in web search engines. Paper 01 often offers SEO as a distractor for an ASO question.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>App Store Optimisation (ASO) means improving an app's store page so it appears higher in search results and more people install it. Use a short title with the main keyword, such as \"SLU Bus Times\", clear screenshots with captions, a bold, simple icon and a description that starts with the main benefit. Good ratings help too, so ask for a rating after a happy moment, such as a successful order.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>ASO has two separate goals: <b>visibility</b> (ranking in search, driven by keywords, downloads, ratings and how recently the app was updated) and <b>conversion</b> (persuading people who see the listing to install, driven by the icon, screenshots and reviews). Diagnose before fixing: many listing visitors but few installs is a conversion problem, while few visitors is a visibility problem. A/B testing through the stores' listing experiments replaces guesswork with evidence. Localisation is an underused Caribbean advantage: listings in Dutch, French, Spanish and Papiamentu can reach audiences that English-only competitors miss. Remember the trap: ASO works inside app stores; SEO works for web search engines.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the weak ASO","intro":"<p>The Belize City Eats app is struggling to get downloads. Here is its Google Play listing plan.</p>","q":"Select every line that is POOR App Store Optimisation.","lines":["Title: \"BCE App Final Version 3\"","Short description: \"Order from 40 Belize City restaurants and track your delivery live.\"","Icon: a photo of the whole restaurant menu with small text","First screenshot: the login screen with no caption","Keywords in the description: delivery, Belize City, Belmopan, rice and beans, fry jacks","A rating request pops up the moment the app is opened for the first time","Every one-star review receives a polite reply and a fix in the next update","The listing has not been updated for two years"],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7],"win":"Sharp eye. Weak titles, cluttered icons, dull screenshots, badly timed rating requests and stale listings all reduce ranking and installs.","miss":"Look again. Ask of each line: would this help the app rank in search, or persuade a viewer to install it?","tutor":[{"h":"Title test","p":"<p>Does the title tell a stranger what the app does and include a search word?</p>"},{"h":"Timing","p":"<p>When is a user most likely to leave a good rating: before or after they have enjoyed the app?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the weak ASO (Foundation)","q":"Select the TWO poor ASO choices.","lines":["Title: \"App Final v3\"","Short description that states the main benefit","Icon crowded with tiny text","Screenshots with clear captions"],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Vague titles and cluttered icons hurt ASO.","miss":"Which two would confuse a user scrolling the store?"},
 "up": {"title":"Spot the weak ASO (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Belize City Eats has fixed its obvious mistakes. Its new listing plan has subtler problems mixed in with good practice.</p>","q":"Select every line that is POOR App Store Optimisation.","lines":["Title: \"Eats\", one word with no location or keyword.","Short description: \"Order from 40 Belize City restaurants and track your delivery live.\"","Apple's keyword field repeats words that are already in the title.","The keywords include a competitor's brand name to capture their searches.","The first two screenshots show the menu and live tracking, with benefit captions.","The listing is in English only, although many users read Spanish and French.","A rating request appears after a successful delivery.","The first lines of the description list the team members' names.","The team tested two icons in a listing experiment and kept the one with more installs.","The listing is polished, but the app crashes on many older phones."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7,9],"win":"Excellent. You spotted the subtle problems: a keyword-free title, wasted keyword characters, competitor brand names, no localisation, weak opening lines and a crash rate that drags down ranking and ratings.","miss":"Look again. Ask of each line: does it help the app rank (visibility) or persuade people to install (conversion)? Remember that crashes and bad ratings also hurt ASO."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Marketing channels for apps","html":"<p>ASO helps people who are already searching the store. To reach everyone else, apps need wider marketing. The main channels are <b>social media, websites, email, influencers, search, advertising, public relations and community outreach</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Social media (organic)</b>: free posts, Reels, Stories and TikToks that show the app solving a real problem. Behind-the-scenes clips of the team building it work well.</li>\n<li><b>Paid social and search advertising</b>: targeted ads on Instagram, Facebook or TikTok, and <b>search ads</b> on Google or Apple Search Ads that appear when people search for keywords. You can target by location, such as people within 20 km of Kingstown, and set a daily budget.</li>\n<li><b>Influencer partnerships</b>: a local creator with a trusted audience demonstrates the app. Micro-influencers (smaller, loyal followings) often suit Caribbean islands better than international stars. Paid posts must be labelled as ads.</li>\n<li><b>Website or landing page</b>: one clear page with the benefit, screenshots, store badges and a QR code. It also helps the app appear in web search (SEO).</li>\n<li><b>Content marketing</b>: blogs, <b>YouTube tutorials</b> and <b>demo videos</b> that teach something useful while showing the app.</li>\n<li><b>Email marketing</b>: newsletters to a pre-launch sign-up list, with launch-day links and updates. Only email people who opted in.</li>\n<li><b>Public relations (PR) and press releases</b>: a short, newsworthy announcement sent to media such as national newspapers, local radio and regional tech blogs. A press kit includes the icon, screenshots, a fact sheet and a contact.</li>\n<li><b>App review sites</b> and tech bloggers who review new apps.</li>\n<li><b>Community outreach</b>: demos at schools, churches, markets, a Carnival fete or a Chamber of Commerce event; posters with QR codes at bus stops; partnerships with local businesses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The marketing content in Module 1 also covers <b>viral marketing</b>, <b>guerrilla campaigns</b> and <b>cross-platform marketing</b>, so you can use these terms too.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"describe\" a channel, give three things: what it is, a specific example for the app in the question, and why it suits that audience. Your SBA's <b>30-second audio-visual ad</b> is itself a marketing asset: plan where it would be shown.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>ASO only reaches people who are already searching the store. Other channels reach everyone else: social media posts and Reels, paid ads on social media and search engines, local influencers, a website with a QR code, emails to people who signed up, press releases to newspapers and radio, and community outreach such as a stall at a market. Choose the channels where your audience already spends time.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Separate <b>owned</b> media (your website, email list and social accounts), <b>paid</b> media (ads and sponsored posts) and <b>earned</b> media (news stories, reviews and word of mouth). Small Caribbean teams often get the best return from owned and earned media, because local audiences are close-knit and trust familiar voices; a micro-influencer with a few thousand engaged followers on one island can outperform a global star. Paid ads work best when they are tightly targeted by location and measured by cost per install. Ethics and law matter too: label paid influencer posts as ads, and only email people who opted in. Justify every channel by linking it to where the target audience actually is.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the marketing channel","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is launching the HurriReady disaster-preparedness app. Match each channel to the tactic she uses.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Influencer partnership","r":"A popular Barbadian comedian shows how he packs his hurricane kit using the app"},{"l":"Press release","r":"A one-page announcement sent to the national newspapers and local radio stations"},{"l":"Search advertising","r":"An ad appears when someone searches \"hurricane checklist app\""},{"l":"Email marketing","r":"A launch-day message to 800 people who signed up on the landing page"},{"l":"Community outreach","r":"A demo stall at a community centre in Oistins with QR posters"},{"l":"Content marketing","r":"A YouTube tutorial: \"5 things to do before a storm\", using the app"},{"l":"App review site","r":"A Caribbean tech blogger tests the app and publishes a rating"}],"win":"Excellent. You matched each channel to a concrete tactic, which is what examiners reward when you \"describe approaches to marketing\".","tutor":[{"h":"Paid or earned?","p":"<p>Search ads are paid. A press release and a review are \"earned\" media: someone else chooses to cover you.</p>"},{"h":"Who delivers the message?","p":"<p>A trusted personality = influencer. Your own inbox list = email.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the marketing channel (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Influencer","r":"A local creator demonstrates the app"},{"l":"Press release","r":"An announcement sent to newspapers"},{"l":"Email marketing","r":"A message to people who signed up"},{"l":"Community outreach","r":"A demo stall at a local event"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the marketing channel (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Keisha's full HurriReady launch plan uses close cousins of the same channels. Match each channel or technique to the tactic that shows it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Micro-influencer","r":"A St. Philip community leader with 6,000 loyal followers demonstrates the app"},{"l":"Earned media","r":"A radio station interviews the developers after receiving the press release"},{"l":"Paid social advertising","r":"Instagram ads shown only to people within 20 km of Bridgetown"},{"l":"Search advertising","r":"An ad appears when someone types \"hurricane checklist\" into the store search"},{"l":"Pre-registration","r":"Users sign up on Google Play so the app installs on launch day"},{"l":"Content marketing","r":"A YouTube series on preparing a home for storm season, using the app"},{"l":"Community outreach","r":"A demo stall in a church hall in Holetown with QR posters"},{"l":"Email marketing","r":"A launch-day message to people who opted in on the landing page"}],"win":"Excellent. You separated paid from earned media, social ads from search ads, and pre-registration from email sign-ups, which lets you justify each channel precisely."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Beta testing and building a community before launch","html":"<p>Smart developers start marketing <b>before</b> the app is finished. A <b>beta test</b> releases a nearly complete version to a limited group to find bugs and gather feedback, while also building a group of early fans.</p>\n<p><b>Beta tools</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Google Play testing tracks</b>: <b>internal</b> (a small team), <b>closed</b> (invited testers by email list) and <b>open</b> (anyone can join from the store listing). New personal developer accounts must run a closed test with a minimum number of testers for about two weeks before public release; check the current Play Console rule.</li>\n<li><b>Apple TestFlight</b>: invite testers by email or a public link; external builds get a quick review first.</li>\n<li>No-code tools such as <b>Thunkable</b> and <b>MIT App Inventor</b> let you share a test build by QR code, which is ideal for a class or SBA trial.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Building a pre-launch community</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Landing page with a sign-up form</b>: collect emails (with permission) from people who want to be notified.</li>\n<li><b>Pre-registration</b>: Google Play lets users pre-register and get the app automatically on launch day; Apple offers <b>pre-orders</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Share the journey</b>: post sneak peeks, polls (\"Which icon do you prefer?\") and team stories.</li>\n<li><b>A WhatsApp or Discord group</b> for beta testers who report bugs and feel ownership.</li>\n<li><b>Act on feedback</b>, then thank testers publicly. Happy testers become your first reviewers and word-of-mouth promoters.</li>\n<li><b>Plan launch day</b>: press release, email blast, influencer posts and a social media countdown all on the same day.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Example: the Kingstown Bus Buddy team in St. Vincent recruited 60 daily commuters as beta testers. They discovered the app drained batteries on older phones, fixed it before launch, and those testers left the first twenty reviews.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> This links directly to the 3.6 testing content (unit testing, UAT, beta). In your SBA <b>Progress and Process</b> section, evidence of beta feedback and the changes you made shows incremental development clearly.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A beta test lets a small group use an almost-finished app before launch, to find bugs and give feedback. Google Play has internal, closed and open testing tracks, and Apple uses TestFlight. At the same time, developers build a community: a landing page for sign-ups, pre-registration on Google Play, sneak peeks on social media and a WhatsApp group for testers. Happy testers often become the first reviewers.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A beta does two jobs at once: quality assurance and marketing. The St. Vincent bus app example shows both: testers found a battery problem on older phones, and the same testers then left the first reviews. To get useful feedback, choose testers who match the target audience, not just friends, and ask them to complete specific tasks. Close the loop by fixing issues and telling testers what changed, which turns them into promoters. Plan the timeline around store rules: new personal Google Play developer accounts must run a closed test with a minimum number of testers for about two weeks before public release, so the beta cannot be squeezed into launch week.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Launch day for Hairouna Trails","intro":"<p>Jerome and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are launching the Hairouna Trails app: a guide to hiking trails, heritage sites and food stops across St. Vincent for residents and cruise visitors. You are the launch manager. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The app works, but nobody outside the team has used it. The client wants it in the stores tomorrow. What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Run a two-week closed beta with 30 local hikers and tour guides, and fix what they report.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. A beta finds real-world bugs and creates your first fans and reviewers."},{"t":"Launch tomorrow; bugs can be fixed after the reviews come in.","to":"bad1","fb":"Early one-star reviews are hard to recover from and hurt ASO ranking."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The beta went well. Now you prepare the store listing. Which listing do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Copy the description of a popular international hiking app word for word.","to":"bad2","fb":"Copying is a policy and copyright problem and ignores what makes your app local."},{"t":"Title \"GT App\", a long paragraph of history, and the login screen as the first screenshot.","to":"bad2","fb":"A vague title and dull first screenshot will not rank or convert."},{"t":"Title \"Hairouna Trails: St. Vincent Hikes\", keywords such as St. Vincent, hiking, beaches, heritage, and captioned screenshots of the trail map first.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong ASO: a clear title with keywords, and the most useful screen first."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>You have a small budget. The audience is residents plus cruise visitors arriving at the Kingstown cruise berth. How do you promote the launch?</p>","choices":[{"t":"QR posters at the cruise terminal and tourist office, a press release to local newspapers and radio, two local hiking influencers, and an email to beta sign-ups.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent mix: outreach where visitors actually are, earned media, trusted voices and your warm audience."},{"t":"Spend the whole budget on one international TV advert.","to":"bad3","fb":"Expensive, poorly targeted and impossible to measure for a small local app."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>One month later, downloads are good but many users stop opening the app after their first hike. What next?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add new trails monthly, send a friendly weekend notification, prompt for a rating after a completed hike and reply to reviews.","to":"good","fb":"This targets retention, update and review: the later stages of the user journey."},{"t":"Send five promotional notifications every day.","to":"bad4","fb":"Too many notifications annoy users, who then mute or delete the app."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> Hairouna Trails keeps a 4.7 rating and steady weekly users. A Grade One answer would explain why: you tested before launch, optimised the store listing, matched marketing channels to the audience and planned for retention, not just downloads.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Rocky launch.</b> The map crashes on older Android phones and the first reviews are one star. Try again and beta test first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Invisible in the store.</b> Nobody searching \"St. Vincent hiking\" finds the app, and visitors who do see it scroll past. Try again and apply ASO.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Budget gone.</b> The TV advert reaches people who will never visit St. Vincent, and there is nothing left for local promotion. Try again and target your audience.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Mass uninstall.</b> Users feel spammed and remove the app. Retention needs value, not volume. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Test before you tell","p":"<p>Marketing a buggy app spreads bad news faster.</p>"},{"h":"Go where the users are","p":"<p>Where do cruise visitors and local hikers actually spend time, online and offline?</p>"},{"h":"The journey does not end at download","p":"<p>What keeps someone opening the app next month?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Launch day (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: launching a hiking app in St. Vincent.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Nobody outside the team has tried the app. What first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Launch immediately.","to":"bad","fb":"Bugs lead to bad early reviews."},{"t":"Run a short beta test with local hikers.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. Find bugs before the public does."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>How do you reach cruise visitors?</p>","choices":[{"t":"QR posters at the cruise terminal and a local news story.","to":"good","fb":"Right place, right audience."},{"t":"An expensive international TV ad.","to":"bad","fb":"Poorly targeted and costly."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You tested first and targeted the right audience.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Think about testing and targeting. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Launch day for Hairouna Trails (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher launch. Hairouna Trails is a guide to hiking trails, heritage sites and food stops across St. Vincent for residents and cruise visitors. Jerome's Google Play account is a new personal account, the budget is small, and the client is impatient. Some choices end the launch early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The app works, but nobody outside the team has used it. The client wants it in the stores tomorrow. What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Explain that the new account needs a closed test of about two weeks anyway, and recruit 30 local hikers and tour guides who match the audience, with set tasks.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. You turned a store rule into a proper beta with representative testers."},{"t":"Run a quick beta with the team's friends and family.","to":"ok1","fb":"A beta is right, but friends and family are not representative and tend to be too kind."},{"t":"Launch tomorrow; bugs can be fixed after the reviews come in.","to":"bad1","fb":"Early one-star reviews are hard to recover from, and the new account cannot publish to everyone yet."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Testers report that the map drains batteries on older phones and that some trail names are spelled wrongly. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Fix both, tell testers what changed, and invite them to leave honest reviews at launch.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Closing the feedback loop turns testers into your first reviewers."},{"t":"Fix the names but leave the battery issue; battery life is the user's problem.","to":"ok2","fb":"Battery drain on older phones is a real UX problem and will show up in reviews."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Which store listing do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Title \"GT App\", a long paragraph of history and the login screen as the first screenshot.","to":"bad2","fb":"A vague title and dull first screenshot will neither rank nor convert."},{"t":"Title \"Hairouna Trails: St. Vincent Hikes\", a benefit-led subtitle, keywords and a listing in English, French and Spanish, and captioned first screenshots of the trail map and offline mode.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Keywords for visibility, localisation for reach and strong screenshots for conversion."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>You have a small budget. The audience is residents plus cruise visitors arriving at the Kingstown cruise berth. How do you promote the launch?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Spend the whole budget on one international TV advert.","to":"bad3","fb":"Expensive, poorly targeted and impossible to measure for a small local app."},{"t":"QR posters at the cruise terminal and tourist office, a press release, two local hiking micro-influencers, an email to sign-ups, and a small location-targeted ad budget measured by cost per install.","to":"n5","fb":"Excellent. Owned, earned and paid media, targeted where the audience is and measured."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>One month later there are 4,000 downloads, but day-30 retention is only 8%. The client wants to spend more on ads. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Send five promotional notifications every day.","to":"bad4","fb":"Too many notifications annoy users, who then mute or delete the app."},{"t":"Pause extra ad spending, find out why users leave (a short survey and the analytics), add new trails monthly, send one weekend notification, and ask for a rating after a completed hike.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You diagnosed a retention problem and treated it at the retain stage instead of buying more downloads."},{"t":"Double the ad budget to replace the users who leave.","to":"ok3","fb":"More ads bring more installs, but most of those users will also leave. The cause is not fixed."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Strong and lasting launch.</b> Hairouna Trails keeps a high rating and steady weekly users. You tested with representative users, closed the feedback loop, optimised and localised the listing, matched channels to the audience and treated retention as a product problem, not an advertising problem.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>False confidence.</b> Friends praise the app, but the battery problem appears only after launch. Try again with testers who match the audience.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Mixed reviews.</b> The names are right, but reviewers with older phones complain about battery drain. Try again and fix the UX problem.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Expensive churn.</b> Downloads rise, but so does the number of users leaving, and the budget runs out. Try again and fix retention first.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Rocky launch.</b> The map crashes on older Android phones and the first reviews are one star. Try again and beta test first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Invisible in the store.</b> Nobody searching \"St. Vincent hiking\" finds the app. Try again and apply ASO.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Budget gone.</b> The TV advert reaches people who will never visit St. Vincent. Try again and target your audience.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Mass uninstall.</b> Users feel spammed and remove the app. Retention needs value, not volume. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Planning and measuring an app marketing campaign","html":"<p>The course outline sets an <b>App Marketing Plan</b> as a Module 3 deliverable, and Paper 02 can ask you to plan a campaign. Use this structure.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Objective</b>: make it SMART, for example \"500 downloads and a 4.5 rating within three months of launch\".</li>\n<li><b>Target audience</b>: age, location, devices, data habits and interests. For an Antigua cricket-scores app: fans aged 15 to 40, mostly Android, heavy WhatsApp users.</li>\n<li><b>Distribution choice</b>: which stores, or a PWA, and why.</li>\n<li><b>Key message</b>: one benefit in one sentence: \"Every ball of every Caribbean match, live, on low data.\"</li>\n<li><b>Channels and tactics</b> mapped to the user journey (discover, test, review, purchase, download, update, retain).</li>\n<li><b>Timeline</b>: pre-launch (beta, sign-ups), launch week, post-launch.</li>\n<li><b>Budget</b>: approximate costs, including developer accounts and any paid ads.</li>\n<li><b>Measurement</b>: how you will know it worked.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Key metrics (KPIs)</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it shows</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Store listing visitors and <b>conversion rate</b></td><td>How many people who saw the listing installed the app; low conversion suggests weak ASO.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Downloads / installs</b></td><td>Reach of the campaign.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Retention</b> (day 1, day 7, day 30)</td><td>The share of users still opening the app after a set time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Daily / monthly active users</b></td><td>Real, ongoing use.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Average rating and review count</b></td><td>Trust and satisfaction.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cost per install</b></td><td>How efficient paid ads are.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The Google Play Console and App Store Connect both provide these statistics free. Social platforms add their own analytics for posts and ads.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A plan without measurement is incomplete. End every marketing answer with at least two named metrics and what result would count as success.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A marketing plan sets a SMART objective, such as \"500 downloads and a 4.5 rating within three months\". It describes the target audience, the key message, the channels, a timeline (before, during and after launch) and a budget. It ends with measurement: downloads, ratings, retention and conversion rate show whether the plan worked. The Google Play Console and App Store Connect give these statistics free.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choose metrics that match the objective, and beware of \"vanity metrics\". Downloads look impressive, but day-30 retention and monthly active users show whether people actually value the app. Read metrics together: a high cost per install with a low conversion rate suggests that ads are bringing people to a weak listing, so fix the listing before spending more. Set a baseline and a target for each metric, and decide in advance what you will change if a target is missed. A Grade One plan links objective, channel, metric and response: \"If day-7 retention is below 25%, we will add weekly content and review the onboarding screens.\"</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Distribution and marketing quick-fire","intro":"<p>Maya and Jerome at the Island Pulse Media face these decisions for clients in Belize and Curaçao. Choose the best answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Belize client wants one product that works on any phone, needs no store approval and updates instantly. Best choice?","options":["an APK on a website","enterprise distribution","a progressive web app"],"answer":2,"fb":"A PWA runs in the browser on any device, avoids store review and updates as soon as the site changes."},{"text":"Many people see the store listing but few install it. Which metric shows this problem?","options":["retention rate","cost per install","conversion rate"],"answer":2,"fb":"Conversion rate compares listing visitors with installs. Low conversion points to weak screenshots, icon or description."},{"text":"Improving an app's title, keywords and screenshots to rank higher in the store is called...","options":["ASO","SEO","PR"],"answer":0,"fb":"App Store Optimisation works inside the app stores; SEO is for web search engines."},{"text":"A Curaçao bank wants an app only its staff can install. Which model?","options":["enterprise distribution","pre-registration","open beta on Google Play"],"answer":0,"fb":"Enterprise distribution keeps a private app within one organisation."},{"text":"Users can sign up on Google Play so the app installs automatically on launch day. This is...","options":["interstitial advertising","sideloading","pre-registration"],"answer":2,"fb":"Pre-registration builds a launch-day audience before the app is released."},{"text":"Which is an example of \"earned\" rather than paid media?","options":["a newspaper article written after a press release","a sponsored Instagram post","a Google search ad"],"answer":0,"fb":"Earned media is coverage you did not pay for directly, such as news stories and independent reviews."}],"win":"Excellent. You can choose distribution models, ASO and marketing tactics and measure their results.","tutor":[{"h":"Store or browser?","p":"<p>No approval and instant updates point to the web.</p>"},{"h":"Seen but not installed","p":"<p>Which metric compares views with installs?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Quick-fire (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"An app for staff only uses...","options":["a public store","enterprise distribution"],"answer":1,"fb":"Private and internal: enterprise."},{"text":"Improving a store listing to rank higher is...","options":["ASO","SEO"],"answer":0,"fb":"ASO is for app stores."},{"text":"Installing an APK from a website is...","options":["pre-registration","sideloading"],"answer":1,"fb":"Outside the store: sideloading."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Distribution and marketing quick-fire (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Maya and Jerome now have numbers from their clients' dashboards. Calculate where needed, then choose the BEST answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"A listing gets 10,000 visitors and 300 installs in a month. What is the conversion rate, and what does it suggest?","options":["300%, a calculation error","3%, which is low, so the icon, screenshots or description may need work","0.3%, which means downloads are failing","30%, which is excellent"],"answer":1,"fb":"300 ÷ 10,000 = 3%. Few visitors become installers, which points to a conversion problem in the listing."},{"text":"Paid ads cost US$600 and bring 400 installs. What is the cost per install?","options":["US$6.00","US$0.67","US$2.40","US$1.50"],"answer":3,"fb":"600 ÷ 400 = US$1.50 per install."},{"text":"1,000 people install in the first week, and 220 of them are still opening the app on day 30. What is day-30 retention?","options":["220%","78%","22%","2.2%"],"answer":2,"fb":"220 ÷ 1,000 = 22% retained after 30 days."},{"text":"Which objective is SMART?","options":["Become the best app in the Caribbean","Reach 500 installs and a 4.5 average rating in St. Lucia within three months of launch","Make people like the app","Get lots of downloads"],"answer":1,"fb":"It is specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound."},{"text":"A Curaçao client wants a public app for customers and a separate app for staff to check stock. Which combination fits BEST?","options":["Both apps sideloaded from a website","The public app in the stores and the staff app through enterprise distribution","The staff app in the stores and the public app through enterprise distribution","Both apps on the public stores"],"answer":1,"fb":"Stores give customers reach and trust; enterprise distribution keeps the staff app private and managed."},{"text":"Which is an example of earned media?","options":["A sponsored TikTok","A Google search ad","An email to your own sign-up list","A Caribbean tech blogger's independent review"],"answer":3,"fb":"Earned media is coverage you did not pay for. Your email list is owned media; ads are paid media."}],"win":"Excellent. You calculated conversion, cost per install and retention correctly, and chose objectives, distribution and media types like a campaign manager."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Distributing and marketing a Caribbean app","genre":"Structured response: describing distribution and marketing approaches (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Grand Case Fish Market co-operative has built FreshCatch, a free app that shows which fishing boats have landed, what fish is available and the day's prices. Customers can reserve fish for collection. The target users are residents and restaurant owners on both sides of St. Martin. The co-operative has a small budget and no marketing experience.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) <b>Describe</b> TWO distribution models FreshCatch could use, giving one advantage of each. (4 marks)<br>(b) <b>Explain</b> how App Store Optimisation could help FreshCatch be found, referring to at least THREE listing elements. (4 marks)<br>(c) <b>Describe</b> a marketing plan for the launch that uses at least THREE channels and includes a pre-launch beta and one way to measure success. (7 marks)</p>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The first model is ... This works by ... An advantage is ... The second model is ...<br><b>(b)</b> ASO means ... The title should ... Keywords such as ... Screenshots should ... Ratings ...<br><b>(c)</b> Before launch, the co-operative should ... At launch, ... After launch, ... Success will be measured by ...</p>","checklist":["I described two distribution models and gave an advantage for each.","I defined ASO and referred to at least three listing elements.","I named at least three marketing channels with specific FreshCatch examples.","I included a pre-launch beta or community-building step.","I named at least one metric to measure success.","I linked my ideas to St. Martin users and the small budget."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Distribution models","marks":4,"look":["google play|app store|store","pwa|progressive|web app|browser","direct|apk|sidelo","review|trust|update|reach|no fee|instant"],"min":2,"strong":"You described the models clearly with advantages. Next step: say which one you would recommend first and why.","weak":"Name two models (for example a Google Play listing and a PWA), explain how each works and give one advantage, such as automatic updates or no store fees."},{"criterion":"App Store Optimisation","marks":4,"look":["aso|optimi","title","keyword","screenshot|icon","rating|review","description"],"min":3,"strong":"Your ASO explanation covers several elements with good examples.","weak":"Define ASO, then explain at least three elements: for example a title with \"fish\" and \"St. Martin\", local keywords, and captioned screenshots of the daily price list."},{"criterion":"Marketing plan and measurement","marks":7,"look":["beta|test","social|instagram|facebook|tiktok|whatsapp","influencer|chef|restaurant","press|daily herald|radio|news","poster|qr|market|outreach|community","email|sign.?up","download|install|rating|retention|active user|conversion"],"min":4,"strong":"A well-structured plan with channels, beta and measurement. Next step: add a simple timeline.","weak":"Plan in stages: a beta with fishermen and restaurant owners, then launch-day channels (QR posters at the market, a Daily Herald story, a local chef on Instagram), then measure downloads, ratings or retention."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> FreshCatch could be distributed through the <b>official app stores</b>, Google Play and the Apple App Store. The co-operative registers developer accounts (about US$25 once for Google Play and about US$99 a year for Apple), uploads the app and passes review. An advantage is trust: users know store apps are reviewed, and updates with new prices or features install automatically. A second model is a <b>progressive web app</b>, opened in the browser from a link or QR code and added to the home screen. An advantage is that it works on any phone with no store fees and no large download, which suits older phones and prepaid data.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> App Store Optimisation is improving the store listing so the app ranks higher in store search and more viewers install it. The <b>title</b> should include keywords, such as \"FreshCatch: St. Martin Fish Prices\", rather than just \"FC\". <b>Keywords</b> in the description such as \"Grand Case\", \"fresh fish\", \"seafood\" and \"Marigot\" match what local users type. The first <b>screenshots</b> should show the live catch list and prices with short captions, because they appear in search results. Finally, asking happy users for <b>ratings</b> after a successful reservation raises the average rating, which improves both ranking and trust.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Pre-launch:</b> run a two-week closed beta with ten fishermen and twenty restaurant owners and regular customers, fix their reported problems, and collect email sign-ups on a simple landing page. <b>Launch:</b> place QR-code posters at the fish market and partner restaurants; send a short press release to The Daily Herald and local radio on both sides of the island; ask a well-known Grand Case chef to post an Instagram Reel of reserving fish for the day's menu; and email everyone on the sign-up list on launch day. These channels are cheap and reach the right people. <b>Post-launch:</b> post daily catch highlights on Facebook and WhatsApp Status, and reply to every review. <b>Measurement:</b> success will be 300 installs and a 4.5 average rating in the first two months, plus a day-30 retention rate above 30%, tracked free in the Google Play Console and App Store Connect.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Mark allocations guide length: 7 marks for (c) means it should be the longest part. Organise a marketing plan by time (pre-launch, launch, post-launch) or by user-journey stage, name specific channels with local examples, and always finish with a measurable target.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Distributing and marketing a Caribbean app (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: distribution and marketing, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> TWO ways FreshCatch could be distributed. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> what ASO means and <b>give</b> TWO parts of the store listing that could help people find FreshCatch. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> THREE marketing channels the co-operative could use, with a FreshCatch example for each. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE thing the co-operative should do before launch. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE way to measure whether the launch worked. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":80,"max":230,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> FreshCatch could be distributed through ... and ...<br><b>(b)</b> ASO means ... Two parts that could help are ... and ...<br><b>(c)</b> First, ... for example ... Second, ... for example ... Third, ... for example ...<br><b>(d)</b> Before launch, the co-operative should ...<br><b>(e)</b> Success can be measured by ...</p>","checklist":["I named two distribution models.","I explained ASO and named two listing elements.","I named three channels with a FreshCatch example for each.","I gave one pre-launch step.","I gave one way to measure success."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Two distribution models","marks":2,"look":["google play|app store|store","pwa|progressive|web app|browser|website"],"min":2,"strong":"You named two distribution models.","weak":"Name two models, such as Google Play and a progressive web app."},{"criterion":"ASO meaning and two elements","marks":3,"look":["optimi|rank|store search|found","title|keyword","screenshot|icon|rating|review|description"],"min":2,"strong":"You explained ASO and named two elements.","weak":"Say ASO means improving the store listing so the app ranks higher, then name two parts, such as the title and screenshots."},{"criterion":"Three channels with examples","marks":3,"look":["social|instagram|facebook|tiktok|whatsapp","poster|qr|market|press|newspaper|radio|daily herald","influencer|chef|restaurant|email|community"],"min":2,"strong":"You named three channels with FreshCatch examples.","weak":"Name three channels, such as QR posters at the fish market, a Facebook post and a chef on Instagram."},{"criterion":"Pre-launch step","marks":1,"look":["beta|test|try|sign.?up|pre.?regist"],"min":1,"strong":"You gave a pre-launch step.","weak":"Say the co-operative should run a beta test with fishermen and restaurant owners."},{"criterion":"Measuring success","marks":1,"look":["download|install|rating|review|retention|users"],"min":1,"strong":"You gave a way to measure success.","weak":"Name a measure, such as the number of downloads or the average rating."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> FreshCatch could be distributed through Google Play and as a progressive web app (PWA) opened from a QR code.</p><p><b>(b)</b> ASO means improving the store listing so the app ranks higher in store search and more people install it. Two parts that could help are a title with keywords, such as \"FreshCatch: St. Martin Fish Prices\", and clear screenshots of the day's catch list.</p><p><b>(c)</b> First, QR-code posters, for example at the Grand Case fish market. Second, social media, for example daily catch photos on Facebook. Third, an influencer, for example a local chef reserving fish in an Instagram Reel.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Before launch, the co-operative should run a beta test with fishermen and restaurant owners.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Success can be measured by the number of downloads in the first month.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): (a) 1 per model; (b) 1 for the meaning, 1 per element; (c) 1 per channel with a FreshCatch example; (d) 1; (e) 1. Push students to make every example specific to St. Martin. Once they can add an advantage to each distribution model, move them to the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Distributing and marketing a Caribbean app (Challenge)","bloom":"Create","genre":"Extended response: distribution and marketing plan, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Grand Case Fish Market co-operative has built FreshCatch, a free app that shows which fishing boats have landed, what fish is available and the day's prices. Customers can reserve fish for collection. The target users are residents and restaurant owners on both sides of St. Martin; many residents on the French side read French first, and about half of restaurant owners use iPhones. The co-operative has a marketing budget of about <b>US$500</b> and no marketing experience. The fishermen also want a separate, private app to log their catches from the boat, which the public must not see.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a distribution and marketing plan for FreshCatch that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>evaluates</b> THREE distribution models for the customer app and <b>recommends</b> a combination, and <b>states</b> how the fishermen's catch-logging app should be distributed; <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>explains</b> an ASO strategy that improves both visibility and conversion, using at least FOUR listing elements, including localisation; <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>plans</b> a launch campaign mapped to at least FIVE stages of the user journey, with a SMART objective, a pre-launch beta and channels that fit the budget; <i>(9 marks)</i></li><li><b>selects</b> THREE metrics, sets a target for each and <b>explains</b> what the co-operative would change if one target is missed. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":450,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Store distribution would ... but ... A PWA would ... whereas ... Direct download would ... I recommend ... The catch-logging app should ...<br><b>(b)</b> For visibility, ... For conversion, ... Localisation ...<br><b>(c)</b> Objective: ... Discover: ... Test: ... Review: ... Download: ... Retain: ... Budget: ...<br><b>(d)</b> Metric 1: ... target ... Metric 2: ... Metric 3: ... If ... is missed, ...</p>","checklist":["I gave a strength and a limitation for store, PWA and direct download, recommended a combination and chose enterprise distribution for the private app.","My ASO strategy separates visibility from conversion and covers at least four elements, including a French listing.","My campaign covers at least five journey stages, starts with a SMART objective, includes a beta and stays within about US$500.","My three metrics each have a target, and I explained what would change if one is missed.","Every part uses details from the stimulus: both sides of the island, French readers, iPhone users, the budget and the private app."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Distribution evaluation","marks":6,"look":["google play|app store|store","pwa|progressive|web","direct|apk|sidelo","enterprise|private|mdm|internal","trust|review|reach|fee|commission|security|malware","recommend"],"min":4,"strong":"All three models evaluated, a justified combination and the right model for the private app.","weak":"Give a strength and a limitation for stores, a PWA and direct download, recommend a combination for customers, and choose enterprise distribution (or a private listing) for the fishermen's app."},{"criterion":"ASO for visibility and conversion","marks":5,"look":["visib|rank|search","conver|install|persuad","title|keyword|subtitle","screenshot|icon|rating|review","french|locali|translat"],"min":4,"strong":"A two-goal ASO strategy with localisation.","weak":"Explain which elements raise ranking (title, keywords, ratings, updates) and which raise installs (icon, screenshots, reviews), and add a French-language listing."},{"criterion":"Campaign mapped to the user journey","marks":9,"look":["discover","test|beta|trial","review|rating","download|install|purchase|get","update","retain|retention|come back","smart|within|by the end|months","\\$|us\\$|budget|500","poster|qr|press|radio|influencer|chef|email|whatsapp|facebook|instagram"],"min":6,"strong":"A complete, staged campaign with a SMART objective, a beta and channels that fit the budget.","weak":"Start with a SMART objective, then give at least one tactic for each of five journey stages, include a beta, and show the budget adds up to about US$500."},{"criterion":"Metrics, targets and response","marks":5,"look":["conversion","retention|active user","rating|review|install|download|cost per install","target|%|at least|above|below","if .{0,60}(missed|below|low|under)|we would|we will change"],"min":3,"strong":"Three metrics with targets and a clear plan if one is missed.","weak":"Choose three metrics (for example conversion, day-30 retention and rating), give each a target, and explain what you would change if one is missed."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Store distribution</b> through Google Play and the App Store gives trust, reach and automatic updates, and it is needed because about half of restaurant owners use iPhones; however, the developer fees and review take time and money. A <b>PWA</b> opened from a QR code works on any phone with no store fees and no large download, which suits residents with older phones, but it is harder to discover and has limited access to some phone features. <b>Direct download</b> of an APK avoids review and fees but exposes users to malware risks and gets no automatic updates, so it is unsuitable for an app that takes reservations. I recommend store listings on both platforms plus a PWA for anyone who will not install an app. The fishermen's catch-logging app should use <b>enterprise distribution</b>, such as a private listing or device management, so only co-operative members can install it.</p><p><b>(b)</b> For <b>visibility</b>, the title \"FreshCatch: St. Martin Fish Prices\" contains the main keywords, the description uses words people type, such as \"Grand Case\", \"fresh fish\", \"seafood\" and \"Marigot\", and regular updates and good ratings help ranking. For <b>conversion</b>, a bold fish icon readable at small sizes and two first screenshots captioned \"Today's catch\" and \"Reserve in one tap\" show the benefit at a glance. <b>Localisation</b> into French, with a translated title, description and screenshots, reaches French-side residents whom an English-only listing would miss.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>SMART objective:</b> 400 installs across both sides of the island and a 4.5 average rating within two months of launch. <b>Test (pre-launch):</b> a two-week closed beta with ten fishermen and twenty restaurant owners and regular customers, plus a landing page collecting email sign-ups. <b>Discover:</b> bilingual QR-code posters at the fish market, partner restaurants and Marigot market (US$120); a press release to The Daily Herald and to radio on both sides (free); and a Grand Case chef posting an Instagram Reel (US$150). <b>Review:</b> testers are invited to leave honest reviews on launch day, and the app asks for a rating after a successful collection. <b>Download:</b> a light app, with store badges and QR codes everywhere, plus the PWA link. <b>Update and retain:</b> fortnightly updates with clear release notes, a morning notification when boats land and every review answered. A small location-targeted Facebook ad (US$200) runs in launch week, measured by cost per install. Total: about US$470.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>Conversion rate:</b> at least 25% of listing visitors install. <b>Day-30 retention:</b> at least 30% of users still open the app after a month. <b>Average rating:</b> 4.5 or higher. If conversion is below target, the co-operative would A/B test new screenshots and the icon before spending more on ads. If retention is low, it would survey users and add features they ask for, such as price alerts for favourite fish, rather than buying more installs.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks): (a) 6 = strength and limitation for each customer model (3), a justified combination (2), enterprise distribution for the private app (1). (b) 5 = visibility, conversion, four elements, localisation. (c) 9 = SMART objective (1), beta (1), five journey stages with tactics (5), budget that adds up (1), channels suited to the audience (1). (d) 5 = three metrics with targets (3) and a specific response to a missed target (2). Accept other reasonable metric targets if they are justified.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M3U8","title":"Revenue models for mobile applications","objectives":["Discuss commercial revenue models: paid apps, freemium, in-app purchases, in-app advertising (banner, interstitial, rewarded video) and subscriptions.","Explain developer fees, app licensing and white-label apps as sources of income for developers.","Describe open-source and community-supported models: crowdfunding, grants, donations and sponsorship, with examples such as Wikipedia and Signal.","Discuss civic, charity and social good apps for disaster preparedness, health, safety, fundraising, education and public services.","Evaluate and justify a revenue model for a Caribbean app using user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and long-term sustainability."],"videos":[{"title":"How do free apps make money?","q":"how do free apps make money revenue models explained"},{"title":"Freemium vs subscription vs ads","q":"freemium vs subscription vs in-app ads app monetization explained"},{"title":"White-label apps explained","q":"what is a white label app explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What is a revenue model?","html":"<p>A <b>revenue model</b> is the plan for how an app earns the money it needs to cover its costs and, for a business, make a profit. Even a free app costs money: developer accounts, servers, maintenance, updates, customer support and marketing.</p>\n<p>Revenue models fall into three families:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Family</th><th>Models</th><th>Main goal</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Commercial</b></td><td>Paid apps, freemium, in-app purchases, in-app advertising, subscriptions, developer fees, licensing, white-label apps</td><td>Profit for the business</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Open source and community-supported</b></td><td>Crowdfunding, grants, donations, sponsorship</td><td>Keep a shared or public tool running</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Civic, charity and social good</b></td><td>Funded by governments, NGOs, charities, grants or partners</td><td>Public benefit rather than profit</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The models to know are freemium, in-app purchases, in-app advertising, subscriptions, developer fees, licensing, <b>paid apps</b>, <b>white-label apps</b>, <b>grants</b> and <b>sponsorship</b>. You must also <b>evaluate</b> models on user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and long-term sustainability.</p>\n<p><b>Paid apps (premium or pay-to-download)</b></p>\n<p>The user pays once before downloading, for example US$2.99. Everything is then unlocked, with no ads.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Advantages</b>: simple, predictable, no ads, and no need to collect personal data for advertising.</li>\n<li><b>Disadvantages</b>: the price is a barrier, especially where many users lack credit cards or have little spending money; the store takes a commission (up to about 30%); there is no ongoing income from existing users unless a new version is sold.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Paid apps suit specialist tools with clear value, such as a professional photo editor or a well-known game. For most Caribbean consumer apps, a price tag before trying stops downloads.</p>\n<p>At the Island Pulse Media, <b>Zara</b> always asks clients one question before design starts: \"Who pays, for what, and why would they?\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss\" means give advantages <b>and</b> disadvantages, then reach a judgement. Never just describe a model; weigh it.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A revenue model is the plan for how an app earns the money it needs. Even a free app has costs: store accounts, servers, updates, support and marketing. There are three families: <b>commercial</b> models (such as paid apps, ads and subscriptions) that aim for profit, <b>community-supported</b> models (donations, grants, sponsorship) and <b>civic or charity</b> models that serve the public. Always ask: who pays, for what, and why would they?</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The person who uses an app is not always the person who pays for it. In an ad-funded app, advertisers pay and users \"pay\" with their attention and often their data; in a licensed school app, the school pays for its students; in a civic app, taxpayers or donors pay. Identifying the real payer is the key to a Grade One answer, because it explains incentives: an app funded by advertisers is pushed to keep users scrolling, while one funded by subscribers is pushed to keep users satisfied. Paid apps are the simplest model but face the biggest barrier in the Caribbean, where many users lack credit cards and few will pay for an app before trying it.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Freemium and in-app purchases","html":"<p><b>Freemium</b> = <b>free</b> + <b>premium</b>. The core app is free; extra features cost money. Users can try the app with no risk, and a small share upgrade. Spotify, Duolingo and Canva all use freemium.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Advantages</b>: no barrier to download, large user base, word of mouth, users pay only once they see the value.</li>\n<li><b>Disadvantages</b>: typically only a small percentage of users ever pay; the free tier must be good enough to attract users but limited enough that people upgrade; serving many free users still costs money.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>In-app purchases (IAPs)</b> are items bought inside the app. There are three main types:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Consumable</b></td><td>Used up, can be bought again</td><td>Coins, extra lives, hints in a game</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Non-consumable</b></td><td>Bought once, kept forever</td><td>Removing ads, unlocking a \"Christmas recipes\" content pack, a new filter set</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Subscription</b></td><td>Recurring access</td><td>Monthly premium tier (covered on its own screen)</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Virtual goods, content packs and unlockable features are the three categories the course outline names. IAPs are processed by the store's billing system (Google Play Billing, Apple In-App Purchase), so the store commission applies.</p>\n<p><b>Freemium and IAPs are related but different.</b> Freemium is the overall strategy (free core, paid upgrade). In-app purchases are the payment mechanism that often delivers the upgrade. A free game with coins for sale uses IAPs; a free app with a paid \"Pro\" tier is freemium.</p>\n<p><b>Ethical concerns</b>: some games use \"pay-to-win\" mechanics or <b>loot boxes</b> (random rewards) that can encourage compulsive spending, especially by teenagers. Parents in Jamaica and Trinidad have reported surprise bills from children's purchases. Good practice: clear prices, parental controls and no pressure tactics.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A classic Paper 01 trap: \"freemium\" is NOT the same as \"free with ads\". Freemium means a paid upgrade exists.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Freemium means the main app is free, but extra features cost money, like a free language app with paid offline lessons. In-app purchases (IAPs) are things you buy inside an app. <b>Consumable</b> items, such as game coins, get used up and can be bought again. <b>Non-consumable</b> items, such as removing ads or a recipe pack, are bought once and kept. <b>Subscriptions</b> charge again and again.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Freemium is a balancing act. If the free tier is too generous, nobody upgrades; if it is too limited, nobody stays long enough to see the value. Many freemium apps convert only a small share of users, so they need a large audience, which is harder on small islands unless the app reaches the whole region or the diaspora. In-app purchases raise ethical questions that examiners reward you for noticing: random-reward loot boxes resemble gambling, and children can run up bills on a parent's account. Good practice includes clear prices, parental controls and no countdown pressure. Remember the trap: freemium is a strategy (free core, paid upgrade), while an IAP is the payment mechanism that often delivers it.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase?","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing how different apps charge users. Sort each description.</p>","main":"Which commercial model does this describe?","bins":["Paid app","Freemium","In-app purchase"],"items":[{"t":"Users pay US$4.99 before they can download a Carnival costume design app.","bin":0,"fb":"Payment before download, with everything unlocked, is a paid (premium) app."},{"t":"A cricket game lets players unlock a new stadium for US$1.99.","bin":2,"fb":"Unlocking a feature inside the app with a single payment is an in-app purchase."},{"t":"A photo editor is free with basic filters; professional tools are in a paid version.","bin":1,"fb":"Basic features free, advanced features paid: freemium."},{"t":"A sailing navigation app costs US$9.99 in the store and has no ads or upgrades.","bin":0,"fb":"One upfront price with no further charges is a paid app."},{"t":"A domino game sells packs of 500 coins that can be bought again and again.","bin":2,"fb":"Coins are consumable virtual goods bought inside the app: in-app purchases."},{"t":"A Caribbean language app is free, but offline lessons and no ads need \"Plus\".","bin":1,"fb":"A free core with a paid upgrade tier is the freemium model."},{"t":"A recipe app sells a one-off \"Christmas in Jamaica\" content pack.","bin":2,"fb":"A content pack bought inside the app is a non-consumable in-app purchase."}],"win":"Well sorted. Paid apps charge before download, freemium offers a free core with a paid tier, and in-app purchases sell items or features inside the app.","tutor":[{"h":"When is money taken?","p":"<p>Before download = paid app. After download, inside the app = freemium or IAP.</p>"},{"h":"Tier or item?","p":"<p>A whole premium version is freemium. A specific item, pack or unlock is an in-app purchase.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Paid, freemium or IAP? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Pay before downloading.","bin":0,"fb":"Upfront payment: paid app."},{"t":"Free app with a paid Pro version.","bin":1,"fb":"Free core, paid upgrade: freemium."},{"t":"Buy coins inside a game.","bin":2,"fb":"Items bought inside: IAP."},{"t":"Buy a bonus content pack inside the app.","bin":2,"fb":"A pack bought inside: IAP."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Paid, freemium or in-app purchase? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Jerome's full review now includes subscriptions and advertising as well. Sort each app into the commercial model it mainly uses. Read the detail carefully: who pays, how often, and for what?</p>","main":"Which commercial model does this mainly describe?","bins":["Paid app","Freemium","In-app purchase","Subscription","In-app advertising"],"items":[{"t":"A free study app lets users pay once to remove ads for good.","bin":2,"fb":"Removing ads with a one-off payment is a non-consumable in-app purchase."},{"t":"A free domino game earns money when players choose to watch a 15-second video for an extra turn.","bin":4,"fb":"The advertiser pays for the rewarded video, so this is in-app advertising."},{"t":"A fitness app charges US$5 every month for new workout plans.","bin":3,"fb":"A recurring monthly fee for continued access is a subscription."},{"t":"A tide-chart app costs US$3.99 once, with no ads or extras.","bin":0,"fb":"One upfront price with everything included is a paid app."},{"t":"A professional navigation app for yacht crews must be bought before it can be downloaded.","bin":0,"fb":"Payment before download is the paid (premium) model."},{"t":"A Carnival game sells costume outfits that players keep forever once bought.","bin":2,"fb":"Virtual goods bought once inside the app are non-consumable in-app purchases."},{"t":"A recipe app charges US$20 a year for access to every new recipe.","bin":3,"fb":"An annual fee for continued access is still a subscription."},{"t":"A free bus-times app shows a small strip advertising a Negril restaurant.","bin":4,"fb":"A banner ad paid for by a business is in-app advertising."},{"t":"A word game sells packs of hints that are used up and can be bought again.","bin":2,"fb":"Hints that are used up are consumable in-app purchases."},{"t":"A free design app keeps its basic tools free, and a 'Pro' tier unlocks advanced tools.","bin":1,"fb":"A free core with a paid upgrade tier is freemium."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated five commercial models by who pays and how often: once before download, a paid upgrade tier, items bought inside the app, a recurring fee, or advertisers paying for attention."}},{"type":"learn","title":"In-app advertising and subscriptions","html":"<p><b>In-app advertising</b></p>\n<p>The app is free and earns money by showing ads from networks such as <b>Google AdMob</b>. Advertisers pay per thousand views (impressions) or per click, so income depends on having many active users.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Format</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>Pros and cons</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Banner ad</b></td><td>A small strip at the top or bottom of the screen</td><td>Least disruptive, but low earnings and easy to ignore; takes up space on small screens</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Interstitial ad</b></td><td>A full-screen ad between screens or levels</td><td>Higher earnings, but annoying if shown too often; badly placed ones cause accidental taps</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Rewarded video</b></td><td>The user <b>chooses</b> to watch a short video in return for a reward (extra life, bonus recipe)</td><td>Users like it because it is optional; good earnings; suits games best</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Native ad</b></td><td>Styled to blend in with the app's content, labelled \"Sponsored\"</td><td>Less intrusive, but must be clearly labelled</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Concerns</b>: ads use data (costly on prepaid plans), drain battery, slow the app and often rely on tracking personal data. Ads in children's apps face strict rules. Local advertising can help: a Grenada events app could sell ad space directly to St. George's shops and Grand Anse restaurants.</p>\n<p><b>Subscription models</b></p>\n<p>Users pay a recurring fee, monthly or annually, for continued access. Examples: <b>Spotify Premium</b>, <b>Netflix</b>, <b>Adobe Creative Cloud</b> and <b>YouTube Premium</b>. Annual plans are usually discounted to encourage commitment, and free trials let users test first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Advantages</b>: predictable <b>recurring revenue</b> that funds ongoing updates and content; the developer is rewarded for keeping users happy.</li>\n<li><b>Disadvantages</b>: <b>subscription fatigue</b> (users tire of many monthly fees); the app must keep adding value or users <b>churn</b> (cancel); prices in US dollars can be steep for Caribbean users; card payments can be a barrier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Subscriptions suit apps with ongoing content or services: news (The Gleaner or Trinidad Express digital editions), music, fitness or cloud storage. They suit a one-off tool, such as a tip calculator, poorly.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Know the three ad formats by name: banner, interstitial and rewarded video. Paper 01 frequently describes one and asks you to name it.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>With in-app advertising, the app is free and earns money each time ads are seen or clicked. <b>Banner</b> ads are small strips, <b>interstitial</b> ads fill the screen between pages, and <b>rewarded videos</b> are ads the user chooses to watch for a reward. With a <b>subscription</b>, users pay every month or year to keep using the app, like Spotify Premium or a newspaper's digital edition.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Ads and subscriptions create opposite incentives. Ad income grows with attention, so ad-funded apps are tempted to add interruptions and collect data for targeting; subscription income grows with satisfaction, so the app must keep delivering value or users churn. In the Caribbean, ad income is limited by small audiences, and ads cost users prepaid data, but locally sold ads (a Grand Anse restaurant in an events app) can be more relevant and earn more per view. Subscriptions face fatigue and US-dollar pricing, so regional pricing and annual discounts help. A strong evaluation names the ad format (banner, interstitial, rewarded video) and judges its effect on the user experience, not just on income.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the model to the example","intro":"<p>Match each revenue term to the app example that illustrates it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Banner ad","r":"A thin strip advertising a Grand Anse restaurant runs along the bottom of a bus-times app"},{"l":"Interstitial ad","r":"A full-screen car advert appears between levels of a Carnival dance game"},{"l":"Rewarded video","r":"A player chooses to watch a 15-second ad to earn an extra life"},{"l":"Subscription","r":"A Barbados fitness app charges US$5 every month for new workouts"},{"l":"Consumable IAP","r":"Buying 100 gems in a game, which can be spent and bought again"},{"l":"Freemium","r":"A free note-taking app with a paid tier for cloud backup and extra storage"},{"l":"Paid app","r":"A tide-chart app for fishermen costs US$2.99 to download"}],"win":"Very good. You can name each commercial model and ad format from a description, exactly as Paper 01 tests it.","tutor":[{"h":"Ad size and choice","p":"<p>Small strip, full-screen interruption, or optional video for a reward?</p>"},{"h":"One-off or recurring?","p":"<p>Repeated monthly payments mean subscription.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the model (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Banner ad","r":"A small strip ad at the bottom of the screen"},{"l":"Interstitial ad","r":"A full-screen ad between levels"},{"l":"Rewarded video","r":"Watch an ad by choice to earn a bonus"},{"l":"Subscription","r":"Pay every month for access"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the model to the example (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The harder set: business-to-business and community models as well as consumer ones. Match each revenue term to the example that illustrates it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Native ad","r":"A post styled like the app's other listings and labelled 'Sponsored'"},{"l":"Non-consumable IAP","r":"A one-off payment unlocks a Christmas recipe pack forever"},{"l":"Licensing","r":"Schools in Grenada pay a yearly fee per student to use an attendance app"},{"l":"White-label app","r":"One ordering app is rebranded with a different name and logo for each restaurant"},{"l":"Development contract","r":"A hotel pays an agency 30% at wireframes, 40% at beta and 30% at launch"},{"l":"Crowdfunding","r":"400 backers pledge small amounts before a farming app is built"},{"l":"Grant","r":"A foundation funds a clinic-finder app and requires progress reports"},{"l":"Sponsorship","r":"A telecoms company pays for a hurricane app, and its logo appears on the start screen"}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify consumer, business and community revenue models from their details, including the difference between a grant, crowdfunding and sponsorship."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Developer fees, licensing and white-label apps","html":"<p>Not all app income comes from end users. Developers also earn from <b>businesses</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Developer fees (development contracts)</b></p>\n<p>A client pays a developer or agency to build an app. Payment may be a fixed project price, an hourly rate, or milestones (for example, 30% at wireframe approval, 40% at beta, 30% at launch). Ongoing <b>maintenance fees</b> cover updates, hosting and support. This is how agencies such as the Island Pulse Media earn most of their income: Jerome builds apps for clients rather than selling his own.</p>\n<p>Note that \"developer fees\" can also mean the <b>fees developers pay</b> to the stores (about US$25 once for Google Play, about US$99 a year for Apple) and the store commission. Read the question carefully to see which meaning is intended.</p>\n<p><b>App licensing</b></p>\n<p>The developer keeps ownership of the software and sells a <b>licence</b>: permission for another organisation to use it, usually for a yearly fee per user, per location or per device. For example, a Trinidad company could license its school-attendance app to schools in Guyana and Grenada at a fee per student per year. Licensing also covers selling the rights to use code, a game engine or content inside someone else's app.</p>\n<p><b>White-label apps</b></p>\n<p>A <b>white-label app</b> is a ready-made app that a developer builds once and then <b>rebrands</b> for many clients with each client's name, logo and colours. The client appears to own a custom app, but the core code is shared.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">Example: the Island Order app lets restaurants take orders. Island Pulse Media rebrands it as \"Lolo Lovers Grill\" for one client and \"Guavaberry Café\" for another, charging each a setup fee and a monthly licence fee.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Advantages</b>: quick and cheaper for clients; repeat income for the developer from one codebase.</li>\n<li><b>Disadvantages</b>: less unique; clients depend on the developer for updates; branding must be done carefully so each version looks distinct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You may see this written as \"white paper fees\" in older materials, but the correct term is <b>white-label apps</b>. Use \"white label\" in your answers.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For a business-to-business (B2B) app, such as one for hotels or schools, licensing or white-label pricing is usually stronger than ads or in-app purchases. Explain why: the business, not the individual user, gets the value.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Some app income comes from businesses, not users. With <b>developer fees</b>, a client pays an agency such as Island Pulse Media to build an app. With <b>licensing</b>, the developer keeps ownership and charges others a yearly fee to use the app, such as a school-attendance app licensed to schools in Guyana. A <b>white-label</b> app is built once and rebranded with each client's name and logo.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>For business-to-business apps the value goes to the organisation, so it makes sense for the organisation to pay. Licensing turns one product into repeat income, but it brings responsibilities: support, updates and keeping each client's data separate and secure. White-label apps scale well across the region because many islands share similar needs, yet the developer must manage many brands from one codebase, and a single bug affects every client at once. Read questions carefully, because \"developer fees\" can also mean the fees developers pay to the stores. A Grade One answer explains <b>who</b> pays, <b>why</b> the model suits that payer, and one risk with a way to reduce it, such as an agreement that guarantees regular updates.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Which business model?","q":"Caribbean Taxi Tech company built one booking app. It now sells the same app to taxi associations in St. Lucia, Antigua and Dominica, each with its own name, logo and colours, for a setup fee plus a monthly charge. Which model BEST describes this?","options":[{"t":"In-app advertising","fb":"Nothing in the scenario mentions ads. The income comes from the taxi associations, not advertisers."},{"t":"White-label app with licensing fees","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. One codebase rebranded for many clients is white-label, and the monthly charge for permission to use it is a licensing fee."},{"t":"Freemium","fb":"Freemium is a free core app with a paid upgrade for end users. Here, businesses pay for a rebranded copy."},{"t":"Crowdfunding","fb":"Crowdfunding collects many small contributions from the public, usually before a product exists."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Same app, many brands","p":"<p>What is it called when one product is rebranded for each client?</p>"},{"h":"Who pays?","p":"<p>Businesses paying for permission to use software suggests licensing.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which business model? (Foundation)","q":"One app is rebranded with different names and logos for many clients. This is a...","options":[{"t":"paid app","fb":"A paid app charges users before download."},{"t":"white-label app","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Same core, different branding."},{"t":"banner ad","fb":"A banner is an ad format."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which business model? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A Jamaican developer's school-attendance app is used by teachers in 40 schools. Option A: free for schools, with banner ads shown on teachers' and students' screens. Option B: an annual licence per student paid by each school, with no ads. Option C: a one-off paid download for every parent. Which evaluation is BEST?","options":[{"t":"Option A is strongest, because a free app is always the best choice for users.","fb":"Free to schools, but ads distract in class, may track students' data, and small audiences earn little."},{"t":"Option C is strongest: schools receive the value, so they pay; having no ads protects students' privacy and attention; and annual fees fund ongoing updates.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The payer matches the beneficiary, the model is ethical for children, and it is sustainable."},{"t":"Option B is strongest, because parents care most about attendance.","fb":"Teachers use the app, not parents; many parents would not pay, and a one-off fee gives no money for updates."},{"t":"All three are equally good, because they all bring in money.","fb":"Evaluation compares user value, privacy, ethics and sustainability, not only whether money comes in."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Open source and community-supported models","html":"<p><b>Open-source</b> software makes its source code public so anyone can inspect, use, change and share it under a licence. Many open-source apps are free and carry no ads. So how do they survive?</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Donations</b>: users give voluntarily. <b>Wikipedia</b> (run by the non-profit Wikimedia Foundation) is funded mainly by donations from readers, which is why you see fundraising banners on its site and app.</li>\n<li><b>Grants</b>: money given by foundations, governments or development agencies for a specific purpose, which does not have to be repaid. Caribbean developers can look to regional bodies such as the Caribbean Development Bank or international programmes; check current calls for proposals.</li>\n<li><b>Crowdfunding</b>: many people each contribute a small amount through platforms such as Kickstarter, Indiegogo or GoFundMe, often <b>before</b> the app is built, sometimes in return for rewards or early access.</li>\n<li><b>Sponsorship</b>: a company or organisation funds the project in return for recognition, or because it benefits from the tool existing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Signal</b>, the private messaging app, is run by the non-profit Signal Foundation. It is funded by donations and grants rather than ads, so it has no reason to collect or sell users' data. This shows how a revenue model links directly to <b>privacy</b>: an ad-funded messenger has an incentive to learn about you; a donation-funded one does not.</p>\n<p>Other community models include paid support or hosting for businesses (the code is free; the service costs money), and volunteer developers who contribute code.</p>\n<p><b>Strengths</b>: trust, transparency, privacy, a community that helps improve the app, no barrier to users.<br><b>Weaknesses</b>: income is unpredictable; it may depend on a few large donors or a grant that ends; volunteers may move on.</p>\n<p>A Caribbean example could be the Kalinago Words app in Dominica, an open-source language-preservation tool funded by a cultural grant, community donations and sponsorship from a local telecoms company.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Distinguish donations (given after, voluntarily, nothing expected back) from crowdfunding (pledged for a specific project, often before it exists, sometimes for a reward). Grants usually come from institutions and have conditions and reports attached.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Open-source software shares its code publicly so anyone can use and improve it. Many open-source apps are free with no ads, so they rely on community support: <b>donations</b> from users (like Wikipedia), <b>grants</b> from foundations or governments, <b>crowdfunding</b> from many people before the app is built, and <b>sponsorship</b> from companies in return for recognition. A language app in Dominica could be funded this way.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Community models show most clearly how funding shapes behaviour. Signal can promise not to collect data for advertising because its non-profit, donation-and-grant model gives it no reason to; an ad-funded messenger has the opposite incentive. The weakness is predictability: grants end, donations rise and fall, and volunteers move on. Sustainable projects therefore combine sources and build a community that contributes code as well as money. Distinguish the terms precisely: donations are voluntary and expect nothing back; crowdfunding pledges support a specific project, often before it exists; grants come from institutions with conditions and reports; sponsorship exchanges money for recognition, which can raise questions about the sponsor's influence.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"How is it funded?","intro":"<p>Identify the community-supported funding model in each situation.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Guyanese team raises US$8,000 from 400 backers on a crowdfunding platform before they start coding a farming app.","options":["subscription","crowdfunding","licensing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Many small pledges for a specific future project is crowdfunding."},{"text":"A foundation gives a Jamaican health-app team money to build a clinic finder, with progress reports required.","options":["donation","in-app purchase","grant"],"answer":2,"fb":"Institutional funding for a defined purpose, with conditions and reports, is a grant."},{"text":"Readers of Wikipedia see a banner asking them to give whatever they can.","options":["donation","sponsorship","interstitial ad"],"answer":0,"fb":"Voluntary gifts from users are donations; this is Wikipedia's main funding source."},{"text":"A telecoms company funds a free hurricane-alert app and its logo appears on the start screen.","options":["sponsorship","white-label","freemium"],"answer":0,"fb":"Funding in return for recognition is sponsorship."},{"text":"Signal can avoid collecting users' data for ads mainly because...","options":["it is funded by donations and grants through a non-profit","it shows only banner ads","it charges a high download price"],"answer":0,"fb":"Signal's non-profit, donation-and-grant model removes the commercial incentive to track users."}],"win":"Excellent. You can distinguish crowdfunding, grants, donations and sponsorship, and link funding to privacy.","tutor":[{"h":"Before or after?","p":"<p>Pledges before a project exists usually mean crowdfunding.</p>"},{"h":"Who gives, and what do they get?","p":"<p>A foundation with conditions = grant. A company wanting its logo seen = sponsor.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"How is it funded? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Many people pledge small amounts online before the app is built.","options":["crowdfunding","subscription"],"answer":0,"fb":"Many small pledges: crowdfunding."},{"text":"Users voluntarily give money to keep Wikipedia running.","options":["ads","donations"],"answer":1,"fb":"Voluntary gifts: donations."},{"text":"A company pays and gets its logo shown.","options":["sponsorship","licensing"],"answer":0,"fb":"Recognition for funding: sponsorship."}]},
 "up": {"title":"How is it funded? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Go beyond naming the model: judge the risks and choices that come with community and civic funding.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Barbados team offers 300 backers early access and a thank-you in the credits for funding the app before it exists. Which model is this, and what is its main risk?","options":["Donations; the money must be repaid","Crowdfunding; backers may lose trust if the app is late or never delivered","Licensing; schools may cancel","A grant; no reports are needed"],"answer":1,"fb":"Pledges for a future project are crowdfunding, and the team must deliver what it promised or lose backers' trust."},{"text":"Which statement BEST distinguishes a grant from a donation?","options":["They are the same thing","A grant must be repaid, but a donation does not","A grant comes from an institution for a set purpose with conditions; a donation is a voluntary gift that expects nothing back","Donations always come from governments"],"answer":2,"fb":"Grants carry conditions and reporting; donations are freely given."},{"text":"A bank offers to sponsor a free literacy app for children if every child sees a pop-up promoting its loans. What is the BEST response?","options":["Decline that condition: keep sponsor recognition modest and never target children with financial adverts","Offer the children's data to the bank instead","Shut the app down","Accept; sponsors decide the content"],"answer":0,"fb":"Sponsorship is acceptable, but not on terms that exploit child users."},{"text":"Why can a donation-funded messenger make stronger privacy promises than an ad-funded one?","options":["It uses no servers","It has no commercial reason to collect and sell users' data","Donors pay for ad tracking","Donation-funded apps are not allowed to store any data"],"answer":1,"fb":"The funding model removes the incentive to track users."},{"text":"An open-source app's code is free. Which is a sustainable way for its team to earn income without charging ordinary users?","options":["Selling users' contact lists","Adding loot boxes","Hiding the code again","Selling paid support and hosting to businesses that use it"],"answer":3,"fb":"Businesses pay for service and reliability while the code stays open and free."},{"text":"A Dominica language app depends on one cultural grant that ends next year. Which plan BEST reduces the risk?","options":["Combine community donations, an annual telecoms sponsorship and a small licence fee for schools","Close the app when the grant ends","Add full-screen ads before every lesson","Hope for a second grant"],"answer":0,"fb":"Several independent sources mean no single ending can close the app."}],"win":"Excellent. You judged the risks of crowdfunding, the conditions of grants, the limits of sponsorship and the value of mixing funding sources."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Civic, charity and social good apps","html":"<p>Some apps exist for <b>public good</b> rather than profit. They are often free to users and paid for by governments, charities, NGOs, international agencies, grants or corporate partners. Key areas are disaster preparedness, fundraising, health, safety, education and public services.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Category</th><th>Caribbean example</th><th>Typical funding</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Disaster preparedness</b></td><td>Hurricane alerts, shelter locations and checklists. After Hurricane Irma in 2017, Sint Maarten residents relied heavily on phones and WhatsApp for updates; an \"Island Ready\" app could bring shelter maps and power outage notices into one place.</td><td>Government disaster offices, regional agencies such as CDEMA, international donors</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Health</b></td><td>Clinic finders, appointment reminders, vaccination records, mental health support</td><td>Ministries of health, PAHO-style programmes, health NGOs</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Safety</b></td><td>Report a crime, emergency SOS button, road hazard reporting</td><td>Police services, municipalities, telecom partners</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fundraising and charity</b></td><td>A Carnival charity-walk app that collects pledges for a children's home in Grenada</td><td>Charity budgets, a small transaction fee, sponsorship</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Education</b></td><td>Free CXC revision apps, library apps, literacy games</td><td>Ministries of education, foundations, grants</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Public services (e-government)</b></td><td>Pay utility bills, renew a licence, book a passport appointment</td><td>Government budgets, sometimes a small service fee</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Why the revenue model still matters.</b> A civic app has no sales, but it has costs. Many social good apps are launched with a one-off grant and then abandoned when the money runs out, leaving users with outdated information, which is dangerous for a disaster app. A sustainable plan names <b>who pays for maintenance</b> each year.</p>\n<p><b>Mixed models</b> are common. A fundraising app may take a small percentage of each donation to cover costs; a health app may be free for patients but licensed to private clinics; a government app may be white-labelled from a regional supplier.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA asks for a product or service that is \"preferably local social or entrepreneurial\". If your group's idea is a social good app, include a short sustainability plan explaining how it will be funded after launch. That is the kind of depth the Proposal section rewards.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Civic and social good apps are made to help the public, not to make a profit. Examples include hurricane alert apps, clinic finders, crime-reporting apps, free revision apps and apps for paying government bills. They are usually free to users and paid for by governments, charities, grants or company partners. They still have running costs, so someone must keep paying for updates and servers.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The biggest risk for civic apps is the \"launch and abandon\" pattern: a one-off grant pays for the build, then the money runs out and the information goes stale. For a disaster app, an outdated shelter list is worse than no app at all. A sustainable plan names who pays for maintenance every year, for how long, and who is responsible for keeping the content accurate. Mixed models are common and sensible: a small transaction fee on donations, a free public version with paid licences for private clinics, or a regional white-label app shared by several governments to split the costs. When evaluating, also ask whether the funding could create a conflict, such as ads appearing before emergency alerts.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Is this civic app sustainable?","intro":"<p>The Grenada Flood Watch team presents its funding plan for a free flood-alert app.</p>","q":"Select every line that makes the app MORE sustainable in the long term.","lines":["A three-year agreement with the national disaster agency to fund server costs","A one-off school prize of US$500 is the only funding","Corporate sponsorship from a local bank, renewed annually","The app will sell users' location data to advertisers","A volunteer community of island developers maintains the open-source code","Full-screen ads appear before every flood warning","A small annual licence fee from neighbouring islands that adopt the app"],"correct":[0,2,4,6],"win":"Good judgement. Long-term agreements, renewed sponsorship, a maintenance community and licensing to other islands all support sustainability without harming users.","miss":"Look again. A one-off prize runs out, selling location data breaks trust and privacy, and ads before emergency alerts delay urgent safety information.","tutor":[{"h":"Will it last?","p":"<p>Does this source of money or support continue after year one?</p>"},{"h":"Does it harm users?","p":"<p>A civic app must stay trustworthy and fast in an emergency.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Is this sustainable? (Foundation)","q":"Select the TWO lines that support long-term sustainability.","lines":["A multi-year government funding agreement","One small prize as the only money","Annual sponsorship from a local company","Selling users' personal data"],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Ongoing, ethical funding keeps the app alive.","miss":"Which sources continue every year without harming users?"},
 "up": {"title":"Is this civic app sustainable? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Grenada Flood Watch has a more detailed funding plan. Judge each line on long-term sustainability AND on whether it keeps users' trust.</p>","q":"Select every line that makes the app MORE sustainable without harming users.","lines":["A three-year agreement with the national disaster agency covers server costs, with a named officer responsible for updates.","A one-off grant pays for development, and no budget is set aside for updates.","Annual sponsorship from a local bank, shown as a small logo on the About screen.","The app is free, but it sells \"anonymised\" location trails to an advertising broker.","Volunteer developers maintain the open-source code, with a documented handover process.","A sponsor's video advert must play before every flood warning.","Neighbouring islands that adopt the app pay a small annual licence fee.","Hosting costs are shared with two other islands through a regional agreement.","All funding depends on one volunteer paying the server bill from his own salary.","A yearly public report shows funders how many alerts were sent and how many people used them."],"correct":[0,2,4,6,7,9],"win":"Excellent. Multi-year agreements with clear responsibility, modest sponsorship, a documented volunteer community, licensing, shared regional costs and transparent reporting all keep the app alive and trusted.","miss":"Look again. Location trails can often be traced back to individuals, ads before warnings delay safety information, and a one-off grant or a single volunteer's salary is not a long-term plan."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Evaluating and choosing a revenue model","html":"<p>Evaluation should consider <b>user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and long-term sustainability</b>. Use these five criteria as a checklist whenever you judge a model.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>User value</b>: does the payment match what users get? Do they pay only once they see the benefit?</li>\n<li><b>Privacy</b>: does the model depend on collecting or sharing personal data (as targeted ads often do)?</li>\n<li><b>Accessibility and affordability</b>: can people with low incomes, no credit card, older phones or disabilities still use it? Do ads break screen readers?</li>\n<li><b>Ethics</b>: is it honest and fair, especially to children? No hidden costs, manipulative loot boxes or misleading \"free\" labels.</li>\n<li><b>Long-term sustainability</b>: will it fund updates, servers and support for years, not just launch?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Comparison table</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Model</th><th>Best for</th><th>Main risk</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Paid</td><td>Specialist tools with clear value</td><td>Price barrier, few downloads</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Freemium</td><td>Apps people use often</td><td>Few users upgrade</td></tr>\n<tr><td>In-app purchases</td><td>Games, content packs</td><td>Overspending, ethics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Advertising</td><td>Very large audiences</td><td>Privacy, annoyance, data cost</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subscription</td><td>Ongoing content or services</td><td>Churn, fatigue, cost in US$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Licensing / white-label</td><td>Business clients</td><td>Small market, dependency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Community-supported</td><td>Open-source, public tools</td><td>Unpredictable income</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Civic / grant-funded</td><td>Public services, safety</td><td>Funding ends</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Caribbean factors to weigh</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Small island populations mean fewer users, so ad income alone is rarely enough; the <b>diaspora</b> and tourists can widen the market.</li>\n<li>Many users have no credit card: consider store gift cards, carrier billing through operators such as TelEm, Digicel or Flow where supported, or local payment partners.</li>\n<li>Prices in US dollars feel high in local currencies (guilders, EC dollars, JMD); regional pricing helps.</li>\n<li>Prepaid data makes heavy video ads costly for users.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Hybrid models</b> are normal: free with ads, plus a paid \"remove ads\" option, plus sponsorship from a local business.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> To \"justify\" a choice, name the model, give two reasons linked to the specific app and audience, admit one weakness and say how you would reduce it. That balance is what lifts an answer into the top band.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To judge a revenue model, use five tests. <b>User value</b>: is the price fair for what users get? <b>Privacy</b>: does it depend on collecting personal data? <b>Accessibility</b>: can people with low incomes or no credit card still use it? <b>Ethics</b>: is it honest, especially with children? <b>Sustainability</b>: will it keep paying for the app for years? In the Caribbean, also think about small populations, US-dollar prices and prepaid data.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The five criteria often conflict, and showing how you resolve the conflict is what lifts an answer. Advertising scores well on accessibility (free to everyone) but poorly on privacy and data cost; subscriptions score well on sustainability but poorly on affordability. That is why hybrid models are normal: a free core for access, a small paid upgrade for those who can pay, and sponsorship or licensing to fill the gap. Caribbean payment realities are a criterion in their own right: offering store gift cards, or carrier billing where operators support it, can decide whether a paid tier works at all. To justify a choice, name the model, give two app-specific reasons, admit one weakness and explain how you would reduce it.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"The monetisation lab: Bim Moves","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is advising Bim Moves, a new app listing events, beach parties, live music and Crop Over activities across Barbados. The audience is local young people aged 16 to 30 and visitors. Help choose a revenue model.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The founders want to charge US$6.99 to download the app so they make money from day one. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Make the core listings free so many people download it; a price would stop most young locals from trying it.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. An events guide depends on a large audience, and a price barrier kills discovery."},{"t":"Agree: a high price shows quality.","to":"bad1","fb":"Few people pay for an unknown app, especially a guide they can partly replace with Instagram."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The app will be free. How should it earn money?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Collect and sell users' location history to marketing companies.","to":"bad3","fb":"This is a serious privacy and ethics breach and may break data protection law."},{"t":"Charge event organisers and bars a fee for featured listings, plus a small banner slot sold to local businesses.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. The businesses that gain customers pay, and local ads are relevant to users."},{"t":"Show a full-screen interstitial ad every time a user opens an event.","to":"bad2","fb":"Constant interruptions drive users away and waste their prepaid data."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Some regular users ask for extra features: early ticket alerts and no ads. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"An optional \"Bim Moves Plus\" at a low monthly price, with clear terms and easy cancellation.","to":"n4","fb":"A freemium tier rewards loyal users without taking anything away from free users."},{"t":"Remove all free features and force everyone onto a subscription.","to":"bad1","fb":"Removing the free tier loses most of the audience that event organisers are paying to reach."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Many young users do not have credit cards. How do you handle payments for Plus?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Accept international credit cards only; that is the users' problem.","to":"bad4","fb":"This excludes a large part of the target audience."},{"t":"Support store gift cards and check carrier billing with local operators, and price it for the local market.","to":"good","fb":"This improves accessibility and affordability for the real audience."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> Bim Moves grows fast, organisers pay for featured spots, and a loyal group pays for Plus. A Grade One answer would explain why: you combined models (free core, B2B listing fees, local ads, freemium tier) and evaluated them on user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and sustainability.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Hardly any users.</b> The price or paywall stops young locals from trying the app, and organisers lose interest. Try again and lower the barrier.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Users leave.</b> Reviews complain about constant full-screen ads and the rating drops to two stars. Try again with less intrusive income.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Scandal.</b> A news story reveals the data sale and users delete the app. Privacy is part of evaluating a model. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded audience.</b> Few young users can pay, so Plus fails. Try again and think about local payment access.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Who benefits?","p":"<p>If businesses gain customers from the app, could they be the ones who pay?</p>"},{"h":"The five criteria","p":"<p>User value, privacy, accessibility, ethics, sustainability: test each choice against them.</p>"},{"h":"Local payment reality","p":"<p>How do Caribbean teenagers actually pay for things online?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Monetisation lab (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: a free events app for young people in Barbados.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Should the app cost US$6.99 to download?</p>","choices":[{"t":"No: keep it free so many people try it.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. A large audience is the app's main asset."},{"t":"Yes: charge upfront.","to":"bad","fb":"A price stops most people trying it."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>How should it earn money?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Sell users' location data.","to":"bad","fb":"A privacy and ethics breach."},{"t":"Event organisers pay for featured listings.","to":"good","fb":"The businesses that benefit pay."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You matched the model to the audience and protected privacy.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Think about price barriers and privacy. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"The monetisation lab: Bim Moves (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief from Bim Moves, the app listing events, beach parties, live music and Crop Over activities across Barbados for local people aged 16 to 30 and visitors. This time a big sponsor is involved, and several choices are partly right. Some choices end the scenario early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The founders want to charge US$6.99 to download the app so they make money from day one. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep the core listings free: an events guide depends on a large audience, and a price would stop most young locals from trying it.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. The audience is the app's main asset, so the barrier to entry must be low."},{"t":"Agree: a high price shows quality.","to":"bad1","fb":"Few people pay for an unknown app, especially a guide."},{"t":"Offer a 7-day free trial, then charge US$6.99.","to":"ok1","fb":"A trial lowers the barrier a little, but few people will pay for a guide they can partly replace with Instagram."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The app will be free. How should it earn money?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Rewarded video ads only.","to":"ok2","fb":"Rewarded video suits games, where users want the reward. Events-app users have little reason to watch."},{"t":"Collect and sell users' location history to marketing companies.","to":"bad3","fb":"This is a serious privacy and ethics breach."},{"t":"A full-screen interstitial ad every time a user opens an event.","to":"bad2","fb":"Constant interruptions drive users away and waste prepaid data."},{"t":"Featured listings paid by event organisers and bars, plus a few local banner slots, with no ads on the ticket screens.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. The businesses that gain customers pay, and ads stay out of the key task."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>An international drinks brand offers a large sponsorship if the app pushes its party adverts to every user, including 16- and 17-year-olds. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Accept; the money is too good to refuse.","to":"bad4","fb":"Promoting alcohol to minors is unethical and would destroy trust with parents and the community."},{"t":"Decline that condition: accept sponsorship only with age-gated content and no alcohol promotion to under-18s, or look for a different sponsor.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. You protected young users and the app's reputation while keeping sponsorship possible."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Regular users ask for early ticket alerts and no ads. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Remove all free features and make everyone subscribe.","to":"bad1","fb":"Removing the free tier loses most of the audience that organisers are paying to reach."},{"t":"An optional 'Bim Moves Plus' at a low monthly price, with clear terms and easy cancellation.","to":"n5","fb":"A freemium tier rewards loyal users without taking anything away from free users."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>Many young users do not have credit cards. How do you handle payments for Plus?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Accept international credit cards only.","to":"bad5","fb":"This excludes a large part of the target audience."},{"t":"Support store gift cards, use carrier billing where local operators support it, and set a price suited to the local market.","to":"good","fb":"This makes the paid tier accessible to the real audience."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>A sustainable, ethical model.</b> Bim Moves grows, organisers pay for featured spots, a loyal group pays for Plus, and the sponsorship protects young users. You combined models (free core, business listing fees, local ads, a freemium tier) and tested each choice against user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and sustainability.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Slow growth.</b> A few people try the trial, but almost nobody pays, and organisers see too small an audience. Try again and remove the barrier.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Little income.</b> Hardly anyone watches the videos, so the app earns very little. Try again and think about who gains most from the app.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Hardly any users.</b> The price or paywall stops young locals from trying the app, and organisers lose interest. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Users leave.</b> Reviews complain about constant full-screen ads and the rating falls. Try again with less intrusive income.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Scandal.</b> A news story reveals the data sale and users delete the app. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Public backlash.</b> Parents and schools complain, local sponsors pull out and the app's reputation collapses. Try again and put young users first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded audience.</b> Few young users can pay, so Plus fails. Try again and think about local payment access.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Steps to choose a revenue model","intro":"<p>Arrange the steps a developer should follow to choose a revenue model for a new Caribbean app.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Define the app's purpose and who benefits from it (users, businesses, the public)."},{"t":"Research the audience: income, payment methods, devices and data habits."},{"t":"Estimate ongoing costs: store fees, servers, updates, support and marketing."},{"t":"List possible models (commercial, community-supported, civic) and how competitors earn."},{"t":"Evaluate each model on user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and sustainability."},{"t":"Choose and justify a model or hybrid, then test it with beta users."},{"t":"Monitor revenue and user feedback after launch, and adjust."}],"win":"Correct order. Purpose, audience, costs, options, evaluation, choice and review: a model chosen this way is justified, not guessed.","tutor":[{"h":"Understand before you decide","p":"<p>You cannot judge a model until you know the users and the costs.</p>"},{"h":"After launch","p":"<p>A revenue model is not fixed forever.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing a model (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Order the four main steps.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Understand the users and purpose"},{"t":"List possible revenue models"},{"t":"Evaluate them against criteria"},{"t":"Choose, justify and monitor"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Steps to choose a revenue model (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The full professional process includes testing willingness to pay and a plan for changing course. Put all nine steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Define the app's purpose and identify who benefits: users, businesses or the public."},{"t":"Research the audience: income, payment methods, devices and data habits."},{"t":"Estimate set-up and running costs for the next three years."},{"t":"List possible models and study how similar apps in the region earn money."},{"t":"Score each model on user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and sustainability."},{"t":"Combine the strongest options into a hybrid and identify its main weakness."},{"t":"Test prices and willingness to pay with beta users."},{"t":"Launch, then monitor revenue, retention and reviews against targets."},{"t":"Adjust the model if targets are missed, and tell users clearly about any changes."}],"win":"Excellent. Purpose, audience, costs, options, evaluation, hybrid, testing, monitoring and transparent adjustment: a revenue model built on evidence."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Discuss revenue models for a Caribbean app","genre":"Structured response: discussing and justifying revenue models (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The CXC Buddy team, three students in Georgetown, Guyana, has built a revision app with past-paper style questions, short videos and a study timer for CSEC and CAPE students across the region. Some features cost money to run (video hosting and servers). The team wants the app to be affordable, respectful of students' privacy, and still running in five years.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) <b>Distinguish</b> between the freemium model and in-app advertising. (4 marks)<br>(b) <b>Discuss</b> TWO other revenue models CXC Buddy could use, giving one advantage and one disadvantage of each. One must be a community-supported or civic model. (6 marks)<br>(c) <b>Recommend and justify</b> a revenue model (or hybrid) for CXC Buddy, evaluating it against user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and long-term sustainability. (5 marks)</p>","min":230,"max":470,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> In the freemium model ... whereas in-app advertising ... For CXC Buddy, ...<br><b>(b)</b> One model is ... An advantage is ... However, ... A second model is ... An advantage ... A disadvantage ...<br><b>(c)</b> I recommend ... because ... In terms of privacy, ... For accessibility, ... Ethically, ... To be sustainable, ... One weakness is ..., which could be reduced by ...</p>","checklist":["I contrasted freemium and in-app advertising on the same feature, using \"whereas\".","I discussed two other models with an advantage and a disadvantage for each.","One of my models was community-supported or civic (grant, sponsorship, donations, crowdfunding).","I made a clear recommendation and justified it.","I referred to user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and sustainability.","I linked my points to Caribbean students and the CXC Buddy app."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Distinguishing freemium and advertising","marks":4,"look":["freemium","premium|upgrade|paid (tier|version|feature)|pro","advert|ads?\\b","banner|interstitial|rewarded|advertiser","whereas|while|however|unlike"],"min":3,"strong":"A clear distinction with both sides explained. Next step: add a CXC Buddy example to each side.","weak":"Freemium gives a free core app with a paid premium upgrade, whereas in-app advertising keeps the app free and earns money from advertisers through banner, interstitial or rewarded video ads."},{"criterion":"Discussion of other models","marks":6,"look":["subscri|paid app|in-app purchase|licens|white.?label","grant|sponsor|donat|crowdfund|ministry|civic","advantage|benefit|strength","disadvantage|drawback|however|risk|weakness"],"min":3,"strong":"Balanced discussion of two models. Next step: make each advantage and disadvantage specific to students in the region.","weak":"Choose two models (for example licensing to schools and a grant or sponsorship) and give one advantage and one disadvantage for each, linked to CXC Buddy."},{"criterion":"Recommendation and evaluation","marks":5,"look":["recommend|i would choose|best model|most suitable","value","privacy|data","access|afford|credit card","ethic|fair|honest|child","sustain|long.?term|five years|ongoing"],"min":4,"strong":"A justified recommendation tested against the syllabus criteria. Well done.","weak":"State one model or hybrid clearly, then test it against all five criteria: user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and long-term sustainability, and note one weakness with a fix."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> In the <b>freemium</b> model the core app is free, but users pay to unlock premium features, so income comes from the minority of users who upgrade. For CXC Buddy, practice questions could be free while full video lessons and mock exams are in a paid \"Plus\" tier. <b>In-app advertising</b>, by contrast, keeps every feature free and earns money from advertisers who pay to show banner, interstitial or rewarded video ads, so income depends on the number of active users and ad views rather than on users paying.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One model is <b>licensing to schools</b>: CXC Buddy could charge a school, such as Hummingbird Ridge High School, an annual fee per student so that all its students get Plus. An advantage is reliable, larger payments from one institution, with students paying nothing. A disadvantage is that schools have limited budgets and long approval processes, so sales may be slow. A second, community-supported model is <b>grants and sponsorship</b>: the team could apply to an education foundation for a grant and seek sponsorship from a regional bank or telecoms company, credited on the start screen. An advantage is that students in every island could use the app free, without ads. A disadvantage is that grants are often for one or two years and sponsors can withdraw, so funding is uncertain.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I recommend a <b>hybrid</b>: a free core funded by sponsorship, a low-cost freemium Plus tier, and licences for schools. This gives strong <b>user value</b>, because every student can practise for free and only pays for extra content they choose. It protects <b>privacy</b> because it avoids targeted ads that track students' data, which matters as many users are under 18. It supports <b>accessibility</b> because schools can buy Plus for students without credit cards, and the app stays light for prepaid data. It is <b>ethical</b> because there are no loot boxes or hidden charges, and prices are shown clearly. For <b>long-term sustainability</b>, three income streams mean the app does not depend on any single source. One weakness is that managing sponsors and school sales takes time; the team could reduce this by offering one simple standard school licence and renewing sponsorship each year with a short impact report.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>A \"discuss\" question needs both sides; a \"recommend and justify\" question needs a clear decision. The strongest answers use the five evaluation criteria as a mini-structure, link every point to the specific app, and acknowledge a weakness with a realistic fix.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Discuss revenue models for a Caribbean app (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: revenue models, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Complete</b>: \"In the freemium model, ...\" and \"In in-app advertising, ...\" <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE other revenue model CXC Buddy could use. <b>Give</b> ONE advantage and ONE disadvantage. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE community-supported or civic way to fund CXC Buddy and <b>say</b> who would pay. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommend</b> a model for CXC Buddy. <b>Give</b> ONE reason linked to students and ONE way it protects privacy or keeps the app affordable. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":90,"max":240,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> In the freemium model, ... In in-app advertising, ...<br><b>(b)</b> Another model is ... An advantage is ... A disadvantage is ...<br><b>(c)</b> CXC Buddy could be funded by ... The money would come from ...<br><b>(d)</b> I recommend ... because students ... It also ...</p>","checklist":["I explained freemium and in-app advertising.","I named another model with an advantage and a disadvantage.","I named a community or civic funding source and who pays.","I recommended a model with a reason linked to students and a privacy or affordability point."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Freemium and advertising","marks":2,"look":["free.{0,60}(pay|upgrade|premium|plus|extra)","advert|ads?\\b"],"min":2,"strong":"You explained both models.","weak":"Say freemium is free with a paid upgrade, and in-app advertising is free with ads that earn money."},{"criterion":"Another model with an advantage and a disadvantage","marks":3,"look":["subscri|paid app|licens|in-app purchase|white.?label|sponsor|grant","advantage|good|benefit|helps","disadvantage|but|however|problem"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a model and weighed it.","weak":"Name a model, such as a subscription, then give one good point and one problem."},{"criterion":"Community or civic funding and who pays","marks":2,"look":["grant|sponsor|donat|crowdfund|ministry|government|foundation","bank|company|telecom|ministry|government|foundation|people|users|backers"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a funding source and the payer.","weak":"Name a grant or sponsorship and say who pays, such as an education foundation or a regional bank."},{"criterion":"Recommendation with reasons","marks":3,"look":["recommend|should use|best","student","privacy|data|afford|cheap|free|credit card"],"min":2,"strong":"You recommended a model with student-linked reasons.","weak":"Choose a model, link a reason to students, and say how it protects privacy or keeps costs low."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> In the freemium model, the app is free, but users pay for extra features such as full mock exams. In in-app advertising, the app is free and earns money from ads shown to users.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Another model is a subscription. An advantage is that monthly payments help pay for servers every month. A disadvantage is that many students do not have credit cards.</p><p><b>(c)</b> CXC Buddy could be funded by sponsorship. The money would come from a regional bank, whose logo would appear on the start screen.</p><p><b>(d)</b> I recommend freemium with sponsorship because students can practise for free and only pay if they want more. It also protects privacy, because there are no targeted ads tracking students.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): (a) 1 per model; (b) 1 model, 1 advantage, 1 disadvantage; (c) 1 source, 1 payer; (d) 1 recommendation, 1 student-linked reason, 1 privacy or affordability point. Encourage students to name who pays in every answer. When they can add a \"however\" to their recommendation, move them to the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Discuss revenue models for a Caribbean app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: evaluating revenue models, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">CXC Buddy, built by three students in Georgetown, Guyana, is a revision app with past-paper style questions, short videos and a study timer for CSEC and CAPE students. It now has about 12,000 users in ten territories; around 70 percent are under 18, and many have no credit card. Running costs (video hosting, servers, store fees and support) are about <b>US$4,000 a year</b>. The team has three offers: a regional bank will pay <b>US$3,000 a year</b> in sponsorship for its logo on the start screen; an advertising network will pay about <b>US$2,500 a year</b> if the app shows targeted ads; and a ministry of education is interested in buying school licences. The team wants the app to be affordable, respectful of students' privacy and still running in five years.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Explain</b> why CXC Buddy needs a revenue model even though students use it for free. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> FOUR models (freemium, targeted advertising, school licensing, and sponsorship or grants) against user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and long-term sustainability. <i>(12 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommend</b> a hybrid model, show with figures that it covers the annual running costs, and <b>justify</b> it. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Identify</b> ONE ethical risk in your recommendation and <b>explain</b> how the team would reduce it. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":400,"max":700,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Even a free app costs ... because ... Without a revenue model ...<br><b>(b)</b> Freemium: user value ... privacy ... accessibility ... ethics ... sustainability ... Targeted advertising: ... School licensing: ... Sponsorship or grants: ...<br><b>(c)</b> I recommend ... Annual income: ... + ... + ... = ..., which covers the US$4,000 costs. This is justified because ...<br><b>(d)</b> One ethical risk is ... The team would reduce it by ...</p>","checklist":["I explained the running costs and what happens if nobody pays them.","I evaluated all four models against the five criteria, with at least one strength and one weakness each.","I recognised that 70 percent of users are under 18 when judging targeted advertising.","My recommendation includes figures that add up to at least US$4,000 a year.","I identified a specific ethical risk in my own recommendation and a practical way to reduce it."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Why a revenue model is needed","marks":3,"look":["cost|hosting|server|store fee|support","4,000|4000|every year|each year|annual","without|otherwise|shut|abandon|stop|sustain"],"min":2,"strong":"You explained the running costs and the consequence of not covering them.","weak":"Explain the yearly costs (about US$4,000 for hosting, servers, fees and support) and that the app would close or decay without income."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of four models","marks":12,"look":["freemium","target(ed)? advert|advertis","licen","sponsor|grant","value","privacy|data|track","access|afford|credit card","ethic|child|under 18|minor","sustain|long.?term|five years|ends|renew"],"min":7,"strong":"All four models evaluated against all five criteria, with student-specific judgements.","weak":"Take each model in turn and judge it on user value, privacy, accessibility, ethics and sustainability, using details such as the 70 percent of users under 18 and the lack of credit cards."},{"criterion":"Costed and justified hybrid","marks":6,"look":["recommend|hybrid","3,000|3000","licen","\\+|plus|total|altogether|in total","4,000|4000|cover","because"],"min":4,"strong":"A clear hybrid with figures that cover costs and a convincing justification.","weak":"Name the parts of your hybrid, add up the yearly income to show it covers US$4,000, and explain why this mix suits the users."},{"criterion":"Ethical risk and mitigation","marks":4,"look":["risk|concern|problem","sponsor|bank|influence|pressure|upsell|pay.?wall|divide|fair","reduce|limit|rule|agreement|policy|cap|only","student|child|under 18|young"],"min":3,"strong":"A specific ethical risk in your own model and a practical safeguard.","weak":"Name a risk in your recommendation (for example sponsor influence over young users, or a paywall widening gaps between students) and explain a concrete safeguard."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Even though students pay nothing, CXC Buddy costs about US$4,000 a year for video hosting, servers, store fees and support. Without a revenue model, the team would pay these costs themselves until they could not, and the app would stop being updated or close, letting down 12,000 students in the middle of their exam preparation.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Freemium</b> offers good user value, because everyone practises for free and pays only for extras such as full mock exams. It protects privacy and is ethical if prices are clear, but accessibility is limited because many students lack credit cards, and only a small share usually upgrades, so on its own it may not be sustainable. <b>Targeted advertising</b> would bring about US$2,500 and keeps the app free, which helps accessibility, but it scores poorly on privacy and ethics: around 70 percent of users are under 18, and tracking their behaviour to target ads is inappropriate. Ads also use prepaid data and distract from study, which lowers user value. <b>School licensing</b> gives high value, because schools buy access for all their students, so no student needs a card. It needs no ads or tracking, and annual licences are sustainable, but sales are slow and depend on ministry and school budgets. <b>Sponsorship or grants</b> would keep the app free and ad-free, which is excellent for accessibility and privacy; the bank's US$3,000 a year covers most costs. However, sponsorship can end or come with pressure on content, and grants are usually short-term, so neither is reliable on its own.</p><p><b>(c)</b> I recommend a hybrid of <b>sponsorship, school licensing and a low-cost freemium tier</b>, with no targeted advertising. Estimated yearly income: bank sponsorship US$3,000 + ten school licences at US$150 each, US$1,500 + Plus upgrades, about US$500 = US$5,000, which covers the US$4,000 running costs with a small reserve. This is justified because every student keeps free access to practice questions, students without cards can get Plus through their school, no student data is sold for advertising, and three independent sources mean the app survives if any one ends.</p><p><b>(d)</b> One ethical risk is <b>sponsor influence</b>: the bank might later ask to promote loans or credit cards to young users. The team would reduce this with a written sponsorship agreement stating that sponsor recognition is limited to a logo on the start screen and that no financial products are promoted to under-18s, and it would renew the sponsorship each year with a short public impact report, so the relationship stays transparent.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks): (a) 3 = costs, figure or frequency, consequence. (b) 12 = 3 per model, awarded for judgements across the five criteria that use the stimulus (under-18 users, no credit cards, prepaid data). (c) 6 = named hybrid, figures that cover US$4,000, justification against at least three criteria. (d) 4 = a specific risk in the student's own recommendation, who is affected, and a practical safeguard. Accept a hybrid that includes non-targeted, age-appropriate ads if privacy is explicitly protected.</p>"}}]},{"id":"M3U9","title":"Factors influencing trends in the mobile ecosystem","objectives":["Explain the factors that drive mobile trends: commoditisation, technology access (pervasiveness), affordability, accessibility, adoption, user behaviour and empowerment.","Describe emerging trends: AI integration, mobile payments, 5G and edge computing, IoT, AR and VR, wearables, foldables, PWAs, low-code/no-code, digital identity, super apps and app-based services.","Discuss the impact of cybersecurity, privacy, data regulation (GDPR and regional data protection laws) and mobile accessibility on app development.","Discuss Caribbean trends, including mobile money, digital identity and e-government services, and their implications for the region.","Use research tools and methods (Statista, data.ai, Sensor Tower, Google Trends, GSMA Intelligence) to discover, monitor and evaluate mobile trends."],"videos":[{"title":"Mobile technology trends","q":"top mobile app development trends AI 5G AR super apps explained"},{"title":"What is a super app?","q":"what is a super app WeChat Grab explained"},{"title":"Edge computing and 5G","q":"5G and edge computing explained simply"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Why trends happen: the driving factors","html":"<p>A <b>trend</b> is a general direction of change over time, such as more people paying by phone each year. A <b>factor</b> is a cause that pushes a trend forward or holds it back. Examiners want you to explain <b>why</b> a trend is happening, not just name it.</p>\n<p>Learn these seven factors. <b>Technology access</b> is also called \"pervasiveness of technology\", and <b>user behaviour</b> covers changing habits.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Mobile example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Commoditisation</b></td><td>A product becomes so common and standardised that brands compete mainly on price.</td><td>Basic Android smartphones are now cheap and similar, so almost everyone can own one.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Technology access / pervasiveness</b></td><td>How widely available the technology and networks are.</td><td>4G coverage across most of Barbados; free Wi-Fi in parts of Bridgetown.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Affordability</b></td><td>Whether people can pay for devices, data and services.</td><td>Prepaid data bundles from Digicel or Flow make mobile Internet possible without a contract.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Accessibility</b></td><td>Whether people of all abilities, ages and literacy levels can use the technology.</td><td>Screen readers, voice assistants and larger text help visually impaired and older users.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adoption</b></td><td>The rate at which people start using something new.</td><td>WhatsApp adoption in the Caribbean was so fast it became the main way families and businesses communicate.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>User behaviour</b></td><td>How people actually use technology: habits, preferences, time spent.</td><td>Short vertical video and voice notes replacing long texts and calls.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Empowerment</b></td><td>Technology giving people new abilities, voice or economic opportunity.</td><td>A vendor in Castries Market selling online through Instagram and taking orders on WhatsApp.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The factors work together. Commoditisation lowers prices, which improves <b>affordability</b>; that raises <b>adoption</b>; wide adoption changes <b>user behaviour</b> and creates <b>empowerment</b>. Barriers in one factor, such as high data prices, slow everything else.</p>\n<p>These same factors appeared in Module 1 (visual design trends) and Module 2 (web publishing trends). In Module 3 you apply them to the <b>mobile ecosystem</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Use a \"factor, therefore trend\" sentence: \"Because smartphones have become commoditised and cheaper, adoption has risen across the Caribbean, which has made mobile banking practical for more people.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A trend is a direction of change over time, such as more people paying by phone each year. A factor is a cause that pushes a trend forward or holds it back. The main factors are commoditisation (phones becoming cheap and similar), technology access, affordability, accessibility, adoption, user behaviour and empowerment. Cheap prepaid data from Digicel or Flow, for example, helps more people in Barbados get online.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Factors work as a chain, and the strongest answers trace it. Commoditisation lowers device prices, which improves affordability, which speeds adoption; once almost everyone is connected, user behaviour shifts (voice notes instead of calls) and empowerment follows (vendors selling on Instagram). A barrier anywhere slows the whole chain: expensive data can hold back adoption even when phones are cheap. Be careful with direction, because a factor can push a trend backwards as well as forwards, as when poor accessibility keeps older users away from e-government. Use the \"factor, therefore trend\" structure, and finish with an implication for a specific group, such as small businesses or rural residents.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the driving factor","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is writing a trends report for clients. Match each factor to the example that best illustrates it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Commoditisation","r":"Dozens of brands sell similar entry-level Android phones, so prices keep falling"},{"l":"Affordability","r":"A Digicel prepaid weekly data bundle lets a student get online without a contract"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"A blind user in Barbados navigates apps using TalkBack and voice commands"},{"l":"Adoption","r":"Within a few years almost every business in Castries takes orders on WhatsApp"},{"l":"User behaviour","r":"Young people now send voice notes rather than make phone calls"},{"l":"Empowerment","r":"A Jamaican farmer checks market prices on her phone and negotiates a better deal"},{"l":"Technology access","r":"Fibre and 4G coverage reach rural villages in Belize for the first time"}],"win":"Very good. You can link each driving factor to a concrete example, which is the basis of any trends discussion.","tutor":[{"h":"Price words","p":"<p>Similar products competing on price = commoditisation. Being able to pay = affordability.</p>"},{"h":"Ability and power","p":"<p>Being able to use it despite a disability = accessibility. Gaining new opportunity = empowerment.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the factor (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Affordability","r":"Cheap prepaid data bundles"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"A screen reader for a blind user"},{"l":"Adoption","r":"Almost everyone starts using WhatsApp"},{"l":"Empowerment","r":"A vendor starts selling online"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Match the driving factor (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Keisha's second report uses less obvious examples. Match each factor to the example that BEST illustrates it. Think about the cause, not just the topic.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Commoditisation","r":"Phones from many brands now have similar specifications, so buyers choose mainly on price"},{"l":"Affordability","r":"A low-cost daily social bundle lets a student in Grenada stay online"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"Voice control lets an older resident with arthritis send messages without typing"},{"l":"Adoption","r":"Within two years, most stalls at a busy island market start accepting QR payments"},{"l":"User behaviour","r":"Visitors now check TikTok rather than guidebooks before choosing a restaurant"},{"l":"Empowerment","r":"A young creator in Guyana earns an income by selling digital art from her phone"},{"l":"Technology access","r":"A new tower brings reliable 4G to a village in Belize for the first time"}],"win":"Excellent. You separated close factors: similar products (commoditisation) from price (affordability), the rate of uptake (adoption) from changing habits (user behaviour), and network reach (technology access) from new opportunity (empowerment)."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Emerging trends 1: AI, payments, 5G, edge and IoT","html":"<p>Here are the main emerging trends. Learn what each one is, an example and how it affects app developers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>AI integration</b>: artificial intelligence built into apps: chatbots, voice assistants, photo enhancement, translation, recommendations and on-device features such as live captions. <i>Impact</i>: developers add AI through APIs or on-device models; they must explain how data is used and check outputs for errors and bias. An Aruba tourism app could offer an AI assistant answering visitors in English, Dutch, Papiamento and Spanish.</li>\n<li><b>Mobile payments</b>: paying by phone using tap-to-pay wallets (Apple Pay, Google Wallet), QR codes, in-app payments and mobile money. <i>Impact</i>: apps need secure payment integration and local payment partners.</li>\n<li><b>5G connectivity</b>: the fifth generation of mobile networks, offering much higher speeds and <b>lower latency</b> than 4G, and support for many more connected devices. Rollout across the Caribbean is uneven; check current operator announcements for your island. <i>Impact</i>: richer streaming, cloud gaming and AR become possible, but apps must still work on 4G and slower networks.</li>\n<li><b>Edge computing</b>: processing data close to the user (on the device or a nearby server) instead of in a distant data centre. <i>Impact</i>: faster responses, less data sent, better privacy. It pairs with 5G for real-time uses such as traffic monitoring.</li>\n<li><b>Internet of Things (IoT)</b>: everyday objects with sensors and Internet connections: smart meters, home security cameras, smart speakers, water-level sensors. The phone becomes the remote control. <i>Impact</i>: apps must connect to devices securely. Example: an electricity utility could use smart meters with an app showing daily electricity use; a Guyana farm could use soil sensors that alert the farmer's phone.</li>\n<li><b>App-based services</b>: services delivered mainly through apps, such as ride-hailing, food delivery, banking, telehealth and government services. Physical businesses increasingly need an app or app partner.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Do not just define a trend. For full marks, add its <b>impact</b> on developers or users and a <b>Caribbean example</b>. Definition + impact + example is a reliable three-mark pattern.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Six trends are changing mobile apps. AI adds features such as chat assistants, translation and live captions. Mobile payments let people pay by tapping a phone or scanning a QR code. 5G is faster, with less delay. Edge computing processes data close to the user. The Internet of Things connects everyday objects, such as smart electricity meters. App-based services deliver taxis, food and banking through apps.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Trends depend on one another. Edge computing and 5G together make real-time uses possible, such as a traffic camera that sends only alerts; IoT produces data that AI can analyse; mobile payments are the glue that turns an app into a service people pay through. For the Caribbean, ask what each trend needs before it can spread: 5G needs spectrum and investment, IoT needs reliable power and security, and AI features need clear rules on how data is used and checks for errors and bias, especially in languages and accents that global systems handle poorly. A Grade One answer gives a definition, an impact and a Caribbean example, then notes a condition or limitation.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Name the trend","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing client requests from around the region. Identify the trend behind each one.</p>","items":[{"text":"A St. Kitts hotel wants guests' phones to unlock room doors and control the air conditioning.","options":["foldable devices","Internet of Things","super app"],"answer":1,"fb":"Connected locks and air-conditioning units controlled by phone are IoT devices."},{"text":"A traffic camera at the Demerara Harbour Bridge processes video locally and sends only alerts, not full footage.","options":["low-code","edge computing","subscription model"],"answer":1,"fb":"Processing data near its source and sending only results is edge computing."},{"text":"A bank app adds a chatbot that answers customer questions in plain language at any hour.","options":["wearable technology","AI integration","PWA"],"answer":1,"fb":"A conversational assistant is AI built into the app."},{"text":"A network upgrade gives near-instant response for cloud gaming and supports thousands of sensors.","options":["sideloading","digital identity","5G"],"answer":2,"fb":"Very low latency and capacity for many devices are the key features of 5G."},{"text":"Customers at a Georgetown café pay by tapping their phone on the card reader.","options":["enterprise distribution","mobile payments","AR"],"answer":1,"fb":"Tap-to-pay with a phone wallet is a mobile payment."}],"win":"Excellent. You can recognise AI, mobile payments, 5G, edge computing and IoT from real requests.","tutor":[{"h":"Things or network?","p":"<p>Physical objects connected to the Internet = IoT. A faster, lower-latency network = 5G.</p>"},{"h":"Where is data processed?","p":"<p>Near the user or device rather than far away = edge.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Name the trend (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A phone controls a smart light bulb.","options":["AR","IoT"],"answer":1,"fb":"Connected objects: IoT."},{"text":"A chatbot answers customers in an app.","options":["AI integration","5G"],"answer":0,"fb":"Conversational assistant: AI."},{"text":"Paying by tapping a phone on a reader.","options":["mobile payments","edge computing"],"answer":0,"fb":"Phone wallet: mobile payments."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Name the trend (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Jerome's harder client requests combine trends or depend on conditions. Choose the BEST answer each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"A hurricane app analyses flood-risk photos on the phone itself, so it still works without sending them to a distant server. Which trend is this?","options":["A subscription model","A super app","Edge (on-device) computing","Sideloading"],"answer":2,"fb":"Processing data on or near the device instead of in a distant data centre is edge computing."},{"text":"A wearable alerts a carer's phone when an elderly resident in Linden falls. Which TWO trends combine here?","options":["Wearables and IoT","Foldables and VR","Low-code and super apps","5G and PWAs"],"answer":0,"fb":"A worn sensor (wearable) connected to other devices over the Internet (IoT) sends the alert."},{"text":"A tourism app's assistant answers visitors in four languages but sometimes gives wrong ferry times. Which responsibility does this AI feature raise MOST?","options":["Checking outputs for errors and showing users where information comes from","Removing every language except English","Switching to a paid app","Making the app icon larger"],"answer":0,"fb":"AI features must be checked for accuracy, and users should know where answers come from."},{"text":"Why does edge computing pair well with 5G for a traffic system at the Demerara Harbour Bridge?","options":["Both remove the need for any network","Low-latency links plus local processing allow real-time alerts with less data sent","5G sends all processing to a distant data centre","Edge computing only works on foldable phones"],"answer":1,"fb":"5G's low delay and local processing together make instant alerts possible without streaming full video."},{"text":"A café's customers pay by tapping their phones, but the card reader fails whenever the Internet drops. Which condition does mobile payments depend on here?","options":["VR headsets","App Store Optimisation","Larger phone screens","Reliable connectivity and a backup payment option"],"answer":3,"fb":"Payments trends depend on resilient connectivity, and businesses need a fallback."},{"text":"Which developments are MOST likely to spread fastest across the Caribbean in the next few years, and why?","options":["Cloud gaming, because it needs 5G everywhere","Mobile payments and PWAs, because they work at low cost on phones people already own","VR headsets, because they are exciting","Foldable phones, because they are new"],"answer":1,"fb":"Low cost and compatibility with existing phones drive faster adoption in price-sensitive markets."}],"win":"Excellent. You recognised combined trends, the conditions they depend on and the responsibilities they bring, and you judged which will spread fastest in the region."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Emerging trends 2: AR, VR, wearables, foldables, PWAs and low-code","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Trend</th><th>What it is</th><th>Impact and example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Augmented reality (AR)</b></td><td>Digital content laid over the real world through the camera</td><td>Try-on filters, furniture placement, heritage tours. A Brimstone Hill AR tour could show the fortress as it looked centuries ago. Tools: ARCore (Android), ARKit (iOS) and Snapchat Lens Studio (Meta closed Meta Spark, its Instagram effects builder, in January 2025).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Virtual reality (VR)</b></td><td>A fully computer-generated environment, usually through a headset</td><td>Training, virtual property tours, immersive tourism previews. Less common on phones alone; headsets remain costly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wearable technology</b></td><td>Smartwatches, fitness bands, smart rings and earbuds</td><td>Apps need small \"glanceable\" screens, health data and notifications. Health data is sensitive, so privacy matters.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Foldable devices</b></td><td>Phones with flexible screens that fold like a book or flip</td><td>Apps must adapt layouts when the screen changes size (\"responsive\" and \"continuity\" design). Still expensive and a small market share.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Progressive web apps (PWAs)</b></td><td>Websites that behave like apps: home-screen icon, offline use, push notifications</td><td>One codebase for all devices, no store needed, light on storage. Good for small Caribbean organisations with tight budgets.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Low-code / no-code</b></td><td>Building apps with visual drag-and-drop tools instead of writing much code</td><td>MIT App Inventor, Thunkable, Glide, Adalo, FlutterFlow. Lets students, small businesses and NGOs build apps quickly; limits on complex features and dependence on the platform.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>You used low-code tools in Unit 3.6. Their growth is a trend in itself: it is <b>empowering</b> more people to become creators rather than just consumers, which helps address the content consumption versus production imbalance you studied in 3.3.</p>\n<p><b>Distinguish AR and VR</b>: AR <b>adds to</b> the real world you can still see; VR <b>replaces</b> it with a virtual one.</p>\n<p><b>Malik</b>, Island Pulse Media's animator, is building 3D models in Blender for an AR Carnival app that lets users place a costumed masquerader in their own living room.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question asks how a trend will affect <b>Caribbean</b> users, weigh cost and access. Foldables and VR headsets are exciting but expensive; PWAs and low-code tools are cheap and therefore likely to spread faster in the region.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>More trends to know. Augmented reality (AR) adds digital images to the real world through the camera, while virtual reality (VR) replaces it with a computer-made world. Wearables include smartwatches and fitness bands. Foldable phones have bending screens. Progressive web apps (PWAs) are websites that work like apps. Low-code tools let people build apps without much coding. A Brimstone Hill AR tour could show the fortress as it once looked.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Not every trend spreads at the same speed, and cost is usually the deciding factor in the Caribbean. Foldables and VR headsets attract headlines but remain expensive, so their reach will stay small for some time; PWAs and low-code tools are cheap, run on the phones people already own and therefore spread faster. Platform risk is real for developers: Meta closed its Spark AR effects builder in January 2025, so creators who depended on it had to move elsewhere. When you evaluate a trend's impact, weigh three things: the cost to users, the skills and tools developers need, and whether the trend reduces or widens the consumption and production imbalance.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Device trend or development trend?","intro":"<p>Some trends are about new <b>hardware</b>; others change how apps are <b>built or delivered</b>; others are about <b>experiences</b>. Sort each example.</p>","main":"What kind of trend is this?","bins":["New hardware / devices","How apps are built or delivered","Immersive experience (AR/VR)"],"items":[{"t":"A Dominica news site can be added to the home screen and read offline.","bin":1,"fb":"A PWA is a delivery method: web technology that behaves like an app."},{"t":"Smart earbuds translate a conversation in real time.","bin":0,"fb":"Wearable devices increasingly carry AI features, but the trend here is the new hardware."},{"t":"A youth group in Curaçao builds its event app in Thunkable with no code.","bin":1,"fb":"Low-code/no-code changes how apps are built and who can build them."},{"t":"A headset lets a buyer in Toronto walk through a villa in Ocho Rios.","bin":2,"fb":"A fully virtual environment through a headset is virtual reality."},{"t":"A Bahamas agency uses FlutterFlow to build an app for both Android and iOS from one design.","bin":1,"fb":"Visual cross-platform builders are part of the low-code trend."},{"t":"A smartwatch that tracks heart rate during a Carnival road march.","bin":0,"fb":"Wearables are new devices with their own app requirements."},{"t":"A camera view shows labels floating over each shop on Frederick Street in Port of Spain.","bin":2,"fb":"Digital labels laid over the live camera view are augmented reality."},{"t":"A phone that unfolds into a small tablet.","bin":0,"fb":"Foldables are a hardware trend that forces apps to adapt their layouts."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can separate hardware trends, development and delivery trends, and immersive experience trends.","tutor":[{"h":"Can you hold it?","p":"<p>If the trend is a physical device, it is hardware.</p>"},{"h":"Adds or replaces?","p":"<p>AR adds to the real view; VR replaces it.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Device, development or AR/VR? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A folding phone.","bin":0,"fb":"Hardware."},{"t":"Building an app with drag-and-drop blocks.","bin":1,"fb":"How apps are built."},{"t":"Labels floating over a live camera view.","bin":2,"fb":"AR."},{"t":"A smartwatch.","bin":0,"fb":"Hardware."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Device trend or development trend? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder examples: some sound like hardware but are really about how apps are built, and some sound like apps but are really immersive experiences. Sort each one by the trend it mainly shows.</p>","items":[{"t":"A smart ring that tracks sleep and pairs with a phone app.","bin":0,"fb":"A new kind of wearable is a hardware trend."},{"t":"A Barbados agency builds a booking app visually in FlutterFlow.","bin":1,"fb":"Visual, low-code building changes how apps are made."},{"t":"A heritage app shows a 3D sugar mill standing on the real field through the camera.","bin":2,"fb":"Digital objects placed in the live camera view are augmented reality."},{"t":"A news site adds offline reading and an Install button in the browser.","bin":1,"fb":"Turning a website into an installable PWA is a delivery trend."},{"t":"Buyers in Toronto tour an Ocho Rios villa inside a headset.","bin":2,"fb":"A fully virtual environment is virtual reality."},{"t":"Earbuds with built-in sensors that measure heart rate.","bin":0,"fb":"New sensor-equipped wearables are a hardware trend."},{"t":"An NGO in Belize builds a volunteer app in Glide from a spreadsheet.","bin":1,"fb":"No-code building from data is a development trend."},{"t":"A shoe shop's app lets customers see trainers on their own feet through the camera.","bin":2,"fb":"Virtual try-on over the live camera is AR."},{"t":"A flip phone with a small outer screen for notifications.","bin":0,"fb":"Foldable and flip designs are hardware trends."},{"t":"A government publishes a service as one PWA instead of two store apps.","bin":1,"fb":"Choosing a PWA is a decision about how the app is delivered."}],"win":"Excellent. You looked at what each example mainly changes: the device in the user's hand, the way the app is built or delivered, or the experience of reality itself."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Super apps and the consolidation of services","html":"<p>A <b>super app</b> is a single app that combines many services, such as messaging, payments, shopping, transport, food delivery and bill paying, often with \"mini-programs\" from other businesses running inside it. Users rarely need to leave it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>WeChat</b> (China): began as messaging and grew into payments, shopping, bill paying, booking appointments and thousands of mini-programs.</li>\n<li><b>Grab</b> (Southeast Asia): began as ride-hailing and added food delivery, parcel delivery, digital payments and financial services.</li>\n<li>Other platforms, such as WhatsApp with its business catalogues and payments in some countries, have been moving in a similar direction. Check current features before quoting them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Why super apps grow</b> (link to the factors): <b>user behaviour</b> (people prefer fewer apps and less switching), <b>affordability and storage</b> (one app instead of ten on a low-storage phone), <b>mobile payments</b> (a built-in wallet ties the services together) and <b>adoption</b> (once everyone has the app, businesses join it).</p>\n<p><b>Consolidation</b> means digital services being brought together under fewer, larger platforms.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Benefits</th><th>Concerns</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Convenience: one login, one wallet</td><td>A single company holds huge amounts of personal data</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Small businesses reach many users without building their own app</td><td>Small developers depend on the platform's rules and fees</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Saves storage and data</td><td>Less competition; harder for local apps to compete</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can include people without bank accounts through built-in wallets</td><td>An outage or security breach affects everything at once</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Caribbean angle</b>: no regional super app on the scale of WeChat exists yet, but the idea is relevant. A \"TT One\" app combining bus times, electricity bill payment, food delivery and event tickets for Trinidad and Tobago could save users from installing many small apps. The challenge is the small market size, which is why a regional (Dutch Caribbean or OECS-wide) approach might be more sustainable.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss the impact of super apps\" needs both sides: benefits to users and businesses, and concerns about privacy, competition and dependence. End with a balanced judgement.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A super app is one app that combines many services, such as chatting, paying, shopping, booking rides and ordering food. WeChat in China and Grab in Southeast Asia are famous examples. People like super apps because they need fewer apps and only one wallet, but one company then holds a lot of personal data. A \"TT One\" app could combine bus times, bill payments and event tickets for Trinidad and Tobago.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Super apps grow through network effects: once most users are inside the app, businesses join, which attracts more users. That same strength creates the main concerns: one company controls the data, rules and fees, small local developers become dependent on it, and an outage or breach affects every service at once. For the Caribbean, scale is the key obstacle; a single island may not have enough users to sustain a super app, so a regional or Dutch Caribbean approach, or building on an existing popular platform, is more realistic. Regulators matter too, because competition and data protection rules shape how much power a super app can hold. End any discussion with a balanced judgement.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Super app or not?","q":"Which description BEST fits the definition of a super app?","options":[{"t":"One app in which users chat, pay bills, order food, book rides and use mini-programs from other businesses.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. A super app consolidates many different services, usually tied together by a built-in payment wallet, as WeChat and Grab do."},{"t":"An app that runs extremely fast on 5G networks.","fb":"Speed relates to network technology, not to the range of services the app offers."},{"t":"A single-purpose app built with a no-code tool.","fb":"A super app is the opposite of single-purpose; the tool used to build it is irrelevant."},{"t":"A very popular app with millions of downloads, such as a hit game.","fb":"Popularity alone does not make a super app. It must combine many different services in one place."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Count the services","p":"<p>A super app is defined by how many different services it brings together.</p>"},{"h":"Examples","p":"<p>Think about what WeChat and Grab let users do.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Super app or not? (Foundation)","q":"A super app is best described as...","options":[{"t":"an app with lots of downloads","fb":"Popularity is not the definition."},{"t":"an app for one task only","fb":"That is the opposite."},{"t":"one app that combines many services such as chat, payments and delivery","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Many services, one app."}]},
 "up": {"title":"Super app or not? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A proposal for \"TT One\" would combine bus times, electricity bill payment, food delivery and event tickets for Trinidad and Tobago in one app with a built-in wallet. Which evaluation is MOST balanced?","options":[{"t":"It should be rejected, because super apps have no benefits for users.","fb":"Super apps do offer convenience, one wallet and less switching. A balanced answer weighs both sides."},{"t":"It is acceptable as long as it uses 5G.","fb":"Network speed does not address the data, competition or scale concerns."},{"t":"It would be convenient and save phone storage, but it would concentrate personal and payment data in one company, make local businesses dependent on its fees and may struggle to reach enough users on one island, so a regional version with strong data protection rules is more realistic.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It weighs real benefits against data, dependence and scale concerns, then reaches a reasoned judgement."},{"t":"It is certain to succeed, because WeChat succeeded in China.","fb":"WeChat grew in a market of over a billion people. Trinidad and Tobago's scale is completely different."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Cybersecurity, privacy, data laws and accessibility","html":"<p>There is an <b>enhanced focus on cybersecurity, privacy and mobile accessibility</b>. These are trends because users, governments and app stores are all demanding more.</p>\n<p><b>Cybersecurity</b>: phones now hold banking, ID, health and work data, so attacks such as phishing texts (\"smishing\"), fake apps and SIM-swap fraud have increased. Developers respond with <b>encryption</b>, <b>two-factor authentication (2FA)</b>, <b>biometric login</b> (fingerprint, face), <b>passkeys</b> and regular security updates.</p>\n<p><b>Privacy</b>: both stores now require <b>privacy labels</b> or a data safety section stating what data an app collects. Users are asked for <b>permission</b> before apps access location, camera or contacts, and Apple requires apps to ask before tracking users across other apps.</p>\n<p><b>Data protection laws</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The EU's <b>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)</b>, in force since 2018, protects people in the EU and applies to any app, anywhere, that processes their data. Key ideas: a lawful basis such as <b>consent</b>, <b>data minimisation</b> (collect only what you need), the right to access and delete data, breach reporting and large fines.</li>\n<li><b>Why it matters here</b>: the French side of St. Martin (Saint-Martin) is part of the EU, so GDPR applies directly there. An app serving both sides of the island, or European tourists, must respect it.</li>\n<li><b>Regional laws</b>: Sint Maarten has a personal data protection law based on the Dutch model; Jamaica's <b>Data Protection Act</b> (2020); Barbados's <b>Data Protection Act</b> (2019); Trinidad and Tobago, the Bahamas and others have laws or proposals at different stages. Check the current status before quoting a law in detail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Impact on app development</b>: <b>privacy by design</b> (plan protection from the start), clear privacy policies, consent screens, secure storage, and the ability to delete accounts. Ignoring this can mean store rejection, fines and loss of trust.</p>\n<p><b>Mobile accessibility</b>: built-in features such as <b>TalkBack</b> (Android) and <b>VoiceOver</b> (iOS), dynamic text size, captions and voice control are improving. Developers should add labels for screen readers, sufficient colour contrast and large touch targets, following guidelines such as <b>WCAG</b>, which you met in Module 2.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Saint-Martin's EU status is a strong, specific Caribbean example of why GDPR matters to local developers. Use it.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Phones now hold our money, ID and health information, so security and privacy are growing trends. Apps use encryption, fingerprint or face login and two-factor codes. App stores make apps state what data they collect. Data protection laws, such as Europe's GDPR, give people rights over their data. GDPR applies on the French side of St. Martin, because Saint-Martin is part of the European Union.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Privacy by design means building protection in from the start, not adding it after a breach. In practice: collect only what the app needs (data minimisation), ask for permissions at the moment they are needed, encrypt data in storage and in transit, and make it easy to delete an account. St. Martin shows why this matters regionally: one island has two legal regimes, since Saint-Martin is part of the EU while Sint Maarten has its own personal data protection law, so an app serving both sides should meet the stricter standard. Security and accessibility can also conflict: a very short time-out protects accounts but can lock out users who need more time, so good design balances both.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Privacy by design check","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is reviewing the Bayside Clinic appointment app, which will serve patients on both the Dutch and French sides of St. Martin.</p>","q":"Select every feature that shows GOOD privacy and security practice.","lines":["The app asks for location only when the user searches for the nearest clinic","It requests access to all contacts \"just in case\"","Medical notes are encrypted on the device and on the server","Login uses a fingerprint or face ID plus an optional one-time code","Patient data is shared with an advertising network to show health product ads","A clear privacy policy explains what is collected and how to delete an account","Passwords are stored in plain text to make support easier"],"correct":[0,2,3,5],"win":"Correct. Asking only when needed, encryption, strong authentication and a clear privacy policy follow privacy by design and laws such as GDPR.","miss":"Look again. Collecting contacts \"just in case\" breaks data minimisation, sharing health data with advertisers breaks consent, and plain-text passwords are a serious security failure.","tutor":[{"h":"Data minimisation","p":"<p>Does the app collect only what it needs, only when it needs it?</p>"},{"h":"Sensitive data","p":"<p>Health data needs the strongest protection.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Privacy check (Foundation)","q":"Select the TWO good privacy practices.","lines":["Encrypting medical records","Sharing patient data with advertisers","A clear privacy policy","Storing passwords in plain text"],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Encryption and transparency protect users.","miss":"Which two would protect a patient?"},
 "up": {"title":"Privacy by design check (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Maya's second review of the Bayside Clinic appointment app, which serves patients on both the Dutch and French sides of St. Martin. These practices are harder to judge.</p>","q":"Select every feature that shows GOOD privacy and security practice.","lines":["Location is requested only when the user taps 'Find nearest clinic', and approximate location is accepted.","The sign-up form asks for date of birth, home address and employer, although only a name and phone number are needed to book.","Medical notes are encrypted on the device, in transit and on the server.","Login uses a passkey or biometrics, with a one-time code as a backup.","Analytics send each patient's diagnosis code to a third-party marketing tool.","A clear privacy policy, in English and French, explains what is collected, why, for how long and how to delete an account.","Records of patients from the French side are handled without considering GDPR because the company is based on the Dutch side.","A breach response plan sets out who must be informed and how quickly.","Passwords are stored as salted hashes, never as plain text.","Old appointment records are kept forever \"in case they are useful\"."],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7,8],"win":"Excellent. You identified purpose-limited permissions, full encryption, strong authentication, a bilingual privacy policy, breach planning and hashed passwords as privacy by design.","miss":"Look again. Extra sign-up fields break data minimisation, sending diagnoses to marketers breaks consent, French-side patients are in the EU, and keeping records forever breaks the principle of keeping data only as long as needed."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Caribbean trends: mobile money, digital ID and e-government","html":"<p>The course outline asks you to give attention to <b>Caribbean implications</b>. Three trends stand out.</p>\n<p><b>1. Mobile money and digital currency</b></p>\n<p>Many Caribbean residents are <b>unbanked</b> or <b>underbanked</b> (no bank account, or limited use of one). Mobile money lets people store, send and receive money using a phone wallet, often topped up with cash at agents.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>Sand Dollar</b> (The Bahamas, launched 2020) is one of the world's first central bank digital currencies, used through mobile wallets.</li>\n<li>The Eastern Caribbean Central Bank piloted <b>DCash</b> across several OECS countries; the pilot faced technical problems and ended in January 2024, and in 2026 the ECCB suspended work on a planned DCash 2.0, which is a useful lesson in risk.</li>\n<li>Jamaica launched <b>JAM-DEX</b>, its digital currency, in 2022.</li>\n<li>Remittances from the diaspora are huge for many islands, and mobile transfer apps compete with traditional money-transfer counters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2. Digital identity</b></p>\n<p>A <b>digital ID</b> lets citizens prove who they are online, securely, to access services. Jamaica's <b>National Identification System (NIDS)</b> is an example of a national ID programme with digital elements. Benefits: faster services, less paperwork, fewer queues. Concerns: privacy, data security, exclusion of people without smartphones, and trust in government data handling. Check the current status of any national programme before quoting it.</p>\n<p><b>3. E-government services</b></p>\n<p>Governments are moving services online and onto phones: tax filing, business registration, licence renewals, birth certificates and appointment booking. Barbados, Trinidad and Tobago and others have digital transformation programmes. In Jamaica, residents increasingly expect to pay electricity bills, check permits or book appointments without queueing in Kingston.</p>\n<p><b>Implications for developers and the region</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Opportunities for local developers to build, integrate or maintain these systems (local job creation from 3.4).</li>\n<li>Apps must be secure, accessible and light enough for older phones and prepaid data.</li>\n<li>Digital literacy programmes are needed so older residents are not left behind.</li>\n<li>Reliable connectivity and power (and resilience after hurricanes such as Irma) are essential.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For Caribbean implications, always give one opportunity and one challenge. Examples such as the Sand Dollar and DCash show you know real regional developments; describe them accurately and avoid inventing figures.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Three trends matter a lot in the Caribbean. <b>Mobile money</b> lets people store and send money by phone, even without a bank account; the Bahamas' Sand Dollar and Jamaica's JAM-DEX are digital currencies. <b>Digital ID</b> lets people prove who they are online. <b>E-government</b> lets people pay bills, renew licences and book appointments by phone instead of queueing at government offices.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each trend depends on trust as much as technology. The ECCB's DCash pilot showed that a serious outage can damage confidence in a digital currency, and slow everyday use of the Sand Dollar and JAM-DEX shows that people need clear reasons, and merchants willing to accept payment, before they switch. Digital ID brings efficiency but concentrates sensitive data, so it needs strong security, clear laws and a non-digital route for people without smartphones. For a Grade One answer, give each trend an opportunity (inclusion, efficiency, local jobs) and a challenge (privacy, reliability, digital literacy), and name the condition that would decide which wins, such as resilient power and connectivity after hurricanes.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Advising the Ministry: a digital services app","intro":"<p>Zara and Maya at the Island Pulse Media are advising a government working group in Sint Maarten that wants a mobile app for public services: bill payments, permit applications and appointment booking. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>A minister wants the app to require the newest phones so it can use the latest AR and AI features. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Agree: the newest technology will make the island look modern.","to":"bad1","fb":"A public service that excludes people with older phones fails its purpose."},{"t":"First research residents' devices, data costs and digital skills; the app must serve everyone, including older residents.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Technology access, affordability and accessibility decide whether a public service is actually used."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Research shows most residents use mid-range Android phones and prepaid data. How should the service be delivered?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A light store app plus a PWA version, both working on 4G and saving forms offline until a connection returns.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong: it matches real devices and data conditions and widens access."},{"t":"A large app that streams high-definition video guides and only works on 5G.","to":"bad2","fb":"This ignores current network coverage and data costs."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The app will handle ID numbers and payments, and some users live on the French side or are EU citizens. What do you build in?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Collect as much data as possible now; security can be added later.","to":"bad3","fb":"Unnecessary data plus weak security invites breaches and legal penalties."},{"t":"Privacy by design: encryption, 2FA, data minimisation, a clear privacy policy and compliance with local law and GDPR.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Security and data protection are essential for public trust."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Before launch, how do you check you are building on real trends, not hype?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use GSMA Intelligence and ITU data for regional mobile use, Google Trends for local search interest, and survey residents.","to":"good","fb":"Combining reliable secondary data with primary research is sound trend evaluation."},{"t":"Copy whatever the most-downloaded app in the US store is doing.","to":"bad4","fb":"Global store rankings do not reflect Sint Maarten's needs or conditions."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The app is used across the community and trusted with sensitive data. A Grade One answer would explain why: you weighed access, affordability, accessibility and user behaviour, applied privacy regulation and used research tools to evaluate trends before acting.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded citizens.</b> Many residents cannot run the app, and queues at government offices continue. Try again and start with access.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Unusable.</b> The app fails on 4G and eats prepaid data. Try again and design for real network conditions.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Data breach.</b> Personal details leak and the government faces public anger and legal action. Try again and build security in from the start.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong fit.</b> The features chosen do not match local needs. Try again and research the local context.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Who are the users?","p":"<p>A public service must work for the oldest, poorest and least connected residents too.</p>"},{"h":"Sensitive data","p":"<p>Which laws apply to an island shared with an EU territory?</p>"},{"h":"Evidence, not hype","p":"<p>Which research tools give reliable mobile data for the region?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Digital services app (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: a government services app for Sint Maarten.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Most residents have mid-range phones and prepaid data. What do you build?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A heavy app that needs the newest phone and 5G.","to":"bad","fb":"Many residents would be excluded."},{"t":"A light app or PWA that works on 4G and offline.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. It fits real devices and data."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The app handles ID numbers. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Nothing extra; add security later.","to":"bad","fb":"Risky with sensitive data."},{"t":"Encryption, 2FA and a clear privacy policy.","to":"good","fb":"Security builds trust."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You matched the app to access and protected privacy.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Think about access and security. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"title":"Advising the Ministry: a digital services app (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A tougher brief for the Sint Maarten working group planning a public services app for bill payments, permit applications and appointment booking. Several choices are partly right. Some end the project early with a partial result.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>A minister wants the app to require the newest phones so it can use the latest AR and AI features. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Build for the newest phones now and release a simpler version later.","to":"ok1","fb":"A later simple version still leaves most residents out at launch, and two versions cost more."},{"t":"Agree: the newest technology will make the island look modern.","to":"bad1","fb":"A public service that excludes people with older phones fails its purpose."},{"t":"Research residents' devices, data costs, digital skills and disabilities first, and design for the phones most people actually own.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Access, affordability and accessibility decide whether a public service is used."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Research shows most residents use mid-range Android phones and prepaid data, and some older residents have no smartphone at all. How should the service be delivered?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A large app that streams video guides and works only on 5G.","to":"bad2","fb":"This ignores current coverage and data costs."},{"t":"A light store app plus a PWA, both working on 4G and saving forms offline, plus an assisted service desk for people without smartphones.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. You matched real devices and data conditions and kept a non-digital route open."},{"t":"A light app only, with no alternative for people without smartphones.","to":"ok2","fb":"It suits most residents, but some of the most vulnerable are left without access."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The app will handle ID numbers and payments, and some users live on the French side or are EU citizens. What do you build in?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Follow only Sint Maarten's law, because the government is on the Dutch side.","to":"ok3","fb":"French-side residents are in the EU, so GDPR may apply to their data. Ignoring it risks legal problems and lost trust."},{"t":"Collect as much data as possible now and add security later.","to":"bad3","fb":"Unnecessary data plus weak security invites breaches and penalties."},{"t":"Privacy by design: data minimisation, encryption, passkeys or 2FA, clear consent, and compliance with Sint Maarten's data protection law and with GDPR for EU users.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Security and lawful data handling are essential for public trust."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The working group now wants an AI chat assistant to answer residents' questions. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Let the assistant answer any question without checks, so the app looks modern.","to":"bad4","fb":"Unchecked answers about permits and payments could mislead residents and damage trust."},{"t":"Pilot it with clear labelling, answers drawn only from official service information, an easy way to reach a person, and checks for errors in every supported language.","to":"n5","fb":"Excellent. You used the trend responsibly, with accuracy checks and a human fallback."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>Before full launch, how do you check you are building on real trends, not hype?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Copy whatever the most-downloaded app in the US store is doing.","to":"bad5","fb":"US store rankings do not reflect Sint Maarten's needs or conditions."},{"t":"Use Google Trends alone.","to":"ok4","fb":"Google Trends shows relative interest only, and a small island may have too little search data."},{"t":"Combine GSMA Intelligence and ITU data, Google Trends for local interest, the app's own analytics from the pilot, and a resident survey.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Several independent sources, including primary research, give a reliable picture."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>A trusted public service.</b> The app is used across the community and trusted with sensitive data. You weighed access, affordability and accessibility, kept a non-digital route, applied privacy law for both sides of the island, used AI responsibly and tested trends against several sources.</p>","end":"good"},"ok1":{"text":"<p><b>Slow uptake.</b> Most residents cannot run the first version, and queues at government offices continue. Try again and start with access.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Partly inclusive.</b> Most residents use the app, but older residents without smartphones still struggle. Try again and keep a route for everyone.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok3":{"text":"<p><b>Legal uncertainty.</b> French-side users complain about how their data is handled. Try again and consider both legal regimes.</p>","end":"ok"},"ok4":{"text":"<p><b>Thin evidence.</b> The search data for the island is too small to show a clear trend. Try again and combine sources.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Excluded citizens.</b> Many residents cannot run the app. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Unusable.</b> The app fails on 4G and eats prepaid data. Try again and design for real network conditions.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Data breach.</b> Personal details leak, and the government faces public anger and legal action. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Misinformation.</b> The assistant gives wrong permit fees, and residents lose trust in the service. Try again and add checks.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong fit.</b> The features chosen do not match local needs. Try again and research the local context.</p>","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"learn","title":"Research tools for tracking mobile trends","html":"<p>You need to know <b>research tools for discovering and tracking trends</b>, and how to use them for <b>monitoring and evaluating</b> trends. You need to know what each tool offers and how to judge the data.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it provides</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Statista</b></td><td>Charts and statistics from many sources on markets, devices and usage worldwide</td><td>Many charts need a subscription; always check the original source it cites.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>data.ai</b> (formerly <b>App Annie</b>)</td><td>App market intelligence: downloads, revenue and usage estimates by app and country</td><td>The syllabus and outline say \"App Annie\"; it rebranded as data.ai in 2022 and was bought by its rival Sensor Tower in 2024. Figures are estimates; full access is paid.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Sensor Tower</b></td><td>App download and revenue estimates, ad intelligence and ASO keyword data</td><td>Useful for comparing competitors; mostly paid, with free summary reports.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Google Trends</b></td><td>Free tool showing how search interest in a topic changes over time and by country</td><td>Shows <b>relative</b> interest (0 to 100), not actual numbers. Small islands may have too little data; compare with a larger region.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>GSMA Intelligence</b></td><td>Mobile industry data and reports from the GSMA (the global association of mobile operators), including regional Mobile Economy reports covering Latin America and the Caribbean</td><td>Highly credible for subscriber and network figures; some reports are free.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ITU and World Bank data</b></td><td>Official statistics on Internet use, mobile subscriptions and affordability by country</td><td>Free and authoritative; figures may lag a year or two.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>App store charts and developer consoles</b></td><td>Top charts, category rankings, and your own app's installs and retention</td><td>Free; your own console data is the most accurate for your app.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Other methods</b>: surveys and interviews (primary research), social listening on TikTok and X, tech news sites, operator press releases (Digicel, Flow, TelEm), and conference reports.</p>\n<p><b>Evaluating a source</b> (link to Module 2's CRAAP-style thinking): Is it <b>current</b>? Who produced it and is it <b>credible</b>? Is it an <b>estimate</b> or a measured figure? Does it cover the <b>Caribbean</b> specifically, or only global or US data? Can you confirm it with a second source?</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 has no Internet, so you cannot look up figures. Learn what each tool is for, and in your answer write \"I would use Google Trends to compare search interest in mobile money in Jamaica over five years\" rather than quoting numbers.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To track mobile trends, use research tools. Google Trends shows how interest in a search term changes over time. GSMA Intelligence and the ITU publish trusted data on mobile users and networks. Statista collects charts from many sources. data.ai and Sensor Tower estimate app downloads and revenue. Your own Google Play Console shows how your app is doing, and surveys of local users add first-hand evidence.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each tool answers a different question, and none is complete on its own. Google Trends shows relative interest (0 to 100), not numbers of users, and small islands may have too little search data, so compare with a larger region. App intelligence firms give estimates, not measured figures. Official ITU and World Bank data are authoritative but can lag a year or two. Statista is a secondary source, so trace its charts back to the original. Good trend monitoring therefore triangulates: confirm a trend in at least two independent sources, add primary research such as a local survey, and note the date of every figure. In Paper 02, describe what you would use each tool for rather than quoting numbers.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Which research tool?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media needs evidence for several client reports. Match each research need to the best tool.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Google Trends","r":"Free comparison of search interest in \"food delivery\" in Trinidad over five years"},{"l":"GSMA Intelligence","r":"A credible regional report on mobile subscribers and network coverage in the Caribbean"},{"l":"data.ai (formerly App Annie)","r":"Estimates of downloads and revenue for the top travel apps by country"},{"l":"Sensor Tower","r":"Keyword and ad intelligence to plan a competitor's ASO strategy"},{"l":"Statista","r":"A ready-made chart of global smartphone shipments by brand, with a cited source"},{"l":"Google Play Console","r":"Day-30 retention for the client's own app"}],"win":"Well done. You matched each research need to the tool designed for it, and you know which data are estimates.","tutor":[{"h":"Free and searchy","p":"<p>Interest in search terms over time comes from one free Google tool.</p>"},{"h":"Your own app","p":"<p>For your own app's real figures, use the store's developer console.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which research tool? (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Google Trends","r":"Search interest over time"},{"l":"GSMA Intelligence","r":"Mobile industry reports from operators' association"},{"l":"data.ai","r":"App download and revenue estimates"},{"l":"Statista","r":"Charts of statistics from many sources"}]},
 "up": {"title":"Which research tool? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Keisha's harder research needs include questions about measured versus estimated data and primary evidence. Match each tool to the need it BEST meets.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"ITU and World Bank data","r":"Official country figures on mobile subscriptions and affordability, published with a time lag"},{"l":"Google Trends","r":"Relative search interest (0 to 100) in 'mobile money' in Jamaica compared with Barbados"},{"l":"GSMA Intelligence","r":"Industry reports on 5G and mobile Internet adoption in Latin America and the Caribbean"},{"l":"Sensor Tower","r":"Competitors' ASO keyword data and estimated ad spending"},{"l":"data.ai (formerly App Annie)","r":"Estimated downloads and revenue of the top finance apps by country"},{"l":"Statista","r":"A secondary-source chart that should be traced back to its original source"},{"l":"Google Play Console","r":"Measured installs, crashes and retention for your own app"},{"l":"Resident survey","r":"First-hand evidence of why older residents avoid e-government apps"}],"win":"Excellent. You matched each need to the right tool and recognised which give official, estimated, measured or first-hand evidence."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Discuss factors and trends in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem","genre":"Structured response: discussing factors and trends with Caribbean implications (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Caribbean Youth Tech Forum, meeting in Bridgetown, has asked students to brief regional leaders on \"The next five years of mobile in the Caribbean\". Leaders want to know what is driving change, which trends matter most for the region, and how to track them.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) <b>Explain</b> how THREE factors (for example commoditisation, affordability, accessibility, adoption, user behaviour or empowerment) are influencing mobile use in the Caribbean. (6 marks)<br>(b) <b>Discuss</b> TWO emerging trends and their implications for the region, including at least one opportunity and one challenge. One trend must be mobile money, digital identity or e-government. (6 marks)<br>(c) <b>Identify</b> TWO research tools the leaders could use to monitor these trends and state what each would show. (3 marks)</p>","min":230,"max":470,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The first factor is ... This means ... In the Caribbean, ... therefore ... The second factor ... The third factor ...<br><b>(b)</b> One trend is ... An opportunity is ... However, a challenge is ... A second trend is ...<br><b>(c)</b> Leaders could use ... to see ... They could also use ... which shows ...</p>","checklist":["I explained three factors, each with a meaning and a Caribbean example.","I used \"therefore\" or \"because\" to link each factor to a change in mobile use.","I discussed two trends, one of them mobile money, digital ID or e-government.","I gave at least one opportunity and one challenge for the region.","I named two research tools and what each would show.","I avoided inventing exact statistics."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Explanation of factors","marks":6,"look":["commodit|cheap|price","afford|prepaid|data cost","accessib|disab|screen reader|older","adopt","behaviou?r|habit","empower|opportunit|entrepreneur"],"min":3,"strong":"Three factors clearly explained and linked to the region. Next step: show how the factors influence one another.","weak":"Pick three factors, say what each means, give a Caribbean example (for example prepaid data bundles for affordability) and explain the effect on mobile use."},{"criterion":"Trends and Caribbean implications","marks":6,"look":["mobile money|sand dollar|dcash|jam.?dex|wallet|digital currenc","digital id|identity|nids|e-?government|online service","ai|5g|iot|super app|pwa|low.?code|ar\\b|augmented|wearable|payment","opportunit|benefit|advantage","challenge|risk|concern|however|privacy|secur|exclu"],"min":3,"strong":"A balanced discussion of trends with real regional implications.","weak":"Name two trends (one must be mobile money, digital ID or e-government), then give an opportunity and a challenge for the Caribbean, such as financial inclusion versus privacy risk."},{"criterion":"Research tools","marks":3,"look":["google trends","gsma","statista","data\\.?ai|app annie","sensor tower","itu|world bank|survey"],"min":2,"strong":"Appropriate tools with a clear purpose for each.","weak":"Name two tools and say what each shows, for example \"Google Trends shows changes in search interest over time; GSMA Intelligence gives mobile subscriber data for the region.\""}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The first factor is <b>commoditisation</b>. Entry-level Android smartphones are now standardised products sold by many brands, so prices have fallen. Therefore far more Caribbean residents, including students and older people, own a smartphone than a decade ago. The second factor is <b>affordability</b> of data. Prepaid bundles from operators such as TelEm, Digicel and Flow let people get online without a contract, which increases daily mobile Internet use; however, data costs still lead many users to prefer light apps and free Wi-Fi. The third factor is <b>empowerment</b>. Because almost everyone carries a connected phone, small vendors, such as food sellers in Bridgetown or craft makers in Grenada, can advertise on Instagram and take orders on WhatsApp, turning users into producers and entrepreneurs.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One trend is <b>mobile money and digital currency</b>, such as the Bahamas' Sand Dollar and Jamaica's JAM-DEX. An opportunity is financial inclusion: unbanked residents can store and send money by phone, and remittances from the diaspora can arrive faster. However, a challenge is trust and reliability, as the ECCB's DCash pilot showed when technical problems interrupted the service; people will not adopt a system they cannot rely on. A second trend is <b>e-government services</b>. Paying bills, renewing licences and booking appointments by phone could reduce queues at government offices and save time and money for citizens. The challenge is that services handle sensitive personal data, so they must be secure and follow data protection laws, and they must be accessible to older residents and people with disabilities, or digital literacy gaps will exclude them.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Leaders could use <b>GSMA Intelligence</b>, whose regional mobile economy reports show subscriber numbers, network coverage and adoption of mobile services across the Caribbean. They could also use <b>Google Trends</b>, which shows how search interest in terms such as \"mobile money\" or \"online government services\" changes over time in each country, revealing growing public interest. Both should be checked for how current they are and confirmed with official ITU or national data.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>\"Explain\" needs cause and effect, so link every factor to a result with \"therefore\" or \"because\". \"Discuss\" needs both an opportunity and a challenge. Name real Caribbean developments accurately, but never invent precise statistics: Paper 02 rewards reasoning, not memorised numbers.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Discuss factors and trends in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem (Foundation)","bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: factors and trends, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> TWO factors that are changing mobile use in the Caribbean. For each, <b>say</b> how it changes mobile use and <b>give</b> an example. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE Caribbean trend: mobile money, digital ID or e-government. <b>Give</b> ONE opportunity and ONE challenge. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO research tools and <b>say</b> what ONE of them shows. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":90,"max":240,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> One factor is ... This means ... For example, ... Another factor is ... This means ... For example, ...<br><b>(b)</b> One trend is ... An opportunity is ... A challenge is ...<br><b>(c)</b> Two research tools are ... and ... ... shows ...</p>","checklist":["I named two factors, said how each changes mobile use and gave an example.","I named a Caribbean trend with an opportunity and a challenge.","I named two research tools and explained one."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Two factors with effects and examples","marks":4,"look":["commodit|afford|accessib|adopt|behaviou?r|empower|access|cheap|price|prepaid","means|so |because|therefore|more people","for example|such as|e\\.g\\.","telem|digicel|flow|whatsapp|instagram|vendor|student|caribbean|sint maarten|jamaica|guyana|grenada"],"min":3,"strong":"You explained two factors with Caribbean examples.","weak":"Name two factors (such as affordability and empowerment), say how each changes mobile use and give a Caribbean example."},{"criterion":"Caribbean trend with an opportunity and a challenge","marks":3,"look":["mobile money|sand dollar|jam.?dex|dcash|digital id|identity|e-?government|online service","opportunit|help|benefit|inclusion|faster|save","challenge|risk|problem|privacy|secur|trust|older|exclu"],"min":2,"strong":"You gave a trend with both sides.","weak":"Name the trend, then give one good effect (such as helping people without bank accounts) and one problem (such as privacy or trust)."},{"criterion":"Research tools","marks":3,"look":["google trends|gsma|statista|data\\.?ai|app annie|sensor tower|itu|world bank|survey","shows|tells|gives|compare|search interest|data|report"],"min":2,"strong":"You named tools and explained one.","weak":"Name two tools, such as Google Trends and GSMA Intelligence, and say what one shows."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> One factor is affordability. This means more people can pay for data, so more people go online every day. For example, prepaid bundles from TelEm and Digicel let students get online without a contract. Another factor is empowerment. This means phones give people new ways to earn money. For example, food vendors in Bridgetown take orders on WhatsApp and advertise on Instagram.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One trend is mobile money, such as the Sand Dollar in the Bahamas. An opportunity is that people without bank accounts can send and receive money by phone. A challenge is trust, because people will not use a system that sometimes fails.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Two research tools are Google Trends and GSMA Intelligence. Google Trends shows how interest in a search term, such as \"mobile money\", changes over time in each country.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): (a) for each factor, 1 for the effect and 1 for a Caribbean example; (b) 1 trend, 1 opportunity, 1 challenge; (c) 1 per tool named, 1 for what it shows. Encourage \"this means ...\" sentences. Move students to the Core task once they can add a third factor and use \"therefore\".</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Discuss factors and trends in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Extended response: factors, trends and monitoring, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Caribbean Youth Tech Forum, meeting in Bridgetown, has asked students to brief regional leaders on \"The next five years of mobile in the Caribbean\". Leaders want to know what is driving change, which trends matter most and how to track them. During the meeting, one leader says: <b>\"AI and 5G will transform everything. We should put our whole digital budget into them and forget the rest.\"</b> Another leader points out that many residents still use mid-range phones on prepaid data, and that some older citizens have never used an app.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a briefing that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>analyses</b> how THREE factors interact, as a chain of causes, to drive ONE named trend in the Caribbean; <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>evaluates</b> THREE trends, one of which must be mobile money, digital ID or e-government and one of which must be AI or 5G, giving an opportunity, a challenge and the region's readiness for each; <i>(9 marks)</i></li><li><b>evaluates</b> the first leader's claim and <b>recommends</b> which trend leaders should prioritise; <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>designs</b> a monitoring plan using THREE research tools, explaining what each would show and ONE limitation of each. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":450,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Because ..., ... which in turn ... As a result, ... drives the trend of ...<br><b>(b)</b> Trend 1: ... An opportunity is ... A challenge is ... The region is ready / not yet ready because ... Trend 2: ... Trend 3: ...<br><b>(c)</b> The claim is partly right because ... However, ... I recommend prioritising ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> Tool 1: ... would show ... but ... Tool 2: ... Tool 3: ...</p>","checklist":["I linked three factors in a cause-and-effect chain that ends in one named trend.","I evaluated three trends (including mobile money, digital ID or e-government, and AI or 5G) with an opportunity, a challenge and readiness for each.","I judged the leader's claim using evidence from the stimulus, such as mid-range phones, prepaid data and older citizens.","My monitoring plan names three tools, what each shows and one limitation of each.","I used real Caribbean developments accurately and did not invent precise statistics."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Factor chain driving a trend","marks":6,"look":["commodit|cheap|price","afford|prepaid","adopt","behaviou?r|empower|access","which in turn|as a result|therefore|leads to|this means","mobile money|payment|e-?government|digital id|whatsapp|social commerce|trend"],"min":4,"strong":"Three factors linked as a chain that clearly drives one trend.","weak":"Show how one factor causes the next (for example commoditisation, then affordability, then adoption) and end with the trend it drives."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of three trends","marks":9,"look":["mobile money|sand dollar|jam.?dex|dcash|digital id|e-?government","\\bai\\b|artificial intelligence|5g","opportunit|benefit|inclusion|efficien","challenge|risk|concern|privacy|secur|trust|cost","ready|readiness|not yet|already|infrastructure|spectrum|skills","caribbean|sint maarten|jamaica|bahamas|eccb|oecs|guyana|barbados"],"min":5,"strong":"Three trends evaluated with opportunities, challenges and readiness, using real regional examples.","weak":"For each trend give an opportunity, a challenge and a judgement about whether the region is ready, with a real example such as the Sand Dollar or DCash."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of the claim and a recommendation","marks":5,"look":["claim|leader|statement","partly|however|although|but","mid-range|prepaid|older|access|afford|exclu","recommend|prioriti","because"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced evaluation of the claim and a justified priority.","weak":"Say what is right and wrong about the claim using the stimulus (mid-range phones, prepaid data, older citizens), then recommend a priority with reasons."},{"criterion":"Monitoring plan with limitations","marks":5,"look":["google trends","gsma|itu|world bank","survey|console|analytics|statista|sensor tower|data\\.?ai","show|track|measure|compare","limit|however|but|estimate|lag|relative|too little|small"],"min":4,"strong":"Three suitable tools, each with a purpose and a limitation.","weak":"Name three tools, say what each would show and give one limitation of each, such as relative data, time lags or estimates."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Because entry-level Android phones have become <b>commoditised</b>, many brands now sell similar handsets at low prices. Combined with cheap prepaid data bundles from operators such as TelEm, Digicel and Flow, this has improved <b>affordability</b>, which in turn has speeded <b>adoption</b>: most adults now carry a connected smartphone. As a result, <b>user behaviour</b> has shifted towards doing everyday tasks on the phone, such as ordering food by WhatsApp. Together these factors drive the trend of <b>mobile payments and mobile money</b>, because once almost everyone is connected and used to messaging businesses, paying by phone becomes the natural next step.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Mobile money and digital currency.</b> The opportunity is financial inclusion: unbanked residents and families receiving remittances can store and send money by phone, as the Bahamas' Sand Dollar and Jamaica's JAM-DEX aim to do. The challenge is trust and reliability; the ECCB's DCash pilot faced technical problems and ended. The region is partly ready: phone ownership is high, but merchant acceptance and confidence are still growing. <b>E-government.</b> Paying bills, renewing licences and booking appointments by phone would save residents time and reduce queues at government offices. The challenge is protecting sensitive personal data and including older citizens who have never used an app. Readiness is moderate: data protection laws exist in several territories, but services need assisted routes and digital literacy programmes. <b>5G.</b> It offers much faster speeds and lower delay, enabling richer streaming and IoT. However, rollout is uneven, it depends on spectrum licensing and costly investment in small markets, and many residents' mid-range phones and prepaid plans would not benefit for some time. The region is not yet ready for 5G to be the main platform.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The leader's claim is partly right: AI and 5G will matter, and AI features such as translation and captions could improve services. However, putting the whole budget into them would ignore the evidence in the stimulus: many residents use mid-range phones on prepaid data, and some older citizens have never used an app, so 5G-dependent services would exclude the people who most need them. I recommend prioritising <b>e-government services built to run on 4G and older phones</b>, with mobile payment options and assisted access, because they deliver benefits to everyone now and create the secure, trusted foundation that AI features can later be added to.</p><p><b>(d)</b> <b>GSMA Intelligence</b> would show subscriber numbers, network coverage and 5G adoption across the region; however, regional figures are dominated by large countries and may not reflect small islands. <b>Google Trends</b> would show how search interest in terms such as \"mobile money\" or \"online services\" changes in each country, but it gives relative interest, not numbers, and small islands may have too little data. A <b>resident survey</b>, repeated each year, would show who uses digital services and why others do not; its limitation is that the sample must be representative, including older and rural residents, or the results will be biased.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks): (a) 6 = three factors (3) and two clear causal links ending in a named trend (3). (b) 9 = 3 per trend: opportunity, challenge and a readiness judgement with a real example. (c) 5 = what is right about the claim, what is wrong using the stimulus, a recommendation and its justification. (d) 5 = three tools with purposes, plus limitations (at least two needed for full marks). Accept AI instead of 5G in (b) if its data, accuracy and language challenges are evaluated.</p>"}}]}],"test":{"title":"Module 3 Test","pass":70,"items":[{"q":"Which of the following is part of the INFRASTRUCTURE component of the mobile ecosystem?","options":["A news app from a national newspaper","A smartwatch","Cell towers and submarine fibre-optic cables","A teenager streaming music in Port of Spain"],"answer":2,"why":"Infrastructure is the physical and technical backbone that carries data, such as towers, spectrum and cables. A smartwatch is tempting because it is hardware, but it is a device, a separate component of the ecosystem."},{"q":"A national regulator sets rules for spectrum licences and personal data protection that all mobile operators and app developers must follow. In the mobile ecosystem, the regulator is part of the","options":["device layer","enabling environment","content layer","consumer base"],"answer":1,"why":"The enabling environment includes policy frameworks, regulators and laws that shape how the ecosystem works. The content layer is tempting because data protection affects apps, but content refers to the apps, media and services themselves, not the rules governing them."},{"q":"After a hurricane damages towers across Grenada, residents cannot use a mobile banking app even though the bank's servers are working. This BEST illustrates","options":["freemium pricing","app store optimisation","interdependence within the mobile ecosystem","fragmentation"],"answer":2,"why":"Each part of the ecosystem relies on the others: without carrier infrastructure, content and devices cannot connect. Fragmentation is tempting because it is a mobile problem, but it describes the variety of Android devices and versions, not network failure."},{"q":"Which feature BEST describes the Android operating system?","options":["Used only on smartwatches","Open source, used by many manufacturers, with a wide range of devices and versions","Closed source and available only on Apple devices","Unable to run apps from Google Play"],"answer":1,"why":"Android is open source and licensed to many manufacturers, which brings variety but also fragmentation. Closed source on Apple devices describes iOS, which is the most tempting confusion between the two platforms."},{"q":"Jerome wants to build a native iPhone app. Which development environment and language should he use?","options":["Android Studio and Kotlin","Xcode and Swift","MIT App Inventor and blocks","VS Code and HTML only"],"answer":1,"why":"Apple's Xcode with the Swift language and the iOS SDK is the official route to native iOS apps. Android Studio and Kotlin is tempting because it is also an official native toolset, but it builds Android apps, not iOS apps."},{"q":"A farmer in St. Kitts places soil-moisture sensors in a field that send readings over the network to an app on her phone. This is an example of","options":["white-label licensing","the Internet of Things","a super app","a progressive web app"],"answer":1,"why":"The Internet of Things connects everyday objects with sensors so they can collect and share data. A progressive web app is tempting because the farmer views data on her phone, but a PWA is a type of web app; the sensors connected to the network are what make this IoT."},{"q":"A start-up in Jamaica has a small budget and wants to reach as many local users as possible quickly. Most of its target users have mid-range Android phones. Which platform decision is MOST sensible?","options":["Build for iOS first because Apple users spend more","Build separate native apps for Android, iOS and HarmonyOS at once","Build for smartwatches only","Build for Android first, or use a cross-platform tool such as Flutter"],"answer":3,"why":"Matching the platform to the target audience, budget and time-to-market points to Android first, or one cross-platform code base. iOS first is tempting because iOS users often spend more on apps, but it ignores where this start-up's actual users are and adds cost."},{"q":"Many Caribbean users spend hours on international streaming and social apps, but relatively few locally made apps and channels exist. This is described as","options":["edge computing","platform fragmentation","the digital divide between islands","the imbalance between content consumption and content production"],"answer":3,"why":"The syllabus highlights that the Caribbean consumes far more digital content than it produces. The digital divide is tempting because it is also an inequality, but it refers to unequal ACCESS to technology, not the gap between consuming and creating content."},{"q":"What is the MAIN role of submarine fibre-optic cables in the Caribbean mobile ecosystem?","options":["They provide GPS signals to phones","They charge mobile phone batteries","They carry international Internet traffic between islands and the rest of the world","They replace the need for local cell towers"],"answer":2,"why":"Most international data travels through undersea cables that link island networks to the global Internet. Replacing cell towers is tempting because cables carry data, but towers are still needed to connect phones wirelessly to the network on land."},{"q":"A Guyanese family living in Toronto sends money home and video-calls relatives every week using mobile apps. This BEST illustrates","options":["the diaspora connection supported by mobile technology","content production","enterprise distribution","e-government services"],"answer":0,"why":"Mobile apps bridge Caribbean communities across the world, supporting remittances and family ties. E-government is tempting because money transfers can be official, but e-government means government services delivered online, not personal family communication."},{"q":"Which is the STRONGEST economic benefit of developing apps locally in Barbados rather than importing them?","options":["Imported apps are always illegal","Local apps cannot be used by tourists","Local development creates jobs and builds skills, and successful apps can be exported to other islands","Local apps never need updating"],"answer":2,"why":"Local app development keeps money and skills in the economy and can grow into regional exports. 'Local apps never need updating' is tempting because local developers are close by, but every app needs ongoing updates for OS changes and security."},{"q":"KIN Heritage Trail app guides visitors to historic sites such as Fort Charles with audio stories told by local elders. Which benefit of local apps does this MOST directly demonstrate?","options":["Reduced data usage","Enterprise distribution","Cultural preservation and social linkage","Hardware fragmentation"],"answer":2,"why":"Recording elders' stories preserves heritage and connects visitors and residents to local culture. Reduced data usage is tempting because good apps optimise data, but it is a UX factor, not a benefit of the app being local."},{"q":"An app for Dominica's disaster preparedness lets residents find shelters, receive alerts and mark themselves safe. This is BEST classified as a","options":["subscription entertainment app","freemium game","white-label shopping app","social impact (civic) app"],"answer":3,"why":"Disaster preparedness apps serve public safety and community well-being, which makes them social impact or civic apps. A subscription app is tempting because alerts are ongoing, but charging people for life-saving alerts would conflict with the app's public purpose."},{"q":"Older users of a health clinic app in Antigua keep tapping the wrong buttons. Which change MOST directly addresses this UX problem?","options":["Add a loading animation","Add more features to the home screen","Use smaller text so more fits on screen","Increase touch target sizes and spacing between buttons"],"answer":3,"why":"Larger touch targets (about 44 to 48 points) with space between them reduce mistaken taps, especially for older users. Adding more features is tempting because it seems helpful, but it crowds the screen and makes mis-taps more likely."},{"q":"Many users of a Guyanese farming app live in rural areas with weak, expensive mobile data. Which design strategy is MOST appropriate?","options":["Require users to stay online at all times","Use high-resolution autoplay videos on every screen","Add a large animated splash screen","Compress images, cache data and provide an offline mode"],"answer":3,"why":"Optimising for low bandwidth means small files, caching and offline features so the app still works with poor coverage. Autoplay video is tempting because it is engaging, but it consumes large amounts of data and fails on weak connections."},{"q":"A delivery app is used by drivers outdoors in bright Caribbean sunlight. Which factor of the mobile user experience is MOST relevant?","options":["App store ranking","Processing power","Revenue model","The physical environment"],"answer":3,"why":"The environment (lighting, noise, movement) affects readability, so designers use high contrast, large text and possibly a high-visibility mode. Processing power is tempting because drivers need fast apps, but it does not explain the difficulty of reading a screen in sunlight."},{"q":"Which feature MOST improves accessibility for blind users of a mobile app?","options":["A colourful splash screen","Text placed inside images","Gesture-only navigation with no buttons","Labels on every button and image that screen readers such as TalkBack and VoiceOver can read"],"answer":3,"why":"Screen readers depend on proper labels to describe buttons and images aloud. Gesture-only navigation is tempting because it removes visual clutter, but hidden gestures are hard to discover and are often unusable with a screen reader."},{"q":"What is the main DIFFERENCE between a wireframe and a clickable prototype?","options":["A wireframe shows static screen layouts; a clickable prototype links screens so users can test the flow","A wireframe is the finished app; a prototype is a sketch","There is no difference","A wireframe is written in Kotlin; a prototype is written in Swift"],"answer":0,"why":"Wireframes plan layout and structure; prototypes add interactivity so user flows can be tested before coding. The first option is tempting because both are stages, but it reverses them: the wireframe comes first and the finished app comes last."},{"q":"A group of CAPE students with no coding experience wants a working Android prototype of a school canteen ordering app within two weeks. Which tool is MOST suitable?","options":["MIT App Inventor or Thunkable","A text editor and Java only","Blender","Xcode"],"answer":0,"why":"No-code and low-code builders let beginners create working apps quickly with visual blocks. Xcode is tempting because it is a professional app tool, but it builds iOS apps and requires Swift coding, which does not fit beginners targeting Android."},{"q":"Before launch, Jerome gives the BusLink GND app to twenty commuters, who check whether it actually meets their needs. This is","options":["debugging","unit testing","app store optimisation","user acceptance testing (UAT)"],"answer":3,"why":"UAT is performed by real target users to confirm the app meets their requirements. Unit testing is tempting because it is also testing, but developers do it on small pieces of code, not real users on the whole app."},{"q":"A Barbados bank releases an internal app only to its own staff, without listing it on Google Play or the App Store. This distribution model is","options":["enterprise distribution","a beta release on a public store","direct download for the public","freemium"],"answer":0,"why":"Enterprise distribution delivers apps privately to an organisation's employees. Direct download is tempting because the app avoids public stores, but direct download usually offers the file to the general public from a website."},{"q":"Keisha rewrites an app's store title, adds the keywords 'St. Lucia bus' and 'Castries', uploads clear screenshots and encourages happy users to leave ratings. This is","options":["a press release","App Store Optimisation (ASO)","enterprise distribution","influencer marketing"],"answer":1,"why":"ASO improves the title, keywords, description, screenshots and ratings so the app ranks higher in store searches. Influencer marketing is tempting because ratings involve other people, but influencer marketing pays or partners with content creators to promote the app outside the store."},{"q":"Which sequence shows the user journey for a mobile app in the correct order?","options":["Discover, test, review, purchase, download, retain","Retain, review, test, discover, download, purchase","Purchase, download, discover, test, review, retain","Download, discover, review, retain, test, purchase"],"answer":0,"why":"Users first discover the app, try or test it, read reviews, purchase (if paid), download and finally are retained. Starting with purchase is tempting because buying feels like the key step, but users rarely buy before they have discovered and judged the app."},{"q":"A Trinidad language-learning app is free to download with basic lessons, but users pay to unlock advanced courses and offline downloads. This revenue model is","options":["white-label licensing","paid upfront","freemium","donation-based"],"answer":2,"why":"Freemium offers a free core product with paid premium features. Paid upfront is tempting because users do pay, but paid upfront means paying before downloading at all."},{"q":"In a free mobile game, players can choose to watch a 30-second advert to earn extra lives. This is","options":["a rewarded video ad","an interstitial ad","a subscription","a banner ad"],"answer":0,"why":"Rewarded video gives users a reward for choosing to watch an advert. An interstitial is tempting because it is also full screen, but interstitials appear automatically at breaks; the user does not choose to watch them for a reward."},{"q":"A St. Lucian developer builds one restaurant ordering app and licenses it to ten restaurants, each of which adds its own logo and colours. This revenue model is","options":["in-app advertising","white-label licensing","freemium","crowdfunding"],"answer":1,"why":"White-label apps are built once and licensed to other businesses to rebrand as their own. Freemium is tempting because the developer may offer a basic version, but freemium earns from end users upgrading, not from businesses licensing the app."},{"q":"Which revenue approach is MOST appropriate for a hurricane-alert app run by a Caribbean charity?","options":["Grants, sponsorship and donations, with the app free to all users","A monthly subscription for alerts","Charging per alert received","Interstitial ads before every alert"],"answer":0,"why":"Civic and social good apps are usually funded by grants, sponsors and donations so that everyone can access safety information. A subscription is tempting because it gives steady income, but it would exclude people who cannot pay from life-saving alerts, which is unethical for this purpose."},{"q":"One app that combines messaging, taxi booking, food delivery and payments, as WeChat and Grab do, is known as a","options":["white-label app","headless app","super app","wearable app"],"answer":2,"why":"Super apps consolidate many services into one platform. White-label app is tempting because it is also a business model term, but it describes one app rebranded for many businesses, not many services in one app."},{"q":"A Caribbean health app developer must explain what personal data is collected, obtain consent and allow users to delete their data. These requirements are MOST influenced by","options":["data protection regulation such as the GDPR and regional data protection laws","App Store Optimisation","foldable devices","5G rollout"],"answer":0,"why":"Privacy laws such as the GDPR and Caribbean data protection acts require transparency, consent and the right to deletion. 5G is tempting because it is a major trend, but it concerns network speed, not how personal data must be handled."},{"q":"Which tool is designed MAINLY for tracking app downloads, rankings and market trends?","options":["Sensor Tower","Balsamiq","Audacity","Blender"],"answer":0,"why":"Sensor Tower (like data.ai, formerly App Annie, and Statista) tracks app market data and trends. Balsamiq is tempting because it is used in app development, but it is a wireframing tool, not a research tool."}]},"paper2":{"type":"write","title":"Paper 02 practice: a Caribbean mobile app concept","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: app concept, mobile UX factors, revenue model and marketing","stimulus":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\">St. Lucia's public transport relies largely on privately operated minibuses that follow routes between Castries, Gros Islet, Rodney Bay, Babonneau and Vieux Fort. There is no official live timetable, so students, workers, older residents and cruise visitors often wait without knowing when a bus will come. Jerome and Keisha at Island Pulse Media want to create <b>BusLink SLU</b>, a mobile app to solve this problem. They note that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>many users have older or mid-range Android phones, and data signals are weak in some hillside areas;</li>\n<li>users often check the app outdoors in strong sunlight, while carrying bags or holding a child;</li>\n<li>cruise visitors may speak English, Spanish, German or French, and some older residents have limited digital skills;</li>\n<li>minibus drivers and owners are willing to share their location if it brings them more passengers;</li>\n<li>the team has a small budget and wants the app to be sustainable after launch.</li>\n</ul>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Describe the app concept for BusLink GEO, stating its purpose, its target users and <b>THREE</b> core features. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>TWO</b> factors from the scenario that impact the mobile user experience, and describe a design strategy to address each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend a suitable revenue model for BusLink GEO. Justify your choice and explain why <b>ONE</b> other model would be less suitable. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Describe how the team should distribute and market the app, including <b>ONE</b> App Store Optimisation technique and <b>ONE</b> pre-launch activity. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","min":250,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> BusLink BGI will ... Its target users are ... Its three core features are ...<br><b>(b)</b> One factor is ... This affects users because ... A strategy is ... A second factor is ... A strategy is ...<br><b>(c)</b> I recommend ... because ... A ... model would be less suitable because ...<br><b>(d)</b> The app should be distributed through ... For ASO, ... Before launch, ... The app will also be marketed by ...</p>","checklist":["I stated a clear purpose, named the target users and listed three specific core features.","I explained two UX factors from the scenario (bandwidth, screen and touch, environment, user profile, device performance) and gave a design strategy for each.","I recommended a named revenue model, justified it for this app and rejected another model with a reason.","I named a distribution route, one ASO technique and one pre-launch activity such as a beta test.","I used correct terms (offline mode, touch targets, freemium, ASO, UAT) and labelled my parts (a) to (d)."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"App concept","marks":3,"look":["purpose|solve|problem|aim","student|worker|resident|visitor|tourist|commuter|passenger","live|map|gps|track|location|eta|arriv","route|stop|alert|notif|fare|language|rating"],"min":3,"strong":"Your concept has a clear purpose, defined users and three specific features.","weak":"State the purpose (show where minibuses are and when they will arrive), the users (students, workers, older residents, cruise visitors) and three features, e.g. live bus map, route finder and arrival alerts."},{"criterion":"Mobile UX factors and strategies","marks":4,"look":["bandwidth|data|signal|connect|offline|cache","sunlight|outdoor|environment|contrast|bright","touch|button|one.?hand|thumb|screen size","older|age|digital skill|language|translat|icon|accessib|user profile"],"min":2,"strong":"You linked two scenario factors to specific, practical design strategies.","weak":"Explain each factor and a fix, e.g. weak hillside signals: cache route maps for offline use and send small location updates; strong sunlight: high-contrast theme with large text and big touch targets for one-handed use."},{"criterion":"Revenue model","marks":4,"look":["freemium|advert|ad|subscription|sponsor|grant|licen|commission|in-app","because|sustain|free for|afford|reach","less suitable|would not|unsuitable|however|instead|discourage|barrier"],"min":2,"strong":"Your revenue model is justified for this audience and you rejected an alternative with a clear reason.","weak":"Recommend a model, e.g. free app funded by local business ads and a small monthly listing fee from bus owners, and explain why a paid app would block students and visitors."},{"criterion":"Distribution and marketing","marks":4,"look":["google play|play store|app store|pwa|web","aso|keyword|title|screenshot|rating|description","beta|test|pilot|uat|pre.?launch|waitlist","social|instagram|tiktok|whatsapp|influencer|poster|qr|cruise|port|radio|press"],"min":3,"strong":"Your plan covers distribution, a specific ASO technique, a pre-launch activity and targeted promotion.","weak":"Distribute on Google Play (with an iOS version or PWA), use the keywords 'St. Lucia bus' in the title, run a beta test with student commuters, and promote with QR posters at bus stops and the cruise pier."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>BusLink SLU</b> will solve the problem of uncertain minibus waiting times in St. Lucia by showing where buses are and when they are likely to arrive. Its <b>target users</b> are students, workers and older residents who rely on minibuses daily, and cruise visitors exploring the island. Its <b>three core features</b> are: (1) a <b>live map</b> showing minibuses on each route using drivers' GPS locations, with estimated arrival times at each stop; (2) a <b>route finder</b> where users type or tap a destination, such as Rodney Bay or Soufrière, to see which route to take and the usual fare; and (3) <b>arrival alerts</b> that notify users when their bus is about five minutes away, available in English, Spanish, German and French.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One factor is <b>bandwidth and data usage</b>. Signals are weak in some hillside areas and many users have limited data, so a heavy app would load slowly or fail. The strategy is to <b>cache route maps and stop lists for offline use</b>, keep images small, and send only tiny location updates, so the app still shows routes with poor coverage. A second factor is the <b>physical environment and portability</b>. Users check the app outdoors in strong sunlight while carrying bags or a child. The strategy is a <b>high-contrast theme</b> with large text, and <b>large touch targets</b> (about 48dp) placed in the thumb zone so the main actions can be done one-handed. Simple icons and a language selector on first launch would also support older residents and visitors with different digital skills.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I recommend a <b>free app funded by advertising and business partnerships</b>, combined with a small monthly fee from minibus owners for a 'verified bus' listing. Keeping the app free removes the barrier for students and visitors, which grows the number of users; that large local audience makes it attractive for businesses such as restaurants along the routes to buy small, relevant banner ads, and owners pay because the app brings them more passengers. This makes the app sustainable after launch. A <b>paid upfront model</b> would be less suitable, because students and short-stay cruise visitors are unlikely to pay before trying an app they may use only a few times, so downloads would stay low and the live map would be less useful.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The app should be distributed mainly through <b>Google Play</b>, since many users have Android phones, with an iOS version or a progressive web app so visitors with iPhones can also use it. For <b>ASO</b>, the store title should be 'BusLink SLU: St. Lucia Bus Times' with keywords such as 'Castries bus' and 'St. Lucia minibus', plus clear screenshots of the live map. Before launch, the team should run a <b>beta test</b> (user acceptance testing) with fifty student commuters and ten drivers for two weeks to fix bugs and gather early ratings. Marketing should include <b>QR code posters</b> at bus stops and at the Castries cruise port, short TikTok and Instagram videos showing a student catching the bus on time, and a press release to local news media.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Mobile app questions reward answers that stay <b>inside the scenario</b>. Every UX factor should quote a detail from the stimulus (weak hillside signals, sunlight, older users) and every strategy should be a concrete design decision. When asked to justify a revenue model, explain who pays, why they would pay and how this keeps the app sustainable, then reject an alternative with a reason. Name specific tools and terms: offline mode, touch targets, ASO, beta test.</p>",
 "easy": {"title":"Paper 02 practice: a Caribbean mobile app concept (Foundation)","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: app concept, UX, revenue and marketing, Foundation (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Read the scenario again. Answer ALL parts in one or two sentences each. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> the purpose of BusLink BGI in one sentence. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO groups of target users. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>List</b> TWO core features the app must have. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Choose</b> ONE factor from the scenario that affects the user experience, and <b>give</b> ONE design strategy for it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> a revenue model for the app and <b>give</b> ONE reason it suits BusLink BGI. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE way to distribute or promote the app. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":100,"max":260,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The purpose of BusLink KIN is to ...<br><b>(b)</b> Two groups of target users are ... and ...<br><b>(c)</b> Two core features are ... and ...<br><b>(d)</b> One factor is ... To fix this, the app should ...<br><b>(e)</b> The app could earn money from ... This suits the app because ...<br><b>(f)</b> The app could be promoted by ...</p>","checklist":["My purpose sentence says what problem the app solves.","I named two groups of users from the scenario.","I listed two core features.","I chose a UX factor from the scenario and gave a strategy for it.","I named a revenue model with a reason.","I gave one way to distribute or promote the app."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Purpose","marks":1,"look":["bus|minibus","when|where|wait|arriv|time"],"min":2,"strong":"Your purpose says what problem the app solves.","weak":"Write one sentence: the app shows where minibuses are and when they will arrive."},{"criterion":"Target users","marks":2,"look":["student|worker|resident|older|elder","visitor|tourist|cruise|passenger|commuter|worker"],"min":2,"strong":"You named two groups of users.","weak":"Name two groups from the scenario, such as students and cruise visitors."},{"criterion":"Core features","marks":2,"look":["map|live|gps|location|track","route|stop|alert|notif|time|fare|language"],"min":2,"strong":"You listed two core features.","weak":"List two features, such as a live bus map and arrival alerts."},{"criterion":"UX factor and strategy","marks":2,"look":["signal|data|bandwidth|connect|sunlight|outdoor|bags|child|one.?hand|older|language|skill","offline|save|light|small|contrast|large|big|button|language|translat|icon|label|simple"],"min":2,"strong":"You linked a scenario factor to a design strategy.","weak":"Choose a factor, such as weak signal in the hills, and give a fix, such as saving route maps for offline use."},{"criterion":"Revenue model with a reason","marks":2,"look":["advert|ads?\\b|sponsor|fee|freemium|subscri|grant|licen","because|so |free|afford|student|visitor|owner|driver"],"min":2,"strong":"You named a model and gave a reason.","weak":"Name a model, such as free with local business ads, and say why, for example students and visitors can use it free."},{"criterion":"Distribution or promotion","marks":1,"look":["google play|app store|store|pwa|poster|qr|social|instagram|tiktok|whatsapp|radio|news|beta"],"min":1,"strong":"You gave a way to distribute or promote the app.","weak":"Give one way, such as Google Play or QR posters at bus stops."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The purpose of BusLink SLU is to show people where minibuses are and when they will arrive, so they do not wait without knowing.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Two groups of target users are students who ride the bus to school and cruise visitors exploring the island.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Two core features are a live map of the minibuses and an alert when your bus is about five minutes away.</p><p><b>(d)</b> One factor is weak data signals in some hillside areas. To fix this, the app should save the route maps on the phone so they still work offline.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The app could earn money from small adverts by local businesses along the routes. This suits the app because students and visitors can use it for free.</p><p><b>(f)</b> The app could be promoted with QR-code posters at bus stops and at the cruise pier.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Foundation version (10 marks): 1 for (a) and (f); 2 each for (b), (c), (d) and (e), with the second mark in (d) and (e) awarded only when the strategy or reason links to the scenario. This version builds the four Core answers one piece at a time. Once students can complete it without the starters, ask them to expand (d) to two factors and (e) to include a rejected alternative, which is the Core task.</p>"},
 "up": {"title":"Paper 02 practice: a Caribbean mobile app concept (Challenge)","genre":"Paper 02 extended response: app concept, UX, revenue, distribution and regional adaptation, Grade One extension (25 marks)","stimulus":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\">St. Lucia's public transport relies largely on privately operated minibuses that follow routes between Castries, Gros Islet, Rodney Bay, Babonneau and Vieux Fort. There is no official live timetable, so students, workers, older residents and cruise visitors often wait without knowing when a bus will come. Jerome and Keisha at Island Pulse Media want to create <b>BusLink SLU</b>, a mobile app to solve this problem. They note that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>many users have older or mid-range Android phones, and data signals are weak in some hillside areas;</li>\n<li>users often check the app outdoors in strong sunlight, while carrying bags or holding a child;</li>\n<li>cruise visitors may speak English, Spanish, German or French, and some older residents have limited digital skills;</li>\n<li>minibus drivers and owners are willing to share their location if it brings them more passengers, but some drivers do not want their location tracked when they are off duty;</li>\n<li>the team has about <b>US$3,000</b> for the first year and wants the app to be sustainable after launch;</li>\n<li>the transport department of another Caribbean territory has said it may want a similar app if BusLink SLU succeeds.</li>\n</ul>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Present the app concept: its purpose, a persona for ONE target user, an MVP organised with MoSCoW (at least two Must haves and one Won't have this time) and ONE measurable success measure. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Analyse</b> THREE factors from the scenario that impact the mobile user experience, giving a specific design strategy for each, and <b>explain</b> how ONE strategy addresses more than one factor. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> TWO revenue models for BusLink DOM and <b>recommend</b> a sustainable model or hybrid, addressing the privacy and ethical issue raised by drivers' location data. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Plan</b> the distribution and marketing of the app, mapped to the user journey, including ONE App Store Optimisation technique, a beta test, and TWO metrics with targets. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> how BusLink DOM could be offered to the other territory as a white-label product, and what would need to change. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[25 marks]</b></p>","min":450,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Purpose: ... Persona: ... Must have: ... Should or Could have: ... Won't have this time: ... Success measure: ...<br><b>(b)</b> Factor 1: ... affects users because ... Strategy: ... Factor 2: ... Factor 3: ... The strategy of ... also helps ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> One model is ... An advantage ... However ... A second model is ... I recommend ... because ... To protect drivers' privacy, ...<br><b>(d)</b> Distribution: ... Test: a beta ... Discover: ... ASO: ... Retain: ... Metrics: ... target ... and ... target ...<br><b>(e)</b> As a white-label product, ... would stay the same, while ... would change ...</p>","checklist":["My concept has a purpose, a persona based on the scenario, MoSCoW with all required categories and a measurable success measure.","I analysed three different factors from the scenario, each with a specific strategy, and showed one strategy helping more than one factor.","I evaluated two revenue models, recommended a sustainable option within the US$3,000 context and addressed drivers' location privacy.","My distribution and marketing plan follows the user journey and includes an ASO technique, a beta and two metrics with targets.","I explained what stays the same and what changes in a white-label version for another territory.","I used correct terms (MVP, offline-first, touch targets, freemium, white-label, ASO, UAT, retention)."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"App concept","marks":4,"look":["purpose|solve|problem","persona|\\d\\d,|years old|aged","must","won't|will not","%|percent|within|by the end|measur"],"min":4,"strong":"A complete concept: purpose, persona, MoSCoW and a measurable success measure.","weak":"Give the purpose, a short persona from the scenario, Must and Won't have features, and a success measure with a number and a time limit."},{"criterion":"UX factors and strategies","marks":6,"look":["bandwidth|data|signal|connect|offline|cache","sunlight|outdoor|environment|contrast|glare","touch|button|one.?hand|thumb|48","older|language|digital skill|user profile|icon|label","perform|battery|older android|mid-range","also|both|as well|more than one|at once"],"min":4,"strong":"Three scenario factors with precise strategies, and a clear overlap explained.","weak":"Analyse three factors from the bullet points (for example weak signal, sunlight with busy hands, and language or skills), give a specific strategy for each, then show how one strategy helps several factors."},{"criterion":"Revenue evaluation and privacy","marks":6,"look":["advert|ads?\\b|sponsor|subscri|freemium|licen|grant|fee","advantage|benefit|strength|because","however|disadvantage|but|risk|barrier","recommend|hybrid","privacy|location|off duty|off-duty|consent|track","sustain|3,000|3000|cover|year"],"min":4,"strong":"Two models evaluated, a sustainable recommendation and a clear privacy safeguard for drivers.","weak":"Weigh two models with an advantage and a disadvantage each, recommend one or a hybrid that can fund the app, and explain how drivers control when their location is shared."},{"criterion":"Distribution and marketing plan","marks":6,"look":["google play|app store|pwa|web","aso|keyword|title|screenshot","beta|uat|closed test|pilot","discover|retain|review|download|user journey","poster|qr|cruise|port|radio|press|tiktok|instagram|whatsapp|influencer","retention|conversion|rating|install|active user"],"min":4,"strong":"A complete plan mapped to the user journey with ASO, a beta and measurable targets.","weak":"Name the distribution route, an ASO technique, a beta, channels for discovery and retention, and two metrics with targets."},{"criterion":"White-label adaptation","marks":3,"look":["white.?label|rebrand|licen","same|reuse|core|code|stays","route|stop|currency|language|logo|brand|payment|law|data protection"],"min":3,"strong":"A clear explanation of what is reused and what must change.","weak":"Explain that the core code and features stay the same, while the name, logo, routes, stops, languages, currency, payments and data rules change for the new territory."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Purpose:</b> BusLink SLU shows where St. Lucia's minibuses are and when they are likely to arrive, so passengers no longer wait without information. <b>Persona:</b> Shanice, 17, a student in Babonneau, takes the bus to school daily on a mid-range Android phone with prepaid data, often while carrying a heavy bag. <b>Must have:</b> a live map of buses on each route using drivers' shared locations; estimated arrival times at each stop; route maps saved for offline use. <b>Should have:</b> arrival alerts. <b>Could have:</b> fare information. <b>Won't have this time:</b> in-app ticket payments. <b>Success measure:</b> within three months, 2,000 weekly active users, and 80 percent of tested users finding their next bus in under 30 seconds.</p><p><b>(b)</b> <b>Bandwidth and connectivity:</b> weak hillside signals and prepaid data mean a heavy app would fail or cost users money. Strategy: an <b>offline-first</b> design that stores route maps and stop lists on the phone and sends only tiny location updates. <b>Physical environment and portability:</b> users read the screen in strong sunlight while holding bags or a child. Strategy: a high-contrast theme with large text, and <b>48 dp touch targets</b> in the thumb zone so the main actions work one-handed. <b>User profile:</b> visitors speak English, Spanish, German or French, and some older residents have limited digital skills. Strategy: a language choice on first launch, plain words and a text label beside every icon. The offline-first strategy also helps <b>device performance</b>: fewer network requests save battery and processing power on older Android phones, so one strategy addresses two factors.</p><p><b>(c)</b> <b>Free with local advertising:</b> keeping the app free removes the barrier for students and short-stay visitors, and businesses along the routes would value the audience. However, ad income is small on one island, and ads use data and screen space, so ads must be light and kept away from the map. <b>Listing fees from bus owners:</b> owners gain passengers, so a small monthly \"verified bus\" fee links payment to value and gives steady income. However, owners may resist paying until the app proves it brings riders. I recommend a <b>hybrid</b>: free for passengers, a low monthly listing fee for owners after a free three-month trial, a few local banner ads, and a sponsorship approach to a telecoms provider, which together could cover hosting, store fees and support within the first-year budget and beyond. To respect drivers' <b>privacy</b>, location sharing would run only when the driver switches the app to \"on duty\", with clear consent, and only current positions, not full route histories, would be stored.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The app would be distributed on <b>Google Play</b>, since most users have Android phones, with a <b>PWA</b> so iPhone users and visitors can open it from a QR code without installing anything. <b>Test:</b> a two-week closed <b>beta</b> with 50 student commuters and ten drivers, fixing their top issues before launch. <b>Discover:</b> QR posters at bus stops and the Castries cruise port, short TikTok clips of a student catching the bus on time, and a press release to local media. <b>ASO:</b> the store title \"BusLink SLU: St. Lucia Bus Times\", with keywords such as \"Castries bus\", and captioned screenshots of the live map. <b>Review and retain:</b> a rating request after a successful journey and weekly service updates. <b>Metrics:</b> day-30 retention of at least 30 percent, and a 4.5 average rating within three months.</p><p><b>(e)</b> As a <b>white-label</b> product, the core code, live map, offline storage and alert features would stay the same, and the other territory's transport department would pay a set-up fee and an annual licence. What would change is the name, logo and colours, the routes and stops, the languages, the currency and any payment providers, and the privacy settings needed to meet that territory's data protection law.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Challenge version (25 marks): (a) 4 = purpose, persona, MoSCoW with Must and Won't items, a measurable success measure. (b) 6 = up to 5 for three scenario factors with their effects and specific strategies, plus 1 for a strategy clearly explained across two factors. (c) 6 = two models with an advantage and a disadvantage each (4), a sustainable recommendation (1), a specific privacy safeguard for drivers (1). (d) 6 = distribution route, ASO technique, beta, journey-based channels, two metrics with targets. (e) 3 = what is reused, what changes, how the other territory pays. Every mark should be earned with a detail from the scenario, as in the Core task.</p>"}}});
